window.CISI_DATA.regulation = {"key":"regulation","title":"UK Financial Regulation","subtitle":"CISI Level 3","examFormat":"75 questions - 1h30 - CBT (multiple choice)","chapters":[{"id":"reg-ch1","number":1,"title":"The Regulatory Environment","examWeight":"12 of 75 questions (~16%)","summaryHtml":"<p><strong>Chapter overview:</strong> This chapter covers the architecture of UK financial regulation following the split of the FSA into the FCA and PRA (2013), the rules firms and individuals must follow, and the gateway into (and out of) regulated status.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FSMA 2000</strong> is the primary legislation. The <strong>general prohibition (s19)</strong> makes it a criminal offence (max 2 years' imprisonment + unlimited fine) to carry on a regulated activity in the UK without authorisation or exemption.</li>\n<li><strong>Twin peaks regulation:</strong> the <strong>PRA</strong> (part of the Bank of England since the Financial Services Act 2012) prudentially regulates banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms with a single objective (safety and soundness), plus an insurance objective. The <strong>FCA</strong> regulates conduct for all firms and is the sole prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms. FCA objectives: 1 strategic (markets function well) + 3 operational (consumer protection, integrity, competition). Since <strong>FSMA 2023</strong>, both regulators share a secondary objective: international competitiveness and growth.</li>\n<li><strong>12 Principles for Businesses</strong> are the FCA's high-level rulebook; breach = disciplinary sanction. Principle 12 (<strong>Consumer Duty</strong>, live since 2023, extended to closed products <strong>31 July 2024</strong>) disapplies Principles 6 and 7 for in-scope retail business. Firms out of scope must still meet Principle 6 and the <strong>FTOC</strong> six outcomes. The PRA has 8 <strong>Fundamental Rules</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>SM&amp;CR</strong> (banking 2016, all firms 2016 onward) has 3 pillars: the <strong>Senior Managers Regime</strong> (SMFs, regulator-approved, Form A, 90-day statutory clock, Statement of Responsibilities, Management Responsibilities Map, Duty of Responsibility), the <strong>Certification Regime</strong> (firm self-certifies \"significant harm\" functions annually — PRA scope = Material Risk Takers only), and the <strong>Conduct Rules</strong> (5 individual rules, 4 additional Senior Manager (SC) rules; Rule 6 replaces Rule 4 where Consumer Duty applies). Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators are excluded from SM&amp;CR. The <strong>Fit and Proper Test (FIT)</strong> assesses honesty/integrity/reputation, competence/capability and financial soundness.</li>\n<li><strong>Prudential standards:</strong> GENPRU/BIPRU (CRD firms), MIFIDPRU (MiFID investment firms, effective 1 Jan 2022) and IPRU-INV (other firms) set capital rules. The CRD/CRR frameworks use 3 Pillars (minimum capital; supervisory add-on; disclosure) and 3 capital tiers (Core Tier 1, Tier 2, Tier 3).</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory infrastructure:</strong> CMA (competition), HMRC (info-sharing), FPC (systemic risk, meets ≥4x/year, publishes the Financial Stability Report), BoE (monetary stability via the MPC, meeting 8x/year, and financial stability, incl. lender of last resort), HMT (overall responsibility; appoints FCA CEO/chair), TPR (auto-enrolment under Pensions Act 2008, ages 22 to State Pension age). Internationally: FATF (40 Recommendations on AML/CTF), BIS (est. 1930, central bank of central banks) and IOSCO set standards that shape UK rules without being directly binding.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulated activities</strong> require both a specified investment and a specified activity under the RAO 2001. Key exemptions: Appointed Representatives (s39), RIEs/RCHs (s285), Lloyd's members (s316), the FSMA Exemption Order 2001 (central banks, supranationals), and Part 20 professional firms (DPBs). Key exclusions: absence of holding out, group/joint enterprise, newspaper advice, trustees/nominees, employee share schemes, overseas persons.</li>\n<li><strong>Authorisation</strong> requires satisfying the <strong>Threshold Conditions</strong> (legal status, location of offices, prudent conduct, effective supervision, appropriate resources, suitability, business model, claims representatives). Statutory determination: 6 months (complete application) / 12 months (incomplete).</li>\n<li><strong>Training and Competence:</strong> firms must assess and supervise competence (T&amp;C Sourcebook); retail investment advisers need a <strong>Statement of Professional Standing (SPS)</strong>, valid 12 months, requiring QCF Level 4 and <strong>35 hours' CPD</strong> per year (min 21 hours structured).</li>\n</ul>","sections":[{"heading":"1. The Regulatory Environment (FCA, PRA, objectives, supervision)","html":"\n<h4>1.1 FSMA 2000 and the Financial Services Act 2012</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Financial Services and Markets Act (FSMA)</strong> came into effect in 2001 and is the primary UK legislation regulating financial services, replacing the largely self-regulated pre-existing framework. Following the <strong>Financial Services Act 2012</strong> (effective April 2013), the single regulator (the FSA) was split into the <strong>Financial Conduct Authority (FCA)</strong>, the <strong>Prudential Regulation Authority (PRA)</strong> and the <strong>Financial Policy Committee (FPC)</strong> (part of the Bank of England).</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th></th><th>PRA</th><th>FCA</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Regulates</td><td>Prudential regulation of banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms</td><td>Authorisation &amp; supervision of all other financial institutions (conduct); also prudential regulator of solo-regulated firms (eg, asset managers, stockbrokers)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Accountable to</td><td>Not directly accountable to any government body; answerable to Parliament (House of Commons Treasury Committee) via the Bank of England</td><td>UK Government via HM Treasury</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Part of</td><td>Bank of England (Financial Services Act 2016 established the Prudential Regulation Committee, PRC)</td><td>Independent private company (special privilege: not required to use \"limited\" in its name)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Funding</td><td colspan=\"2\">Both funded entirely from fees paid by regulated firms</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The FCA has powers to investigate and prosecute <strong>insider dealing</strong> and <strong>market abuse</strong>, is the UK <strong>Listing Authority</strong>, oversees the <strong>Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS)</strong>, and (jointly with the PRA) the <strong>Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)</strong>.</p>\n<p>This framework is the <strong>\"twin peaks\" approach</strong>: two supervisors oversee deposit-takers, insurers and investment banks — PRA (prudential) and FCA (conduct/market).</p>\n<p>FSMA applies to all \"persons\" (natural and legal persons, incl. firms, partnerships, trusts). It is a <strong>criminal offence</strong> to conduct regulated activity by way of business unless authorised or exempt — the <strong>general prohibition</strong> (<strong>Section 19 FSMA</strong>).</p>\n<h4>1.2 Statutory Objectives</h4>\n<p><strong>FCA</strong> — 1 strategic objective + 3 operational objectives (Financial Services Act 2012):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Strategic objective</strong> — ensuring relevant markets function well.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer protection objective</strong> — securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers (not preventing all risk of loss); FCA has regard to differing risk levels, consumer experience/expertise, need for advice/information, and the principle that consumers take some responsibility for their decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Integrity objective</strong> — protecting/enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system, which includes: soundness, stability and resilience; not being used for financial crime; not being affected by market abuse; orderly operation of markets; reliability/transparency of price formation; suitability of the listing rules.</li>\n<li><strong>Competition objective</strong> — promoting effective competition in consumers' interests. Factors: needs of different consumer types (incl. need for informed-choice information); ease of switching provider; ease of new entrants; extent competition encourages innovation.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>PRA</strong> — single general objective: <em>\"promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons\"</em>, advanced by (a) ensuring PRA firms' business avoids adverse effects on UK financial stability, and (b) minimising the adverse effect of a PRA firm's failure on stability. PRA also has an <strong>insurance objective</strong> — appropriate protection for policyholders.</p>\n<p><strong>Secondary objective (both regulators, Financial Services and Markets Act 2023):</strong> facilitate the international competitiveness of the UK economy and its medium/long-term growth (subject to international standards) — only applies when advancing existing operational objectives.</p>\n<p><strong>Accountability mechanisms:</strong> Annual report to Treasury (scrutinised by Parliamentary committees); rules must relate to statutory objectives; judicial review if objectives misapplied; regulatory failure accountability for serious supervisory failures.</p>\n<h4>1.2.1 Supervisory approach</h4>\n<p>FCA supervision focuses on senior management competence and delivers <strong>credible deterrence</strong> via a consistent, transparent penalty framework. PRA supervision follows 3 principles: <strong>judgement-based, forward-looking, and focused on key risks</strong> (assessed against the \"Threshold Conditions\"). PRA uses a <strong>risk element framework</strong>: gross risk (potential impact on UK financial stability + external context/business model risk) is offset by mitigating factors (actions the firm takes) to reach a <strong>net risk</strong> assessment.</p>\n<p>Post-crisis, the FCA moved from a \"light touch\" (reactive) model to a more <strong>intrusive, proactive</strong> model with two features: (1) enhanced analysis and risk identification (business model analysis, mystery shopping, on-site visits); (2) greater focus on <strong>outcome testing</strong> rather than just systems and controls.</p>\n<h4>1.2.2 Risk-based approach</h4>\n<p>Following the 2007–08 global financial crisis, the FCA increased focus on firm culture and conduct. In 2019 it published <em>\"FCA Mission: Approach to Supervision\"</em>, outlining 8 supervisory principles. The FCA's supervisory model has <strong>3 types of work</strong>: <strong>Proactive</strong> (pre-emptive harm identification via business model analysis/culture review), <strong>Reactive</strong> (dealing with emerging issues), <strong>Thematic</strong> (wider diagnostic/remedy work across multiple firms).</p>\n<h4>1.2.3 Conduct Risk</h4>\n<p>The FCA does not give a precise definition of <strong>conduct risk</strong> — broadly, the risk to customers and market integrity from how firms and staff conduct themselves. <strong>Treating Customers Fairly (TCF)</strong>, with its 6 principles, was introduced in 2006 and has since been renamed <strong>Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC)</strong>.</p>\n<h4>1.3 Rule-Making Powers</h4>\n<p><strong>Part 9A FSMA</strong> empowers the FCA to make legally binding rules on authorised firms (regulated and unregulated activity) to advance its operational objectives. The PRA has similar powers limited to PRA-authorised persons. FSMA also empowers both regulators to: grant/vary/cancel authorisation (<strong>Part 4A permission</strong>); supervise firms on an ongoing basis; impose disciplinary measures/sanctions; and enforce the regulatory framework using credible deterrence.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2. Principles for Businesses (incl. Consumer Duty and FTOC)","html":"\n<h4>2.1 The FCA's 12 Principles for Businesses</h4>\n<p>Breach of a Principle is a disciplinary offence (Principles are legally binding on all authorised firms):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Integrity</strong> — conduct business with integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Skill, care and diligence</strong> — conduct business with due skill, care and diligence.</li>\n<li><strong>Management and control</strong> — organise/control affairs responsibly and effectively with adequate risk management systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Financial prudence</strong> — maintain adequate financial resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Market conduct</strong> — observe proper standards of market conduct.</li>\n<li><strong>Customers' interests</strong> — due regard to customers' interests, treat them fairly.</li>\n<li><strong>Communications with clients</strong> — clear, fair, not misleading communications.</li>\n<li><strong>Conflicts of interest</strong> — manage conflicts fairly.</li>\n<li><strong>Customers: relationships of trust</strong> — ensure suitability of advice/discretionary decisions.</li>\n<li><strong>Clients' assets</strong> — arrange adequate protection for client assets.</li>\n<li><strong>Relations with regulators</strong> — open/cooperative dealings; disclose appropriately anything the PRA/FCA would reasonably expect notice of.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer Duty</strong> — act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>2.2 Consumer Duty</h4>\n<p><strong>Implementation:</strong> rules effective 2023 for new/existing products; <strong>31 July 2024</strong> for closed products (products only available to existing customers, no new investment, being wound down).</p>\n<p><strong>Scope</strong> (retail customers): consumer credit (CMCOB/FPCOB); deposit-taking (BCOBS) — covers consumers, micro-enterprises, charities with turnover &lt; <strong>£1 million</strong>, and trustees acting outside trade/business/profession; insurance (ICOBS, excludes reinsurance); investments (COBS, business with non-professional clients — Duty applies to the process used to determine client status even if the client later elects professional status); mortgages (MCOB, regulated mortgages only — not unregulated buy-to-let/commercial lending). Applies to prospective as well as existing customers. Firms are only responsible for their own activities unless a regulatory/legal obligation exists (not for overseeing the whole distribution chain).</p>\n<p><strong>Excluded wholesale activities</strong> (non-exhaustive): manufacture for wholesale-only purposes (retail market business definition); non-retail financial instruments with minimum denomination/investment of <strong>£50,000</strong>; certain market activities; insurance for large commercial risks or risk located outside the UK; group insurance policy distribution; benchmark administration activity. Duty applies on a proportionate basis to wholesale firms that can materially influence retail outcomes (eg, product design/price, distribution, communications, customer support).</p>\n<p><strong>Consumer Principle:</strong> Principle 12 applies to in-scope firms; where it applies, <strong>Principles 6 and 7 no longer apply</strong> to that retail business. Firms out of scope continue to comply with Principles 6 and 7 (not 12).</p>\n<p><strong>Cross-cutting rules</strong> — firms must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Act in good faith</strong> towards retail customers (honest, fair, open dealing; consistent with reasonable customer expectations; considered at product/service design stage).</li>\n<li><strong>Avoid foreseeable harm</strong> to retail customers (customers must understand/accept inherent risk; firms not responsible for protecting against ALL foreseeable harm, but must be clear/transparent).</li>\n<li><strong>Enable and support</strong> retail customers to pursue their financial objectives (firms not responsible for customers' own decisions, but must be clear/transparent).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>4 \"Good Outcomes\":</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Products and Services</strong> — designed to meet the target market's needs (interacts with PROD sourcebook); manufacturers ensure fit for target market and review performance; distributors ensure marketing/distribution reflects target market and take reasonable steps to prevent sales outside it.</li>\n<li><strong>Price and Value</strong> — FCA does not regulate price itself, but expects fair value (relationship between price and benefit received); fair value ≠ identical outcomes for all; new/existing customer pricing can differ but must be clearly communicated.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer Understanding</strong> — clear, timely communication enabling informed decisions about features, costs/risks/benefits, and suitability for needs; tailored to the target market's financial understanding.</li>\n<li><strong>Consumer Support</strong> — appropriate service throughout the relationship; fair, clear, not misleading; matched to target market's financial awareness.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>2.2.2 Accountability</h4>\n<p>New Conduct Rule under SM&amp;CR for in-scope firms: <strong>Conduct Rule 6</strong> — \"You must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers.\" (Rule 6 does not apply to firms out of scope of the Duty.) Senior managers are held accountable via SM&amp;CR for embedding and ongoing compliance. The requirement for a Consumer Duty \"champion\" was <strong>removed by the FCA in February 2025</strong>.</p>\n<h4>2.3 Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC)</h4>\n<p>Applies to firms <strong>out of scope</strong> of the Consumer Duty (no direct retail relationship / material influence, or product/service falls under a Duty exemption). Expressed via Principle 6 and the <strong>\"client's best interests rule\"</strong>: <em>\"A firm must act honestly, fairly and professionally in accordance with the best interests of its client.\"</em></p>\n<p><strong>6 FTOC outcomes:</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>Fair treatment is central to firms' corporate culture.</li>\n<li>Products/services are designed to meet identified consumer group needs and targeted accordingly.</li>\n<li>Consumers get clear information, kept appropriately informed before/during/after sale.</li>\n<li>Advice given is suitable and takes account of circumstances.</li>\n<li>Products perform as led to expect; service is of an acceptable, expected standard.</li>\n<li>No unreasonable post-sale barriers to change product, switch provider, claim or complain.</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>2.4 PRA Fundamental Rules</h4>\n<p>Apply proportionately to all PRA firms. Firms must:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conduct business with integrity.</li>\n<li>Conduct business with due skill, care and diligence.</li>\n<li>Act in a prudent manner.</li>\n<li>Maintain adequate financial resources at all times.</li>\n<li>Have effective risk strategies and risk management systems.</li>\n<li>Organise and control affairs responsibly and effectively.</li>\n<li>Deal with regulators openly/cooperatively and disclose appropriately.</li>\n<li>Prepare for resolution, so the firm can be resolved in an orderly manner with minimum disruption to critical services.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"heading":"3. Fitness and Propriety (SM&CR, Certification Regime, Conduct Rules, FIT)","html":"\n<h4>3.1 Background</h4>\n<p>Following the 2007–08 crisis and conduct failings (eg, LIBOR manipulation), the <strong>Parliamentary Commission for Banking Standards (PCBS)</strong> recommended a new accountability framework focused on senior management. This led to the <strong>Senior Managers &amp; Certification Regime (SM&amp;CR)</strong>, introduced to banking in <strong>2016</strong> and later extended to <strong>all FSMA-authorised firms</strong>, except <strong>Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators</strong>.</p>\n<p>SM&amp;CR comprises: <strong>Senior Managers Regime (SMR)</strong>, <strong>Certification Regime (CR)</strong>, and <strong>Conduct Rules</strong>.</p>\n<p>For solo-regulated firms, 3 proportionate tiers apply: <strong>Core scope</strong> (baseline requirements, majority of firms), <strong>Limited scope</strong> (fewer requirements), <strong>Enhanced scope</strong> (significantly greater requirements).</p>\n<p>A senior manager is only guilty of misconduct if the FCA <strong>proves</strong> they failed to take reasonable steps to prevent a regulatory breach (burden of proof on the FCA), though senior managers should be able to evidence reasonable steps taken.</p>\n<h4>3.2 Senior Managers Regime (SMR)</h4>\n<p>Captures individuals performing <strong>Senior Management Functions (SMFs)</strong> — eg, CEO, CFO, CRO, and others such as head of compliance, MLRO, head of internal audit. SMF holders require <strong>regulatory approval</strong> and are subject to <strong>annual fitness and propriety assessments</strong>. Persons holding SMFs include the Chair and NEDs, who can be held accountable for boardroom decisions.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Form A</strong> — application submitted by the firm for regulatory approval of an SMF candidate; an abbreviated form may apply for existing approved persons moving to a similar function.</li>\n<li><strong>Regulatory references</strong> — firms must take all reasonable steps to obtain references from the candidate's current and all previous employers over the past <strong>6 years</strong>, before submitting Form A.</li>\n<li><strong>Statement of Responsibilities</strong> — sets out the aspects of the firm's affairs the SMF holder will manage; each responsibility is normally allocated to a single senior manager (if shared, division must be clearly articulated).</li>\n<li><strong>90-day statutory clock</strong> — FSMA requires the FCA/PRA to determine fitness and propriety within <strong>90 days</strong> of receiving Form A; the clock stops if the regulator requests further information, resuming once received.</li>\n<li><strong>Prescribed Responsibilities</strong> — allocated to the most senior SMF holders; recorded in the Statement of Responsibilities.</li>\n<li><strong>Management Responsibilities Map</strong> — documents a firm's management/governance arrangements, incl. allocation of prescribed responsibilities, for transparency of accountability.</li>\n<li><strong>Duty of Responsibility</strong> — requires SMF holders (esp. those with prescribed responsibilities) to take reasonable steps to fulfil their responsibilities and to prevent, stop or remedy breaches.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>3.2.1 Application to Banks and Deposit Takers (selected SMFs)</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>SMF</th><th>Function</th><th>Regulator</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1</td><td>Chief Executive</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2</td><td>Chief Finance</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3</td><td>Executive Director</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4</td><td>Chief Risk</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>5</td><td>Head of Internal Audit</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>6</td><td>Head of Key Business Area</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>7</td><td>Group Entity Senior Manager</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>8</td><td>Credit Union SMF (small credit unions only)</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>9</td><td>Chairman</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>10</td><td>Chair of Risk Committee</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>11</td><td>Chair of Audit Committee</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>12</td><td>Chair of Remuneration Committee</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>13</td><td>Chair of Nominations Committee</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>14</td><td>Senior Independent Director</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>15</td><td>(Insurance companies only)</td><td>PRA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>16</td><td>Compliance Oversight</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>17</td><td>Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO)</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n<tr><td>18</td><td>Other Overall Responsibility</td><td>FCA</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>SMFs 9–14 should be held by <strong>approved Non-Executive Directors (NEDs)</strong>, not executives.</p>\n<h4>3.2.2 Application to FCA Solo-Regulated Firms</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Firm type</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Limited Scope</td><td>Fewer requirements — eg, limited permission consumer credit firms, sole traders, non-mainstream authorised professional firms, oil/energy market participants, service companies, local authority/RSL subsidiaries, certain insurance intermediaries, internally managed AIFs.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Core</td><td>\"Baseline\" SM&amp;CR requirements.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enhanced</td><td>Small proportion of solo-regulated firms; extra rules apply.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Core/Enhanced firms typically require: SMF1 (CEO), SMF3 (Executive Director), SMF9 (Chair), SMF16 (Compliance Oversight), SMF17 (MLRO), SMF18 (Other Overall Responsibility — Enhanced only), SMF27 (Partner). Sole traders with no employees need only SMF16. Limited Permission Consumer Credit firms use SMF29 (Limited Scope Function, equivalent to the old APER CF8).</p>\n<h4>3.3 The Certification Regime (CR)</h4>\n<p>Certified individuals are <strong>not approved by the regulator</strong> — the firm itself assesses and certifies (annually) that they are \"fit and proper,\" issuing an annual <strong>certificate</strong>. Firms must assess at recruitment and annually thereafter.</p>\n<p><strong>PRA scope:</strong> limited to <strong>Material Risk Takers (MRTs)</strong> / \"Code staff\" as defined in the Capital Requirements Regulation (CRR) — subject to the Remuneration Code.</p>\n<p><strong>FCA scope</strong> includes \"significant harm functions\": CASS oversight, benchmark submission/administration, proprietary trading, significant management, qualification-required functions, managers of certified staff, and MRTs — as well as legacy SIF roles now outside the SMR.</p>\n<p>Where a certificate is refused (individual not fit and proper), the firm must give written notice of proposed steps and notify the FCA. Multiple certification functions performed by one individual require fitness/propriety assessment of each, but can be covered by a single certificate.</p>\n<p><strong>Overseas staff:</strong> MRTs/Code staff are always in scope, wherever located. Individuals dealing with UK clients from overseas are in scope. Overseas individuals not dealing with UK clients and not MRTs are not certified persons.</p>\n<h4>3.4 The Conduct Rules</h4>\n<p>Apply to all relevant employees except ancillary staff (eg, cleaners, catering). Breaches and formal disciplinary action must be notified to the regulator.</p>\n<p><strong>Individual Conduct Rules (all staff):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><td>Rule 1</td><td>Act with integrity.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 2</td><td>Act with due skill, care and diligence.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 3</td><td>Be open and cooperative with the FCA, PRA and other regulators.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 4</td><td>Pay due regard to customers' interests and treat them fairly.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 5</td><td>Observe proper standards of market conduct.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Rule 6</td><td>Act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers (Consumer Duty firms only).</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Rules 4 and 6 are mutually exclusive</strong>: Rule 6 applies (replacing Rule 4) only for individuals at firms in scope of the Consumer Duty; Rule 4 continues to apply at firms out of scope. So only 5 individual rules apply to any one individual at any one time.</p>\n<p><strong>Senior Manager Conduct Rules (SC1–SC4)</strong> — additional to the 5 individual rules:</p>\n<table>\n<tr><td>SC1</td><td>Take reasonable steps to ensure the business for which you are responsible is controlled effectively.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SC2</td><td>Take reasonable steps to ensure the business complies with relevant regulatory requirements/standards.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SC3</td><td>Take reasonable steps to ensure delegation is to an appropriate person, with effective oversight.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>SC4</td><td>Disclose appropriately any information the FCA/PRA would reasonably expect notice of.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Detail is found in the FCA's <strong>SYSC</strong> sourcebook: SYSC 23 (introduction/classification), SYSC 24 (allocation of prescribed responsibilities), SYSC 25 (management responsibilities maps/handover), SYSC 26 (overall/local responsibility), SYSC 27 (certification regime).</p>\n<h4>3.5 The Fit and Proper Test (FIT)</h4>\n<p>Individuals performing SMFs must be approved as fit and proper by the regulator before taking up the role; the firm bears responsibility for satisfying the regulator. Three key criteria:</p>\n<h5>3.5.1 Honesty, Integrity and Reputation</h5>\n<p>Regard to whether the person has: been convicted of a criminal offence (esp. fraud/dishonesty/financial crime); been subject to an adverse civil finding/settlement; been subject to regulatory investigation/disciplinary proceedings; been the subject of a justified complaint; been refused/had revoked a trading licence; been involved in an insolvent business; or been disqualified as a director/dismissed from a position of trust. Each case assessed on its merits, considering seriousness and time elapsed.</p>\n<h5>3.5.2 Competence and Capability</h5>\n<p>Regard to whether the person satisfies the Training and Competence Sourcebook requirements, has demonstrated relevant experience/training, and has adequate time to perform the function.</p>\n<h5>3.5.3 Financial Soundness</h5>\n<p>Regard to whether the person is subject to an outstanding judgment/award, or has filed for bankruptcy/been adjudged bankrupt/had assets sequestrated/made arrangements with creditors. The FCA will not normally require a statement of assets/liabilities; limited financial means alone does not affect suitability.</p>\n"},{"heading":"4. Prudential Standards (Capital Adequacy)","html":"\n<h4>4.1 Capital Adequacy Requirements</h4>\n<p><strong>Principle 4</strong> (Financial prudence) requires firms to \"maintain adequate financial resources\" — capital and liquidity resources sufficient to meet liabilities as they fall due.</p>\n<p>Three FCA Handbook modules set the standards:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>GENPRU</strong> (General Prudential Sourcebook) — high-level standards/principles/capital requirements for firms subject to the UK Capital Requirements Directive (UK CRD).</li>\n<li><strong>MIFIDPRU</strong> (Prudential Sourcebook for UK MiFID Investment Firms) — effective <strong>1 January 2022</strong>; applies to UK MiFID firms that are not banks/credit institutions.</li>\n<li><strong>IPRU-INV</strong> (Interim Prudential Sourcebook for Investment Businesses) — capital rules for firms not subject to CRD/CRR or MIFIDPRU (eg, authorised professional firms, Lloyd's underwriting agents/members' advisers, fund managers incl. SIPP operators, exempt CAD/local firms, service companies, financial advisory firms under a MiFID exemption, credit unions offering junior ISAs).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>CRD/CRR</strong> apply to credit institutions (banks/deposit takers), are managed by the PRA, and use a <strong>3-Pillar</strong> framework:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pillar 1</strong> — minimum capital requirements for credit, market and operational risk.</li>\n<li><strong>Pillar 2</strong> — firm/supervisor judgement on whether additional capital is needed beyond Pillar 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Pillar 3</strong> — public disclosure of risk, capital and risk management information to improve market discipline.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A firm must notify its regulator <strong>immediately</strong> if it knows its capital resources have fallen below the prescribed minimum. CRD firms must also consider the loss-absorbency (quality) of capital, split into 3 <strong>tiers</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Core Tier One</strong> — eg, permanent share capital, reserves, externally verified interim profits.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Two</strong> — eg, long-term subordinated debt, revaluation reserves.</li>\n<li><strong>Tier Three</strong> — eg, short-term subordinated debt, interim trading book profit and loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Under IPRU-INV, all firms must comply with two overarching requirements: (1) hold the amount/type of financial resources required by the rules at all times; (2) notify the regulator immediately if in breach, or expecting to be in breach, of the financial resources rule.</p>\n<p>The <strong>Investment Firms Prudential Regime (IFPR)</strong> simplifies requirements for FCA solo-regulated MiFID investment firms, focusing on the potential harm a firm poses to consumers/markets and ensuring sufficient capital/liquid assets for an orderly wind-down. It follows the EU's Investment Firms Directive/Regulation (effective 2021 in the EU), with the UK regime effective <strong>2022</strong>, contained in <strong>MIFIDPRU</strong>. FCA-stated outcomes: business-model-aligned prudential regime; meaningful/consistent requirements; less time on complex capital calculations; understandable, accessible rules consolidated into a single sourcebook.</p>\n"},{"heading":"5. The UK Regulatory Infrastructure","html":"\n<h4>5.1 Competition and Markets Authority (CMA)</h4>\n<p>Not a financial services body, but works closely with the FCA. Promotes competition for consumers by: investigating mergers that could restrict competition; conducting market studies/investigations; investigating breaches of anti-competitive agreement/dominant position prohibitions; bringing criminal cartel-offence proceedings; enforcing consumer protection legislation; cooperating with sector regulators; advising on rights/obligations; and monitoring government subsidies.</p>\n<h4>5.2 HM Revenue &amp; Customs (HMRC)</h4>\n<p>The FCA, PRA and HMRC have agreed to share information and expertise on banks' behavioural responses to regulatory/tax changes, to identify and address issues more quickly and in a coordinated way.</p>\n<h4>5.3 Financial Policy Committee (FPC)</h4>\n<p>Since 2009 the Bank of England has had a statutory objective to \"contribute to protecting and enhancing the stability of the financial systems of the UK.\" From April 2013, the independent <strong>FPC</strong> was created at the BoE to identify, monitor and act on systemic risks. The FPC meets <strong>at least 4 times a year</strong> and publishes records of formal meetings, plus the BoE's <strong>biannual Financial Stability Report (FSR)</strong>.</p>\n<h4>5.4 The Bank of England (BoE)</h4>\n<p>Central bank functions split into two areas:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Monetary stability</strong> — stable prices/currency confidence; the <strong>Monetary Policy Committee (MPC)</strong> sets the base interest rate, meeting <strong>8 times a year</strong> (roughly every 6 weeks), to meet the Government's inflation target.</li>\n<li><strong>Financial stability</strong> — surveillance/market intelligence to detect threats; BoE may act as <strong>lender of last resort</strong> in exceptional circumstances. Works with HM Treasury and other central banks/international organisations.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.5 HM Treasury (HMT)</h4>\n<p>Government department responsible for public finance and economic policy; has overall responsibility for the UK financial services sector. The <strong>FCA reports directly to HMT</strong>. The <strong>Chancellor of the Exchequer appoints the FCA's CEO and chair</strong>.</p>\n<h4>5.6 Occupational Pension Schemes</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Pensions Regulator (TPR)</strong> protects workplace pensions and safeguards member rights by regulating employers, trustees, pension specialists and advisers. Schemes may be <strong>contributory</strong> (employee + employer contributions) or <strong>non-contributory</strong> (employer only); and <strong>defined contribution (DC)</strong> (no guaranteed final pot value) or <strong>defined benefit (DB)</strong> (guaranteed % of final salary, eg, final salary schemes).</p>\n<p>The <strong>Pensions Act 2008</strong> introduced <strong>automatic enrolment</strong>: employers must auto-enrol eligible employees (those <strong>aged 22 to State Pension age</strong>, earning above a set threshold) into a workplace pension and contribute. Any employer with at least one employee has this legal duty. TPR enforcement includes compliance notices, penalty notices and fines; late compliance requires backdating contributions to the date the employee first met the age/earnings criteria; unpaid debts can be recovered through the courts.</p>\n<h4>5.7 Impact of Overseas Legislation on UK Financial Services</h4>\n<p>The <strong>European Union (Withdrawal) Act 2018</strong> converted direct EU legislation into UK law and preserved UK laws implementing EU obligations (\"assimilated law\"), so the same rules applied post-Brexit as before, as far as possible. EU Guidelines/Guidance from ESMA and the EBA continue to apply until replaced by the FCA/PRA.</p>\n<p>UK firms lost automatic <strong>passporting</strong> rights into the EU (and vice versa); local authorisation (or local exemptions) is now needed.</p>\n<p><strong>UK MiFIR</strong> (onshored version of Regulation (EU) No 600/2014) has applied to all UK regulated firms since <strong>1 January 2021</strong>, alongside <strong>UK MiFID</strong> (implementing MiFID 2014/65/EU via FCA rules).</p>\n<p><strong>HMT's Wholesale Markets Review (2021)</strong> assessed how much retained EU regulation needed adapting for UK markets, guided by: upholding high regulatory standards; promoting openness/competitiveness; delivering fair/proportionate regulation; supporting economic growth.</p>\n<p><strong>Financial Services and Markets Act 2023</strong> — received Royal Assent <strong>June 2023</strong>; some provisions effective <strong>August 2023</strong>, remainder <strong>1 January 2024</strong>. Gives HMT, PRA and FCA powers to revoke retained EU law and replace it with UK-specific rules, and introduces the new secondary objective on international competitiveness/growth for both regulators.</p>\n<h5>5.7.1 Why Overseas Legislation Matters</h5>\n<p>Global firms face overlapping extraterritorial regimes. Example: <strong>Dodd-Frank</strong> and <strong>EMIR</strong> both have extraterritorial reach based on client identity/domicile — eg, a UK bank branch has a UK EMIR reporting obligation regardless of where a derivative trade is executed or the counterparty's domicile. Listing/disclosure obligations also depend on where an issuer lists.</p>\n<h5>5.7.2 International Intergovernmental Bodies</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>FATF</strong> (Financial Action Task Force) — global AML/counter-terrorist-financing standard-setter; has developed <strong>40 Recommendations</strong>; over <strong>200 countries</strong> have committed to implementing them; monitors compliance and holds non-compliant countries to account.</li>\n<li><strong>BIS</strong> (Bank for International Settlements) — established <strong>1930</strong>; \"bank for central banks\"; owned/governed by central banks; Board of Directors has <strong>18 members</strong>, including <strong>6 ex-officio Directors</strong> (Central Bank Governors of Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, the UK and the USA); <strong>63 central banks/monetary authorities</strong> are members; the <strong>Governor of the BoE</strong> represents the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>IOSCO</strong> (International Organisation of Securities Commissions) — global securities regulation standard-setter; forum for regulators from over <strong>130 jurisdictions</strong>; Board has <strong>34</strong> securities regulators; the <strong>CEO of the FCA</strong> represents the UK on the IOSCO Board.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.8 Oversight and Other Influencing Bodies</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Internal Audit</strong> — independent, informed view of firm risks based on full access to records/data; reports directly to the audit committee/board, helping oversee senior management.</li>\n<li><strong>External Audit</strong> — must have relevant industry knowledge/competence, be objective/independent, report directly to the supervisor (or indirectly via the firm if direct reporting isn't permitted), exercise professional scepticism, and identify/respond to risks of material misstatement.</li>\n<li><strong>Trustees</strong> — hold/manage assets on behalf of a beneficiary/organisation; can delegate investment management but remain ultimately responsible; may use an investment sub-committee to advise the trustee board.</li>\n<li><strong>Compliance</strong> — starts at the top; independent, sufficiently resourced, and subject to periodic review by internal audit. The <strong>BIS</strong> issued 10 principles for banks (applicable, proportionately, to all firms). ESMA guidelines on MiFID II compliance functions continue to apply post-Brexit for Level 3 measures existing pre-Brexit, but subsequent ESMA changes do not automatically apply in the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>Industry Bodies</strong> — generalist (not product-specific), champion the sector's success (eg, <strong>TheCityUK</strong>, promoting the UK as a global financial centre). Members subject to a code of conduct (eg, the <strong>FX Global Code</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>Professional Bodies</strong> — voluntary membership (except accountancy/actuarial sectors, which have statutory powers); members bound by codes of conduct that take precedence over an employer's internal code (potential ethical dilemma). Responsible for professional exams (eg, <strong>CISI</strong>, Chartered Banker Institute). CISI's objective: set standards of professional excellence/integrity for securities, investment, wealth and financial planning professionals.</li>\n<li><strong>Trade Associations</strong> — represent members in a specific sector; advocate for proportionate rules with legislators/regulators. <strong>Product-specific</strong>: ISLA (securities lending), ICMA (bonds/repos), ISDA (OTC derivatives), FIA (exchange-traded derivatives). <strong>Generalist</strong>: AFME, UK Finance, ABI, Association of Foreign Banks.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"heading":"6. Regulated and Prohibited Activities","html":"\n<h4>6.1 The General Prohibition</h4>\n<p>FSMA Part II sets out the <strong>general prohibition</strong>: no person may carry on (or purport to carry on) a regulated activity in the UK unless authorised or exempt. Contravention is a <strong>criminal offence</strong> — max <strong>2 years' imprisonment and an unlimited fine</strong>. Agreements made in contravention are <strong>unenforceable</strong> by the offender; the other party can recover money/property transferred and claim compensation. <strong>Defence</strong>: the person took all reasonable precautions and exercised all due diligence to avoid committing the offence.</p>\n<p><strong>Example:</strong> X buys shares via UNA Ltd, an unauthorised broker. X can either keep the shares and do nothing, or sue UNA to recover money/damages (returning the shares) for breach of the general prohibition. UNA's staff also face criminal liability (up to 2 years + unlimited fine).</p>\n<p><strong>Key exemption provisions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Section 39 FSMA + FSMA (Appointed Representatives) Regulations 2001 — appointed representatives (ARs).</li>\n<li>Section 285 FSMA — recognised investment exchanges/clearing houses.</li>\n<li>Section 312A FSMA — MTF operators exercising certain rights.</li>\n<li>Section 316 FSMA — members of the Society of Lloyd's.</li>\n<li>FSMA Exemption Order 2001 — central banks, supranational bodies, local authorities, charities, industrial/provident societies, certain government-overseen bodies.</li>\n<li>Part 20 FSMA — special regime for professional firms.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>6.2 Prohibition Orders</h4>\n<p><strong>Section 56 FSMA</strong> allows the FCA/PRA to make a <strong>prohibition order</strong> against an individual not considered fit and proper, barring them from a specified function (or functions generally). Breach = criminal offence, liable to a fine (defence: reasonable care/due diligence). An authorised firm must take reasonable care that individuals it is responsible for do not breach prohibition orders; orders can be varied/revoked on application. Prohibition orders can also apply to exempt persons/professional firms under the Part 20 regime.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 59 FSMA</strong> — a firm must take reasonable care that no one performs a role requiring regulatory approval (SMF/controlled function) without confirmed approval.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 71 FSMA</strong> — a private person can sue a firm for damages for loss suffered from breach of statutory duty where an individual acted in breach of a s56 prohibition order or performed a controlled function without approval (s59); non-private persons (businesses, trusts) can also sue in prescribed circumstances.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 138D, Financial Services Act 2012</strong> — a person suffering loss from an authorised person's breach of an FCA rule (unless stated otherwise) has a right to claim damages; claimant need only show the rule breach and resulting loss.</p>\n<h4>6.3 Regulated Activities (RAO)</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (Regulated Activities) Order 2001 (\"RAO\")</strong> defines regulated activities by reference to (a) specified investments and (b) specified activities carried on in relation to them. An activity is only \"regulated\" if BOTH a specified activity AND a specified investment are involved.</p>\n<h5>6.3.1 Specified Investments (selected, with key thresholds)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Deposits</strong> — repayable money (excludes e-money, advance payment for goods/services, security deposits).</li>\n<li><strong>Electronic money</strong> — stored monetary value, electronic substitute for cash.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under contracts of insurance</strong> — long-term and general insurance.</li>\n<li><strong>Shares</strong> — excludes OEIC shares (captured as CIS units) and some building society shares (captured as deposits).</li>\n<li><strong>Debentures</strong> — incl. debenture stock, loan stock; excludes trade bills/cheques and insurance/government securities.</li>\n<li><strong>Government and public securities</strong> — eg, gilts, US treasuries; excludes NS&amp;I products.</li>\n<li><strong>Warrants</strong> — entitlement to subscribe for shares/debentures/gilts.</li>\n<li><strong>Certificates representing securities</strong> — eg, ADRs, GDRs.</li>\n<li><strong>Units in a collective investment scheme (CIS)</strong> — authorised or unregulated; incl. AUT units, OEIC/ICVC shares.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a stakeholder pension scheme</strong> (Welfare Reform and Pensions Act 1999).</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a personal pension scheme</strong> — incl. SIPPs.</li>\n<li><strong>Options</strong> — on securities/contractually based investments, currencies, certain precious metals (gold/silver), or futures/CFDs.</li>\n<li><strong>Futures</strong> — excludes commercial (non-investment) purpose contracts.</li>\n<li><strong>Contracts for differences (CFDs)</strong> — eg, spread bets, interest rate swaps.</li>\n<li><strong>Lloyd's syndicate capacity and membership.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a funeral plan contract.</strong></li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a regulated mortgage contract*</strong> — secured by first legal charge, at least <strong>40%</strong> used as the borrower's (or related party's) dwelling; incl. lifetime mortgages.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a home reversion plan*</strong> — sell part/all of home for lump sum/payments, retain right to occupy.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a home purchase plan*</strong> — Sharia-compliant mortgage alternative.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights under a regulated sale and rent-back agreement*</strong> — seller remains in occupation of at least <strong>40%</strong> of the property.</li>\n<li><strong>Credit agreements</strong> (4 sub-types), consumer hire agreements, greenhouse gas emission allowances, alternative finance investment bonds (sukuk), rights/interests in other specified investments, emission allowances.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>* Investments marked with an asterisk are collectively \"home finance\" transactions.</p>\n<h5>6.3.2 Specified Activities (selected)</h5>\n<ul>\n<li>Accepting deposits; issuing e-money; effecting/carrying out contracts of insurance as principal; insurance risk transformation.</li>\n<li><strong>Dealing in investments as principal or agent</strong> — as principal: securities and contractually-based investments; as agent: securities, contractually-based investments, plus pure protection/general insurance rights.</li>\n<li>Arranging home finance transactions; operating an MTF; operating an OTF (MiFID II — equity-like instruments not permitted on OTFs); bidding in emission auctions; credit broking; operating electronic lending systems.</li>\n<li><strong>Managing investments</strong> — discretionary management of another's securities/contractually-based investments (non-discretionary management is instead \"dealing\"/\"advising\").</li>\n<li>Assisting in administration/performance of an insurance contract; debt adjusting, counselling, collecting, administration.</li>\n<li><strong>Safeguarding and administering investments</strong> — must do BOTH to be caught (eg, a custodian bank); sub-divided into with/without arranging.</li>\n<li><strong>Advising on investments</strong> — covers securities/relevant investments; excludes advice on deposits and generic advice/information (not tailored recommendations); sub-divided into (a) advising on investments (except pension transfers/opt-outs) and (b) advising on pension transfers/opt-outs.</li>\n<li>Sending/allowing dematerialised instructions (eg, CREST); managing/acting as trustee or depositary of a UCITS or AIF; establishing/operating/winding up a CIS or a pension scheme; basic advice on stakeholder products; Lloyd's market activities; entering a funeral plan contract as provider; advising on home finance transactions; agreeing to carry on specified activity; dormant account fund operation; specified benchmarks.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Advising, dealing, arranging and administration activity connected with insurance contracts is collectively \"insurance mediation activity.\"</p>\n<h4>6.4 Exemptions</h4>\n<h5>6.4.1 Appointed Representatives (ARs)</h5>\n<p>An AR contracts with an authorised <strong>principal</strong>, who accepts legal responsibility for the AR's regulated activity, making the AR an exempt person. FCA/PRA rules do not apply directly to the AR (the principal must hold the relevant permissions). Governors and customer-function individuals within the AR must be approved. Permitted AR activities include: dealing in investments as agent (pure protection/general insurance only); arranging deals/home finance transactions; assisting with insurance contract admin; arranging safeguarding/administration of assets; advising on investments/home finance/stakeholder products; agreeing to carry on a regulated activity; operating electronic lending platforms; certain consumer credit activities. <strong>ARs cannot deal as principal or manage investments.</strong></p>\n<h5>6.4.2 Recognised Investment Exchanges (RIEs) and Recognised Clearing Houses (RCHs)</h5>\n<p>FSMA gives the FCA responsibility for recognising, regulating and supervising exchanges (eg, the <strong>London Stock Exchange</strong>) and clearing houses. Once recognised, they are exempt persons — not requiring separate authorisation for exchange/clearing business.</p>\n<h5>6.4.3 FSMA Exemption Order 2001</h5>\n<p>Exempts the <strong>BoE, ECB and EEA central banks</strong> from authorisation for any regulated activity <strong>except effecting/carrying out contracts of insurance</strong>. Also exempts supranational bodies (eg, EIB, IMF). Municipal banks, local authorities and charities are exempt <strong>only for the regulated activity of accepting deposits</strong>.</p>\n<h5>6.4.4 Members of Lloyd's</h5>\n<p>The general prohibition is disapplied for Lloyd's members in relation to contracts of insurance written at Lloyd's (extended to former members who ceased underwriting after 1996), because Lloyd's activity is supervised by the Society of Lloyd's.</p>\n<h5>6.4.5 Members of the Professions</h5>\n<p><strong>Part 20 FSMA</strong> covers <strong>5 professions</strong>: accountants, solicitors, actuaries, chartered surveyors, licensed conveyancers — regulated via <strong>Designated Professional Bodies (DPBs)</strong> (eg, ICAEW, the Law Society, Institute of Actuaries, RICS) rather than direct FCA/PRA authorisation. Regulated activity must be incidental to the professional service, and firms must return to the client any commissions received (only pecuniary reward is from the client). Excluded activities include: accepting deposits; dealing as principal (market making); lending/administering home finance transactions (unless trustee/personal representative); establishing/operating/winding up a CIS or pension scheme; managing Lloyd's underwriting capacity/advising on syndicate participation; effecting/carrying out insurance contracts; providing funeral plan contracts.</p>\n<h4>6.5 Exclusions from Authorisation</h4>\n<h5>6.5.1 Exclusions from Dealing as Principal</h5>\n<p><strong>Absence of holding out</strong> — dealing as principal is only \"regulated\" for those holding themselves out as market makers, regularly soliciting the public. Other exclusions: bank lending/taking a debt instrument; a company issuing/buying back its own shares (Companies Act 1985 treasury shares); using options/futures/CFDs purely for risk management (mainly unregulated business); acting as bare trustee/nominee; sale of goods/services; group/joint enterprise; sale of a body corporate; employee share scheme; overseas person; incoming electronic commerce provider.</p>\n<h5>6.5.2 Group and Joint Enterprise Exclusions</h5>\n<p>Covers: dealing as principal; dealing as agent; arranging deals; managing investments; safeguarding/administering investments; sending dematerialised instructions; advising on investments.</p>\n<h5>6.5.3 Advice in Newspapers</h5>\n<p>No authorisation needed for advice in a newspaper/journal/periodical or regularly updated news service, provided the publication's principal purpose is news, not investment advice (subscription \"tipping\" periodicals DO require authorisation).</p>\n<h5>6.5.4 Trustees, Nominees and Personal Representatives</h5>\n<p>Excluded if the person is acting as a representative, not generally holding out as carrying on regulated activities, and not receiving additional remuneration. Covers dealing as principal, arranging, managing/safeguarding investments, sending dematerialised instructions, advising, and home finance activities. <strong>Not available</strong> for activity connected with a contract of insurance.</p>\n<h5>6.5.5 Employee Share Schemes</h5>\n<p>Excludes 4 activities: dealing as principal; dealing as agent; arranging deals/making arrangements; safeguarding and administering investments.</p>\n<h5>6.5.6 Overseas Persons</h5>\n<p>Excluded where there is no permanent UK place of business and business is done via a UK authorised/exempt person, or follows an unsolicited approach from a UK client. Covers dealing (principal/agent), arranging, advising, agreeing to carry on certain activities, operating MTF/OTF, and home finance activity/administration.</p>\n<h4>6.6 Authorisation</h4>\n<p>Persons carrying on regulated activity \"by way of business\" (broadly, commercially) must be authorised or exempt. Factors relevant to \"by way of business\": degree of continuity, commercial element, scale, and proportion relative to other (unregulated) activities.</p>\n<h5>6.6.1 The Permissions Regime</h5>\n<p><strong>Part 4A permission</strong> specifies precisely which regulated activities a firm may carry out, the relevant investments, and any special conditions (eg, a restriction preventing a firm from holding client money).</p>\n<h5>6.6.2 The Authorisation Process</h5>\n<p>Dual-regulated firms apply to the <strong>PRA as lead regulator</strong>, but the PRA can only authorise once the <strong>FCA has also consented</strong>. Application pack covers: core details (legal form, ownership, systems); controllers; business supplement/regulatory business plan (incl. capital adequacy, PI insurance, compliance procedures, governance); disclosure of significant events; approved persons forms (SMF/controlled function); checklist and declaration, incl. the application fee (FCA uses <strong>10 pricing categories</strong> reflecting complexity — <strong>non-refundable</strong> under any circumstances). <strong>Statutory deadlines: 6 months for complete applications; up to 12 months for incomplete applications.</strong> If a previously unincorporated firm incorporates (with no material change to activities), a simplified \"change of legal status\" pack with reduced fee may be used.</p>\n<h5>6.6.3 The Threshold Conditions (TCs)</h5>\n<p>Minimum standards for becoming/remaining authorised. The <strong>FSMA (Threshold Conditions) Order 2013</strong> redistributed TC responsibility between FCA and PRA; the latest TCs were issued in <strong>August 2023</strong>. For dual-regulated firms, the FCA assesses conduct issues and the PRA assesses prudential issues; for solo-regulated firms, the FCA assesses both.</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Legal status</strong> — restrictions apply, eg, individuals/partnerships cannot hold banking deposits or conduct insurance business.</li>\n<li><strong>Location of offices</strong> — UK-incorporated companies need head/registered office in the UK; non-body-corporate firms with UK head office must carry on business in the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>Business to be conducted in a prudent manner</strong> — appropriate (not just \"adequate\") financial and non-financial resources.</li>\n<li><strong>Effective supervision</strong> — incl. the former \"close links\" condition (eg, group structure impact); no impediment from activity complexity, products offered, or business organisation.</li>\n<li><strong>Appropriate resources</strong> — considers quality as well as quantity; nature/scale of business; continuity risk; non-financial resources (systems, controls, plans, policies, information, human resources).</li>\n<li><strong>Suitability</strong> — fit and proper in light of proposed activities; conducted soundly/prudently; minimising financial crime risk; cooperative with the regulator; management acting with probity and adequate skills/experience.</li>\n<li><strong>Business model</strong> — strategy suitable for the regulated activities, sound/prudent, in the interests of consumers and market integrity.</li>\n<li><strong>Appointment of claims representatives</strong> — motor insurers only.</li>\n</ol>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Condition</th><th>FCA-only firm</th><th>Dual-regulated: FCA</th><th>Dual-regulated: PRA</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Legal status</td><td>N/A</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Location of offices</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Prudent conduct</td><td>N/A</td><td>No</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Effective supervision</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Appropriate resources</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Suitability</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Business model</td><td>Yes</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr>\n</table>\n"},{"heading":"7. Training and Competence","html":"\n<h4>7.1 Overall Requirements</h4>\n<p><strong>Principle 3</strong> (management and control) requires firms to take reasonable care to organise/control affairs responsibly and effectively — including ensuring employees achieve and maintain competence for regulated activity. The FCA Handbook/PRA Rule Book <strong>SYSC</strong> chapters contain a high-level competence requirement applying to ALL UK-authorised firms (incl. wholesale). This is supplemented, for firms dealing with retail customers (and in limited cases other customers), by the <strong>Training and Competence (T&amp;C) Sourcebook</strong>, which focuses on outcomes.</p>\n<h4>7.2 Requirements for Retail Firms: Assessing and Maintaining Competence</h4>\n<p><strong>Assessment</strong> — firms must not deem an employee competent for specified activities until they have demonstrated competence and (where required) passed each module of an appropriate exam; the assessment need <strong>not</strong> occur before the employee starts the activity.</p>\n<p><strong>Supervision</strong> — employees must be appropriately supervised at all times; supervision should be significantly more intensive <strong>before</strong> an employee is assessed as competent than after. Firms must have clear criteria/procedures for when reduced supervision becomes appropriate, considering relevant experience.</p>\n<p><strong>Supervisors</strong> — must have the necessary coaching/assessment skills and technical knowledge; firms should consider requiring supervisors to pass an appropriate exam if their supervisees have not yet been assessed as competent. <strong>Where the employee advises on packaged products to retail customers, the supervisor MUST have passed an appropriate examination.</strong></p>\n<h4>7.3 Qualification Requirements Before Starting Activities</h4>\n<p>Firms must not allow employees to undertake (other than an overseeing activity) a specified activity requiring a qualification without first passing the relevant regulatory module. Activities requiring a qualification before starting: certain advising/dealing activities; acting as a broker fund adviser; advising on Lloyd's syndicate participation; acting as a pension transfer specialist.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemption conditions</strong> (for advising on packaged products, broker fund advising, Lloyd's syndicate advice, pension transfer specialism) — the employee must have: at least <strong>3 years'</strong> up-to-date relevant experience gained outside the UK; not previously been required to fully comply with the exam requirements; and passed the relevant exam module. (Last two conditions do not apply where the <strong>30-day rule</strong> exemption is used, unless the person is advising retail clients on packaged products/broker funds.)</p>\n<p><strong>30-day rule</strong> — the \"customer function\" does not extend to an individual based overseas who, in a 12-month period, spends no more than <strong>30 days</strong> in the UK, provided they are appropriately supervised by an approved person.</p>\n<p>Firms select an appropriate qualification from the FCA's list (<strong>TC App 4</strong> — \"appropriate qualification tables\"). Firms must assess training needs at the outset and at regular intervals (incl. on role change), and review training quality/effectiveness.</p>\n<h4>7.3.1 Maintaining Competence</h4>\n<p>Firms must regularly review competence, considering: technical knowledge and its application; skills/expertise; and changes in the market, products, legislation and regulation. <strong>Continuing Professional Development (CPD)</strong> — conferences, webinars, seminars, reading regulator/industry publications — helps maintain competence.</p>\n<p><strong>Activities within T&amp;C scope</strong>: designated investment business for retail clients (advising, dealing, managing, day-to-day overseeing); regulated mortgage/reversion activity (advising, designing scripted questions for non-advised sales, overseeing non-advised sales); non-investment insurance business (advising).</p>\n<h4>7.4 Statements of Professional Standing (SPS)</h4>\n<p>Under the Adviser Charging rules, retail investment advisers must hold a valid <strong>SPS</strong> from an FCA-accredited body (eg, the CISI). The SPS confirms the adviser has:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Completed a <strong>QCF Level 4</strong> qualification on the FCA's approved list.</li>\n<li>Completed an annual programme of <strong>35 hours of CPD</strong>, with at least <strong>21 hours structured learning</strong>.</li>\n<li>Acted in accordance with APER and/or SM&amp;CR requirements.</li>\n<li>Agreed to adhere to a code of ethics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The SPS is <strong>valid for 12 months</strong> and contains: the adviser's name; the accredited body's name/contact details and a named signatory; the verification end date (max 12 months from verification); confirmation the qualification(s) are verified; confirmation of the adviser's annual declaration (knowledge kept up to date; adherence to ethical standards); the adviser's FCA Register reference number; and a recommendation to check the FCA Register.</p>\n<p>Qualification requirements (per <strong>TC App 4</strong> / Appendix 1.1.1R of the T&amp;C Sourcebook) apply to individuals: advising on (not dealing in) securities; advising on (not dealing in) derivatives; advising on packaged products; advising on friendly society tax-exempt policies; advising on AND dealing in securities; advising on AND dealing in derivatives.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"Under the 'twin peaks' model of UK financial regulation, which statement correctly distinguishes the roles of the FCA and the PRA?","options":["The PRA regulates conduct for all firms, while the FCA regulates prudential standards for banks and insurers","The PRA is responsible for prudential regulation of banks, building societies, credit unions, insurers and major investment firms, while the FCA regulates conduct for all firms and is also the prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms","The FCA and PRA have identical objectives and jointly authorise every regulated firm","The PRA regulates only insurers, while the FCA regulates only banks"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA's remit is prudential regulation of deposit-takers, insurers and major investment firms (part of the Bank of England). The FCA regulates conduct for all authorised firms and is the sole prudential regulator for solo-regulated firms such as asset managers and stockbrokers."},{"question":"To which body is the FCA directly accountable for how it carries out its functions?","options":["The Bank of England","HM Treasury","The Financial Policy Committee","The Pensions Regulator"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA is accountable to the UK Government via HM Treasury. The PRA, by contrast, is not directly accountable to any government body but is answerable to Parliament (Treasury Committee) via the Bank of England."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the FCA's statutory objectives under the Financial Services Act 2012?","options":["The strategic objective of ensuring relevant markets function well","The consumer protection objective","The prudential soundness objective for PRA-authorised persons","The competition objective"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons is the PRA's single general objective, not an FCA objective. The FCA has one strategic objective (markets function well) and three operational objectives: consumer protection, integrity and competition."},{"question":"The PRA and FCA secondary objective introduced by the Financial Services and Markets Act 2023 requires the regulators to:","options":["Prioritise consumer protection above all other objectives","Facilitate the international competitiveness of the UK economy and its medium to long-term growth, subject to international standards","Merge into a single regulator by 2025","Adopt identical rulebooks to the EU"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FSMA 2023 introduced a shared secondary objective to facilitate international competitiveness and growth, subject to international standards, which only applies when the regulators are advancing their existing operational objectives."},{"question":"A firm carries on a regulated activity in the UK without authorisation or an applicable exemption. Under Section 19 FSMA (the general prohibition), what is the maximum criminal penalty?","options":["6 months' imprisonment and a fine of £5,000","2 years' imprisonment and an unlimited fine","5 years' imprisonment only, with no fine","There is no criminal penalty, only a civil claim for damages"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Contravention of the general prohibition (s19 FSMA) is a criminal offence carrying a maximum sentence of 2 years' imprisonment and an unlimited fine. It is a defence to show all reasonable precautions and due diligence were taken."},{"question":"When Principle 12 (Consumer Duty) applies to a firm's retail business, what happens to Principles 6 and 7?","options":["They continue to apply in addition to Principle 12","They cease to apply in respect of the products/services covered by the Consumer Duty","They are replaced permanently for all of the firm's business, including wholesale business","They are suspended for 12 months only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where Principle 12 applies, Principles 6 (customers' interests) and 7 (communications with clients) no longer apply to the firm in respect of the retail products/services in scope of the Consumer Duty."},{"question":"From what date did the Consumer Duty rules take effect for closed products and services (products only available to existing customers, being wound down)?","options":["31 July 2023","1 January 2024","31 July 2024","1 January 2025"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Consumer Duty rules applied from 2023 for new and existing (open) products/services, and from 31 July 2024 for closed products and services."},{"question":"Under the deposit-taking (BCOBS) scope of the Consumer Duty, which entities are treated as within scope alongside consumers?","options":["Micro-enterprises and charities with a turnover of less than £1 million, and trustees acting outside their trade/business/profession","All corporate customers regardless of size","Only individuals classified as vulnerable consumers","Professional clients who have opted in writing to retail treatment"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"For deposit-taking activities, the Consumer Duty scope extends to consumers, micro-enterprises, charities with turnover under £1 million, and natural persons acting as trustees if acting for purposes outside their trade, business or profession."},{"question":"Which of the following is one of the Consumer Duty's three cross-cutting rules?","options":["Maximise short-term product sales","Act in good faith towards retail customers","Guarantee a minimum investment return","Refer all complaints to the Financial Ombudsman Service within 24 hours"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The three cross-cutting rules require firms to: act in good faith towards retail customers; avoid foreseeable harm to retail customers; and enable and support retail customers to pursue their financial objectives."},{"question":"The Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) initiative applies to which firms?","options":["Only firms that are dual-regulated by the FCA and PRA","Firms out of scope of the Consumer Duty, either because they do not materially impact retail customer outcomes or their products/services are exempt","Only wholesale firms dealing exclusively with professional clients","All FCA-regulated firms without exception, regardless of Consumer Duty status"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FTOC applies to firms that are out of scope of the Consumer Duty requirements — because they don't deal directly with (or materially impact) retail customers, or their products/services fall within a Consumer Duty exemption."},{"question":"How many PRA Fundamental Rules are there?","options":["5","6","8","12"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There are 8 PRA Fundamental Rules, covering integrity, skill/care/diligence, prudent conduct, adequate financial resources, risk management, organisation/control, open dealings with regulators, and resolution planning."},{"question":"Which two categories of firm are specifically excluded from the Senior Managers & Certification Regime (SM&CR)?","options":["Sole traders and partnerships","Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators","Banks and building societies","Investment firms subject to MIFIDPRU"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The SM&CR applies to all FSMA-authorised firms except Appointed Representatives and Benchmark Administrators."},{"question":"Before submitting Form A for a candidate to a Senior Management Function, a firm must take all reasonable steps to obtain a regulatory reference covering the candidate's employment over what period?","options":["The past 2 years","The past 3 years","The past 5 years","The past 6 years"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Under SM&CR, firms must take all reasonable steps to obtain regulatory references from the candidate's current employer and any previous employers within the past 6 years, prior to submitting Form A."},{"question":"FSMA imposes a statutory obligation on the FCA and PRA to reach a determination on a person's fitness and propriety within how many days of receiving Form A?","options":["30 days","60 days","90 days","180 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FSMA requires the regulators to determine an SMF application within 90 days of receiving Form A, although the statutory 'clock' stops if the regulator requests further information from the firm."},{"question":"Under the Certification Regime, the PRA limits its scope to which category of individual?","options":["All customer-facing staff regardless of seniority","'Material Risk Takers' (Code staff) as defined in the Capital Requirements Regulation","Only individuals holding a Senior Management Function","Ancillary staff such as cleaners and catering staff"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA's Certification Regime scope is limited to 'material risk takers' (MRTs)/'Code staff' as defined in the CRR, who are subject to the Remuneration Code. The FCA's scope is broader, covering various significant-harm functions."},{"question":"A firm is in scope of the Consumer Duty. Which individual Conduct Rule now applies to its staff in place of Conduct Rule 4?","options":["Conduct Rule 1 (Integrity)","Conduct Rule 3 (Open and cooperative with regulators)","Conduct Rule 6 (Act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers)","SC2 (Comply with regulatory requirements)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where the Consumer Duty applies, Conduct Rule 6 ('You must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers') replaces Rule 4 for that firm's staff. Rule 4 continues to apply only at firms out of scope of the Duty — the two rules are mutually exclusive."},{"question":"How many additional Conduct Rules (the 'SC' rules) apply specifically to Senior Managers, on top of the individual Conduct Rules?","options":["2","3","4","5"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There are 4 Senior Manager Conduct Rules (SC1–SC4), covering effective control of the business, compliance with regulatory requirements, appropriate delegation/oversight, and appropriate disclosure to regulators."},{"question":"When assessing an individual's fitness and propriety under the FIT test, which three factors are the most important criteria considered?","options":["Educational background, nationality and length of service","Honesty/integrity/reputation, competence/capability, and financial soundness","Age, physical health and marital status","Salary level, seniority and number of direct reports"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FIT test considers a person's honesty, integrity and reputation; competence and capability; and financial soundness. Limited financial means alone does not, in itself, affect suitability."},{"question":"The Prudential Sourcebook for UK MiFID Investment Firms (MIFIDPRU) came into effect on which date?","options":["1 January 2021","1 January 2022","31 July 2023","1 January 2024"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MIFIDPRU, containing the UK's Investment Firms Prudential Regime (IFPR), came into effect on 1 January 2022, applying to UK MiFID investment firms that are not banks/credit institutions."},{"question":"How frequently does the Bank of England's Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) meet to set the base interest rate?","options":["Once a year","4 times a year","8 times a year (roughly every 6 weeks)","Every month"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The MPC meets 8 times a year, roughly every six weeks, to set the Bank of England's base interest rate in pursuit of the Government's inflation target."},{"question":"Under the Pensions Act 2008, automatic enrolment obligations apply to employees who are:","options":["Aged 18 to 65, regardless of earnings","Aged 22 to State Pension age, earning above a certain threshold","Aged 16 to 21 only","Any age, provided they have opted in writing"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Pensions Act 2008 requires employers to auto-enrol eligible employees — those aged 22 to State Pension age, earning above a set threshold — into a workplace pension scheme."},{"question":"How many Recommendations has the Financial Action Task Force (FATF) developed to ensure a coordinated global response to money laundering, terrorist financing and organised crime?","options":["10","25","40","50"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FATF has developed 40 Recommendations, which over 200 countries have committed to implementing, covering AML, counter-terrorist financing and related organised crime/corruption issues."},{"question":"For rights under a regulated mortgage contract to be a 'specified investment' under the RAO, what minimum proportion of the property must be used as the borrower's (or a related party's) dwelling?","options":["25%","40%","50%","75%"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A regulated mortgage contract requires that at least 40% of the mortgaged property is used as the borrower's, or a related party's, dwelling. The same 40% occupation threshold applies to regulated sale and rent-back agreements."},{"question":"An Appointed Representative (AR) is exempt from the general prohibition under Section 39 FSMA. Which of the following activities is the AR NOT permitted to carry out on an exempt basis?","options":["Advising on investments","Arranging deals in investments","Dealing in investments as principal","Assisting in the administration and performance of a contract of insurance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Appointed Representatives are specifically prohibited from dealing in investments as principal, or managing investments, under the AR exemption regime — these activities fall outside the scope of permitted AR activity."},{"question":"Under the FSMA Exemption Order 2001, the Bank of England, the ECB and EEA central banks are exempt from authorisation for any regulated activity EXCEPT:","options":["Accepting deposits","Effecting or carrying out contracts of insurance","Dealing in investments as principal","Advising on investments"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FSMA Exemption Order 2001 exempts central banks (BoE, ECB, EEA central banks) from authorisation for any regulated activity, apart from effecting or carrying out contracts of insurance."},{"question":"A firm submits a complete application for authorisation to the FCA. Within what statutory maximum period must the FCA determine the application?","options":["3 months","6 months","9 months","12 months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The regulators have statutory deadlines to determine authorisation applications: 6 months for complete applications, and up to 12 months for incomplete applications."},{"question":"Under the Threshold Conditions applicability table, which of the following conditions applies to a dual-regulated firm's PRA assessment but NOT its FCA assessment?","options":["Suitability","Effective supervision","Legal status","Appropriate resources"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"For dual-regulated firms, 'Legal status', 'Location of offices' and 'Prudent conduct' are assessed by the PRA, not the FCA. 'Appropriate resources' and 'Business model' are, conversely, FCA (not PRA) conditions for dual-regulated firms. 'Suitability' and 'Effective supervision' apply to both."},{"question":"Under the Training and Competence Sourcebook, when must an employee advising retail clients on packaged products have a supervisor who has passed an appropriate examination?","options":["Only if the supervisor also advises clients directly","The supervisor must always have passed an appropriate examination in this scenario","Only if the employee has fewer than 3 years' experience","Never — supervisors are exempt from examination requirements"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where an employee is advising on packaged products to retail customers, the firm must ensure the supervisor has passed an appropriate examination — this is a mandatory requirement, not merely something to 'consider'."},{"question":"Under the T&C Sourcebook's '30-day rule', an overseas-based individual performing a customer function is excluded from the qualification requirement provided they spend no more than how many days in the UK in a 12-month period (and are appropriately supervised)?","options":["10 days","20 days","30 days","90 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The customer function does not extend to an overseas-based individual who spends no more than 30 days in the UK in a 12-month period, provided they are appropriately supervised by an approved person."},{"question":"A Statement of Professional Standing (SPS) for a retail investment adviser is valid for a maximum of how long, and how many hours of CPD must be completed annually?","options":["6 months validity; 20 hours CPD, 10 structured","12 months validity; 35 hours CPD, at least 21 structured","24 months validity; 50 hours CPD, all structured","12 months validity; 15 hours CPD, no structured minimum"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The SPS is valid for a maximum of 12 months. Advisers must complete a minimum of 35 hours of relevant CPD each year, of which at least 21 hours must be structured learning."},{"question":"Under the individual accountability regime, which of the following is a conduct rule applying to certified persons?","options":["Act with due skill, care and diligence","Combat financial crime","Deliver the best possible result to clients","Communicate information in a way which is clear, fair and not misleading"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is Conduct Rule 2."},{"question":"An authorised firm must ensure that only certificated persons perform certification functions. Which of the following is most likely to be an example of a certification function?","options":["External auditor","Compliance oversight function","Money laundering reporting officer","Retail customer adviser"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Retail customer advisers are certification functions. Compliance oversight and MLRO are senior manager functions."},{"question":"According to the Regulated Activities Order 2001, which of the following are specified investments?","options":["Commercial property, including buy-to-let investments","Gold bullion","Insurance covering vehicle breakdown","Units in a collective investment scheme"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Tangible assets and car breakdown insurance are not specified investments."},{"question":"Which of the following are specified investments, as defined by the Regulated Activities Order?\nIOptions on the FTSE 100 index\nIICurrencies\nIIIOptions on gold\nIVGold","options":["III only","I and III","I, II and III","I, III and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Neither tangible assets (including precious metals such as gold or silver), nor currencies are specified investments within the meaning of the RAO and FSMA 2000. Options are classified as specified investments under FSMA 2000 if the underlying asset of the option is itself a specified investment (such as an option on a future), or is a precious metal (i.e. gold, silver, platinum or palladium)."},{"question":"Under the Individual Accountability Regime a firm must take reasonable care to ensure that individuals undertaking significant harm functions are:","options":["Appointed representatives","Approved persons","Certified persons","Authorised persons"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Individuals undertaking such functions are known as certified persons."},{"question":"On which of the following trading venues is an exchange trade reported automatically and published immediately?","options":["CREST","SEAQ","SETSqx","SETS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Reporting and publication are automatic and immediate respectively on SETS."},{"question":"When providing services to a professional client, for which of the following investments would a firm not need to gain Part 4A Permission?","options":["Options on a copper future","Gold","Regulated mortgages","Gilts"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"All are specified investments except gold."},{"question":"Which of the following is a regulated activity?","options":["Dealing in currencies","Administering the estate of a deceased person","Establishing a stakeholder pension","Advising on secondary mortgages"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Currencies are not a specified investment, personal representatives and executors of a will are excluded activities, and advising on secondary mortgages are not regulated mortgage products."},{"question":"Which of the following activities would be 'out of scope' under the FCA's Consumer Duty initiative?","options":["Giving advice on listed shares to a retail customer","A fund management firm managing a regulated collective investment scheme for a range of customers","Providing group life assurance products to employees of a company","A retail bank providing deposit-taking activities to its customers"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Providing group life assurance products is specifically excluded under the Consumer Duty, whether to employees of a company or to anybody else. The other three activities are all, either directly or indirectly, provided to retail customers."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a specified investment under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Gilt repo","Funeral plan","Residential property","Spread bet"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Property is not specified under FSMA 2000."},{"question":"Which of the following does the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 make responsible for ensuring a firm complies with the appropriate regulations?","options":["FCA","The firm itself","The Treasury","The RDC"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"It is the responsibility of the firm to ensure it complies with the appropriate regulations."},{"question":"Which of the following may cause an individual not to be 'fit and proper'?","options":["Undischarged bankruptcy","Conviction for tax evasion","Refusal of entry into a trade association associated within financial services","All may cause an individual to fail the test"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All would be considered in assessing if an individual is 'fit and proper'."},{"question":"According to the Capital Requirements Directive, what should a firm do if its financial resources fall below the financial resources requirement set by the PRA?","options":["Borrow money from a bank to increase its financial resources","Change its financial resource requirement","Inform the PRA immediately","Cease trading immediately"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm must notify the PRA immediately if it has breached or believes it may shortly breach the financial resources requirement."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT regulated under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Managing a Lloyd's of London syndicate","Producing tip sheets","Share advice in a newspaper column","Managing a unit trust invested in property"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Investment advice in newspapers is not a specified investment according to the Regulated Activities Order."},{"question":"When a firm is found in breach of the regulations, place the following in the order that procedures would occur:\nIRegulatory Decision Committee sends a decision notice\nIIDisciplinary action\nIIIFCA sends a preliminary findings letter\nIVFCA launches an investigation","options":["III, IV, I, and II","II, IV, I, and III","IV, III, I, and II","IV, I, III, and II"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The first stage is to launch an investigation, followed by sending a preliminary findings letter. Having considered representations from the firm, the Regulatory Decision Committee sends a decision notice to the firm."},{"question":"Which of the following is true with regard to the Training and Competence Sourcebook?","options":["Authorised firms are only required to ensure that their certified persons are competent for their role in the firm","​​​​​​FCA firm employees must have their competency reviewed regularly​","Appropriate examinations must be obtained for relevant roles within three years of working in investment banking","All employees must take appropriate examinations"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under FCA Principle for Businesses 3, management and control, all employees must be competent. The approved examinations must generally be passed within 30 months. Appropriate examinations are only a requirement for employees undertaking certain specific functions."},{"question":"Section 168 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 permits the FCA to appoint competent persons to carry out investigations on its behalf if:","options":["An offence has been committed","Too many complaints have been received","The firm is being liquidated","The Ombudsman insists that this is the case"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"S168 permits the FCA to do this if an offence has been committed. This could be for a wide range of offences including, but not exclusively, market abuse."},{"question":"Under which piece of legislation is the requirement for a firm to be authorised set out?","options":["Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Companies Act 2006","Criminal Justice Act 1993"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In order to be authorised, a person applies to the Regulator under Part 4A of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000."},{"question":"Under the SM&CR, a firm must take all reasonable steps to obtain a 'regulatory reference' from a candidate's current employer and anyone who has been a candidate's employer in how many past years?","options":["Three years","Five years","Six years","Seven years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Prior the application being submitted to the regulators a firm must, under the SM&CR, take all reasonable steps to obtain a 'regulatory reference' from a candidate's current employer and anyone who has been a candidate's employer for the past six years."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012, which of the following is NOT a statutory objective of the Financial Conduct Authority?","options":["Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system","Promoting effective competition in the interests of consumers","Securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers","Reducing financial crime"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The need to reduce financial crime was a statutory objective of the FSA. The FCA is accountable for reducing financial crime - but this is now subsumed into the integrity objective."},{"question":"A bank wants its compliance function to align with recognised good practice. Which arrangement is most consistent?","options":["Locating compliance within front office teams to improve commercial responsiveness","Making compliance independent from business areas and adequately resourcing it","Restricting compliance to specialist staff and excluding wider employee responsibility","Allowing compliance to operate without periodic review by internal audit"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The compliance function should be independent from the business units it oversees, have adequate resources and be subject to periodic and independent review by internal audit. Compliance is not solely the responsibility of specialist staff, so the other options conflict with the text."},{"question":"S71 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 allows private persons to claim damages for losses caused by which of the following?","options":["An authorised firm","Any firm, whether authorised or not","Authorised firms and approved individuals","Approved individuals"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under s71, a private person can sue an authorised firm for loss resulting from the employment of a prohibited individual or allowing an unapproved person to undertake a controlled function."},{"question":"According to the Financial Services Act 2012, which of the following bodies are the primary financial services regulators?\nIFinancial Services Authority\nIIPrudential Regulation Authority\nIIIFinancial Conduct Authority\nIVFinancial Policy Committee","options":["I and II","II and III","III and IV","II and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA and the FCA are the primary regulators for firms in the financial services industry."},{"question":"The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 states that a Recognised Investment Exchange (RIE) is exempt from the need for authorisation in respect of S19 - the General Prohibition. Which of the following bodies recognises these investment exchanges?","options":["The Department of Trade and Industry","His Majesty's Treasury","The Home Office","The Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The status of RIE is granted by the Financial Conduct Authority."},{"question":"S56 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 allows the FCA to prohibit individuals from carrying out specified functions in relation to regulated activities, i.e. a 'prohibition order'. What penalty can the FCA impose on an individual in breach of S56 FSMA 2000?","options":["6 months in jail","2 years in jail","A fine","A jail term the regulator considers appropriate"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"S56 FSMA allows the FCA and/or the PRA to prohibit individuals from carrying out specified functions in relation to regulated activities, i.e. a 'prohibition order'. A prohibition order may be issued to anyone - whether they are approved or not. To issue a prohibition order, the regulator must first issue a warning notice followed by a decision notice. The individual may appeal to the Tax and Chancery Chamber of the Upper Tribunal. A breach of a prohibition order is a criminal matter, but is not an imprisonable offence."},{"question":"Which of the following statements about the Financial Conduct Authority is FALSE?","options":["The Chancellor of the Exchequer appoints the CEO of the FCA","The FCA is a company","The board of the FCA can delegate its statutory objectives to another body","The FCA is the conduct regulator for the financial services industry"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Although answerable to the Treasury, the FCA is a company - it is limited by guarantee, directly by the UK government. Its board cannot delegate its statutory objectives to another body."},{"question":"Which of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["To levy specific fines in the case of money laundering activities","To ensure financial promotions issued by authorised firms are not misleading","To ensure that the relevant markets function well","To promote guidelines as to the conduct of authorised firms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA has four objectives.\nStrategic objective: ensuring that the relevant markets function well\nOperational objectives: securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers, protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system, promoting effective competition in the interests of consumers."},{"question":"Who is responsible for reducing risks to the financial system as a whole?","options":["Financial Policy Committee","National Crime Agency","Financial Conduct Authority","Her Majesty's Treasury"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FPC has responsibility for reducing risks to the financial system as a whole.\nThe PRA takes instructions from the Financial Policy Committee (FPC) at the Bank of England."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is CORRECT concerning a multi-lateral trading facility?","options":["It is a facility where a firm engages in multiple core investment services","It is a system where a firm provides similar services to those offered by exchanges","It is a system where a firm takes proprietary positions with a client","It is a facility where a firm operates in more than one location"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An MTF is a system which provides an alternative order matching system to an exchange. The client orders match with other clients so that the firm operating the MTF does not take proprietary positions."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be exempt from requiring authorisation?","options":["Recognised investment exchange","Appointed representative","Lloyd's of London","Members of certain professions"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Lloyds of London is not exempt. It is only the members of Lloyds' syndicates that are exempt."},{"question":"Which of the following bodies oversees the macro-economic and financial issues that could cause instability in the UK?","options":["Competition and Markets Authority","Prudential Regulation Authority","Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Policy Committee"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This is the role of the FPC. Its aim is to gain a bigger picture of the financial services and markets in order to better predict problems that may occur. It exists within the Bank of England."},{"question":"Which of the following is not a PRA Senior Manager Function for a UK bank?","options":["Chief risk function","Executive director function","Head of internal audit function","Chief finance function"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Executive director function is an FCA function, not a PRA function."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012 who is responsible for the soundness of 'significant investment firms'?","options":["HMRC","FCA","PRA","FSA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The focus of the PRA is stability - the safety and soundness of deposit taking firms, insurers and systematically important investment firms."},{"question":"A firm is deciding which oversight body is directly accountable to the UK Government through HM Treasury for how it carries out its functions. Which body fits this description?","options":["The Prudential Regulation Authority","The Financial Policy Committee","The Bank of England","The Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Financial Conduct Authority is accountable to the UK Government via HM Treasury for how it carries out its functions. By contrast, the Prudential Regulation Authority is answerable to Parliament via the House of Commons Treasury Committee through the Bank of England, while the Financial Policy Committee is a Bank of England committee."},{"question":"Which of the following statements are correct?\nIThe FCA is responsible for ensuring consumers get a fair deal\nIIThe FCA is responsible for stability of the markets\nIIIThe PRA is responsible for ensuring consumers get a fair deal\nIVThe PRA is responsible for stability of the markets","options":["I and III","I and IV","II and III","II and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA is responsible for ensuring that financial markets work well so that consumers get a fair deal, whilst the focus of the PRA is on stability - the safety and soundness of deposit taking firms, insurers and systematically important investment firms."},{"question":"What is the maximum number of employees permitted to work in an authorised firm's compliance department?","options":["10","20","50","No maximum"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There is no maximum (or minimum) limit to the number of employees permitted to work in a firm's compliance department."},{"question":"The Financial Conduct Authority is funded by contributions from which of the following?","options":["The Treasury indirectly through taxes","The LSE through a levy charged on transactions","Regulated firms authorised under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","The FCA through its own activities on the market"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Although the FCA is answerable to the Treasury, it is in fact a limited liability company. It is also funded by the financial services industry - businesses authorised under the FSMA 2000."},{"question":"Which of the following is not a conduct rule which must be followed by ALL accountability regime persons?","options":["You must act with integrity","You must observe proper standards of market conduct","You must act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers","You must act with due skill, care and diligence"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This conduct rule (COCON Rule 6) is only applicable to SMCR roles at firms within scope of the Consumer Duty. Employees of out-of-scope firms would follow COCON Rule 4 instead - 'You must pay due regard to the interests of customers and treat them fairly'."},{"question":"Who is responsible for the regulation of individual savings accounts (ISAs)?","options":["Department of Business Innovation and Skills","HM Revenue and Customs","The Prudential Regulation Authority","The Stock Exchange"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"HM Revenue and Customs is responsible for the regulation of Individual Savings Accounts (ISAs). ISAs are tax efficient savings and investments."},{"question":"For which of the following actions can a firm NOT appeal to the Tax and Chancery Chamber of the Upper Tribunal?","options":["Refusal of Part 4A permission","Imposition of penalties under the Market Abuse Regime","Refusal of listing on the LSE","Imposition of penalties under the Criminal Justice Act"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm can make an appeal to the TCCUT in response to decisions made by the Regulator. The courts decide the penalties under the Criminal Justice Act. The Regulator decides on Part 4A permission, market abuse and listing on the LSE. The Regulator also decides on approved person status and the Regulator's disciplinary measures."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a specified investment?","options":["Floating rate note","Forward rate agreement","National savings certificates","Contract for differences"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"National Savings Certificates are not tradable financial instruments and therefore are not specified investments."},{"question":"Under the FCA's Senior Managers regime which of the following is ALWAYS performing a senior management function?","options":["Audit manager","Chairman of the Board of Directors","Compliance manager","Administration manager"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Any director is, by definition, ALWAYS a senior manager under the Senior Managers Regime. The other three roles mentioned may, or may not, be Senior Managers."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of the Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC) regime implemented by the FCA?","options":["It is based on six outcomes for firms to achieve","Once implemented the firms have achieved their objectives","Firms should set reasonable expectations about the product performance","It looks to create a positive culture in the financial services industry"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"FTOC is seen as a continuous process where firms continually update their policies and behaviours to meet the objectives. The purpose is to embed a culture of fair treatment of customers in the financial services. N.B. Since the advent of the Consumer Duty, FTOC only applies to firms that are out-of-scope of the Duty."},{"question":"In the Supervision sourcebook (SUP) the FCA sets out the roles within a firm that require approval. Which of the following is not included as a senior manager function?","options":["Director","Partner","Non executive director","Investment adviser"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An investment adviser is performing a certification function."},{"question":"The purpose of the Competition and Markets Authority is to ensure healthy competition between companies, and make markets work well, for the benefit of whom?","options":["UK Government","The FCA","Consumers and the economy","Companies"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Competition and Markets Authority helps to ensure healthy competition between companies in the UK for the ultimate benefit of both consumers and of the economy."},{"question":"When the Regulator receives an application for a senior manager to become an approved person and finds some problems, they will return the application:","options":["To the individual and stop the process","To the individual and wait for clarification or amendments before considering their decision","To the firm and stop the process","To the firm and wait for clarification or amendments before considering their decision"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Applications for senior manager status involve the firm applying to the Regulator."},{"question":"Which one of the following is not a senior manager function?","options":["Director function","Head of key business area","Chief Risk Officer","Significant harm function"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Significant harm functions are also known as 'certified functions'."},{"question":"Which of the following is true of designated professional bodies (DPB)?","options":["All UK professional bodies have DPB status under FSMA 2000, giving members the opportunity to conduct a range of regulated activities","Membership of a DPB gives authorisation to conduct the regulated activities prescribed in the Regulated Activities Order","Exemption via membership of a DPB is only allowable where the activities are incidental to the main business of the professional","DPBs, having exempt status, do not need to follow the FCA rules when conducting regulated activities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"If the regulated activities are incidental and the professionals do not receive separate remuneration, they are exempt from the need for authorisation. Only lawyers, accountants, actuaries, conveyancers and chartered surveyors may receive an exemption when they are are members of a DPB. Membership of a DPB does not give authorisation for regulated activities and does not exclude them from the rules."},{"question":"Which is not a specified investment?","options":["A short term bank deposit","An option on physically delivered gas oil","A gilt repo","A forward rate agreement"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As gas oil options are physically delivered, they are not a financial instrument."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a regulated activity under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Managing a futures fund","Advising on share portfolios","Dealing in US dollars","Administering mortgages"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Dealing in currencies, including US dollars, is not a regulated activity under the FSMA 2000."},{"question":"An employer with one employee has not considered automatic enrolment because it believes the rules apply only to larger businesses. Under the Pensions Act 2008, what is its position?","options":["It must comply because any employer with at least one employee has a legal duty","It must comply because all employers irrespective of employees have a legal duty","It need not because only employers with at least five employees have a legal duty","It may defer compliance because only employers with an existing scheme have a legal duty"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Any employer with at least one employee has a legal duty to comply with automatic enrolment requirements under the Pensions Act 2008. The other options introduce thresholds or conditions not stated as the legal test."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["Ensuring the UK financial markets are competitive worldwide","Providing services that allow a firm to manage and control their risks","Implementing and monitoring financial legislation","Securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This is one of the statutory objectives of the FCA."},{"question":"A firm wants to identify the main purpose of automatic enrolment under the Pensions Act 2008. What does this require employers to do?","options":["Offer salary sacrifice arrangements to all employees","Contribute to a pension scheme alongside eligible employees","Provide financial advice on pension transfers to all employees","Guarantee a minimum retirement income for eligible employees"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The main aim of the Pensions Act 2008 was to introduce automatic enrolment, requiring employers to automatically enrol eligible employees into a pension scheme and contribute towards it. The other options describe obligations not set out in the source."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a specified activity?","options":["Acting as principal and end user when dealing in shares","Acting in relation to a collective investment scheme","Providing safe custody services in investments","Managing investments on behalf of others"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Transactions are not regulated if the investor is also the end user and no service is offered to others."},{"question":"Personal responsibility for senior manager functions is enforced by:","options":["A set of Conduct Rules backed by a duty of responsibility","Four general principles supplemented by a code of ethics","A code of practice which incorporates a range of principles","Seven principles and a code of conduct"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"There are ten conduct rules that apply to senior managers. In addition, prescribed responsibilities are laid out for each senior manager in a Statement of Responsibility, which is legally enforceable. This imposes a duty of responsibility."},{"question":"According to Section 19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (the 'General Prohibition'), which of the following activities would require a firm to gain authorisation by the FCA/PRA?","options":["Investment advice in newspapers and magazine articles","Television, radio and cable broadcasts about investments","Professional bodies providing advice in the normal course of business where the advice is separately remunerated","Trustees who are not being separately remunerated"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The following activities are not regarded as regulated activities:\nInvestment advice in newspapers (providing a tip sheet however is a regulated activity)\nTelevision broadcasts and similar activities\nTrustees, nominees and personal representatives (not separately remunerated) \nMembers of designated professional bodies (accountants, solicitors or actuaries) carrying on certain incidental regulated activities. However, if they are separately remunerated for those activities they do need authorisation."},{"question":"Which statement is the correct aim of the FCA's Consumer Duty?","options":["To ensure that professional clients receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services","To ensure that retail customers receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services","To ensure that eligible counterparties receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services","To ensure that financial services firms receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services."],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The aim of the FCA's Consumer Duty is to ensure that retail customers receive good outcomes when they purchase products or services."},{"question":"Which of the following Conduct Rules apply to all SMCR functions?","options":["Observe proper standards of market conduct","Disclose appropriately any information of which the FCA or PRA would reasonably expect notice","Take reasonable steps to ensure the business is organised so that it can be controlled","Take reasonable steps to ensure the firm complies with relevant requirements and standards of the regulatory system"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"'Observe proper standards of market conduct' is the correct answer. The other options apply to senior manager functions only."},{"question":"Which of the following is a specified investment under the Regulated Activities Order?","options":["Options on crude oil","Shares in an unincorporated business","US dollars","Premium bonds"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Currencies and premium bonds are not classed as specified investments under the FSMA 2000, nor are options on commodities (other than options on precious metals). Shares in any business would be a specified investment."},{"question":"Which of the following is true of the training and competence rules?","options":["Authorised firms are required to ensure their employees are competent for their role in the firm under Principle for Businesses 3, Management and Control","Approved examinations must be obtained within one year of working for an authorised firm","An employee must have passed the regulatory module before dealing with professional clients","An overseas individual may be allowed to sit just the regulatory module if they have at least four years relevant experience outside the UK"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Approved examinations must usually be obtained within 30 months of working for an authorised firm.\nAn employee must have passed the regulatory module before dealing with retail clients.\nAn overseas individual may be allowed to sit just the regulatory module if they have at least three years' relevant experience outside the UK.\nNote that a firm must regularly review the competence of its employees."},{"question":"All of the following are specified by the FCA as a certification function EXCEPT:","options":["Material risk takers","Dealing directly with clients","Supervisors of Certification Functions","Information providers"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Certification functions are: CASS oversight, proprietary trader, significant management, functions requiring qualifications, managers of certification functions, material risk takers and client dealing."},{"question":"To whom do the PRA's rule-making powers extend?","options":["Only PRA-authorised persons","All authorised persons","All regulated activities","All regulated activities and also activities that are not regulated"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Part 9A of the the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 empowers the FCA to make rules that are legally binding on authorised firms concerning regulated activity and also activity that is not regulated. The PRA has similar rule-making powers, however the powers only extend to PRA-authorised persons."},{"question":"Which of the following investments is regulated by the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000?","options":["Debentures","Property","Metals","Gambling"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Tangible assets and gambles are not regulated (unless in the form of a CFD)."},{"question":"Assuming that the activity is to be performed in the UK, which of the following would require authorisation under s19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, the 'General Prohibition'?","options":["Dealing as principal for your own account","Arranging currency options","Dealing in commodities","Newspapers giving incidental investment advice"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Dealing as principal, newspaper advice and dealing in commodities are excluded activities."},{"question":"Which one of the following is NOT a statutory objective of the FCA under the Financial Services Act 2012?","options":["Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection","Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system","Promoting effective competition","Promoting the safety and soundness of authorised persons"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA has a single strategic objective, which is ensuring that the markets function well. In addition the FCA has 3 operational objectives: Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection; Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system; and Promoting effective competition The PRA has been given a single general objective of promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons."},{"question":"Which statement best distinguishes an industry body from a professional body in UK financial services?","options":["An industry body has statutory regulatory powers, while a professional body does not","An industry body usually represents broader sector interests, while a professional body focuses on individual competence and ethical standards","An industry body handles customer complaints, while a professional body supervises prudential risk","An industry body authorises firms, while a professional body approves senior managers"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Industry bodies tend to be more generalist and support broader sector interests, while professional bodies focus on examinations, competence, knowledge and ethical behaviour of individuals. Neither type authorises firms, supervises prudential risk or operates the complaints scheme."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is not one of the Principles for Businesses laid down for firms in the financial sector?","options":["Financial prudence","Customers' interests","Conflicts of interests","Prevention of money laundering"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Prevention of money laundering is not one of the principles for businesses."},{"question":"From which point in time must senior managers and certification functions comply with the Conduct Rules under the FCA's Accountability Regime?","options":["Once their firm processes their annual appraisal","From the moment a firm files a certificate with the regulator","All relevant staff must comply with the Conduct Rules from appointment","From the moment an affected employee is certificated/approved as a fit and proper person"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All relevant staff must comply with the Conduct Rules from appointment."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is not true of the Principles for Businesses?","options":["All authorised firms must comply with the PRIN","Failure to comply with the PRIN can result in regulatory enforcement action for FCA firms","They only apply to firms authorised by the FCA","They represent a set of fundamental objectives backed by the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The PRIN are general statements of the fundamental obligations of FCA firms under FSMA 2000 and the regulatory system. They have been devised by, and are enforced by, the FCA. They apply to all authorised firms, PRA authorised firms also comply with the PRA's Fundamental Rules (FR)."},{"question":"All of the following are true of recognised investment exchanges, except:","options":["Membership does not confer authorisation as an investment business","It guarantees a market place where investors can always deal at fair prices","It provides an organised market framework within which transactions in investments can be effected","It will monitor the conduct of the market so as to prevent manipulation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Fair prices are not guaranteed, although they should be likely."},{"question":"Which of the following are not regulated investments, as defined by the Regulated Activities Order?","options":["ADRs and deep discounted bonds","Life assurance policies and certificates of deposit","Premium bonds and currencies","Bank deposits and exchange traded futures"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Premium bonds and currencies are not regulated investments. ADRs are American Depository Receipts and are specified investments."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000, a person will not be carrying on a regulated activity if (s)he does which of the following?","options":["Establishes a collective investment scheme","Advises on foreign currency","Issues a tip sheet","Arranges deals in shares"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Foreign currency is not a specified investment."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012, who is the prudential regulator for the UK insurance industry?","options":["His Majesty's Treasury","The Prudential Regulation Authority","Parliament","The Department for Business, Innovation and Skills"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA, or Prudential Regulation Authority, is the prudential regulator for the UK Insurance industry."},{"question":"Jo Wood has twice failed a key regulatory exam and the bailiffs have repossessed most of his belongings. He might not be considered fit and proper for which of the following reasons?","options":["Financially unsound and incapable","Incapable and dishonest","Dishonest and criminal","Criminal and financially unsound"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"He has no criminal convictions or dishonesty, but he is financially unsound and not competent or incapable because he has failed two key exams."},{"question":"Which one of the following statements concerning the Financial Policy Committee (FPC) is correct? The FPC meets","options":["Annually and produces an annual Financial Stability Report","Annually and produces a biannual Financial Stability Report","Quarterly and produces a biannual Financial Stability Report","Quarterly and produces an annual Financial Stability Report"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FPC meets at least four times a year and publishes a record of its formal meetings. It is also responsible for the Bank's biannual Financial Stability Report. Don't confuse the FPC with the Monetary Policy Committee (MPC), which meets monthly. Although the FPC does not meet 'quarterly', but 'at least 4 times a year', C is the best answer."},{"question":"A firm wishes to provide services in investments. According to the Regulated Activities Order, which of the following investments would NOT require the firm to seek authorisation from the FCA/PRA?","options":["Forward rate agreements","Commercial paper","Floating rate notes","Foreign exchange"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Foreign exchange (currencies) is not specified by the RAO."},{"question":"Which of the following does not reflect the range of sanctions that the PRA can impose on a firm breaching PRA rules?","options":["Maximum fine of £250,000","Varying Part 4A permission","Cancelling permission","Public censure"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The maximum fine is unlimited."},{"question":"Which types of directors are defined as senior manager functions in an FCA authorised firm?","options":["Both executive and non-executive directors, and the Chairperson","Executive directors only","Both executive and non-executive directors, but not the Chairperson","Non-executive directors only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Every member of the board of directors is defined as a senior manager in any authorised firm, irrespective of their title or status."},{"question":"Which of the following is responsible for funding the FCA?","options":["Authorised firms","His Majesty's Treasury","The Bank of England","His Majesty's Revenue and Customs"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA is a company limited by guarantee. The guarantor is the Treasury. However, on an ongoing basis, the FCA is funded by charging annual levies on its member firms."},{"question":"To whom is the FCA directly accountable?","options":["HM Treasury","Bank of England","UK Government","Its shareholders"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Although the FCA is ultimately accountable to the UK government on how it carries out its functions, it does so via HM Treasury. The FCA has no shareholders."},{"question":"A compliance officer is identifying the FCA sourcebook that applies prudential requirements to UK-regulated firms subject to MiFID, excluding banks and credit institutions. Which sourcebook applies?","options":["GENPRU","COBS","MIFIDPRU","IPRU-INV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"MIFIDPRU contains prudential requirements that came into effect on 1 January 2022 for UK-regulated firms subject to MiFID that are not banks or credit institutions. GENPRU and IPRU-INV apply in different prudential contexts, while COBS is not a prudential sourcebook."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be considered acts of misconduct by a firm in the financial industry?","options":["A breach of the Financial Services and Markets Act","A breach of a Principle for Businesses","Failure to comply with instructions from an FCA inspector","Not immediately producing adequate information for an FCA inspection"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Failure to comply with an FCA statement of principle is an act of misconduct. This is also true for breaches of FSMA 2000, provision of false information and not complying with the instructions of an FCA inspector. Firms are permitted 'a reasonable time period' (generally taken to mean 48 hours, unless instructed otherwise) to produce adequate information for an FCA inspection."},{"question":"If an advisor carries on regulated activities without having sought Part 4A permission from the FCA, what is the effect on any contract with a client?","options":["It is void as from day one","It is void as from the date of the conviction of the advisor","It is voidable at the option of the client","It is voidable at the option of the firm"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Any agreements made by a person in contravention of the general prohibition are unenforceable by that person against the other party to the agreement. A consequence of a breach of the general prohibition is that certain agreements or contracts can be voidable, at the option of the client."},{"question":"When the FCA grants an exchange with recognised status, it:","options":["Confers authorisation to do investment business","Requires cooperation from the exchange with overseas regulators","Requires that the exchange must be fit and proper","Implies that all transactions will be settled in a timely manner"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In order to be recognised by the FCA, these exchanges and clearing houses need to be fit and proper for their purpose. Once recognised, the exchanges are referred to as recognised investment exchanges (RIEs), and the clearing houses are referred to as recognised clearing houses (RCHs). RIEs and RCHs are exempt persons in that they do not need to seek authorisation from the FCA to carry out regulated activities – they are, instead, recognised."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the FCA's attitude with regard to the enforcement of the Principles for Business?","options":["A firm can be prosecuted for failure to comply with the principles","The FCA will fine firms for violation of the principles","FCA firms can be disciplined because of a principle violation","The FCA doesn't enforce the principles"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm cannot be criminally prosecuted for breach of the Principles for Business but they can be disciplined by the regulator."},{"question":"Which of the following is MOST LIKELY to be a certified function?","options":["Head of internal audit","Head of financial reporting","Money laundering reporting officer","Financial planner"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A financial planner is a significant harm function, as if they fail to perform their job correctly this causes conduct risk for the consumer using their services. As such, they are always a certified function. MLROs and heads of functional areas in firms (such as the Head of Internal Audit and the Financial Controller (a.k.a. 'Head of financial reporting', this is the chief accountant reporting into the Chief Financial Officer, who is a director)) are ALWAYS Senior Manager Functions."},{"question":"The FCA is granted the power to enforce their rules and sanction and discipline those who do not follow them. Which of the following would the FCA not seek disciplinary action for?","options":["Failure to get a financial promotion approved","Not following FCA guidelines","Not following FCA rules","Not following FCA principles"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm may choose not to follow FCA guidelines (on cold calling hours for example) but failure to comply with rules and principles is an offence."},{"question":"Under the FCA's SM&CR, which of the following is most likely to be a senior management function for a UK bank?","options":["Compliance officer","Money laundering reporting officer","Customer advisor","Investment manager"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MLRO is always a senior manager function. All others are more likely to be certified functions."},{"question":"According to the Financial Services Act 2012, which one of the following is a statutory objective of the FCA?","options":["To balance the burdens and restrictions on firms with the benefits of regulation for consumers and the industry","The firm to use its resources in the most economic and efficient way","That individuals involved in the management of authorised firms must take appropriate responsibility","Protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The objectives are: Strategic objective: ensuring that the relevant markets function well Operational objectives are the following: securing an appropriate degree of protection for consumers, protecting and enhancing the integrity of the UK financial system, promoting effective competition in the interests of consumers."},{"question":"According to the general prohibition (S19 of the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000), which of the following activities would NOT require a firm to gain authorisation by the Regulator?","options":["Purchasing ADRs on behalf of a private client","Advising on the purchase of Indian rupees","Managing a futures portfolio for a discretionary client","Broking shares for an execution only private client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Advising on currencies, such as rupees, is not an authorised activity under FSMA 2000 as detailed in the Treasury Regulated Activities Order 2001."},{"question":"Which of the following does not require FCA/PRA authorisation?","options":["Sending dematerialised instructions in investments","Running a collective investment scheme","Dealing on behalf of others","Writing a newspaper column advising on investments"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Newspapers have a specific exemption."},{"question":"Which of the following Principles for Businesses requires firms to have appropriate risk management controls in place?","options":["Management and control","Integrity","Market conduct","Financial prudence"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Principle for Businesses 3: Management and control requires firms to take reasonable care to organise and control its affairs responsibly and effectively, with adequate risk management systems."},{"question":"Which of the following is not one of the Principles for Businesses?","options":["Skill, care and diligence","Management and control","Financial prudence","Senior management functions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Senior management functions are regulated via the Accountability Regime (SM&CR), not the Principles for Businesses."},{"question":"What is the maximum penalty for those operating an unauthorised firm carrying out regulated activities in the UK as stated in the Financial Services Markets Act 2000?","options":["Six months imprisonment and a £5,000 fine","Six months imprisonment and an unlimited fine","Two years imprisonment and a £5,000 fine","Two years imprisonment and an unlimited fine"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"If you are convicted of a breach of the General Prohibition (s19 FSMA 2000) in a Crown Court the maximum sentence is two years in jail and/ or an unlimited fine. The maximum sentence for cases heard in a magistrates court is six months and/ or a £5,000 fine."},{"question":"Which of the following are not specified investments as defined by the Regulated Activities Order?","options":["ADRs and deep discounted bonds","Life assurance policies and certificates of deposit","Premium bonds and currencies","Bank deposits and exchange traded futures"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"ADR is an American Depository Receipt.\nPremium bonds and currencies are not regulated investments."},{"question":"How would you describe a firm which has gained Part 4A permission?","options":["Authorised","Registered","Permitted","Regulated"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Part 4A permission authorises a firm to carry on regulated activities."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the objective of conduct risk regulation?","options":["Rules do matter, but outcomes for consumers and markets are the principal focus of regulatory concern","Prescriptive rules to manage the conduct of employees of financial firms are the only regulatory concern","Outcomes for consumers and markets do matter, but rules are the principal focus of regulatory concern","Outcomes for consumers and markets are the only regulatory concern"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA takes a balanced approach to the management of conduct risk. The rules do matter, but outcomes for consumers and markets are the principal focus of regulatory concern."},{"question":"Which one of the following is the FCA's strategic objective?","options":["Ensuring that the markets function well","Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection","Promoting effective competition","Promoting the safety and soundness of authorised persons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA has a single strategic objective, which is ensuring that the markets function well. In addition the FCA has 3 operational objectives: Securing an appropriate degree of consumer protection; Protecting the integrity of the UK financial system and Promoting effective competition\nThe PRA has been given a single general objective of promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons."},{"question":"A Multilateral Trading Facility (MTF) is best described as:","options":["A system that brings together multiple parties that are interested in buying and selling financial instruments and which allows them to do so","A market place for eligible buyers and sellers who trade on a firm commitment basis but do not meet the minimum standards set out in MiFID","An exchange which is given a recognition order by the local competent authority to conduct its activities in a public forum","An account that meets the requirements set out by His Majesty's Revenue and Customs for a Minor's Trust Fund"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An exchange which is given a recognition order' is a recognised investment exchange and 'an account that meets the requirements of a Minors Trust Fund' is a distracter that refers to a child trust fund. 'A system that brings together multiple parties that are interested in buying and selling financial instruments and which allows them to do so' is the correct answer."},{"question":"A regulated firm is reviewing whether to strengthen its internal audit function. Which role best reflects internal audit's contribution to oversight within the firm?","options":["Developing an independent view of risks and reporting findings directly to the board","Setting prudential rules for authorised firms and monitoring market-wide stability","Approving financial promotions before they are communicated to retail clients","Investigating eligible complaints and issuing awards against financial businesses"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Internal audit is expected to develop an independent and informed view of the risks faced by a firm and be able to discuss findings directly with the audit committee and board. The other options describe functions of regulators, complaints bodies or approval processes rather than internal audit oversight 1."},{"question":"When the UK regulator allows a firm to undertake regulated activities, it grants which of the following?","options":["Authorisation","Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 prohibition","Regulatory exemption","Part 4A Permission"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Authorisation confers status, but activities must be permitted by Part 4A of the Act (Part 4A permissions)."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a type of work under the FCA supervision model?","options":["Proactive Firm Supervision","Reactive Supervision","Thematic","Proactive Intervention Framework"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Proactive Intervention Framework is a supervisory approach adopted by PRA and not the FCA."},{"question":"Provisions in the FCA’s Handbook indicated with the letter ‘R’ are binding upon whom?","options":["Approved persons","Authorised persons","The Financial Conduct Authority","Exempt persons"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Provisions in the FCA’s Handbook indicated with the letter ‘R’ are RULES – and these are binding on authorised persons (i.e. firms). If a firm contravenes a rule, it may be subject to discipline."},{"question":"Which of the following is/are considered in assessing the fitness and propriety of a firm?","options":["The competence and experience of the board","The ability to comply with the financial resources requirement regulations on an on-going basis","The specific activities to be performed","All are considered"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Firms must apply to the Regulator for authorisation under Part 4A of the FSMA 2000 and demonstrate that they are 'fit and proper'. In other words, the FCA has to satisfy itself that the firm has sufficient financial resources and management skills to undertake the relevant functions."},{"question":"What was the effect of Brexit on passporting for UK financial services firms?","options":["UK firms could continue passporting if they had FCA authorisation only","UK firms could continue passporting for wholesale business but not retail business","UK firms could no longer passport services throughout the EU without local authorisation","UK firms could no longer passport services unless they used an appointed representative"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"After Brexit, UK firms were no longer able to passport products or services throughout the EU without appropriate authorisation from the local regulator. The other options add conditions or distinctions not stated."},{"question":"Which of the following is not taken into account when the FCA assesses a firm's fitness and propriety?","options":["The ability of senior management to direct the business effectively and establish internal controls","Whether those seeking approval have suffered bankruptcy or defaulted on personal loans","The ability of the firm to meet its financial obligations on an ongoing basis","The market share the firm has within its sector of the financial services industry"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The fitness and propriety of a firm is rooted in the threshold conditions.\nThe ability of the senior management to control their firm is part of the suitability test.\nThe ability for an employee to pay their debts is part of the approved person fit and proper test, but it also plays a part in the adequate resources of a firm - employees are part of a firm's resources.\nThis condition also links with the firm's ability to meet its financial obligations.\nIt does not matter what market share the firm has."},{"question":"From which point in time must certification functions comply with the Conduct Rules under the SMCR regime?","options":["One year and no later from the certification of the employee","From the moment the firm files a valid certificate with the regulator","All relevant staff are immediately on appointment bound by the Conduct Rules","From the moment the employee receives their certificate from the RDC"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All relevant staff must comply with the six individual Conduct Rules. This includes all staff except ancillary staff."},{"question":"If an applicant for approved person status has a criminal history covered by the Rehabilitation of Offenders Act 1974 which of the following describes the right of the FCA to take this into account?","options":["It will not be taken into account","It might be taken into account if relevant","It would be taken into account if the offence occurred within five years","It would be taken into account if the offence occurred within ten years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is no specified time limit here. The FCA reserves the right to consider any conviction."},{"question":"All of the following are specified by the FCA as SM&CR functions except:","options":["Executive director function","Compliance oversight function","Supervisor of certification functions","Health and safety function"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The list of senior managers is long and includes compliance oversight and directors. Certification functions also include supervisors of certification functions. health and safety is likely to be conduct staff or even ancillary staff."},{"question":"Where a firm operates under the Senior Manager and Certification Regime, which ONE of the following roles always requires approval from the Regulator? Somebody:","options":["Recruiting staff","Working in compliance","Acting as a non-executive director","With staff supervisory responsibilities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"All four could potentially require the regulator's approval as a Senior Manager Function, but only non-execs would always require such status."},{"question":"Who regulates UK banks?","options":["His Majesty's Treasury","The Prudential Regulation Authority","The London Stock Exchange","The European Central Bank"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The PRA is the regulator - it is part of The Bank of England."},{"question":"The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 does NOT require businesses carrying out which of the following to be authorised?","options":["Managing a bond fund","Advising on currencies","Establishing a collective investment scheme","Arranging deals in investments"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Currencies are not specified investments."},{"question":"If the FCA is asked to pursue 'redress' it will:","options":["Consider the different methods of redress available to it","Impose a maximum penalty of £100,000","Refer the case to the FSCS","Follow the thresholds set out in the Claims Level Guidance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A is the best possible answer as the FCA could seek a restitution order or require an investor to contact the FSCS/FOS."},{"question":"According to the provisions under Senior Managers Regime, the responsibilities of directors must be:","options":["Clearly and appropriately apportioned","Reviewed by external auditors on an annual basis","Recorded and the records kept for twelve months from when the responsibilities change","Clear, fair and not misleading"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"As part of the application process, firms need to include a statement of responsibilities setting out the aspects of the firm’s affairs the person will be responsible for managing when performing the function. The regulators expect that each responsibility will be allocated to a single senior manager. Where a responsibility is shared between more than one senior manager regulators expect the division of responsibility and accountability for that responsibility to be clearly articulated."},{"question":"The FCA guidance on electronic communication with a client states that a firm should do all of the following, except:","options":["Be able to demonstrate that the client wishes to communicate using this type of media","If entering an agreement, make it clear to the client that a contractual relationship has legal consequences","Have in place arrangements to ensure secure transmission and receipt of the communication","Be able to demonstrate that it made it clear to the client that instructions communicated in this manner may not be acted upon immediately"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The firm should also be able to verify the authenticity and integrity of the communication, together with the date and time sent and received."},{"question":"Which of the following best reflects the 'thematic' work of the FCA?","options":["Pre-emptive identification of harm through review and assessment","It is remedial work to deal with problems that are emerging or have happened","Dealing with issues that are emerging or have happened to prevent harm growing","Wider diagnostic or remedy work where there is actual or potential harm across a number of firms"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Wider diagnostic or remedy work where there is actual or potential harm across a number of firms = Thematic\n\nIt is remedial work to deal with problems that are emerging or have happened = Reactive\n\nPre-emptive identification of harm through review and assessment = Proactive"},{"question":"Which of the following is not a sanction available to the FCA in relation to a breach of the conduct of business rules?","options":["Public censure","Fine","Prohibition order","Disqualification of directors"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Directors may only be disqualified through a court order."},{"question":"Which of the following roles should be performed by a Senior Manager Function according to SUP 10C and SYSC 24 of the FCA handbook?","options":["Working in compliance","Supervising other employees","Recruitment and training","Chair of the nominations committee"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Most chairperson functions are classified as senior manager functions and would require the individual to be approved.\nNote 1: Working in compliance is not necessarily a controlled function unless it is the compliance oversight function.\nNote 2: Only individuals supervising approved persons would need to be approved themselves.\n\nIn addition, do not be thrown by the use of specific Handbook references. Most questions that do this can still be answered without the Handbook reference: \"Which of the following roles should be performed by a senior manager function?\""}],"flashcards":[{"front":"FSMA 2000","back":"Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 (effective 2001) — the primary UK legislation regulating financial services; created the general prohibition and the regulated activities framework."},{"front":"Twin peaks regulation","back":"The FCA (conduct) and PRA (prudential) both supervise deposit-takers, insurers and major investment firms, each with a different regulatory focus."},{"front":"PRA general objective","back":"Promoting the safety and soundness of PRA-authorised persons, by avoiding adverse effects on UK financial stability and minimising the impact of firm failure."},{"front":"FCA's 3 operational objectives","back":"Consumer protection, market integrity, and effective competition."},{"front":"Financial Policy Committee (FPC)","back":"Bank of England committee (created April 2013) focused on identifying/reducing systemic risk; meets at least 4 times a year; publishes the biannual Financial Stability Report."},{"front":"Consumer Duty non-retail instrument exclusion","back":"Financial instruments with a minimum denomination/investment of £50,000 are excluded from Consumer Duty scope."},{"front":"Consumer Duty BCOBS charity threshold","back":"Charities with turnover below £1 million are within Consumer Duty scope for deposit-taking activities."},{"front":"Consumer Duty 4 outcomes","back":"Products and Services; Price and Value; Consumer Understanding; Consumer Support."},{"front":"FTOC — who it applies to","back":"Firms out of scope of the Consumer Duty (no material impact on retail outcomes, or product/service exempt)."},{"front":"PRA Fundamental Rules — how many","back":"8 rules, applying proportionately to all PRA-regulated firms."},{"front":"SM&CR — 3 components","back":"Senior Managers Regime (SMR), Certification Regime (CR), and Conduct Rules."},{"front":"SM&CR — solo-regulated firm tiers","back":"Limited scope (fewer requirements), Core scope (baseline requirements), Enhanced scope (extra requirements)."},{"front":"Regulatory reference lookback period","back":"Firms must take reasonable steps to obtain regulatory references covering a candidate's employment over the past 6 years before submitting Form A."},{"front":"Management Responsibilities Map","back":"Documents a firm's management and governance arrangements, including how prescribed responsibilities are allocated, for transparency of accountability."},{"front":"Duty of Responsibility","back":"Requires SMF holders to take reasonable steps to fulfil their prescribed responsibilities and to prevent, stop or remedy regulatory breaches."},{"front":"SMF17","back":"Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO) — an FCA-designated Senior Management Function."},{"front":"Material Risk Taker (MRT)","back":"An individual whose activities could have a material impact on the firm's risk profile; defined in the PRA Remuneration Rulebook/CRR; the PRA limits Certification Regime scope to MRTs ('Code staff')."},{"front":"Conduct Rule 6","back":"'Act to deliver good outcomes for retail customers' — replaces Rule 4 for firms in scope of the Consumer Duty."},{"front":"Senior Manager Conduct Rules (SC1-4)","back":"SC1 Effective control; SC2 Compliance with regulatory requirements; SC3 Appropriate delegation/oversight; SC4 Appropriate disclosure to regulators."},{"front":"FIT test — 3 criteria","back":"Honesty, integrity and reputation; competence and capability; financial soundness."},{"front":"CRD three pillars","back":"Pillar 1: minimum capital (credit/market/operational risk). Pillar 2: supervisory add-on for uncovered risks. Pillar 3: public disclosure for market discipline."},{"front":"Capital shortfall notification duty","back":"A firm must notify its regulator immediately if it knows its capital resources have fallen below the prescribed minimum."},{"front":"Investment Firms Prudential Regime (IFPR)","back":"UK regime (effective 2022, in MIFIDPRU) simplifying prudential requirements for FCA solo-regulated MiFID investment firms, focused on potential harm to consumers/markets."},{"front":"Bank of England — lender of last resort","back":"In exceptional circumstances the BoE may extend credit to banks/financial institutions when no other institution will."},{"front":"Pensions Act 2008 — auto-enrolment","back":"Requires employers with at least one employee to auto-enrol eligible employees (aged 22 to State Pension age, earning above a threshold) into a workplace pension."},{"front":"BIS","back":"Bank for International Settlements — established 1930; 'bank for central banks'; Board of 18 (incl. 6 ex-officio Governors: Belgium, France, Germany, Italy, UK, USA); 63 member central banks; BoE Governor represents the UK."},{"front":"IOSCO","back":"International Organisation of Securities Commissions — global securities regulation standard-setter; Board of 34; FCA CEO represents the UK."},{"front":"Appointed Representative (AR)","back":"An exempt person conducting regulated activity under contract with an authorised principal, who accepts legal responsibility; cannot deal as principal or manage investments."},{"front":"FSMA Exemption Order 2001 — central banks","back":"BoE, ECB and EEA central banks are exempt from authorisation for all regulated activity except effecting/carrying out contracts of insurance."},{"front":"Designated Professional Body (DPB) examples","back":"ICAEW, the Law Society, the Institute of Actuaries, RICS."},{"front":"Regulated mortgage contract threshold","back":"At least 40% of the mortgaged property must be used as the borrower's (or related party's) dwelling."},{"front":"Authorisation statutory deadlines","back":"6 months for a complete application; up to 12 months for an incomplete application."},{"front":"Section 56 FSMA","back":"Gives the FCA/PRA power to make a prohibition order barring an individual (not fit and proper) from performing a specified function."},{"front":"Section 138D Financial Services Act 2012","back":"Gives a person who suffers loss from an authorised firm's breach of an FCA rule the right to claim damages."},{"front":"T&C Sourcebook — supervision intensity","back":"Supervision must be significantly greater before an employee is assessed as competent than after."},{"front":"30-day rule (T&C)","back":"An overseas-based individual performing a customer function is excluded from qualification requirements if they spend no more than 30 days in the UK in a 12-month period, and are appropriately supervised."},{"front":"CPD hours for advisers","back":"Minimum 35 hours of CPD per year, of which at least 21 hours must be structured learning."},{"front":"Consumer Duty champion","back":"The requirement for firms to appoint a Consumer Duty 'champion' was removed by the FCA in February 2025."},{"front":"Conduct risk","back":"No precise FCA definition — broadly, the risk to customers and market integrity arising from how firms and staff conduct themselves."},{"front":"PRA risk element framework","back":"Gross risk (potential impact + external context/business model risk) offset by mitigating factors = net risk to PRA objectives."}]},{"id":"reg-ch2","number":2,"title":"Conduct of Business and Client Assets","examWeight":"35 of 75 questions (~47%)","summaryHtml":"\n<p><strong>Chapter 2 is the single biggest topic in the exam (~35/75 questions).</strong> It covers COBS (Conduct of Business Sourcebook) end-to-end: who it applies to, how clients are categorised, what firms must tell clients, how promotions are policed, suitability/appropriateness, product disclosure, conflicts, inducements, dealing/execution, transaction reporting, and CASS (client assets). Precision on numbers is everything.</p>\n\n<h4>Application of COBS</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>COBS applies to UK firms doing MiFID business with UK clients from a UK establishment. Post-Brexit, COBS no longer applies to a UK firm's MiFID business from an EEA branch; it applies to non-UK (third-country) MiFID firms operating a UK branch.</li>\n<li>Exception: investment research and personal account dealing rules apply on a <strong>home state basis</strong> regardless of where business is conducted.</li>\n<li>ECP business disapplies large parts of COBS 2, 4, 6, 8A, 10, 11, 12 and 16.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Client categorisation (Section 3)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Three categories: <strong>retail client</strong> (most protection), <strong>professional client</strong> (per se or elective), <strong>eligible counterparty</strong> (least protection).</li>\n<li>Large undertaking (non-MiFID) needs 2 of: balance sheet EUR12.5m, turnover EUR25m, 250 employees; OR called-up share capital/net assets >=GBP5m for 2 years. MiFID large undertaking: 2 of balance sheet EUR20m, turnover EUR40m, own funds EUR2m.</li>\n<li>Elective professional client: qualitative test (firm assesses expertise) plus quantitative test for MiFID business (2 of 3: 10 significant trades/quarter over 4 quarters; portfolio >EUR500,000; 1 year relevant professional experience).</li>\n<li>Local authorities are retail by default; can elect professional if portfolio >GBP10 million plus one other condition.</li>\n<li>Elective ECP: only per se professional clients (undertakings) that request it.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Communicating with clients / financial promotions (Section 5)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Must be fair, clear and not misleading (Principles 6 and 7, or Principle 12/Consumer Duty).</li>\n<li>Approving a promotion for an unauthorised person requires the FCA \"gateway\" permission (2023).</li>\n<li>Past performance: cover minimum 5 years (or life of product if shorter), in complete 12-month periods.</li>\n<li>Cooling-off: minimum 24 hours for first-time high-risk investment customers.</li>\n<li>NMMIs banned to retail; RMMIs restricted with risk warnings, no incentives to invest.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Advising and selling (Section 7)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Suitability applies to personal recommendations and managing investments; requires knowledge/experience, financial situation (ability to bear loss), and objectives/risk profile.</li>\n<li>Suitability report timing: life policy before conclusion (or immediately after if oral/urgent cover); pension/stakeholder within 14 days if cancellation rules apply; otherwise before transaction concluded.</li>\n<li>Appropriateness applies to non-advised sales of complex products; not required for execution-only in non-complex instruments.</li>\n<li>Churning = excessive dealing for commission; switching = replacing one investment with another when not suitable in aggregate.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Product disclosure and cancellation (Section 8)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>KIID (UCITS), KID (PRIIPs, max 3 A4 pages), KFD (packaged products).</li>\n<li>Cancellation periods: life/pension products <strong>30 calendar days</strong>; everything else (cash ISA, non-life/pension advised or distance) <strong>14 calendar days</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Conflicts, inducements and research (Sections 9-10)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Disclosure of a conflict is a last resort, only when organisational arrangements cannot manage it; must be in a durable medium.</li>\n<li>Minor non-monetary benefits are allowed if disclosed, reasonable, and cannot impair duty to the client.</li>\n<li>Research must be paid for separately (own resources or RPA) unless: SME research (market cap under GBP200 million exemption), FICC research, open access research, or research from providers unconnected to execution services.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Dealing, execution and reporting (Sections 11-12)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Best execution = \"all sufficient steps\" (MiFID firms) vs \"all reasonable steps\" (AIFMs/residual CIS operators); factors: price, cost, speed, likelihood of execution/settlement, size, nature.</li>\n<li>Order execution policy reviewed at least annually.</li>\n<li>Transaction reporting (UK MiFIR): by close of next business day. Trade reporting (LSE): within 3 minutes. Post-trade transparency: 1 minute (equity), 5 minutes (non-equity). Short selling notification: 3.30pm next trading day.</li>\n<li>Periodic statements: at least quarterly (monthly if leveraged; annually if deal-by-deal confirmations given). 10% portfolio depreciation notification required for portfolio management (note: the requirement to notify clients of contingent liability/leveraged investment depreciation was removed from 7 June 2023).</li>\n<li>Deal confirmations: no later than the next business day.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>Client assets (Section 13)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>CASS creates a statutory trust over client money (CASS 7). Bank acknowledgement letters must now be signed and returned before client money is placed (the old 20-business-day grace period was removed).</li>\n<li>DvP exemption unless settlement fails to occur within 3 business days.</li>\n</ul>\n","sections":[{"heading":"2.1 Application and General Provisions of COBS","html":"\n<p>The <strong>Conduct of Business Sourcebook (COBS)</strong> sits in the Business Standards block of the FCA Handbook. It shifts regulation towards outcomes rather than box-ticking, and implements MiFID's conduct-of-business provisions (high-level standards, not prescriptive detail). COBS also covers non-MiFID investment business.</p>\n<h4>1.1 Firms subject to COBS - geographic scope</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>General rule: COBS applies to firms carrying out relevant activities from an establishment (including that of an appointed representative) <strong>maintained in the UK</strong>.</li>\n<li>COBS generally applies where a UK firm does business with a UK client from an <strong>overseas establishment</strong>, unless the overseas person qualifies for certain exclusions.</li>\n<li>Post-Brexit: COBS no longer applies to a UK MiFID firm's business carried on from an <strong>EEA branch</strong>.</li>\n<li>Rules derived from MiFID apply to a <strong>non-UK (third-country) MiFID investment firm</strong> operating a UK branch; post-Brexit EEA firms with a UK branch are classed as third-country firms for UK MiFID business.</li>\n<li><strong>Exception</strong>: investment research and personal account dealing rules apply on a <strong>home state basis</strong> only - ie, they apply to UK firms regardless of where in the world the business is conducted.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>1.2 Activities subject to COBS</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Designated investment business.</li>\n<li>Long-term insurance business relating to life policies.</li>\n<li>Accepting deposits - only in part (eg financial promotion rules, product information rules).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Eligible Counterparty (ECP) business disapplication</h4>\n<p>A range of COBS rules are disapplied for firms carrying on business with an ECP, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Parts of COBS 2 (inducements, agent as client, reliance on others).</li>\n<li>Much of COBS 4 (fair, clear, not misleading; financial promotions; direct offer promotions; pre-service disclosure).</li>\n<li>COBS 6 (costs and charges information, compensation information).</li>\n<li>COBS 8A (client agreements).</li>\n<li>COBS 10 (appropriateness / non-advised services).</li>\n<li>Parts of COBS 11 (best execution, client order handling).</li>\n<li>Parts of COBS 12 (labelling of non-independent research).</li>\n<li>COBS 16 (reporting information to clients).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Members/participants of a regulated market do not need to comply with COBS 11.4 (client limit orders) between each other, but must comply when executing orders on behalf of a client.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.2 Appointed Representatives (ARs)","html":"\n<p>COBS applies to a principal firm in respect of the relevant regulated activities carried out on its behalf by its <strong>appointed representatives (ARs)</strong>. ARs are exempt persons who can carry out regulated activities under the principal's umbrella responsibility.</p>\n<p>Although a financial promotion communicated by an AR is technically an <strong>exempt promotion</strong> (so does not need separate approval), COBS makes clear that the <strong>principal firm remains responsible</strong> for the content of financial promotions communicated by its ARs.</p>\n<h4>2022 AR regime reforms (effective end of 2022, following a 2021 FCA consultation)</h4>\n<p>Two main areas of change:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Additional information on ARs and notification requirements for principals.</li>\n<li>Clarifying/strengthening principals' responsibilities and expectations.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Information and notification requirements:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Principals must notify the FCA of a new AR appointment <strong>30 days before</strong> it takes effect.</li>\n<li>Principals must provide information on their business and their ARs, including the nature of regulated activities the ARs will undertake.</li>\n<li>Within <strong>60 days</strong> of the new rules coming into force, principals had to provide information on existing ARs to the FCA.</li>\n<li>Complaints and revenue data on ARs must be provided by principals.</li>\n<li>The FCA updates the Register with the regulated activities principals permit their ARs to undertake.</li>\n<li>Principals must notify the FCA whether they intend to provide (or cease providing) regulatory hosting services.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Responsibilities of principals / FCA expectations:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Enhanced oversight: adequate systems and controls, sufficient resources, monitoring growth.</li>\n<li>More effective responsibility: monitoring/assessing risk of harm; overseeing ARs to a standard comparable to direct employees.</li>\n<li>Clarity on when/how to terminate an AR relationship and assist an orderly wind-down.</li>\n<li><strong>Annual review</strong> of ARs' activities, business and senior management, including a self-assessment document prepared by the principal.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"heading":"2.3 Accepting Clients (Client Categorisation)","html":"\n<h4>3.1 Application of client categorisation rules</h4>\n<p>A firm carrying on designated investment business must categorise its clients. For mixed MiFID/non-MiFID business, MiFID categorisation applies throughout unless the two are conducted entirely separately.</p>\n<h4>3.1.1 Definition of a client</h4>\n<p>A client is someone to whom a firm provides, intends to provide, or has provided a service in carrying out a regulated activity, and (for MiFID/equivalent third-country business) anything that is an <strong>ancillary service</strong>. Includes potential clients and agents. For financial promotion rules, includes anyone communicated with, whether or not an actual client. Clients of a firm's AR/tied agent are also clients of the firm.</p>\n<h4>3.1.2 The client categories</h4>\n<table><tr><th>Category</th><th>Protection level</th><th>Key notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Retail client</td><td>Highest</td><td>Any client who is not professional or ECP. \"Customer\" = retail + professional clients combined.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Professional client</td><td>Medium</td><td>Per se or elective.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Eligible counterparty (ECP)</td><td>Lowest</td><td>Narrower list than per se professional; excludes large undertakings.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p><strong>Per se professional clients</strong> include: entities required to be authorised/regulated (credit institutions, investment firms, other authorised financial institutions, insurance companies, CIS/management companies, pension funds/management companies, commodity/commodity derivatives dealers, other institutional investors); large undertakings; national/regional governments, public bodies, central banks, supranational institutions; institutional investors whose main business is investing in financial instruments.</p>\n<h4>Large undertaking thresholds</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>MiFID/equivalent third-country business</strong>: meets any 2 of - balance sheet total <strong>EUR20 million</strong>; net turnover <strong>EUR40 million</strong>; own funds <strong>EUR2 million</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-MiFID business</strong> - a company is a large undertaking if:\n<ul>\n<li>Called-up share capital or net assets are/have been (in the past two years) at least <strong>GBP5 million</strong>; OR</li>\n<li>Meets 2 of: balance sheet total <strong>EUR12.5 million</strong>, net turnover <strong>EUR25 million</strong>, average <strong>250 employees</strong> in the year.</li>\n</ul></li>\n<li>Partnership/unincorporated association: net assets at least GBP5 million (past 2 years) - for limited partnerships, calculated without deducting loans owed to partners.</li>\n<li>Trustee of a trust (other than pension schemes below): assets of at least <strong>GBP10 million</strong> (past 2 years).</li>\n<li>Trustee of occupational pension scheme/SSAS, or trustee/operator of personal/stakeholder pension: scheme has (or has had) at least <strong>50 members</strong> and assets under management of at least <strong>GBP10 million</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Eligible counterparties (ECPs)</h4>\n<p>Narrower than per se professional clients (excludes large undertakings): credit institutions, investment firms, other authorised/regulated financial institutions (EEA law or national law), insurance companies, UCITS CIS/management companies, pension funds/management companies, national governments/debt management offices, supranational organisations, central banks, and undertakings exempt from MiFID under Article 2(1)(k) or 2(1)(l).</p>\n<p><strong>ECP business</strong> = dealing on own account, execution of orders, or reception/transmission of orders (plus directly related ancillary services, and non-MiFID arranging) carried on <strong>with or for an ECP</strong>. If an ECP wants investment management or advice, it must be treated as a per se professional client for that business.</p>\n<h4>3.2 Agents</h4>\n<p>If a firm knows A is acting as agent for B, it should treat A as its client - unless (a) it has agreed in writing with A to treat B as the client, or (b) A's involvement is mainly to reduce the firm's duties to B, in which case B must be treated as the client regardless.</p>\n<h4>3.3 Recategorising clients - lower protection</h4>\n<p><strong>Elective professional client</strong>: retail client may be reclassified if:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Qualitative test</strong>: firm assesses expertise, experience and knowledge and believes the client can make own investment decisions and understands the risks; AND (for MiFID business)</li>\n<li><strong>Quantitative test</strong> - any 2 of 3:\n<ul>\n<li>Average of <strong>10 significantly sized transactions per quarter</strong> over the last <strong>4 quarters</strong> on the relevant market.</li>\n<li>Financial instrument portfolio (cash deposits + financial instruments) exceeds <strong>EUR500,000</strong>.</li>\n<li>Works/has worked in the financial sector for at least <strong>1 year</strong> in a professional position requiring knowledge of the transactions envisaged.</li>\n</ul></li>\n</ul>\n<p>Process: client requests reclassification in writing (specifying products/services); firm gives a clear written warning of protections lost; client separately confirms in writing they are aware of the consequences.</p>\n<p><strong>Local authorities/municipalities</strong> (MiFID II) are retail clients by default. To become elective professional: qualitative test (COBS 3.5.3(1)) plus quantitative test (COBS 3.5.3B(2)): portfolio exceeds <strong>GBP10 million</strong>, AND either (a) 10 significant transactions/quarter average over 4 quarters, (b) the authorised person has 1+ year's relevant professional experience, or (c) it is an administering authority of the Local Government Pension Scheme acting in that capacity.</p>\n<p><strong>Elective ECP</strong>: a professional client that is an undertaking, is a per se professional client (not merely because it's an institutional investor), and requests ECP treatment.</p>\n<h4>3.3.1 Recategorising clients - higher protection</h4>\n<p>Firms must allow professional clients/ECPs to request recategorisation to a higher-protection category, and may also do this on their own initiative. Recategorisation can be general or transaction-specific; a client can (in theory) be classified differently per instrument type, though most firms classify once for simplicity.</p>\n<p><strong>Protections lost on becoming elective professional (from retail)</strong>: fair/clear/not misleading communications and financial promotions; firm/services/remuneration info; suitability; appropriateness; dealing/carrying out orders; client reporting; remuneration/incentivisation rules; investor compensation (FOS/FSCS); exclusion of liability; share trading obligation; collateral transfer rules; some client money limitations.</p>\n<p><strong>Protections lost on becoming elective ECP (from professional)</strong>: client agreement; appropriateness; best execution; order handling; product governance; disclosure on instruments/costs/charges/remuneration/commissions; package products rules.</p>\n<h4>3.4 Notifications of client classification</h4>\n<p>New clients must be told how they have been classified, and (before services are provided) of their right to request recategorisation and any resulting limits on protection.</p>\n<h4>3.5 Policies, procedures and records</h4>\n<p>Firms need written internal policies/procedures for categorisation, and must retain records of each notice/agreement for the relevant period after ceasing business with the client.</p>\n<h4>3.6 Reliance on others</h4>\n<p>A UK MiFID firm receiving an instruction via another UK MiFID firm (or equivalent) can rely on client information and recommendations relayed to it - the third-party firm remains responsible for the accuracy/completeness of information and appropriateness of recommendations; the receiving firm is responsible for concluding the service/transaction. Reliance information should be given in writing. For non-MiFID business, it is generally reasonable to rely on written information from an unconnected authorised person/professional firm unless there are grounds to doubt its accuracy.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.4 Client Agreements and Information Provision","html":"\n<h4>4.1 Requirement to provide a client agreement</h4>\n<p>Applies to: designated investment business for a retail client; MiFID business and ancillary services (or equivalent third-country business) for a retail and/or professional client. <strong>Does not apply</strong> to insurance firms issuing life policies as principal.</p>\n<p>Must be a written basic agreement on paper or other <strong>durable medium</strong>, setting out essential rights/obligations. Must be provided, in good time before the client is bound (or before providing services, whichever earlier), together with information about the firm/services (COBS 6.1.4, COBS 6.1ZA.2) - authorised status, conflicts policy, etc. Exception: if the client requested distance communication and the firm follows the voice telephone rules, the agreement/information may be given immediately after the client is bound.</p>\n<p>Material changes to terms must be notified in good time before they take effect, in a durable medium.</p>\n<p><strong>Record retention</strong> - client agreement records kept for the longer of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>5 years</strong>;</li>\n<li>the duration of the relationship with the client; or</li>\n<li><strong>indefinitely</strong> for pension transfers, pension opt-outs, or free-standing additional voluntary contributions (FSAVCs).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>4.2 Information requirements</h4>\n<p>COBS 2.2/2.2A: firms doing MiFID (or equivalent third-country) business must give retail and professional clients comprehensible information about the firm/services, designated investments/strategies (with risk warnings), execution venues, and costs/charges. Non-MiFID firms dealing with retail clients in derivatives, warrants or stock lending must also provide this.</p>\n<p><strong>Information about the firm</strong> (to retail, professional clients and ECPs where relevant): name/address/contact details; (MiFID business) languages of communication; communication methods including for orders; statement of authorisation and regulator's details; statement if acting via an AR/tied agent; nature/frequency/timing of performance reports (COBS 16); summary of the firm's conflicts of interest policy.</p>\n<p><strong>Managing investments</strong> - must establish an appropriate evaluation method (eg a benchmark). For retail clients, must also disclose (where applicable): valuation method/frequency; any delegation of discretionary management; specified benchmark; permitted investment/transaction types and limits; risk level reflected in the manager's discretion and any constraints.</p>\n<p><strong>Safeguarding investments/money</strong> - firms holding client money/investments (subject to MiFID custody/client money rules) must disclose: that a third party may hold assets/money; firm's responsibility for that third party's acts/omissions; consequences of third-party insolvency; risk if assets can't be separately designated overseas; that assets are subject to non-UK law and what this means for client rights; summary of protective steps taken (including compensation/deposit guarantee scheme details); details of any security interest/lien/set-off; and full information (durable medium, good time before) on the firm's obligations/risks.</p>\n<h4>4.3 Disclosure of costs</h4>\n<p>Firms must disclose to retail clients: total price (all fees, costs, charges, expenses, taxes via the firm), or basis of calculation if not known upfront; costs charged by the firm itemised separately; currency/conversion info if paid in foreign currency; statement that further costs/taxes not paid via the firm may apply, plus how paid/levied and compensation scheme info. ECPs/professionals can request cost details as a commercial (not regulatory) matter.</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.1 Timing/medium</strong>: good time before service provision, unless distance communication at client's request prevented this and voice telephone rules are complied with (then immediately after). Durable medium or firm's website (if website conditions met).</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.2 Keeping clients up to date</strong>: notify material changes in good time, in a durable medium if originally so provided.</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.3 Existing clients</strong>: repeat transactions in the same instrument type need not be treated as a new service each time, but the client must still receive relevant updated info (eg changed charges).</p>\n<p><strong>4.3.4 Compensation information</strong>: MiFID business firms must make available info identifying the relevant compensation scheme, amount/scope of cover; on request, conditions/formalities for claiming. Durable medium or compliant website.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.5 Communicating with Clients, including Financial Promotions","html":"\n<h4>5.1 Application of the rules</h4>\n<p>The 12 Principles for Businesses apply to regulated activities, dealing as principal, ancillary designated investment activities, home finance, insurance mediation, accepting deposits, and communication/approval of financial promotions. Firms subject to the <strong>Consumer Duty</strong> apply Principle 12 (plus cross-cutting rules/outcomes) instead of Principles 6 and 7; firms outside Consumer Duty scope remain subject to <strong>FTOC</strong> (Fair Treatment of Customers).</p>\n<p>MiFID (2007) introduced the general obligation that firms \"act honestly, fairly and professionally, and in accordance with the best interests of the client\" - implemented via COBS.</p>\n<p>It is an <strong>FSMA offence</strong> to communicate a financial promotion unless the communicator is an authorised firm, or the promotion has been approved by an authorised firm. Exemptions from COBS 4.1.1 include: qualifying credit/home purchase/home reversion plans; non-investment insurance contracts; promotion of an unregulated CIS (never permitted to communicate/approve); credit agreements/consumer hire/credit-related regulated activities; authorised professional firms under COBS 18. These financial promotion rules generally do not apply where the client is an ECP.</p>\n<p><strong>FCA power</strong>: can direct removal/banning of a promotion without going through full enforcement; firm can make representations; FCA decides to confirm/amend/revoke and (if confirmed) publishes the direction, the promotion and its reasons.</p>\n<h4>5.1.2 Fair, clear and not misleading</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Communications where capital is at risk must make this clear.</li>\n<li>Yield quotes must give a balanced short/medium/long-term impression.</li>\n<li>Complex products/charges/multiple remuneration elements communicated fairly and clearly, tailored to the audience's information needs.</li>\n<li>If FCA is named as regulator but some business is unregulated, this must be made clear.</li>\n<li>Third-party packaged/stakeholder products: give a fair impression of the producer/manager.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>5.1.3 Identifying promotions</h4>\n<p>Must be clearly identifiable as financial promotions (exceptions for professional clients/ECPs). Methods: real-time (face-to-face, phone, presentations); unsolicited real-time (\"cold calls\"); non-real-time (emails, letters, websites); direct offer promotions (invite a response).</p>\n<h4>5.1.4 Sustainability Disclosure Requirements / anti-greenwashing (2024)</h4>\n<p>Applies to all FCA-regulated firms making sustainability claims to retail or professional clients (regardless of where the communication originated, if distributed by an FCA firm). Claims must be correct/substantiated, clear, complete (no cherry-picking/omission), and support fair comparisons. Complements (does not override) existing PRIN/COBS/Consumer Credit fair-communication rules and the Consumer Duty.</p>\n<h4>5.2 Exceptions to the financial promotion rules</h4>\n<p>Disapplied for \"excluded communications\": aimed at high-net-worth individuals (FPO Article 48) and sophisticated investors (Articles 50/50A); exempt under the Financial Promotion Order (FPO) (unauthorised communicator, or originates outside UK with no UK effect); from outside the UK and would be FPO-exempt if a separate unauthorised person; subject to/exempt from the Takeover Code; one-off promotions that are not cold calls. <strong>Does not generally apply to UK MiFID business.</strong></p>\n<p>Nov 2023 HM Treasury changes (effective 2024): businesses must give their own details in exemption-based communications; \"certified\" removed from the high-net-worth individual exemption title; updated HNWI and self-certified sophisticated investor statements; changes extended to CIS-equivalent exemptions. Old investor statements are no longer valid.</p>\n<h4>5.3 Prospectus advertisements</h4>\n<p>COBS 4 does not apply where the Admissions to Trading on a Regulated Market (PRM) prospectus rules apply (PRM 12.1). A prospectus advertisement must: state a prospectus has been/will be published and where to obtain it; be clearly recognisable as an advertisement; be accurate/not misleading; be consistent with the prospectus; and (if written) carry a bold, prominent statement that it is not a prospectus.</p>\n<p><strong>Social media/influencers</strong>: FCA guidance requires balance and correct risk warnings; firms are responsible for all their promotions including via influencers; unapproved promotion by an influencer may be a criminal offence. FCA removed over 10,000 misleading adverts in one year (up from around 8,500 the year before).</p>\n<h4>5.4 Approval of financial promotions</h4>\n<p>SYSC 3/SYSC 4 require systems/controls for communicating or approving promotions. Since <strong>2023</strong>, a \"gateway\" permission from the FCA is needed before a firm may approve financial promotions for unauthorised third parties (firms without this permission may only approve their own promotions).</p>\n<p>Before approving, the firm must confirm compliance; if it later becomes non-compliant, approval must be withdrawn and known relying parties notified as soon as reasonably practicable. Firms may <strong>not</strong> approve real-time promotions (personal visits, phone calls, interactive dialogue). Firms cannot approve unregulated CIS promotions unless they could legitimately promote it themselves. Approval limited to disapplied-rule circumstances must state that limitation; communicating outside it is an offence.</p>\n<p><strong>5.4.2 Relying on another's approval</strong> (non-MiFID business): not a breach if the firm takes reasonable care to establish another authorised firm confirmed compliance, communicates only to the intended recipient type, and (as far as it should know) it remains fair/clear/not misleading and has not been withdrawn.</p>\n<h4>5.5 Communicating with retail clients</h4>\n<p>Rules: firm name included; accurate, balanced risk/benefit presentation; understandable to the average target recipient; no disguising/diminishing key warnings. Comparisons must be meaningful/fair/balanced (MiFID: cite data sources, key facts/assumptions). Tax treatment: explain it depends on individual circumstances and may change. Disapplied for third-party prospectuses, image advertising, and (non-MiFID) excluded communications.</p>\n<p><strong>High-risk investment promotion rules (2020, updated 2023)</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>NMMIs</strong> (Non-Mass Market Investments) - banned to retail investors.</li>\n<li><strong>RMMIs</strong> (Restricted Mass Market Investments) - restricted to retail (eg no non-readily realisable securities, no peer-to-peer agreements).</li>\n<li>Risk warnings: could lose all money invested; prescribed risk summaries taking no more than <strong>2 minutes</strong> to read (firms may diverge for valid reasons).</li>\n<li>Ban on monetary/non-monetary incentives to invest (eg refer-a-friend bonuses).</li>\n<li>Minimum <strong>24-hour cooling-off period</strong> for first-time investors with a firm.</li>\n<li>Personalised risk warnings including the client's name.</li>\n<li>Retail clients must self-certify (with evidence) they meet criteria to receive high-risk promotions.</li>\n<li>Appropriateness test made more robust; firms cannot coach investors to pass it.</li>\n<li>Additional record-keeping on categorisation/appropriateness metrics.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>2023 guidance: approver's name and approval date must be shown; approvers need appropriate competence/expertise; must actively monitor ongoing compliance and appropriateness; enhanced conflicts requirements; must undertake preliminary suitability assessments before NMMIs are promoted to retail investors.</p>\n<h4>5.5.1 Cryptoasset financial promotions (final rules Sept 2023)</h4>\n<p>A \"qualifying cryptoasset\" is a cryptographically secured, transferable, fungible digital representation of value/rights (excludes e-money and existing controlled investments). Four legal routes to promote: (1) communicated by an authorised person; (2) made by an unauthorised person but approved by an authorised person (via gateway); (3) communicated by/for a cryptoasset business registered under the MLRs relying on the bespoke FPO exemption; (4) otherwise compliant with an FPO exemption. Cryptoassets classified as RMMIs: risk warnings, ban on incentives, positive frictions (24-hour cooling off, direct offer promotion rules, client categorisation - self-certified sophisticated investor category NOT available), appropriateness assessments.</p>\n<h4>5.6 Past, simulated past and future performance</h4>\n<p><strong>Past performance</strong>: not the most prominent feature; covers at least the preceding <strong>5 years</strong> (or whole life of investment/index/service if shorter), shown in complete 12-month periods; reference periods/sources shown; prominent warning that past performance isn't a reliable indicator of future results; currency stated with fluctuation warning if not sterling; gross figures must disclose effect of commissions/fees/tax/charges.</p>\n<p><strong>Simulated past performance</strong>: relates to an investment/index; based on actual past performance of the same/underlying investment(s)/indices; otherwise meets the past performance rules; prominent warning it is simulated and not a reliable indicator.</p>\n<p><strong>Future performance</strong>: not based on/referring to simulated past performance; based on reasonable assumptions supported by objective data; gross figures disclose charges; prominent warning that forecasts are not reliable indicators.</p>\n<h4>5.7 Direct offers or invitations</h4>\n<p>Must contain prescribed information: about the firm/services; management of investments (if relevant); safekeeping of investments/money; costs and charges; nature/risks of investments (incl. tax); statement that a prospectus has been published (Prospectus Regulation); description of combined-investment risk where relevant; sufficient detail on third-party guarantees. Can be omitted if the client can refer to another document. Must include a statement recommending the client seek personal advice if unsure of suitability.</p>\n<h4>5.8 Unwritten promotions and cold calling</h4>\n<p><strong>Unwritten promotions</strong> outside a firm's premises: appropriate time of day; identify self/firm and reason for contact at the outset; clarify if client wants to continue/terminate (and comply); give a contact point if an appointment is arranged.</p>\n<p><strong>Cold calling</strong> permitted only where: an existing client relationship exists and the call is expected; it relates to a generally marketable packaged product (not higher-volatility fund or linked life policy); or it relates to a controlled activity for limited investment types (deposits, readily realisable investments, excluding warrants/geared packaged products).</p>\n<p><strong>Overseas persons</strong>: firms may not approve/communicate overseas promotions unless the promotion states who approved/communicated it and explains (where relevant) that investor protection rules won't apply, the extent of UK compensation scheme cover (or its absence), and any overseas compensation/deposit protection details. The firm must have no doubt the overseas firm will deal honestly/reliably with UK retail clients.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.6 Retail Clients (Consumer Duty and FTOC)","html":"\n<p>This section cross-references Chapter 1, Section 2 for the detailed content on the <strong>Consumer Duty</strong> (four outcomes: products and services; price and value; consumer understanding; consumer support) and the <strong>Fair Treatment of Customers (FTOC)</strong> initiative, which applies to firms outside the scope of the Consumer Duty.</p>\n<p>Key exam point: firms subject to Consumer Duty apply Principle 12 as the overarching standard (replacing Principles 6 and 7 for in-scope activities); firms not captured by Consumer Duty (no retail customers, out of scope, or exempt) remain subject to FTOC obligations and Principles 6/7.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.7 Advising and Selling Standards (Suitability and Appropriateness)","html":"\n<h4>7.1 Application of suitability rules</h4>\n<p>Apply when firms: make personal recommendations on designated investments (buy/hold/sell advice); manage investments for a retail client; manage assets of an occupational, stakeholder or personal pension scheme (outside MiFID/equivalent/optional exemption business). Basic advice rules may be applied instead for stakeholder products. Non-MiFID business: rules apply only to retail clients or pension scheme asset management (as above).</p>\n<p>Firms must obtain information on: knowledge and experience relevant to the specific investment type; financial situation, including ability to bear losses; investment objectives and current risk profile. Under UK MiFID, suitability extends to <em>all</em> decisions to trade, not just buy recommendations, including holding. Portfolio managers must assess suitability before a recommendation that requires altering the client mandate, and must keep client information up to date on an ongoing basis.</p>\n<p>FCA expectations on incentive schemes: check for spikes/trends in individual adviser sales patterns; monitor face-to-face sales conversations; manage risks in discretionary incentive schemes/balanced scorecards; monitor non-advised sales to prevent unauthorised personal recommendations; oversee AR incentive schemes; recognise that near-100% variable, sales-based pay raises misselling risk.</p>\n<h4>7.2 Information required for a suitability assessment</h4>\n<p>MiFID II requires firms giving investment advice or portfolio management to gather info on: knowledge/experience (types of service/transaction/investment familiar with; nature, volume, frequency, period of involvement; education/profession); financial standing (ability to bear losses); investment objectives/risk appetite. Firm must have a reasonable basis to believe the transaction meets objectives, risk is bearable, and risk is understood. Firms must not discourage clients from providing information, but can rely on it unless manifestly out of date/inaccurate/incomplete. <strong>If sufficient information cannot be obtained, the firm must not make a personal recommendation or take a trading decision for the client.</strong></p>\n<h4>7.3 Assessing suitability - professional clients</h4>\n<p>A firm may <strong>assume</strong> a professional client (per se and elective, for UK MiFID business) has the necessary knowledge/experience. For <strong>per se</strong> professional clients only, the firm may also assume the client can financially bear the related investment risks consistent with objectives. This assumption <strong>cannot</strong> be made for elective professional clients regarding financial ability to bear risk.</p>\n<h4>7.4 Churning and switching</h4>\n<p><strong>Churning</strong> = over-dealing/trading more frequently than necessary to generate fees/commission (relevant especially to discretionary portfolios). <strong>Switching</strong> = selling one investment and replacing it with another. Suitability guidance: a series of transactions each suitable in isolation may not be suitable overall if made so frequently as to be detrimental to the client (frequency vs the client's investment strategy).</p>\n<h4>7.5 Suitability report</h4>\n<p>Required whenever a firm makes a personal recommendation, covering (among others): equities, derivatives, structured products, unregulated CIS, acquiring/selling holdings in a regulated CIS/investment trust (via savings scheme or ISA promoted for that trust), pension contract actions (buy/sell/surrender/convert/cancel/suspend contributions), income withdrawal/short-term annuity elections, pension transfers/opt-outs, and life assurance recommendations.</p>\n<p><strong>Exceptions</strong> (no suitability report needed): investment manager's personal recommendation on a regulated CIS for a retail client; client habitually resident outside UK/not present in UK when consenting; friendly society recommendation on a small life policy with premium not exceeding <strong>GBP50/year</strong> (or <strong>GBP1/week</strong>); recommendation to increase a regular premium on an existing contract; additional single premium/contribution to an existing packaged product.</p>\n<p><strong>Timing</strong>: life policy - before contract concluded (unless info given orally or immediate cover needed, then durable medium immediately after conclusion); personal/stakeholder pension where cancellation rules apply - within <strong>14 days</strong> of concluding the contract; any other case - before the transaction is concluded (unless a distance marketing communication is used and client consents, then as soon as possible after execution).</p>\n<p>Content: client's demands and needs; possible disadvantages of the transaction; why the firm concluded the transaction is suitable, having regard to client-provided information. Telephone sale of a life policy (only contact by phone): must comply with the Distance Marketing Directive, be provided immediately after conclusion, in a durable medium.</p>\n<h4>7.6 Insistent clients</h4>\n<p>An <strong>insistent client</strong> is one where: the firm gave a personal recommendation; the client decides not to follow it (or wants something different); and the client wants the firm to facilitate the (unrecommended) transaction anyway. The firm must clearly, fairly communicate: that it did not recommend the transaction and it is not in line with its recommendation; the reasons the transaction is not in line with its recommendation; the risks of the client's proposed transaction; the reasons the firm did not recommend it.</p>\n<h4>7.7 Appropriateness (non-advised sales)</h4>\n<p>Applies to: MiFID investment services other than personal recommendations/managing investments; arranging/dealing in warrants and derivatives for retail clients responding to a direct offer promotion; firms assessing appropriateness for other UK MiFID firms.</p>\n<p>Firm must ask about the client's knowledge/experience relevant to the specific product/service type, to assess whether it is appropriate. May assume a professional client has necessary knowledge/experience for products/services within their professional classification. Firm can rely on client-supplied info unless known to be out of date/inaccurate/incomplete, and may infer knowledge from experience.</p>\n<p>If the product/service seems <strong>not appropriate</strong>, the firm must warn the client (can be standardised). If the client declines to give information, firm must warn it cannot assess appropriateness (also can be standardised); if the client insists on proceeding, it's the firm's judgement whether to proceed.</p>\n<h4>7.7.3 When appropriateness assessment is unnecessary</h4>\n<p>Not required if: the service is <strong>execution-only</strong> or reception/transmission of orders, for particular (\"non-complex\") financial instruments, at the client's initiative, and the client has been clearly told the firm won't assess appropriateness (losing that protection); AND the firm complies with its conflicts of interest obligations (Principle 8).</p>\n<p><strong>Non-complex financial instruments</strong> include: shares listed on a UK/EEA regulated market (or equivalent third-country market); money market instruments, bonds, other securitised debt without embedded derivatives; UCITS fund holdings; other instruments meeting the \"non-complex\" definition (not a derivative; sufficiently liquid; client liability doesn't exceed acquisition cost; publicly available comprehensive information exists) - see COBS 10A.4.1.</p>\n<p>No need to reassess for a series of similar transactions, but must reassess before a new type of service. Clients dealing before MiFID (2007) could be assumed to have necessary experience/knowledge (but not necessarily meet other criteria).</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.8 Product Disclosure and the Right to Cancel","html":"\n<h4>8.1 Purpose and nature of the rules</h4>\n<p>Ensures clients get sufficient/relevant information to decide whether to invest, whether recommended or self-selected.</p>\n<h4>8.1.1 KIIDs</h4>\n<p>Authorised fund managers (AFMs) of UCITS schemes must produce a <strong>Key Investor Information Document (KIID)</strong> - fair, clear, not misleading, consistent with the prospectus. Must cover: identification of the scheme; short description of objectives/policy; past performance or performance scenarios; costs and charges; risk and reward profile with guidance/warnings.</p>\n<h4>8.1.2 Key Features Documents (KFDs)</h4>\n<p>Required, in good time, for: each packaged product; cash deposit ISA; cash deposit CTF; a key features illustration for each packaged product produced. Also, nature/risk information on designated investments for MiFID business and for retail clients in certain other business.</p>\n<h4>8.1.3 PRIIPs and KIDs</h4>\n<p>PRIIPs Regulation requires a <strong>Key Information Document (KID)</strong> for packaged retail and insurance-based investment products - covers CIS and other packaged bank/insurer products, aiming to help investors compare features, risks, rewards and costs. UCITS KIID/KFD get a 5-year exemption from PRIIPs, extended to expire <strong>31 December 2026</strong>.</p>\n<p>Four PRIIP categories: investment funds; insurance-based investment products; retail-structured securities; structured term deposits.</p>\n<p>A KID is a stand-alone, standardised document, <strong>maximum 3 A4 pages</strong>, prepared by manufacturers (or published on their website); anyone advising on/selling a PRIIP to a retail investor must provide the KID in good time before the transaction. Applies to retail investment providers, life companies, discretionary managers, insurance-based investment firms, fund managers, stockbrokers/advisers on funds/structured products/derivatives, financial advisers, and platform operators.</p>\n<p>KID sections (fixed sequence): What is this product? / What are the risks and what could I get in return? / What happens if [manufacturer] is unable to pay out? / What are the costs? / How long should I hold it and can I take money out early? / How can I complain? / Other relevant information.</p>\n<h4>8.2 Cancellation and right to withdraw</h4>\n<p>Applies to: firms providing retail financial products based on designated investments/deposits; distance contracts with consumers for deposits/designated investments.</p>\n<table><tr><th>Product</th><th>Cancellation period</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Life and pension products</td><td><strong>30 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash deposit ISAs</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life/pension products (advised, non-distance)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life/pension products (distance contracts)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Example: an advised purchase of an AUT or OEIC has a 14-day cancellation right. The period starts from contract conclusion, or from receipt of contractual information if later.</p>\n<p>Firms must tell consumers (durable medium, good time before, or immediately after if not possible before) of: existence and duration of the right; conditions for exercising it (incl. any amount payable); consequences of not exercising it; practical details of exercising it (incl. address for notification). Not required if the consumer already has this information from elsewhere under COBS distance marketing/product information rules.</p>\n<p>Notification of cancellation is deemed observed if dispatched before the deadline; no reason needs to be given. Cancelling doesn't guarantee return of the full amount invested (eg CIS proceeds reflect price movement of underlying holdings).</p>\n<p><strong>Record-keeping</strong>: indefinitely for pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs; at least 5 years for life policy/pension contract/personal pension/stakeholder pension; at least 3 years in any other case.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.9 Conflicts of Interest","html":"\n<h4>9.1 Principles and rules</h4>\n<p>Principle 8: \"A firm must manage conflicts of interest fairly, both between itself and its customers and between a customer and another client.\" Detailed rules are in SYSC, applying to common platform firms (MiFID business/ancillary services) and non-MiFID firms/business. Firms must take all appropriate steps to identify, prevent and/or manage conflicts between: the firm (incl. managers, employees, ARs/tied agents, connected parties) and a client; and one client and another.</p>\n<p>Conflict types: realising a gain/avoiding a loss at a client's expense; having an interest in an outcome distinct from the client's; incentive to favour one client over others; carrying on the same business as the client; receiving inducements beyond standard commission/fee.</p>\n<p>Firms must: maintain effective organisational/administrative arrangements to prevent conflicts harming clients; for externally-facing research, have information systems/controls and barriers preventing research information flowing to the rest of the firm.</p>\n<p>If a conflict cannot be managed, it must be <strong>disclosed</strong> to the client before undertaking business - letting the client decide whether to continue. Disclosure must be in a durable medium, with sufficient detail for an informed decision. <strong>Disclosure alone is not sufficient compliance</strong> - the firm's arrangements should be designed to prevent conflicts arising in the first place. Firms must: prepare/maintain/implement an effective conflicts policy; give retail (and potential retail) clients a description of the policy; keep records of activities where a conflict has arisen.</p>\n<h4>Information barriers (\"Chinese walls\")</h4>\n<p>Arrangements whereby information held in one part of the business is withheld from (or not used by) people in another part. Firm must: withhold/not use the information; permit staff in one part to withhold it from staff in another, but only where at least one part carries on regulated activity (or a connected activity). Firms must take reasonable steps to keep such arrangements effective and adequately monitored.</p>\n<p><strong>Example</strong>: corporate finance (one side of the wall) may have price-sensitive plans for a company; the equity research/market-making arm (other side) has no knowledge of these until public/acceptable to release. Not passing this on is not a breach of duty to clients.</p>\n<h4>9.2 Disclosing conflicts</h4>\n<p>Disclosure is required only where arrangements are <strong>not sufficient</strong> to prevent, with reasonable confidence, the risk of damage to the client's interests - ie, disclosure is a <strong>last resort</strong>. Must be in a durable medium with sufficient detail for an informed decision. Over-reliance on disclosure without adequate management of conflicts is not permitted (SYSC 10.1.8 vs SYSC 10.1.3).</p>\n<h4>9.3 Conflicts of interest policies</h4>\n<p>Firms need a written policy appropriate to the scale/nature of the business (and, for group members, considering group-wide conflicts). Policy must: identify circumstances giving rise to conflicts (especially where one person performs 2+ roles, eg research/advice, proprietary trading, portfolio management, corporate finance); and set out processes/procedures covering: control of information flow; separate supervision of conflicting-interest staff; removing direct remuneration links to conflict-prone activities; measures against inappropriate influence; measures controlling involvement in multiple conflicting activities. Corporate finance firms managing securities offerings need specific detail on allocation and pricing arrangements.</p>\n<h4>9.4 Investment research and research recommendations</h4>\n<p><strong>Investment research</strong>: information recommending/suggesting an investment strategy (with opinion on value/price), intended for public distribution, clearly labelled as research, presented as objective/independent, and must not constitute a personal recommendation. <strong>Investment recommendations</strong> (captured under UK MAR) are short-term trade ideas/strategies for one or more clients - a distinct concept.</p>\n<h4>9.4.1 Measures and arrangements (dealing ahead)</h4>\n<p>Firms producing research must, per SYSC 10.1.11R, prevent: financial analysts/relevant persons with knowledge of unpublished research from personally trading (or trading for others) ahead of publication (exceptions: eg execution-only client instructions, good-faith market maker activity); personal account transactions in the relevant investments without prior compliance/legal approval (exceptional circumstances only); accepting inducements from those with a material interest in the research subject matter; promising favourable coverage to issuers; anyone other than financial analysts reviewing draft research containing a recommendation/target price (except for compliance verification).</p>\n<p>Exemption if disseminating another party's research: that person is not in the same group; the firm doesn't substantially amend it; doesn't hold itself out as producer; verifies the producer is subject to conflicts rules (or has an equivalent policy).</p>\n<p><strong>Non-independent research</strong> is treated as a marketing communication (COBS 4 applies): must be clearly identified as a marketing communication and carry a clear, prominent statement that it wasn't prepared per independence requirements and isn't subject to dealing-ahead prohibitions.</p>\n<h4>Research recommendation standards</h4>\n<p>Firms preparing/disseminating research recommendations must: present fairly; disclose interests/conflicts; give firm name and regulator; give preparer's identity/job title; distinguish facts from interpretation/estimates/opinion; indicate reliability of sources; clearly label and explain assumptions behind projections/forecasts/price targets; be able to substantiate the recommendation to the FCA on request.</p>\n<p><strong>Fair presentation</strong> requires: material sources indicated (incl. issuer, and whether disclosed/amended pre-dissemination); valuation basis summarised; meaning of buy/sell/hold explained with time horizons, risk warnings, sensitivity analysis; planned update frequency/coverage changes referenced; date of first release and price dates given; substantive changes vs the last 12 months' recommendation clearly flagged.</p>\n<p><strong>Disclosure of interests/conflicts</strong>: relationships that may impair objectivity (incl. issuer relationships); relationships of persons involved in preparation (incl. remuneration links to investment banking deals); accessible interests prior to dissemination; shareholdings/financial interests over <strong>5%</strong> in the issuer; market maker/lead manager/investment banking service statements; conflict-prevention arrangements (incl. information barriers); price/date of acquisition if a preparer acquired shares.</p>\n<p><strong>Quarterly disclosure</strong>: proportion of recommendations that are buy/hold/sell; proportion of issuers (per category) that received material investment banking services in the prior 12 months.</p>\n<p>Disseminating third-party recommendations: state firm identity/regulator; ensure fair/clear/not misleading summaries; identify source and where third-party disclosures are accessible; make full disclosures as if it had produced the research itself. Materially amending a third-party recommendation: describe alterations (esp. direction changes, eg buy to sell) or comply with disclosure rules as though self-produced; maintain a written policy directing recipients to the original producer/recommendation/disclosures.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.10 Inducements and Payment for Research","html":"\n<h4>10.1 Inducements</h4>\n<p>The rules are essentially a <strong>payment prohibition</strong> unless expressly permitted. Apply to firms doing MiFID and non-MiFID designated investment business, but only regarding <strong>professional and retail clients</strong> - not ECP business.</p>\n<p><strong>Minor non-monetary benefits</strong> (permitted alongside independent advice, restricted advice, portfolio management to retail clients) must be: disclosed prior to the service; capable of enhancing service quality; of a scale/nature that could not impair the duty to act honestly, fairly, professionally in the client's best interests; reasonable, proportionate, unlikely to influence behaviour detrimentally; and consist of: generic/personalised product-related information/documentation; third-party written material commissioned/paid for by a corporate issuer to promote a new issuance; conference/seminar/training participation on a specific instrument/service; reasonable de minimis hospitality (food/drink at a meeting/conference/seminar).</p>\n<p>For MiFID business, firms cannot pay/accept fees, commissions or non-monetary benefits except: those paid to/by the client (or someone on their behalf, eg management fees); proper fees necessary for the service (custody, legal, settlement) that cannot give rise to conflicts; those paid to/by a third party, permissible only if they don't impair the client's-best-interests duty, are designed to enhance service quality, and are disclosed to a set standard before the service.</p>\n<p>Disclosure can be satisfied by: disclosing essential arrangements in summary form; undertaking to give further detail on request; and actually doing so on request. Full records of third-party payments must be kept for UK MiFID business. For non-MiFID firms/business, inducement rules aren't fully implemented: for third-party payments, only the \"does not impair\" test applies (not disclosure/enhancement tests). Retail non-MiFID packaged product sales are subject to inducement disclosure requirements; reasonable non-monetary benefits need not be disclosed in detail for non-MiFID business.</p>\n<h4>10.2 Inducements and research</h4>\n<p>UK regulated firms producing research must apply a <strong>separately identifiable charge</strong> when providing it to an entity not entitled to receive it free (ie a firm providing portfolio management, or independent/restricted advice to retail/professional clients). In-scope firms may only consume research if paid for via: (1) the firm's own resources; or (2) a <strong>research payment account (RPA)</strong> funded by a specific client charge.</p>\n<p>Doesn't apply to non-UK regulated research producers, or firms located outside the UK consuming research. The FCA \"gold-plated\" MiFID II by not applying the rules to AIFs/CISs whose core policy isn't investing in MiFID financial instruments (eg private equity, commercial property funds).</p>\n<h4>2022 rule changes (from 2021 FCA review)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>SME research exemption</strong>: research on companies with market capitalisation below <strong>GBP200 million</strong> is exempt from the inducement rules (UK managers need not pay, though may choose to). Reviewed <strong>3 years</strong> after implementation (<strong>1 March 2025</strong>).</li>\n<li><strong>FICC research exemption</strong>: fixed income, currency and commodity research based on underlying instruments, not an investment strategy primarily about FICC instruments; macro-economic research does <strong>not</strong> meet the FICC definition and must still be paid for.</li>\n<li><strong>Independent research providers exemption</strong>: research from providers not engaged in execution services and not part of a group including an execution/brokerage investment firm.</li>\n<li><strong>Openly available research</strong>: research openly available to firms and the public can be received free.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>10.3 Research Payment Account (RPA)</h4>\n<p>Funded only by a specific research charge agreed with clients, based on a research budget for third-party research. Must only pay for research, not other goods/services. NOT research (non-exhaustive): post-trade analytics; unanalysed price feeds/historical price data; seminar fees; corporate access services; order/execution management services; RPA administration.</p>\n<h4>10.4 Payment optionality (from August 2024)</h4>\n<p>UK buy-side firms (asset managers, pension schemes, CIS managers) may <strong>bundle</strong> payments for third-party research and execution services, provided relevant requirements around execution and research costs are met.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.11 Dealing and Managing","html":"\n<h4>11.1 Application</h4>\n<p>Covers: best execution (COBS 11.2, 11.2A, 11.2B, 11.2C); client order handling (COBS 11.3); client limit orders (COBS 11.4); record keeping (COBS 11.5A); personal account dealing (COBS 11.7); recording telephone/electronic communications (SYSC 10A). Applies generally to authorised firms, with variations by business nature/location; some provisions apply to non-MiFID firms as if rules. Personal account dealing rules apply to designated investment business of UK-established firms.</p>\n<h4>11.2 Best execution</h4>\n<p>COBS splits best execution into: <strong>COBS 11.2</strong> - AIFMs and residual CIS operators (must take <strong>all reasonable steps</strong>); <strong>COBS 11.2A</strong> - MiFID investment firms (must take <strong>all sufficient steps</strong> - a higher standard); <strong>COBS 11.2B</strong> - UCITS management companies. Applies where a firm owes contractual/agency obligations and acts on the client's behalf. <strong>Does not apply to ECP business.</strong></p>\n<p><strong>Execution factors</strong>: price, costs, speed, likelihood of execution and settlement, size, nature, or any other relevant consideration. Relative importance depends on: the client (incl. categorisation); the client order; the financial instruments involved; the execution venues available. Best execution is not just best price - other factors can take precedence depending on circumstances.</p>\n<p><strong>The four-fold test</strong> (does the firm owe a duty of best execution, eg in OTC transactions - assessed cumulatively):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did the investment firm initiate the transaction?</li>\n<li>Does the client only ask for a quote (not shop around)?</li>\n<li>Will the client be unable to identify relative price transparency levels in the market?</li>\n<li>Has the client confirmed reliance on the investment firm?</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Role of price</strong> (retail clients): total consideration = price of the instrument + execution-related costs (venue fees, clearing/settlement fees, third-party fees).</p>\n<p><strong>Competing execution venues</strong>: firm must weigh its own costs and venue costs; commissions must not unfairly discriminate between venues (no differential commission/spread unless it reflects real cost differences).</p>\n<h4>11.3 Order execution policies</h4>\n<p>Firms need a policy to obtain (and demonstrate) best possible results. Explicit client consent is <strong>not required</strong> for the policy itself - it's provided at onboarding/on the website; continued dealing implies acceptance; if a client disagrees, they simply stop transacting with the firm.</p>\n<p><strong>MiFID</strong>: firms must disclose the order execution policy before undertaking MiFID business and obtain <strong>prior consent</strong> (general agreement or per-transaction). Must review the policy/arrangements at least <strong>annually</strong>, or on material change.</p>\n<p><strong>Non-MiFID</strong>: policy must cover, per instrument class, the execution venues used and factors affecting venue choice, ensuring consistently best results. More detail required for retail clients. Consent can be implied by continued trading after MiFID Terms of Business are provided. Review whenever a material event occurs, but at least annually; clients must be notified of material changes (FCA doesn't define \"material change\").</p>\n<p>Firms must monitor effectiveness of arrangements/policies and correct deficiencies; demonstrate compliance to clients on request. Portfolio managers must act in clients' best interests when placing orders; firms receiving/transmitting orders must act in clients' best interests when passing to brokers (considering execution factors, unless specific instructions given). Portfolio managers/receivers/transmitters must maintain execution policies (no consent needed) identifying the entities used per instrument class.</p>\n<h4>11.4 Specific client instructions</h4>\n<p>If a client gives a specific instruction, the firm must follow it and is deemed to have satisfied best execution <strong>only for the instructed part/aspect</strong> of the order; other aspects remain subject to best execution. Firms must not induce clients to give instructions that the firm knows will likely prevent obtaining the best possible result.</p>\n<h4>11.5 Client order handling</h4>\n<p>Firms must ensure prompt, fair, expeditious execution of client orders relative to other orders/the firm's own trading interests (links to avoiding conflicts). Comparable orders should be executed in the order received. Firms should ensure: prompt/accurate recording and allocation; sequential/prompt execution of comparable orders unless impracticable or client interests dictate otherwise; retail clients told immediately of material execution difficulties; prompt/correct delivery of assets/money where the firm oversees settlement. Firms must not misuse client order information (eg front-running).</p>\n<h4>11.6 Aggregation and allocation</h4>\n<p>Firms may only aggregate a client's deal with its own, or aggregate multiple clients' deals, if: unlikely to disadvantage any aggregated client; the possible disadvantage is disclosed; and a precise allocation policy exists covering volume/price effects and partial allocations. Partial execution of an aggregated order must be allocated per the policy. Own-account orders aggregated with client orders must not be allocated to clients' detriment - client orders take priority unless the firm can show less favourable terms would otherwise have resulted (then proportional allocation is allowed). Procedures must prevent detrimental reallocation of own-account transactions.</p>\n<h4>11.7 Client limit orders</h4>\n<p>Unless instructed otherwise, a firm receiving a client limit order for shares on a regulated market that cannot be immediately executed under prevailing conditions must make it <strong>public immediately</strong> (unless over normal market size), via a regulated market/MTF order book, or other means ensuring easy execution.</p>\n<h4>11.8 Personal account dealing</h4>\n<p>Firms must prevent \"relevant persons\" (directors, partners, ARs, or other staff involved in regulated activities, incl. via outsourcing) from: (1) entering a personal transaction contrary to UK MAR, involving misuse/improper disclosure of confidential information, or conflicting with the firm's duty to a customer; (2) improperly advising/procuring another to enter such a transaction; (3) improperly disclosing information likely to lead another to take such action (outside normal employment duties).</p>\n<p>Relevant provisions: rules on financial analysts' personal account transactions; rules on misuse of pending client order information. Firms must record all notified personal transactions and any authorisation/prohibition given.</p>\n<p><strong>Exceptions</strong> (rules do not apply to): deals under a discretionary management service with no prior communication between manager and relevant person about the deal; deals in units/shares of UCITS/OEICs/unit trusts where the relevant person is not involved in fund management; personal transactions in life policies. Staff (other than directors/partners/ARs) are subject only to the extent involved in investment/portfolio management services. Restrictions don't apply separately to each transaction in an ongoing series under a prior unchanged instruction (unless the instruction changes, or is withdrawn while acquired investments are simultaneously disposed of).</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.12 Reporting of Transactions and Reporting to Clients","html":"\n<h4>12.1 Reportable transactions</h4>\n<p>Covers: instruments admitted/traded (or applied for admission) on a trading venue; instruments with an underlying traded on a trading venue; instruments with an underlying index/basket of instruments traded on a venue. Applies regardless of whether execution was on a UK, Gibraltar or EU trading venue, or off-venue. Must be reported <strong>as soon as possible, no later than close of the next business day</strong>. UK investment firms determine whether their traded instrument is reportable. \"UK investment firm\" = person whose regular occupation/business is providing investment services to third parties, or performing investment activities professionally.</p>\n<h4>12.2.1 UK MiFIR</h4>\n<p>Transaction reporting gives regulators post-trade information to monitor market abuse (incl. insider dealing) and general market surveillance. The FCA's <strong>Transaction Monitoring Unit (TMU)</strong> oversees compliance; reports go via <strong>Approved Reporting Mechanisms (ARMs)</strong>, eg UnaVista, TRAX. Reporting is on a <strong>trade date + 1</strong> basis.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade reporting</strong> feeds market depth/liquidity/transparency data (single-sided - only the reporting institution's side). <strong>Transaction reporting</strong> can be dual-sided (both counterparties in scope).</p>\n<p>MiFID II post-trade transparency covers equities <em>and</em> non-equity instruments admitted to Regulated Markets, MTFs, OTFs. Reporting deadlines (unless waivers/deferrals apply): <strong>1 minute</strong> for equity transactions; <strong>5 minutes</strong> for in-scope non-equity instruments.</p>\n<h4>12.2.2 UK EMIR</h4>\n<p>All derivatives contracts must be reported to a <strong>trade repository</strong>. UK counterparties report under UK EMIR; EU counterparties under EU EMIR - even if one party is non-financial. Both counterparties must generally report (exceptions for mandated financial-counterparty reporting exist); by prior arrangement, one party can report for both (must be indicated). <strong>Non-UK entities have no UK EMIR reporting obligation</strong> (incl. UK branches of third-country firms, eg a Canadian bank's London branch). Reporting may be delegated to a third party (CCP, trading venue, counterparty bank).</p>\n<h4>12.2.3 Short Selling Regulation (SSR)</h4>\n<p>Private/public notification regime for net short positions. FCA intervention powers (adverse market conditions): extend notification/disclosure scope; require lenders to notify fee changes; restrict short selling/similar transactions; restrict/limit sovereign CDS transactions. Firms must notify the FCA (Electronic Submission System) by <strong>3.30pm</strong> the trading day after the position was reached; calculations made as at midnight on the day the position was reached. Market makers/authorised primary dealers can be exempt.</p>\n<h4>12.2.4 UK SFTR</h4>\n<p>Reporting for securities financing transactions (SFTs), similar to EMIR derivatives reporting, plus limits on collateral reuse. Reporting can be dual-sided; made to a trade repository, which submits to the FCA. Non-financial counterparties (corporates) are exempted by HM Treasury from SFTR reporting.</p>\n<h4>12.3 Reporting channels / responsibility</h4>\n<p>LSE systems: SETS (UK equities) and IOB (depositary receipts) - trade reporting is <strong>automatic</strong> for order-book trades. <strong>Off-order-book transactions</strong> (eg telephone trades) must be reported by the member firm as close to real time as possible, and in any case <strong>within 3 minutes</strong> of execution during the trade reporting period.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade reporting period</strong>: <strong>7.15am to 5.15pm</strong> each business day. If executed 7.15am-8.00am: report before 8.00am or within 3 minutes if later. If executed in the last 3 minutes of the period: report before 5.15pm. If executed outside the period: report before the start of the next trade reporting period.</p>\n<p>Responsibility rests with the <strong>more senior party</strong> (market maker member firm reports); if equal seniority, the <strong>selling</strong> member firm reports. Report must include: reporting member/counterparty identity; date/time; purchase or sale; dealing capacity (agency/principal); security and quantity; price; settlement due date; special conditions (eg ex-dividend).</p>\n<p>Exchange publishes reports incl. the <strong>Daily Official List (DOL)</strong> - closing prices, used for portfolio valuation. CREST settlement transaction reports: deadline <strong>8.00pm</strong> on trade day. International equities: Omgeo (DTCC subsidiary) online confirmation, both participants report by <strong>9.00pm</strong> on trade day.</p>\n<p>Post-Brexit: UK branches of EEA firms report to both the FCA and their home regulator; EEA branches of UK firms report to both home regulator and FCA. UK branches of third-country firms need not report to the FCA transactions executed outside the UK (exceptions may apply if a UK-based individual was involved in execution/pricing/client engagement).</p>\n<h4>12.4.1 Confirmation of transactions and periodic statements</h4>\n<p>Firms (other than those managing investments) executing a client order must: give essential information promptly, in a durable medium (for derivatives/options transactions); provide order status information on request; send a confirmation notice <strong>as soon as possible, no later than the next business day</strong> (or the business day after receiving confirmation from a third party).</p>\n<p>Confirmation notice contents: firm ID; client name/designation; trading day/time; order type; execution venue; financial instrument; buy/sell indicator; nature of order if not buy/sell (eg option exercise); unit price; total consideration; total commissions/expenses/mark-up-down; exchange rate (if currency conversion involved); client's settlement responsibility.</p>\n<p><strong>Record keeping</strong> for confirmations: UK MiFID business - at least <strong>5 years</strong> from dispatch; other business - at least <strong>3 years</strong>. Average pricing across tranches is acceptable for unit price calculation.</p>\n<h4>Periodic reporting</h4>\n<p>Investment managers must provide retail/professional clients with periodic statements (unless another party does so), containing: firm name; client's account name/designation; portfolio contents/valuation (each investment, value, cash balance start/end of period, performance); total charges (itemised: management fees vs execution costs, with note that a fuller breakdown is available on request); performance vs relevant benchmark(s); total dividends/interest/other payments received; corporate action information.</p>\n<p><strong>Frequency</strong>: at least <strong>quarterly</strong>; <strong>every 12 months</strong> if the client receives deal-by-deal confirmations and certain higher-risk investments are excluded; <strong>monthly</strong> if the client's portfolio is authorised to be leveraged. Deal-by-deal confirmations must still meet the transaction reporting content/timing rules.</p>\n<p><strong>Record keeping</strong> for periodic statements/execution notices: UK MiFID business - at least <strong>5 years</strong>; other business - at least <strong>3 years</strong> (exceptions for certain overseas customers and JISAs).</p>\n<p>Firms managing investments or operating accounts with uncovered open positions must report losses over a pre-agreed limit by the <strong>end of the business day</strong> the limit is breached (or the next full business day if breached on a non-business day). Portfolio management: must notify a client if the portfolio falls <strong>10% or more</strong> since the last valuation (and multiples of 10% thereafter), and similarly at investment level for contingent liability/leveraged holdings - <strong>but from 7 June 2023</strong> (following HM Treasury's 2022 Markets in Financial Instruments (Investor Reporting) (Amendment) Regulations) firms no longer have to provide this contingent-liability/leveraged-investment notification.</p>\n"},{"heading":"2.13 Client Assets (CASS)","html":"\n<h4>13.1 Purpose of the client money and custody rules</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Client Assets Sourcebook (CASS)</strong> ensures firms take adequate steps to protect client money and client assets they are responsible for. CASS applies generally to every firm (subject to specific exemptions), and directly to activities for <strong>all client categories</strong> - retail, professional and ECP.</p>\n<p>Client money must be <strong>segregated</strong> from the firm's own money, so that on firm failure it is not available to the firm's creditors. Achieved by promptly placing client money in a separately designated client bank account, held by the firm on <strong>statutory trust</strong> (created under <strong>CASS 7.17.2R</strong>).</p>\n<p><strong>CASS 6</strong> - custody rules: apply when a firm holds client financial instruments in the course of MiFID business, and when safeguarding/administering investments in non-MiFID business.</p>\n<p><strong>CASS 7</strong> - Client Money and Distribution Rules: apply to a UK MiFID investment firm when it holds client money in MiFID business (or any investment agreement with/for a client); when a non-MiFID firm opts into the MiFID client money rules; or when the firm holds money under CASS 5 (insurance mediation client money) and elects to apply CASS 7.</p>\n<p>Firms holding safe custody assets must: safeguard client ownership rights (esp. on insolvency); prevent client assets being used on the firm's own account without express client consent; introduce organisational arrangements minimising risk of loss/diminution.</p>\n<p>Firms must register correct legal title to assets in the name of the client, a nominee, or another authorised person, as appropriate. If a firm owns a nominee, it accepts the same responsibility as if it held the assets itself.</p>\n<h4>Client bank accounts</h4>\n<p>On opening a client bank account, the firm must notify the bank that client money must not be combined with the firm's own accounts; the account title must distinguish it from other firm accounts. Under the old rule, a UK bank had to acknowledge trustee status within <strong>20 (business) days</strong>, or the firm had to withdraw the money and place it elsewhere. <strong>Current rule</strong>: firms must obtain a signed acknowledgement letter for <strong>all</strong> client bank accounts (not just UK ones), and the <strong>20-business-day grace period has been removed</strong> - in effect, client money cannot be placed in the account until the signed acknowledgement letter has been returned.</p>\n<h4>Exemptions from the client money rules</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Money held in connection with a <strong>delivery versus payment (DvP)</strong> transaction - unless settlement fails to occur by close of business on the <strong>third business day</strong> following the delivery obligation's due payment date.</li>\n<li>Money that has become due and payable to the firm.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Firm failure - distribution of client assets</h4>\n<p>On firm failure, initial distribution of client assets is made on the basis of the firm's own records, from an <strong>initial client money pool</strong>. Any residual money after initial distribution goes into a <strong>second pool</strong>, distributed via the firm's claims process.</p>\n<h4>13.2 Exemptions from CASS</h4>\n<p>CASS does not apply to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Investment companies with variable capital (ICVCs).</li>\n<li>UCITS-qualifying schemes.</li>\n<li>A credit institution (bank) holding deposits with itself as banker (not as trustee).</li>\n<li>Coins held for the value of their metal.</li>\n<li>Money held in connection with a DvP transaction (unless payment fails to occur after 3 business days).</li>\n<li>Money due and payable to the firm.</li>\n<li>Cases where the firm carries on business in its own name but on the client's behalf, where required by the transaction's nature and the client agrees.</li>\n<li>CASS 6 and CASS 7 do not apply where a client transfers full ownership of a safe custody asset or client money to a firm to secure/cover present, future, actual, contingent or prospective obligations (title transfer collateral arrangements).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Specific individual rules within CASS may be disapplied depending on the nature of a firm's activities.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"A UK MiFID investment firm carries on MiFID business from a branch established in an EEA member state. Following Brexit, does the COBS Handbook apply to this business?","options":["Yes, COBS always applies to UK-incorporated firms regardless of location","No, the COBS Handbook no longer applies to a UK MiFID firm's business carried on from an establishment in an EEA state","Only the best execution rules apply","Only if the client is a retail client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Following Brexit, COBS no longer applies to the MiFID business of a UK MiFID firm carried on from an establishment in an EEA member state. COBS does apply to third-country firms (including former EEA firms) operating a UK branch."},{"question":"Which two areas of COBS apply on a 'home state basis' to UK firms regardless of where in the world the business is conducted?","options":["Best execution and client order handling","Client agreements and suitability","Investment research and personal account dealing","Client money and custody"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The general geographic application rule for COBS has an exception for investment research and personal account dealing, which apply on a home state basis - ie, to UK firms wherever in the world they conduct that business."},{"question":"A firm wishes to communicate a new financial promotion on behalf of one of its appointed representatives (ARs). Which statement best describes the regulatory position?","options":["The AR's promotion needs no approval at all and the principal has no responsibility for it","The promotion is technically an exempt promotion not requiring approval, but the principal firm remains responsible for its content","The AR must personally seek FCA approval before the promotion is issued","ARs are prohibited from communicating any financial promotions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A financial promotion communicated by an AR is technically exempt and does not need approval, but COBS makes clear the principal firm is responsible for the content of financial promotions communicated by its ARs."},{"question":"Under the FCA's 2022 reforms to the appointed representatives regime, how far in advance must a principal notify the FCA of a new AR appointment?","options":["7 days","14 days","30 days","60 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Principals must notify the FCA of a new AR appointment 30 days before it takes effect. The 60-day period relates to principals providing information on existing ARs after the new rules came into force."},{"question":"Under the non-MiFID large undertaking test, a partnership must have net assets of at least what amount (at any time in the past two years) to qualify as a per se professional client?","options":["£2 million","£5 million","£10 million","£12.5 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A partnership or unincorporated association qualifies as a large undertaking (non-MiFID business) if its net assets are, or have at any time in the past two years been, at least £5 million (or currency equivalent)."},{"question":"For MiFID and equivalent third-country business, a company is a 'large undertaking' (and so a per se professional client) if it meets any two of which size thresholds?","options":["Balance sheet €20m, turnover €40m, own funds €2m","Balance sheet €12.5m, turnover €25m, 250 employees","Net assets £5m, turnover €25m, 50 members","Balance sheet €10m, turnover €20m, own funds €5m"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under MiFID/equivalent third-country business, a large undertaking must meet any two of: balance sheet total €20 million; net turnover €40 million; own funds €2 million. The €12.5m/€25m/250-employee test is the non-MiFID alternative test."},{"question":"A trustee of an occupational pension scheme wishes to qualify as a per se professional client (non-MiFID business). What is the minimum required combination?","options":["10 members and £5 million AUM","50 members and £10 million AUM","100 members and £20 million AUM","25 members and £2 million AUM"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A trustee of an occupational pension scheme, SSAS, or trustee/operator of a personal/stakeholder pension qualifies as a large undertaking if the scheme has, or has had, at least 50 members and assets under management of at least £10 million."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT part of the quantitative test for a retail client to become an elective professional client for MiFID business?","options":["Ten significantly sized transactions per quarter on average over the last four quarters","A financial instrument portfolio exceeding €500,000","At least one year's relevant professional experience in the financial sector","A minimum net worth of £250,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The quantitative test requires any two of: 10 significant trades/quarter average over 4 quarters; portfolio (cash + instruments) exceeding €500,000; and at least 1 year's relevant professional experience. There is no separate £250,000 net worth requirement in this test."},{"question":"Firm X provides services to person A, and knows that A is acting as agent for person B. In general, who should Firm X treat as its client?","options":["B, in all circumstances","A, unless the firm has agreed in writing to treat B as the client, or A's involvement is mainly to reduce the firm's duties to B","Whichever of A or B has greater net assets","The firm may choose either A or B at its discretion at any time"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The default position is to treat A (the agent) as the client, unless the firm has agreed in writing with A to treat B as its client, or A's involvement is mainly to reduce the firm's duties to B (in which case B must be treated as the client regardless)."},{"question":"Under COBS, for how long must a firm retain client agreement records, at minimum?","options":["3 years, or the duration of the relationship if shorter","5 years, or the duration of the relationship, whichever is longer (indefinitely for pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs)","7 years in all cases","10 years for retail clients only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Client agreement records must be kept for the longer of 5 years or the duration of the relationship with the client, and indefinitely in the case of pension transfers, pension opt-outs, or free-standing additional voluntary contributions (FSAVCs)."},{"question":"A firm agrees a client agreement using a distance communication at the client's request, and complies with the voice telephone communication rules. When may it provide the agreement and required information to the client?","options":["It must always be provided before the client is bound","It may be provided immediately after the client is bound by the agreement","It never needs to be provided","Only within 30 days after the transaction"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where the client requested use of distance communications and the firm complies with the voice telephone communication rules, the firm may provide the agreement and information immediately after the client is bound, rather than beforehand."},{"question":"Which of the following is an example of an 'excluded communication' potentially exempt from the financial promotion rules?","options":["A direct offer promotion of a regulated CIS to retail clients","A promotion communicated to a high-net-worth individual under Article 48 of the FPO","A promotion relating to UK MiFID investment advice for a retail client","A cold call to a retail client with no existing relationship"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Excluded communications include those aimed at high-net-worth individuals (FPO Article 48) and sophisticated investors (Articles 50/50A). However, the exemption for excluded communications generally does not apply to UK MiFID business."},{"question":"Since 2023, before an authorised firm may approve financial promotions for communication by unauthorised third parties, what must it obtain from the FCA?","options":["A specific 'gateway' permission","Nothing - approval by any authorised firm is automatic","A letter of comfort from the client","Sign-off from the Prudential Regulation Authority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In 2023 the FCA introduced a 'gateway' - firms wishing to continue approving financial promotions for third parties needed to apply to the FCA for permission. Firms without this permission may now only approve their own promotions."},{"question":"A firm's marketing materials include indications of past performance for an investment fund that has existed for eight years. What is the minimum period the past performance data must cover under COBS?","options":["1 year","3 years","5 years, shown in complete 12-month periods","10 years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Past performance information must cover at least the immediately preceding 5 years (or the whole period the investment/index/service has existed if shorter), and must show complete 12-month periods."},{"question":"Under the FCA's 2020 high-risk investment promotion rules, what is the minimum cooling-off period that must be applied to a first-time investor with a firm before they can invest in a high-risk product following a direct offer financial promotion?","options":["No cooling-off period is required","24 hours","48 hours","7 calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA's rules introduced a minimum 24-hour cooling-off period for first-time investors with a firm, although the FCA encourages firms to apply higher standards than this minimum."},{"question":"Which category of high-risk investment is completely banned from being promoted to retail investors under the FCA's rules?","options":["Restricted Mass Market Investments (RMMIs)","Non-Mass Market Investments (NMMIs)","UCITS funds","Cash deposit ISAs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The promotion of Non-Mass Market Investments (NMMIs) is banned to retail investors entirely. Restricted Mass Market Investments (RMMIs) may be promoted to retail investors but subject to restrictions (eg not permitted for non-readily realisable securities and peer-to-peer agreements)."},{"question":"A prescribed risk summary accompanying a high-risk investment promotion should, according to FCA guidance, take no longer than how long to read (absent valid reasons to diverge)?","options":["30 seconds","2 minutes","5 minutes","10 minutes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Prescribed risk summaries should take no more than two minutes to read, though firms can diverge from this where there are valid reasons to do so."},{"question":"Under the FCA's cryptoasset financial promotion rules, which client categorisation option is NOT available for cryptoasset promotions, unlike other RMMI promotions?","options":["Restricted investor","High-net-worth investor","Self-certified sophisticated investor","Certified high-net-worth investor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"For cryptoasset promotions classified as RMMIs, the self-certified sophisticated investor category will not be applied - it is specifically excluded from the categorisation routes available for cryptoasset promotions."},{"question":"A firm wishes to cold call a retail client with whom it has no existing relationship, to promote a higher-volatility fund. Is this permitted under COBS?","options":["Yes, cold calling is always permitted for packaged products","No - cold calling is not permitted where it relates to a higher-volatility fund or a life policy linked to such a fund","Yes, provided the call takes place between 9am and 5pm","Yes, provided the client is informed of their right to cancel"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Cold calls are permitted only in limited circumstances - eg where an existing relationship exists and the call is expected, or the call relates to a generally marketable packaged product that is NOT a higher-volatility fund or a life policy linked to such a fund."},{"question":"Under the suitability rules, what may a firm assume about a per se professional client (for UK MiFID business) that it may NOT assume about an elective professional client?","options":["That the client has sufficient knowledge and experience","That the client is able to financially bear the related investment risks consistent with their investment objectives","That the client wants to receive a suitability report","That the client is an eligible counterparty"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm may assume both per se and elective professional clients have the necessary knowledge and experience (for MiFID business). However, it may only assume the client's ability to financially bear investment risk for PER SE professional clients, not elective professional clients."},{"question":"A discretionary fund manager repeatedly trades a client's portfolio, generating substantial commission, in a pattern that is not justified by the client's investment strategy. This is best described as:","options":["Switching","Insistent client dealing","Churning","Aggregation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Churning is the activity of over-dealing or trading more frequently for a client than is warranted, in order to generate additional fees or commissions - most relevant where a firm manages a portfolio on a discretionary basis."},{"question":"A firm makes a personal recommendation to a retail client to take out a personal pension scheme, and the statutory cancellation rules apply. Within what period must the firm provide the suitability report?","options":["Before the contract is concluded, in all cases","Within 14 days of concluding the contract","Within 30 days of concluding the contract","No suitability report is required for pension contracts"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For a personal pension scheme or stakeholder pension where the cancellation rules apply, the suitability report must be provided within 14 days of the contract being concluded."},{"question":"A friendly society makes a personal recommendation in connection with a small life policy, with a premium of £45 a year. Is a suitability report required?","options":["Yes, always required for life policies","No - there is an exception for friendly society recommendations on small life policies with premiums not exceeding £50/year (or £1/week)","Only if the client is a retail client aged over 65","Yes, but only within 30 days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is a specific exception to the suitability report requirement where a friendly society makes a personal recommendation on a small life policy with a premium not exceeding £50 a year (or £1 a week if paid weekly). £45/year falls within this exception."},{"question":"A client rejects the personal recommendation given by their adviser and instead instructs the firm to execute a different, riskier transaction, which the firm agrees to facilitate. What is this client known as, and what must the firm do?","options":["A professional client; no further action is required","An insistent client; the firm must clearly communicate that the transaction is not recommended, the reasons why, and the risks involved","An elective ECP; the firm must refuse to execute the transaction","A vulnerable customer; the transaction must be referred to the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This describes an insistent client. The firm must communicate, clearly and fairly, that it has not recommended the transaction, why it is not in line with its recommendation, the risks of the proposed transaction, and why it did not recommend that transaction."},{"question":"Under the appropriateness rules, which of the following would generally be classed as a 'non-complex' financial instrument, meaning the appropriateness test need not be applied for an execution-only transaction?","options":["An exchange-traded derivative contract","A structured product with embedded derivatives","Shares listed on a UK regulated market","A warrant"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Shares listed on a UK or EEA regulated market (or equivalent third-country market) are treated as non-complex instruments. Derivatives, embedded-derivative products, and warrants are excluded from the non-complex category."},{"question":"A retail client declines to provide the information a firm needs to assess appropriateness for a non-advised transaction. What must the firm do?","options":["Refuse to proceed with the transaction under any circumstances","Warn the client that it is unable to assess appropriateness, then use its judgement on whether to proceed if the client still wants to","Proceed automatically without any warning","Refer the matter to the Financial Ombudsman Service"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If a client declines to provide the required information, the firm must warn the client that it cannot assess the appropriateness of the product/service (this warning may be standardised). If the client still asks to proceed, it is up to the firm's judgement whether to do so."},{"question":"What is the maximum length, in A4 pages, of a Key Information Document (KID) under the PRIIPs Regulation?","options":["1 page","2 pages","3 pages","5 pages"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A KID is a stand-alone, standardised document that can be up to a maximum of three A4 pages."},{"question":"UCITS schemes currently produce a KIID rather than a PRIIPs KID. Until what date does this exemption run?","options":["31 December 2024","31 December 2025","31 December 2026","31 December 2028"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"UCITS KIID/KFD were given a five-year exemption period following implementation of PRIIPs, which has been extended to expire on 31 December 2026."},{"question":"A retail client makes an advised purchase of units in an authorised unit trust (AUT) and later wishes to exercise their statutory right to cancel. Within how many days of the relevant trigger date must they do so?","options":["7 calendar days","14 calendar days","21 calendar days","30 calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Non-life and pension products (whether advised, non-distance contracts, or distance contracts) carry a 14 calendar day cancellation period. Only life and pension products have the longer 30 calendar day period."},{"question":"What is the cancellation period applicable to life and pension products under the COBS cancellation rules?","options":["14 calendar days","21 calendar days","30 calendar days","60 calendar days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Life and pension products carry a 30 calendar day cancellation period - longer than the 14 calendar day period applicable to most other designated investments and cash deposit ISAs."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes when disclosure of a conflict of interest to a client is appropriate under SYSC/COBS?","options":["Disclosure should be the firm's first response whenever any conflict is identified","Disclosure is a last resort, to be used only when the firm's organisational arrangements are not sufficient to manage the risk of client detriment","Disclosure is never an acceptable method of managing a conflict","Disclosure is only required for eligible counterparties"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Disclosure of a conflict of interest is a last resort - firms should first have arrangements designed to prevent conflicts arising. Disclosure alone, without adequate management, is not permitted (SYSC 10.1.8 vs SYSC 10.1.3)."},{"question":"Under the rules on investment research, a firm's financial analyst becomes aware of the content of unpublished research recommending a 'sell' on a stock. What is the analyst generally prohibited from doing?","options":["Discussing the research with the compliance department","Undertaking a personal transaction in the relevant investment before clients have had a reasonable opportunity to act on the research","Attending internal research meetings","Reading the final published version of the research"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms must prevent 'dealing ahead' - financial analysts and other relevant persons with knowledge of unpublished research must not undertake personal transactions (or trade for others) until clients have had a reasonable opportunity to act on the research, subject to limited exceptions."},{"question":"What distinguishes 'non-independent research' from 'investment research' under the COBS conflicts of interest rules?","options":["Non-independent research is treated as a marketing communication subject to COBS 4, and must be clearly labelled as such","Non-independent research is exempt from all disclosure requirements","Non-independent research can only be produced by ECPs","There is no material difference between the two"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Non-independent research (not presented as objective/independent) is treated as a financial promotion/marketing communication and subject to COBS 4. It must be clearly identified as a marketing communication and disclose that it is not subject to the dealing-ahead prohibitions that apply to independent investment research."},{"question":"Under the inducements rules, which of the following is a permitted 'minor non-monetary benefit' that a firm may accept in connection with providing independent advice to a retail client?","options":["A cash payment from a product provider","Reasonable de minimis hospitality such as food and drink at a business meeting or conference","An all-expenses-paid overseas holiday","A percentage-based commission linked to sales volume"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Minor non-monetary benefits permitted include generic/personalised product information, third-party issuer-commissioned material promoting a new issuance, conference/seminar/training participation, and reasonable de minimis hospitality (eg food and drink at a meeting or conference)."},{"question":"A UK asset manager wishes to receive third-party investment research on a company with a market capitalisation of £150 million without paying for it separately. Is this permitted?","options":["No, all research must always be paid for separately regardless of market capitalisation","Yes - research on companies with a market capitalisation below £200 million is exempt from the inducement rules","Only if the company is listed outside the UK","Only with prior written approval from the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA introduced an exemption from the inducement rules for research on companies with a market capitalisation of less than £200 million, meaning UK-based fund managers do not have to pay for this research separately (though they may choose to)."},{"question":"Under COBS, what standard must a MiFID investment firm meet when seeking to obtain the best possible result for its clients when executing orders?","options":["All reasonable steps","All sufficient steps","Best efforts only","No specific standard is required"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MiFID investment firms (COBS 11.2A) must take 'all sufficient steps' to obtain the best possible result - a higher standard than the 'all reasonable steps' that continues to apply to AIFMs and residual CIS operators (COBS 11.2)."},{"question":"How often must a MiFID investment firm review the ongoing appropriateness of its order execution policy and arrangements, at minimum?","options":["Monthly","Quarterly","At least annually, or when material changes occur","Every 5 years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must review the ongoing appropriateness of their order execution policy and arrangements at least annually, or when there are material changes affecting the firm's ability to continue obtaining the best possible result for clients."},{"question":"A client gives a firm a specific instruction relating to only one aspect of an order (eg the venue), leaving other aspects (eg timing) to the firm's discretion. What is the effect on the firm's best execution obligation?","options":["The firm is entirely relieved of its best execution obligation for the whole order","The firm is deemed to have satisfied best execution only in relation to the specifically instructed part of the order; the rest remains subject to best execution","The firm must ignore the client's instruction and apply its own execution policy throughout","The client's instruction is automatically void"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Following a specific client instruction satisfies best execution only for the part or aspect of the order covered by that instruction. Any other aspects of the order not covered remain subject to the general best execution obligations."},{"question":"A firm wishes to aggregate a client order with its own proprietary order. Under COBS, how must it allocate the trades if only some of the aggregated order is executed?","options":["The firm's own order must always be filled first","Client orders must be allocated in priority over the firm's own order, unless the firm can show that without including its own order, less favourable terms would have resulted","Orders must always be allocated pro-rata regardless of circumstances","The firm may choose to allocate however it wishes with no restriction"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Where a firm aggregates its own-account orders with client orders, it must allocate client orders in priority over its own, unless it can show that without including its own order, less favourable terms would have resulted - in which case proportional allocation is permitted."},{"question":"A firm receives a client limit order for shares listed on a regulated market, which it cannot immediately execute given prevailing market conditions. What must it generally do, absent contrary client instructions?","options":["Cancel the order immediately","Make the limit order public immediately, unless it is over normal market size","Hold the order privately until it can be executed at any point in the future","Execute the order at a worse price to guarantee completion"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unless the client instructs otherwise, a firm receiving a client limit order that cannot be immediately executed must make it public immediately (via a regulated market/MTF order book or other accessible means), so it can be executed as soon as possible - except for orders over normal market size."},{"question":"Under the personal account dealing rules, which of the following transactions is generally EXEMPT from the restrictions?","options":["A discretionary management service deal where there was no prior communication between the manager and the relevant person about the deal","A direct personal instruction to buy shares in a company the relevant person's firm is advising on a takeover of","Disclosure of confidential information to a family member who then trades on it","A transaction that conflicts with the firm's duties to a customer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Personal account dealing rules do not apply to deals under a discretionary management service where there is no prior communication between the portfolio manager and the relevant person about the deal, deals in units of certain funds where the relevant person isn't involved in fund management, and personal transactions in life policies."},{"question":"Under UK MiFIR, by when must a UK investment firm report a reportable transaction to the FCA?","options":["Immediately, in real time","As soon as possible, no later than the close of the next business day","Within 5 business days","Within 1 calendar month"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Complete and accurate details of reportable transactions must be reported to the FCA as soon as possible, and no later than the close of the next business day (a 'trade date + 1' basis)."},{"question":"Under MiFID II post-trade transparency rules, within what deadline must an equity transaction generally be publicly reported (absent waivers/deferrals)?","options":["Immediately with no delay permitted","1 minute","5 minutes","15 minutes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Unless waivers or deferrals apply, post-trade transparency reporting deadlines are 1 minute for equity transactions and 5 minutes for in-scope non-equity financial instruments."},{"question":"A UK branch of a Canadian bank (a third-country firm) executes a derivatives trade. Does it have a reporting obligation under UK EMIR?","options":["Yes, all branches operating in the UK must report under UK EMIR","No - non-UK entities, including UK branches of third-country firms, do not have a UK EMIR reporting obligation","Only if the trade exceeds £1 million in notional value","Only if the counterparty is a UK financial institution"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under UK EMIR, non-UK entities do not have a reporting obligation. This expressly includes UK branches of third-country firms, such as the London branch of a Canadian bank."},{"question":"Under the UK Short Selling Regulation (SSR), by what time must a firm notify the FCA of a net short position via the Electronic Submission System?","options":["9.00am on the same trading day","12 noon on the same trading day","3.30pm on the trading day after the position was reached","5.00pm on the trading day the position was reached"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must notify the FCA by 3.30pm on the trading day after the day the position was reached, with calculations made as at midnight on the trading day the position was reached."},{"question":"Under UK SFTR, which category of counterparty is exempted by HM Treasury from the obligation to report securities financing transactions?","options":["UK financial counterparties","Non-financial counterparties (ie, corporate companies)","Central counterparties (CCPs)","Trade repositories"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Under UK SFTR, non-financial counterparties (ie, corporate companies) are exempted by HM Treasury from having to report their securities financing transactions."},{"question":"An off-order-book equity trade is executed on the London Stock Exchange via telephone at 10.00am. Within how many minutes must the member firm report the trade, in normal circumstances?","options":["1 minute","3 minutes","10 minutes","30 minutes"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Off-order-book transactions must be reported by the member firm as close to real time as possible, and in any case within three minutes of execution, by reference to the LSE's trade reporting period (7.15am to 5.15pm)."},{"question":"By when must a firm (other than one managing investments) send a client a confirmation notice following the execution of a client order?","options":["Immediately, with no exceptions","As soon as possible, but no later than the next business day","Within 3 business days","Within 1 week"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm must send a notice confirming the deal details as soon as possible, but no later than the next business day; where confirmation is received from a third party, it must be passed on no later than the business day following receipt."},{"question":"How frequently must a firm managing investments send periodic statements to a retail client, as a general minimum requirement?","options":["Weekly","Monthly","At least quarterly","Annually"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Periodic statements must generally be sent at least quarterly. Exceptions: monthly if the client's portfolio may be leveraged; every 12 months if the client receives deal-by-deal confirmations and certain higher-risk investments are excluded."},{"question":"A firm carrying on portfolio management for a retail client must notify the client if the portfolio falls by what percentage since the last valuation?","options":["5% or more","10% or more (and multiples of 10% thereafter)","20% or more","25% or more"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm providing portfolio management must provide a depreciation notification if the client's portfolio falls 10% or more since the last valuation, and at each subsequent multiple of 10%."},{"question":"Following the Markets in Financial Instruments (Investor Reporting) (Amendment) Regulations, from what date do firms no longer need to provide a separate depreciation notification for contingent liability or leveraged investments at an individual holding level?","options":["1 January 2022","7 June 2023","31 December 2023","1 March 2025"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Following HM Treasury's 2022 Markets in Financial Instruments (Investor Reporting) (Amendment) Regulations, from 7 June 2023 firms no longer have to provide the depreciation notification at an investment level for contingent liability or leveraged investments."},{"question":"Under CASS, client money held by a firm is protected through which legal mechanism?","options":["A floating charge in favour of the client","A statutory trust created under CASS 7.17.2R","A personal guarantee from the firm's directors","An insurance policy underwritten by the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Client money is segregated and held on a statutory trust, created by the rules laid down in CASS 7.17.2R, ensuring that on the firm's failure the money is not available to repay the firm's general creditors."},{"question":"Under the current CASS rules, when may a firm place client money into a newly opened client bank account?","options":["Immediately upon opening the account, regardless of bank acknowledgement","Only after the bank has signed and returned the acknowledgement letter confirming trustee status - there is no grace period","Within 20 business days of opening the account, even without acknowledgement","At any time within 60 days of account opening"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The old rule gave banks 20 business days to acknowledge trustee status. The current rule removed this grace period: firms must obtain a signed acknowledgement letter for all client bank accounts before placing client money into them."},{"question":"Under CASS, the client money exemption for delivery versus payment (DvP) transactions ceases to apply if settlement has not occurred by when?","options":["Close of business on the same day as the transaction","Close of business on the third business day following the due payment date of the delivery obligation","Close of business on the fifth business day","There is no time limit on the DvP exemption"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The client money rules do not apply to money held in connection with a DvP transaction, unless the DvP does not occur by close of business on the third business day following the date of payment of a delivery obligation."},{"question":"On the failure of a firm holding client money, how is the initial distribution of client assets carried out?","options":["On the basis of the firm's own records, from an initial client money pool","Only after a full independent audit has been completed, which may take years","Equally among all clients regardless of their actual holdings","By the FCA directly, bypassing the firm's records entirely"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"On firm failure, the initial distribution of client assets is made on the basis of the firm's own records, from an initial client money pool. Any residual money after this initial distribution goes into a second pool for further distribution via the claims process."},{"question":"A buy-side firm wants to bundle payment for third-party research with execution services. What do the rules permit?","options":["Bundling is prohibited because research must always be paid from the firm's own resources","Bundling is permitted if the firm meets the relevant requirements on execution and research costs","Bundling is permitted only where the research relates to small-cap equity issuers","Bundling is prohibited unless the research provider is outside the firm's financial group"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA introduced payment optionality allowing UK buy-side firms greater flexibility, including bundling payments for third-party research and execution services, provided relevant requirements around execution and research costs are met. The other options contradict or narrow the rule incorrectly."},{"question":"All of the following statements regarding Chinese walls are true except:","options":["They are a method by which a firm can avoid a conflict of interests","They may be physical or artificial barriers preventing the flow of information","The FCA specify that Chinese walls must be implemented in all asset management firms","They may be operated by firms with several departments"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA defends the use of Chinese walls but does not require Chinese walls to be put in place in all firms without exception."},{"question":"The following are true of investment advertisements sent to retail clients except that they must:","options":["Not use any past performance data","Be fair, clear and not misleading","Indicate that the value of shares can go down as well as up","Warn that past performance is no guarantee of future performance"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Past performance data may be used, provided a 'wealth warning' is given."},{"question":"The FCA Conduct of Business rules apply to all the following firms EXCEPT:","options":["An adviser recommending life policies","A company operating a stakeholder pension scheme","A bank providing retail broker services","A building society offering FX transactions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FX transactions are not regulated activities and are not covered under the conduct of business rules."},{"question":"Which of the following is a 'personal recommendation' under MiFID?","options":["Advising a client how to fill in an application form","Discussing company news or announcements","Explaining the risks of a particular investment instrument","Suggesting the purchase of a particular investment instrument"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The others are not 'personal recommendations'. They are general advice and, as far as MiFID is concerned, not business."},{"question":"Churning relates to:","options":["The overtrading of investments only","UK clients only","Retail clients only","Changing between packaged products only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Churning is the activity of dealing in securities with excessive frequency for a client in order to generate additional fees/commissions for the firm. It is relevant when, for example, a firm manages a client's portfolio on a discretionary basis."},{"question":"In relation to providing investments, the rule on inducements in the Conduct of Business rules, which ONE of the following is TRUE?","options":["The rules apply only when conducting business with retail clients","The rule does not prohibit the firm from paying an inducement to the client","The rule prohibits any non-monetary benefit, no matter how minor it may appear to be","The inducement must not be disclosed to the client until after the service is provided"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The rule \"\"A firm must not pay to, or accept from, a person other than the client, any fee, commission or non-monetary benefit\"\" allows payment to, and from, the client.\n\nThe inducement rules apply to business conducted with all customers, i.e. professional clients and retail clients.\n\n\nDisclosure of the inducement must be made to the client before providing the service. The prohibition does not apply to 'minor non-monetary benefits'."},{"question":"A firm needs to assess the appropriateness of an investment or service for a retail client when the service is:","options":["Based on shares that trade on a regulated market","Only the reception and transmission of the client's orders of a bond","Provided at the initiative of the client","To arrange the purchase of a contingent liability instrument"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Assessing appropriateness is generally needed when the firm provides services other than recommendations and managing investments, such as introducing a client to an investment opportunity. However, it does not apply simply to execution only trades.\nIt is also required for arranging deals in derivatives and warrants for retail clients and assessing appropriateness for another MiFID firm.\nAlthough services on regulated markets that fall under the above requirements are covered, it is not only those markets that are covered."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT taken into account when reclassifying a retail client as a professional client?","options":["Knowledge and understanding","Frequency of previous deals","Financial standing","Integrity and reputation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"When determining if a retail client can be classified as a professional client (an 'expert' customer) a firm has to consider the following factors:\nThe client's knowledge and understanding of the relevant designated investments and markets, and the inherent risks.\nHow long the client has been active in these markets.\nHow frequently the client has been dealing in the markets.\nThe extent to which a client has relied on advice on investments.\nThe size and nature of the transactions undertaken by the client."},{"question":"To whom should a firm produce a document about compensation claims when carrying on MiFID business?","options":["Professional clients only","Retail and professional clients only","Retail clients only","All clients"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A firm which carries on MiFID business must make available to any client who has used or intends to use those services, the information necessary for the identification of the compensation scheme or any other investor compensation scheme of which the firm is a member or any alternative arrangement provided for in accordance with the investor compensation scheme."},{"question":"A firm is outside the scope of the Consumer Duty. Which requirement continues to apply to its treatment of customers?","options":["Principle 12 requiring good outcomes for retail customers","Principle 6 requiring due regard to customers' interests and fair treatment","A duty to apply retail customer outcomes to its products and services","A duty to oversee the actions of firms in the distribution chain"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms not subject to the Consumer Duty must continue to adhere to Principle 6 and Principle 7. Principle 6 requires firms to pay due regard to the interests of customers and treat them fairly, which underpins Fair Treatment of Customers."},{"question":"Assuming that the client has approached the firm to request recategorisation, which one of the following could not be considered an elective eligible counterparty?","options":["The management company of a CIS","An investment firm based overseas","A commodity firm that trades for commercial purposes only","An individual trading for their own account with 10 years of professional experience"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An individual and other institutional investors cannot choose to be an elective eligible counterparty."},{"question":"When must a firm disclose charges to a retail client?","options":["Before providing the service","Along with the first contract note","After completion of the first deal","At the discretion of the customer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm must notify RETAIL CLIENTS of the amount or basis of charges IN WRITING, BEFORE any work is done. The FCA is comfortable with professional clients agreeing the basis of charges in arrears of any dealings, but this arrangement is not appropriate for retail clients."},{"question":"With regard to the approval of a non-real time financial promotion, which of the following is true? A firm:","options":["Can use any third party to approve the financial promotion but it remains the responsibility of the firm","Cannot use a third party to approve the financial promotion","Can use any third party authorised to approve the financial promotion and the promotion remains the responsibility of the firm","Can use any third party authorised to approve the financial promotion and the promotion becomes the responsibility of the third party"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In approving a financial promotion, the approving firm takes on responsibility for the promotion."},{"question":"Which one of these is not an excluded communication under the financial promotion rules?","options":["An unapproved financial promotion by an unauthorised firm","A financial promotion that is exempt under the FPO","A one-off financial promotion that is not a cold call","A personal quotation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is not an excluded communication, and would be considered a criminal offence, as it would in fact be a breach of s21 FSMA."},{"question":"A firm that manages a client's investments must ensure that which of the following occurs?","options":["Portfolio are benchmarked against the FTSE All Shares Index","Portfolio performances are benchmarked at least monthly","Any index they use to benchmark performance is specified in advance","Any deviation from the benchmark triggers a benchmark review"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The first benchmark (FTSE All Shares) might be comparable in some cases but not all and so is not the best answer here. The timing of performance benchmarking is not specified in the COBS. The firm should also include an outline of any designated investments that may be included in the portfolio, not just those in which it is currently invested."},{"question":"If providing execution only services in a non-complex product, the only information a firm requires is the client's:","options":["Name and address","Financial situation","Experience and expertise","Investment objectives"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The choices available reflect the rule on suitability or appropriateness:\n- Client's knowledge\n- Client's financial capability\n- Client's objectives\nThese are not required for execution only services in non-complex products. The only choice left is the name and address of the client. This makes logical sense, in that the firm needs to know to whom they must send the contract notes, etc."},{"question":"EMIR sets out requirements for reporting and risk management in the OTC derivative markets. Which firms are covered by this regulation?","options":["All EEA-incorporated and established entities","EEA fund management entities only","EEA financial entities only","EEA non-financial entities only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The rules apply to all EEA-incorporated and established entities, catching for the first time non-financial corporate entities and not just financial entities, such as banks."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is true with respect to cancellation rights on the sale to a retail client of a packaged product?","options":["None required","Cancellation rights should be offered","Cancellation and withdrawal rights of 21 days are compulsory","Cancellation rights may be offered for a minimum period specified by the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is the best answer. Depending upon the type of product, cancellation periods can vary between 14 and 30 calendar days."},{"question":"When considering best execution criteria for a retail client, which of the following would NOT be considered when obtaining the best possible result?","options":["The characteristics of the order","The characteristics of the investment","The costs incurred by the client payable to the firm","The costs incurred by the client payable to the execution venue"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The characteristics of the client, the order, the investment and the execution venue will all be taken into consideration. In addition for a retail client, costs directly relating to execution - including execution venue fees and clearing and settlement fees - must also be considered.\nCosts payable to the firm that the client is dealing with are not to be included in this assessment."},{"question":"When would an employee of an authorised firm MOST LIKELY be permitted to enter into a personal account transaction? If the transaction:","options":["It involves the use of confidential information acquired through the employees work","It might lead to a potential conflict of interest with the firm's clients","It involves the use of information about the transactions of one of the firm's clients","The employee is neither involved in a higher control function nor the provision of designated investment business"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In the three incorrect answers, there are obviously conflicts of interest or problems with client interests. The correct answer allows the person to not be considered as a relevant person in these circumstances."},{"question":"How long after a transaction must a confirmation be sent to the client?","options":["T+3","Not sent unless client requests it","Within three minutes","By the next business day"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Confirmations should be sent promptly (T+1)."},{"question":"A member of the compliance team is confirming the compliance of a financial promotion with FCA rules. Which of the following must be included in the promotion?","options":["Capital at risk products are clearly referred to as such","It labels the regulator as MiFID","It quotes a variety of expected returns","Reference to the British Bankers' Association Code of Conduct"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"MiFID is a directive, not a regulator. The expected returns figure should give a balanced view of the long and short term prospects of the investment instrument, but it is not something that must be included in the promotion."},{"question":"Which of the following sets of information is not required in a direct offer financial promotion to a retail client?","options":["The firm and its services","Costs and charges","Safekeeping of client money","Execution venues where trading will occur"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Execution venues will be in an order execution policy."},{"question":"Under the COBS, a professional client or an eligible counterparty can ask for a re-categorisation in which of the following cases?\nIIt is permitted on a trade by trade basis\nIIIt is permitted in general for all trades\nIIIIt is permitted on individual instrument classes\nIVIt is only available for complex financial instruments","options":["IV only","I, II, III","I and III","I and II"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The counterparty could ask or be offered the treatment in IV, but it is not limited to only complex financial instruments; it may be given for any product or service on either a trade by trade or general basis."},{"question":"When an FCA firm undertakes a promotion to a retail client, which is TRUE?","options":["Past performance can only be given for products for which there is five year performance history","The past performance must be the most prominent feature","Past performance data is mandatory when promoting to retail clients","There must be a warning that any performance data is not a reliable indicator of future performance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Past performance data can be included for less than five years, if it is for the life of the investment."},{"question":"The requirement for the firm to deliver the best possible result when executing client orders is expected for:","options":["Readily realisable instruments only","Money market instruments only","Securities, financial options and financial futures only","All types of financial instruments"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Best execution according to COBS applies to all types of financial instruments."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, which of the following is permissible for the presentation of past, simulated past or future performance of a UK investment?","options":["The information must be a prominent part of the information presented to the client","The gross performance is presented","The figures are also stated in euros","The reference period and source of information are clearly stated"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The information may not be the most prominent feature of communication. If the performance is presented as gross, the commissions etc. must be disclosed. If the currency is in a non-home currency it must be stated that there is a possible currency impact."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be automatically given retail client protection by an FCA member firm under client categorisation?","options":["An independent person not authorised by the FCA","A trustee of a trust with assets of €5 million and funds of €1 million","A listed company with funds of €2 million and assets of €25 million","A body corporate with net turnover of €30 million and assets of €1 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A listed company with funds of €2 million or more and assets of €20 million or more would be considered a professional client. All of the others fail to meet the criteria required for professional categorisation and would be treated as retail clients."},{"question":"If a client can be considered as a professional client, then the firm may assume that they have sufficient knowledge of which risks?","options":["All designated investments offered by an authorised firm","Options on non-complex underlying assets","Designated investments for which they are classed as professional","Generally marketable packaged products"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Professional client status can be for particular investment services or transactions, so this would be the best answer.\n\nThere is no requirement that all designated investments are understood and the status does not specifically refer to options or packaged products (generally marketable or not)."},{"question":"Which personal transaction by a relevant person is exempt from the personal account dealing rules?","options":["A transaction in a listed bond","A transaction in an overseas equity","A transaction in a life policy","A transaction in an option"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Personal transactions in life policies are specifically excluded from the personal account dealing rules. The other investments are not identified as exempt categories in this context."},{"question":"Which of the following is not defined as a packaged product according to COBS?","options":["A regulated CIS","A personal pension scheme","An interest in an investment trust savings scheme","A government or public security"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A government or public security would be considered a readily realisable security, according to COBS."},{"question":"If the firm aggregates a client's order with a transaction for its own account, which of the following is true of the allocation policy?","options":["The firm's allocation of the order, if the order is partially allocated, must take priority","The client's allocation of the order, if the order is partially allocated, must take priority","The allocation of the orders must be in proportion with the order size","A retail client's allocation of the order, if the order is partially allocated, must take priority over a professional client's order"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"COBS states that the client must take priority on an aggregated order allocation. There is no stipulation as to whether the client needs to be a retail or professional client."},{"question":"Churning is the name given to:","options":["Dealing in derivatives","Unsuitable recommendations of packaged products","Dealing in advance of disseminating a research recommendation","Overtrading in securities"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Churning is the practice of over-dealing in a client's securities in an attempt to generate fees."},{"question":"Which of the following is least likely to give rise to a conflict of interest?","options":["Advising a client to sell when the firm has a client wanting to buy","Encouraging a client to buy when a firm has gone short","Advising a client to buy when the firm has a long over-exposure","Encouraging a client to sell when the firm's analyst rated the stock highly"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm that had gone short would be hoping for prices to fall. Encouraging a client to buy would not assist the firm in any way; it suggests that the firm has properly ignored its own position in giving advice."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is LEAST likely to cause a conflict of interest?","options":["Encouraging customer A to sell so B can buy","Recommending customer sells shares which an analyst rates highly","Customer instructs firm to buy shares when the firm holds a position","Advising both sides in a takeover situation"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The question shows three situations where conflicts of interest could arise and one situation where it is unlikely. \n\n'Customer instructs firm to buy shares when the firm holds a position' is least likely to give rise to a conflict of interest.  The customer is instructing the firm to buy shares (i.e. execution only order). Remember, the question is not saying that the other situations will give rise to conflicts of interests in all cases, but that we should identify them as potential conflicts."},{"question":"Under the European Markets Infrastructure Regulation, which of the following is not a requirement on those that trade derivatives?","options":["Risk management procedures must be adopted for OTC derivatives","Certain OTC derivatives must be cleared through a qualifying central counterparty","To report all OTC derivative transactions to a trade repository","To ensure the orderly functioning of markets"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"To ensure the orderly functioning of markets is an objective of the regulator.\n\nEMIR focuses on the transparency and risk management of the OTC derivatives markets."},{"question":"A firm is preparing information for retail customers about a structured product. Which approach best reflects the Consumer Understanding Outcome?","options":["Providing technical product literature so customers can assess the full legal structure and complexity","Presenting key features, likely costs, risks and benefits so customers can make informed decisions","Delaying disclosure of product risks until they have become clear through customer interaction","Using the same detailed materials for retail and professional clients to ensure consistency"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Consumer Understanding Outcome requires firms to communicate in a way that enables customers to understand the features, likely costs, risks and benefits of products and services. Information should be clear, timely and appropriate for the target market, rather than overly technical or delayed."},{"question":"Which of the following are true with relation to a firm providing designated investment business to a retail client?\nIThe firm must pay due regard to the information needs of clients when communicating with them\nIIThe information should be sufficient to enable the client to understand the nature and risk of the service and investment\nIIIThe information communicated must be fair, clear and not misleading\nIVThe firm must provide appropriate information about itself to retail clients","options":["I and II","II and III","I, II and III","I, II, III, and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Note: the rules on information about the firm are an application of Principle of Business 7, 'Communications with Clients'."},{"question":"Which European regulation relates to the transparency and risk management in the OTC derivative markets?","options":["Markets in financial instruments directive","Capital requirements directive","Alternative investment fund manager directive","European markets infrastructure regulation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The European markets infrastructure regulation (EMIR) relates to the transparency and risk management in the OTC derivative markets. It requires trade reports to a trade repository, adequate risk management for trades, position management and collateral, and mandatory use of a central counterparty for some contracts."},{"question":"Which is FALSE in terms of the aggregation of orders?","options":["Firms must aggregate firm and client orders together","The firm must disclose its use of aggregation","Aggregation must be unlikely to operate to the disadvantage of clients","An order allocation policy must be in place"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Aggregation of trades is not mandatory."},{"question":"Where an investment bank is providing research to a fund manager a firm must apply a separately identifiable charge for this research. The FCA implemented changes under UK MiFID that fund managers do not have to pay if the research provided relates a company with a market capitalisation below:","options":["£50 million","£100 million","£200 million","£270 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"With respect to the provision of research under UK MiFID, the following new rules came into effect from 1 March 2022 under  the Inducement Rules for small or medium enterprises. Research on companies with a market capitalisation of below £200 million will be exempt – meaning that UK-based fund managers do not have to pay for this research. UK asset managers can continue to pay for this research if they so wish – due to operational limitations."},{"question":"Which of the following are not protected by the conduct of business rules sourcebook?","options":["Personal recommendations to a retail client","Pension transfers for retail client","Acting as investment manager for a retail client","Property purchases by a retail client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The purchase of property is not classed as designated investment business."},{"question":"A firm produces research with the sole intent of making this the focus of a recommendation to clients. When presenting such research recommendations, the firm must be considered as being bound by:","options":["Financial Promotion Rules","Distance Promotions Directives","Product Disclosure Rules","Client Asset Rules"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"All research recommendations are considered as being non-independent; research is to be considered as a marketing tool and so as a financial promotion."},{"question":"A firm plans to telephone a prospective client without prior appointment to discuss an investment. In which circumstance may the call be made under the cold calling rules?","options":["The recipient has an existing client relationship and would expect such a call","The recipient has seen the firm's website and may recognise the firm name","The recipient has previously invested elsewhere and understands investment risk","The recipient has been referred by another client and is likely to be interested"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm must not cold call unless one of the permitted conditions applies. One permitted condition is where the recipient has an existing client relationship with the firm and would envisage or expect to receive such a call."},{"question":"The financial promotion rules do not apply to marketing communications in respect of which one of the following?","options":["A predator company posting details of a takeover to shareholders","An authorised firm communicating details of derivative investments to clients","An independent financial adviser communicating its services in relation to pensions","An insurance company communicating a financial promotion regarding life policies"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Communications regarding takeovers are subject to the takeover code, not the FCA Financial Promotions Rules."},{"question":"A firm has just executed an order for a retail client. Within what period of time must the confirmation of trade be sent?","options":["Immediately","Within one business day","Within five business days","Within seven calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The notice must be given as soon as possible and not later than one business day as specified in COBS."},{"question":"What must be disclosed in all written communication with a retail client?\nIThe name and business address of the firm making the communication\nIIThe fair and prominent indication of any relevant risks\nIIISufficient information that is clear to the average person likely to receive it\nIVInformation that is consistent with the information that is in the prospectus","options":["I and II","II and III","I, II, III","I, II, III, IV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must ensure that where they provide information about designated investment business, or issue and/or approve a financial promotion which is likely to be received by a retail client, they adhere to certain rules in COBS. These rules state that the:\n\n• firm’s name is included on the communication\n\n• information is accurate and does not emphasise potential benefits without also giving fair and prominent indication of any relevant risks\n\n• information is sufficient for, and presented in a way likely to be understood by, the average member of the group at whom it is directed or by whom it is likely to be received, and\n\n• information does not disguise, diminish, or obscure important items, statements or warnings."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT a requirement for an elective professional client status according to the Conduct of Business rules?","options":["The client has carried out relevant trades at least ten times per quarter for the last four quarters","The value of the client's portfolio, including all financial assets, exceeds 500,000 euros","The client has been working in the financial sector in a professional capacity for at least one year","The client's cash deposits are in excess of 20,000 euros"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There is no explicit requirement on the size of the client's cash deposits. The requirement is that the portfolio, including cash deposits must be greater than 500,000 euros."},{"question":"Limit orders not executed immediately must be made public to:","options":["ESMA","A Primary information provider","A regulated market","The FCA"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Orders must be made public to a regulated/unregulated market or an MTF."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, how many transactions per quarter (over the previous four quarters) must a client have carried out in order to be treated as an elective professional client?","options":["50","25","10","5"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"One of the criteria in the quantitative test is average frequency of transaction of ten per quarter."},{"question":"All of the following could be considered a professional client. Which of them is MOST LIKELY to have reached that status through election?","options":["Local authority","National body that manages public debt","Limited liability partnership","Designated investment exchange"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The others would all be considered per se professional clients. Local authorities are per se retail clients."},{"question":"An authorised firm communicates a financial promotion on a specific product containing past performance figures. Which of the following is true?","options":["Where the product does not have five years performance, the firm is not permitted to show past performance","A clear and prominent warning must assure investors the past performance is a reliable indicator of future performance","The past performance figures must be cited as gross and exclude the impact of fees and commissions","A warning about the effects of currency fluctuations must be included, if the figures are in a currency other than the client's domestic currency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Firms must ensure that information including indications of past performance is such that:\n- the past performance is not the most prominent feature\n- it covers at least the immediately preceding five years (or the whole period for which the investment has been offered, and/or the financial index has been established, and/or the service has been provided if this is less than five years); in any event, it must show complete 12-month periods\n- reference periods and relevant sources are clearly shown\n- there is a clear and prominent warning that the data and/or figures refer to the investment's past performance and that past performance is not a reliable indicator of future results\n- if the figures are in a currency other than that of the EEA state in which the client is resident, that the currency is stated and there is a warning about the possible effects of currency fluctuations\n- if the performance is cited gross, that the effects of commissions, fees, tax and other charges are disclosed."},{"question":"A firm communicates a financial promotion for an overseas firm. Which additional statement may be required in the promotion?","options":["It may need to explain the extent to which UK compensation scheme arrangements will apply","It may need to confirm that all overseas promotions are approved by the FCA","It may need to state that UK conduct rules apply in full to the overseas firm","It may need to guarantee that retail clients will have identical legal protections"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A promotion for an overseas firm must, if relevant, explain the extent to which UK compensation scheme arrangements will be available and, if they will not, that fact. The other options overstate the position or impose requirements not set out in the rules."},{"question":"When can a retail client expect to receive information regarding their right to cancel an agreement to purchase units in an authorised unit trust?","options":["Before entering into the agreement at all times","After entering into the agreement at all times","Before and after entering into the agreement at all times","Before or after entering into the agreement depending on the situation"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"If cancellation rights apply, firms must tell consumers of their rights in good time before (or where that is not possible, immediately after) the consumer is bound by the contract; and they must do so in a durable medium."},{"question":"What is the minimum amount of net turnover that a trust must have to be classified as professional client for MiFID business?","options":["€100 million","€75 million","€50 million","€40 million"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"MiFID requires at least €40 million in addition to at least one other of the quantitative tests.\nThe other quantitative tests are:\n- net assets €20m - own funds €2m\nThis is true of any undertaking, whether a company, charity or trust."},{"question":"Who performs the regulatory check of a mail-shot sent by an appointed representative?","options":["The compliance department of the appointing firm","The marketing department of the appointing firm","The overseer of the appointed representative who authorised the mail shot","The Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The general non-real time financial promotion rules require each promotion to be signed off by compliance or another employee with sufficient knowledge to do so."},{"question":"A firm plans an unsolicited real-time call to promote an investment product to a retail client. Which product may be promoted under the cold calling rules?","options":["A life policy linked to a higher-volatility fund","A generally marketable non-geared packaged product","A warrant traded on a recognised investment exchange","A readily realisable security issued by a public company"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm may cold call where the call relates to a generally marketable packaged product, provided it is neither a higher-volatility fund nor a life policy linked to such a fund. A warrant is excluded from the limited range of readily realisable investments, and the other products listed do not fall within the permitted categories for unsolicited real-time communications"},{"question":"A retail client who is habitually resident overseas does not wish to be sent a basic agreement letter for insurance business. The firm should:","options":["Report them to the FCA","Send them the client agreement regardless","Not send them a client agreement","Not do business with them"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Effecting insurance contracts is an exception to the rule requiring retail clients to be sent a client agreement. The location of the client is irrelevant."},{"question":"Under FCA rules on client categorisation, which of the following would ALWAYS be considered a per se professional client?","options":["Special purpose vehicle","A public company","A public body","An overseas individual"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Special purpose vehicles come under the definition of 'any other institutional investor'. The other choices would need to fulfil certain criteria before qualifying as a per se professional client."},{"question":"When communicating with retail clients, a firm producing information must ensure that:\nIThe name of the firm is included\nIIThe information does not emphasise any potential benefits of relevant business without risks being related\nIIIIt is presented in a manner that is likely to be understood by the average member of the target group\nIVIt does not obscure important items","options":["II only","I, II and III","I, III and IV","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All of these are included in the General Rule of COBS."},{"question":"A firm provides personal recommendations to a retail client. After ensuring suitability, in which of the following situations would the firm need to provide the client with a suitability report?","options":["When a firm manages assets in a regulated CIS","When the firm recommends the investor increases their regular premiums to an existing pension policy","When the firm recommends the investor to invest in an ISA wrapped investment trust","When an investor purchases a \"small-life policy\" sold by friendly society with a premium no more than £50"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Suitability reports are needed for recommendations to deal in regulated collective investment schemes - unless the firm is acting as investment manager for the client - investment trusts (whether held in an ISA or not) and pensions."},{"question":"Which of the following will be included in a trade confirmation notice?\nIThe date on which the trade took place\nIIThe time at which the trade took place\nIIIThe total consideration paid/received in the transaction\nIVExecution venue","options":["I and II only","III only","I and IV","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All of these are necessary."},{"question":"In order to gain favourable terms for its clients, a firm aggregates several client orders with its own order. During execution, only part of the order is filled. How must the order be allocated?","options":["In a pro rata fashion","On a first in, first out fashion","Based on the relative size of order","In line with the allocation policy"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The pro rata etc. might be part of the allocation policy."},{"question":"In the description of an investment within a financial promotion, in light of the 'fair, clear and not misleading' rule, the firm should always include which of the following?","options":["A clear representation of the long term yield figure of the investment alone","A clear representation of the short term yield figure of the investment alone","A single figure for the charges of the firm irrespective of their source","If fees cannot be specifically given, the basis on which these fees are assessed should be presented"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The firm should provide a yield figure that gives a balanced impression of both long and short term prospects. If the charging structure is complex then the fees should be explained in such a manner as to be fair, clear and not misleading to the recipient."},{"question":"Martin gives an instruction to a stockbroking firm to sell a holding of shares at a limit price much higher than the firm is willing to buy. Assuming that there have been no specific instructions beyond the order itself, which of the following best reflects what the firm should do next?","options":["Wait for the market to move to the limit price and then execute","Report Martin to the Regulator for market abuse","Execute immediately at the price it is willing to trade","Place the order on a regulated market"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"This question looks at the limit orders rule. Limit orders must be executed immediately or made public. As the firm is unwilling to execute the trade, they must place the order on a regulated market such as the LSE. The comment about \"assuming that there were no specific instructions\" is a reference to an exemption to this rule if the client has expressly stated that they do not want the orders to be made public: This exemption has not been triggered in this question."},{"question":"An authorised firm provides a service to a retail client. The FCA requires that the firm discloses its charges to that client. When should this disclosure occur?","options":["At the time of doing business","Before doing business","After a reasonable period of doing business","At the discretion of the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Charges must be made clear to clients before doing business or before the client is bound by the terms."},{"question":"In relation to the conduct of business rules in respect of disclosure of conflicts of interest, which ONE of the following is FALSE of the disclosure?","options":["It must be in writing","It can be made verbally","It must be made before acting on behalf of the client","It must be made in a durable medium"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The disclosure of a conflict of interest must be made in a durable manner and therefore cannot be made verbally.\nDetails of the conflict of interest must be disclosed to the client prior to acting on behalf of the client."},{"question":"If the information presented by a firm contains an indication of simulated past performance, then which of the following is appropriate?","options":["The simulated past performance must relate to a derivatives index that can react quickly to any movement in the underlying asset","The actual past performance of the investment should be ignored in order to avoid misleading the client","Simulated past performance is based on actual past performance of investments that are the same as the investments being simulated","A statement that the simulated past performance is a reliable indicator of the future performance of the instrument"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"When firms give figures based on simulated (ie, notional, not having taken place in reality) past performance because the product or service does not have a track record, a firm must ensure that the simulated past performance figures:\n\n• relate to an investment or financial index (not necessarily a derivatives index)\n\n• are based on the actual past performance of one or more investments and/or indices which are the same as, or underlie, the investments being simulated\n\n• meet the rules set out in relation to past performance (except for the statement that they relate to that investment’s past performance, since they do not)\n\n• contain a prominent warning that they relate to simulated past performance and that past performance is not a reliable indicator of future performance."},{"question":"Which of the following statements regarding Chinese walls are false?","options":["They are a method by which a firm can avoid conflicts of interests","They may be physical or artificial barriers preventing the flow of information","The FCA specifies that Chinese walls must be implemented by investment firms","They may be operated by investment firms with several departments"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"FCA regulations require that companies implement systems and procedures to prevent conflicts of interest. Yet the use of Chinese walls is not mandatory. This is because in certain situations (e.g. a sole trader) Chinese walls would not be necessary."},{"question":"If a firm is aware that Bob is acting as an agent for Bill, then Bill will be the firm's client if:","options":["Bob and the firm have agreed in writing to treat Bill as the client","Bill and the firm have agreed in writing to treat Bill as the client","Bill and Bob have agreed in writing that Bill will be treated as a client","Only if all three agree in writing"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"This is given in COBS. It would also be the case if Bob were not a firm or overseas financial institution and the main purpose were to avoid the firm's responsibilities to Bill."},{"question":"The FCA rules on financial promotions expand which Principle for Businesses?","options":["Market Conduct","Financial Prudence","Customers' Interests","Clients' Assets"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It is important that a firm has their clients' interests in mind when they communicate financial promotions."},{"question":"In which of the following situations would the firm need to apply the client money rules? If the money:","options":["Is paid by the client as part of a transaction due for settlement within one business day","Paid by the client is due to the firm for the firm's own account","Is interest paid on any holdings of client money","Has been paid to the client or authorised representative of the client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Client money rules govern money held by an authorised firm that belongs to a client, held in a client account. Money held by a firm is either 'client money' or 'firm's money'. \n\nWithin a delivery versus payment, if the money is due within three business days it is not necessary to count the money as client money. Once it is paid to a client then the money ceases to be client money. Although there is no obligation to pay interest on client money, if interest is paid, it needs to be treated as client money.\n\nIf money has been paid to a client, then it has left the firm's control and is now no longer client money"},{"question":"The COBS relating to financial promotions require an individual with appropriate expertise to check that the promotion is fair, clear and not misleading. For which of the following types of financial promotion is such a confirmation exercise required in advance of communicating the promotion?","options":["Real-time financial promotions","Non real-time financial promotions","All financial promotions","Real-time and non real-time financial promotions"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Only non-real time financial promotions can be approved in advance of the communication.\n\nReal time financial promotions are spontaneous and it would be very difficult to approve in advance."},{"question":"Which clients need to have the most comprehensive risk explanation provided to them?","options":["A discretionary retail client selling shares to the firm","An execution only retail client selling warrants","A discretionary professional client engaged in derivatives","An execution only retail client buying options"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The risks should be explained to the client as specified by the client's knowledge etc. given that the first two are sales of assets pre-owned. A professional client would most likely not have a significant explanation of the risks given the requirements, as they are considered to have sufficient knowledge and experience."},{"question":"An investor opens a cash ISA with an authorised bank to benefit from the tax advantages offered. What is the minimum cancellation period the bank must offer?","options":["5 business days","10 business days","7 calendar days","14 calendar days"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The cancellation period for cancellable contracts is generally 14 days. However, for life policies and some pensions it is 30 days."},{"question":"If a firm communicates with a client solely by telephone, when must information on any agreement be provided?","options":["In good time before the consumer is bound by a distance offer or contract","Within seven calendar days after the conclusion of the contract","Within five business days after the conclusion of the contract","There would be no need to provide documentation for this type of communication"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In addition it must comply with the distance marketing rules and be in a durable medium."},{"question":"When a professional client is initially classified, which of the following terms is used?","options":["Person","Per se","Automatic","Elective"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A professional client will initially be classified as a per se client. They have to choose to be considered as an elective professional client."},{"question":"During the provision of a service to a professional client, a firm discovers a conflict of interest that it is unable to manage. What should the firm disclose?","options":["Nothing to the client, so as to respect client confidentiality","The general nature of any possible conflicts after the transactions were carried out","The general nature of the conflicts after the transactions, in a durable medium","The sources of the conflicts before any transactions proceed, in a durable medium"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The disclosure must also use sufficient detail to allow the client to make an informed decision with respect to the services provided."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the cancelation period offered to a client of the firm?","options":["Should be offered on all products","Optional on all products","Compulsory for certain products","Always a maximum of 14 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Reflection periods of 14 or 30 days have to be offered when the firm sells packaged products or cash deposit ISAs."},{"question":"A firm receives a client limit order in shares admitted to trading on a regulated market, but market conditions prevent immediate execution. What must the firm do?","options":["Send the order to the Financial Conduct Authority for immediate review","Record the order internally for later execution when prices improve","Make the order public immediately in a way accessible to other market participants","Aggregate the order with house business for execution at the next dealing point"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"An unexecuted client limit order in shares admitted to trading on a regulated market must be made public immediately unless the client has instructed otherwise. The other options do not satisfy the obligation to publicise the order."},{"question":"All are true in respect of churning and switching except:","options":["The objective is to earn commission income","Neither are generally considered in the interests of the client","The rules on churning and switching apply to retail clients only for advisory services and any client for discretionary services","Dealing may only take place if it would not be regarded as too frequent in the circumstances and is justified from the customer's point of view"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Churning and switching are still considered against the interests of the clients. The FCA provides them as guidance to the suitability rules, rather than rules themselves. They cover advisory and discretionary services for both retail and professional clients."},{"question":"Which of the following is client money?","options":["A transaction fee due to the firm","Money held by an approved bank","Settlement charge due to the firm","Money held in excess to a client's obligations"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Any money 'due to the firm' is not client money. Approved banks do not need to treat money deposited with them as client money.\nWhere a firm sells assets held as security against client obligations, the amount equal to the obligation is the firm's money. However, anything in excess of this obligation has to be returned to the client or treated as client money."},{"question":"To be treated as an elective eligible counterparty for MiFID business, a company must satisfy which conditions using the net turnover and/or funds criteria?","options":["Net turnover of at least €20 million","Company funds of at least €40 million","Net turnover of at least €20 million and company funds of at least €2 million","Net turnover of at least €40 million and company funds of at least €2 million"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"These are two of the quantitative requirements that must be met by a company to be an 'elective eligible counterparty'."},{"question":"Under the FCA Financial Promotions Rules a firm is permitted to disclose its opinion of the future performance of an investment. Which of the following is not permitted when taking advantage of this?","options":["A statement about the unreliability of forecasts","Projecting future performance from simulated past performance","Stating the assumptions on which the projection is based","Using past performance to predict the future performance"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Indications of future performance cannot be based on simulated past performance."},{"question":"What must the firm's conflict of interest policy ensure?","options":["All groups within the firm are unable to discuss investment business with each other","All groups have sufficient guards on the information flow between each other","Measures to ensure that the relevant persons cannot find themselves on the wrong side of a Chinese wall","Chinese walls exist"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Clearly wall crossing is possible, but once it occurs it must be dealt with. Chinese walls are not a necessity: the FCA has them as a recommendation. The first choice (A) is suggesting that all information flows are impossible without conflicts of interest and this is excessive."},{"question":"Which one of the following, if unsolicited, would NOT be regarded as a cold call under the financial promotion rules?","options":["An email","A phone call","A personal visit","A conversation in the street"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A cold call is an unsolicited unwritten financial promotion. Emails are written communications - so cannot fall in the category of 'cold call'."},{"question":"Which one of the following would automatically be treated as a per-se professional client for MiFID business?","options":["A company with a balance sheet of €13m and a turnover of €30m","A Trust with a balance sheet of €22m and own funds of €1.5m","A company with own funds of €2.5m and a balance sheet of €12.5m","An authorised firm with a balance sheet of €18m and own funds of €32m"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All authorised/regulated firms are treated as per se professional for both MiFID and Non-MiFID business.\nThe quantitative tests for a large undertaking (company) to be treated as a per se professional for MiFID business are:\n(2 out of 3)\n- Minimum balance sheet: €20m\n- Minimum turn-over: €40m\n- Minimum own funds: €2m."},{"question":"A firm has searched through its client list, looking for those that have been inactive for some time. They plan to cold call these clients. If these clients are retail clients, which of the following products are permitted to be sold on the cold call?","options":["A generally marketable low volatility packaged product","A life policy linked to high volatility funds","A firm cannot sell any product on a cold call to a retail client","Shares in an inverse leveraged exchange traded fund"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The others are prohibited - either due to the increased risk through volatility or gearing, or the derivative aspects of the products. Where it comes to shares, they must be readily realisable. It is also worth noting that it is only the services relating to these shares that can be sold on the cold call, not the shares themselves."},{"question":"An authorised firm wishes to increase its retail client base by launching a cold calling campaign. Which of the following would be permitted?","options":["An insurance company marketing life assurance contracts with a high volatility element","A securities broker marketing warrants traded on the London Stock Exchange","A wealth management company marketing advisory services about main market shares","Marketing through cold calls is not permitted to retail clients under any circumstance"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cold calls can be used to sell services that relate to liquid securities. Listed shares would meet this definition. Note: the securities themselves could not be sold on the cold call."},{"question":"As an employee of an authorised firm, you are just about to make a personal recommendation to a client. Before doing so you must ensure that the investment:","options":["Has no risk","Is compatible with the client's level of experience and understanding of the risks involved","Contains only packaged products in the case of retail clients","Allows the client to take advantage of gearing opportunities whenever available"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Clearly portfolios may have risk. The opportunity of gearing opportunities is too strong in this statement. Retail clients are able to invest in more than only packaged products."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is true with respect to cancellation rights on the sale to a retail client of a packaged product? Cancellation rights:","options":["Are not required","Should be offered","Of 21 days are compulsory","May be offered for a minimum period specified by the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is the best answer. Depending upon the type of product, cancellation periods can vary between 14 and 30 calendar days."},{"question":"When a firm communicates initially with a retail client, COBS requires it to provide 'appropriate information', which includes all of the following except:","options":["The firm's name and address","A statement that it is FCA authorised or regulated","The name and status of the person they can contact","Who to refer complaints to if they cannot be resolved by the firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Reference to any complaints resolution body is not necessary."},{"question":"In relation to the following communication which ONE could an authorised firm approve on behalf of a firm which is NOT authorised?","options":["Personal visit","Magazine advert","Telephone conversation","A webcam conversation"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An authorised firm cannot approve any communication which cannot be provided in advance i.e. telephone conversation, conversations during a personal visit or meeting or other interactive dialogues.\nAn authorised firm could approve a magazine advert as this can be approved in advance of it being communicated to the client."},{"question":"In the case of MiFID business, a firm must keep a copy of the periodic reports for:","options":["At least three years from the beginning of the relevant year","A period of five years from the date of dispatch","A period of three years from the date of dispatch","At least five years from the beginning of the relevant year"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MiFID generally requires records be kept for five years from the date of dispatch to the client."},{"question":"What is MOST likely to be covered by the FCA rules on financial promotions?","options":["One-off financial promotions","Promotion to investment professionals","Promotion aimed at private investors","Promotion to a sophisticated investor"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Retail clients (of which 'private investors' are an example) are protected by the financial promotion rules contained within the COBS. One-off financial promotions, promotions to investment professionals, and sophisticated investors are normally exempt."},{"question":"A client requests an investment manager to carry out a trade in a listed security. Who is responsible for the transaction report?","options":["The client","Investment manager","London Stock Exchange","UKLA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As a member firm the investment manager is required to file a transaction report."},{"question":"How do the conduct of business rules apply to an appointed representative of a firm?","options":["Directly","Indirectly","Not at all","Only regarding the financial promotion rules"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA cannot impose the COBS on appointed representatives directly, but obliges the firm whom they represent (their principal) to ensure that they behave appropriately."},{"question":"When a firm uses a website to promote a product and gives the facility for an online application this would be considered to be?","options":["Indirect real-time offer","Direct real-time offer","Direct non real-time offer","Indirect non real-time offer"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Website promotions are deemed to be non-real time financial promotions."},{"question":"From which of the following must a firm keep client money segregated?","options":["Money belonging to the firm","Money belonging to the firm and eligible counterparties","The firm and other retail client money","The firm and all other client money"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The aim of the rules is to keep client money segregated from that of the firm."},{"question":"What information must always be included in a firm's order execution policy for retail clients?","options":["The process the firm uses to specify relative importance of the execution factors","The exchange that the transaction will definitely be traded on","The price that the firm will get for the transaction","The foreign exchange rate that will be used in the transaction"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The firm will include a list of POSSIBLE venues... not necessarily the specific exchange. It will not have to specify the price nor the FX rate in the execution policy details."},{"question":"A firm has given a personal recommendation to a client. For which of the following would a suitability report NOT be required?","options":["A life policy for a retail client where a savings element is included","A transfer of, or opt-out from, an occupational pension scheme","Transactions in a regulated collective scheme for a professional client","A withdrawal by a retail client from short-term annuity"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Suitability reports are only required for retail clients in respect of investment products. There is an assumption that the members of occupational pension schemes are retail clients."},{"question":"A UK branch of a third-country firm executes a transaction outside the UK. Which statement best describes its obligation to report that transaction to the Financial Conduct Authority?","options":["It must report in every case by the next business day","It must report only if settlement occurs in the UK","It does not have to report because there is no UK effect","It does not have to report if the client is a UK professional client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Transactions made by UK branches of third-country firms do not have to be reported to the FCA when executed outside the UK. However, exceptions may apply depending on whether an individual based in the UK branch was involved in execution, pricing or client engagement."},{"question":"If an eligible counterparty requests to be dealt with for certain transactions as a professional client, what may the firm do?","options":["Ignore the request as an eligible counterparty is always treated as such","Treat them as a retail client for all the relevant transactions","Treat them as a professional client","Refer the eligible counterparty to the FCA for suspected market abuse"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is made explicit in COBS. The eligible counterparty is 'opting down' to professional client."},{"question":"A technology firm sells its shares to the public via the internet without the need to produce a prospectus. Which of the following rules would govern this offer?","options":["AIM rules for growth companies","COBS financial promotions rules","FCA prospectus regulation rules","Recognised investment exchange rules"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Normally a company offering new shares would be subject to prospectus regulation rules. However, this only applies if a prospectus is required. In this case, the COBS financial promotion rules will apply."},{"question":"According to the conduct of business rules and in relation to MiFID business, a firm must have in place a client agreement when it carries on:","options":["Designated investment business for a retail client only","Eligible counterparty business","Any regulated activity for a retail client and a professional client only","Designated investment business for a retail client and MiFID business for a professional client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Designated Investment Business is made up of MiFID and non-MiFID business. In respect of MiFID business, both retail and professional are protected. RC get protected for all designated investment business.\n\nNOTE: whilst designated investment business includes MiFID and non-MiFID business NOT all non-MiFID business is designated investment business. For example, bank deposits and pure protection insurance is not designated investment business."},{"question":"Which of the following could be classified as communicating a financial promotion?\nIInternet website\nIIMailshot\nIIICall centre making calls\nIVSales aid","options":["I, II and III","I, II, III and IV","II and IV","II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"All could be used for communicating financial promotions."},{"question":"According to COBS 4, the financial promotion rules always apply to a firm in relation to the communication of a financial promotion:","options":["Regarding a home purchase plan","Directed to an eligible counterparty only","In respect of designated investment business","Promoting a non-investment insurance contract"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The rules in COBS Chapter 4 apply to firms communicating with clients regarding their designated investment business and communicating or approving a financial promotion exemptions apply:\n• for qualifying credit, a home purchase plan or a home reversion plan\n• a promotion for a non-investment insurance contract, or\n• the promotion of an unregulated CIS which it is not permitted to approve.\n\nThe majority of the financial promotion rules do not apply to eligible counterparties."},{"question":"A firm wishes to execute a client order outside a regulated market or multilateral trading facility. When would this be permitted under the FCA conduct of business rules?","options":["This would never be permitted","It is always permitted","If there is a written notification sent to the client","If there is prior consent from the client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In order for there to be an extra-regulated market transaction, the firm must get the prior consent from its client."},{"question":"The firm is holding money belonging to clients. When would it NOT need to comply with the client money rules? If the money is:","options":["Coins held for the value of their metal","Held in a central bank","Interest paid by a Financial Conduct Authority authorised firm","Being held in a money market deposit account"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A firm does not need to apply the client money rules to coins held for the value of their metal on behalf of clients."},{"question":"A firm outsources part of its investment service to another provider. Which control is required for personal account dealing under the outsourcing arrangement?","options":["The outsourcing firm must maintain a record of personal transactions and provide it promptly on request","The outsourcing firm must seek annual approval from the Financial Conduct Authority for its monitoring procedures","The outsourcing firm must disclose all personal transactions to affected clients at the end of each quarter","The outsourcing firm must prohibit all relevant persons from dealing in designated investments"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Where outsourcing takes place, the arrangements must ensure the outsourcing firm keeps a record of personal transactions undertaken by any relevant person and provides it promptly on request. The other options introduce requirements that are not part of the rule."},{"question":"Which of the following is not a requirement for recategorisation as a professional client according to conduct of business rules in relation to MiFID business?","options":["The firm has ascertained that the client has sufficient experience and understanding and passes the quantitative tests","The firm has given a written warning of protections that will be lost","The client has given written consent to the protections that will be lost","The client has stated they are of sufficient experience, have sufficient understanding and pass the quantitative tests"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The customer must give written consent after it has received a written warning from the firm about lost protection, which the firm will only send if it believes the customer has sufficient experience and understanding. The onus is on the firm to assess."},{"question":"When is it allowable for an unauthorised person to communicate a financial promotion?","options":["After the unauthorised person has fulfilled the compliance requirements under the relevant COBS rules","At any time as long as the promotion is in relation to designated investment business","Takes reasonable care that it communicates it to any type of recipient regardless of was intended at the time of the confirmation","After reasonable care has been made to establish that an authorised firm has confirmed that it complies with the rules"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An unauthorised firm is not in breach of the rules if it communicates a financial promotion that has been produced by another party and:\n• takes reasonable care to establish that another authorised firm has confirmed that the promotion complies with the rules\n• takes reasonable care that it communicates it only to the type of recipient that was intended at the time of the confirmation\n• as far as it is (or should be) aware, the promotion is still fair, clear and not misleading, and has not been withdrawn by the other party."},{"question":"A firm is considering putting its own buy order on the same ticket as one of its client's buy orders. Which of the following is true before the firm does this?","options":["The firm has obtained consent from the client that this is acceptable","The firm truly believes that by aggregating the order, the terms of the trade will be improved","The firm has disclosed to the client that aggregation may operate to the disadvantage of clients","A firm may aggregate orders if it wishes"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A disclosure that aggregation may operate to the disadvantage clients is essential."},{"question":"When entering into a distance marketing contract for a life policy, how much time must a retail client be given to cancel the contract if they wish to do so?","options":["14 calendar days","14 business days","30 calendar days","30 business days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Cancellation is usually 14 calendar days, but for life policies (and pensions) it is 30 calendar days."},{"question":"A firm receives an order from a retail client, but there are problems in carrying out the instruction. When must a firm inform the client about this problem?","options":["Immediately","Within one business day","Within the expected settlement period","With the trade confirmation"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The COBS specifies that retail clients must be informed of any material difficulty in the prompt execution of their order,\nimmediately on the firm becoming aware of this."},{"question":"Where a communication by a firm contains information about past performance, assuming an existing product that has been in existence for 25 years, this must cover a minimum period of:","options":["One year","Two years","Five years","Ten years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Any performance figures used in a financial promotions must cover at least the immediately preceding five years (or the whole period for which the investment has been offered, and/or the financial index has been established, and/or the service has been provided if this is less than five years).\n\nFor a product that is 25 years old, the minimum performance data that needs to be shown is 5 years."},{"question":"A firm may treat a client as an elective professional client for non-MiFID business if:","options":["The qualitative test alone is passed","The quantitative test alone is passed","Both the quantitative and qualitative tests are passed","The qualitative test is passed and there is a statement that protection will be lost"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The quantitative test is only used for MiFID business. The qualitative test and a statement of protection loss are always required. The client must state that it wishes to be treated as a professional client, the firm must warn the client that the loss of protection might occur and the client has to state, in writing, separate to any contracts, that it is aware of this loss of protection."},{"question":"The website of a firm shows two products; details on one are available only by post and of the other only online. Which of the two is a written financial promotion?","options":["Both","Neither","The one available by post only","The one available online only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Both products are considered written financial promotions."},{"question":"In which of the following situations would a firm need to perform appropriateness checks?","options":["For all services in all products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in all products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in complex products provided to both retail and professional clients","For non-advised services in complex products provided to retail clients only"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Appropriateness rules protect all customer (both retail and profession) in respect of non-advised services in complex products. Complex products are best thought of as derivatives and other contingent liability investments or highly illiquid or opaque investment."},{"question":"A firm has received several execution only client orders for purchases in Company A. The firm also has an order to purchase shares in that company. Which ONE of the following statements is FALSE in respect of the execution of these orders?","options":["The firm may aggregate the orders if it is unlikely that the aggregation will work to the disadvantage of any client","The firm can only rely on aggregation if the orders cannot be executed sequentially","If aggregation occurs, the firm must disclose to the client that the effect of aggregation may work to their disadvantage","The firm should aggregate the trade if it is in the client's best interests"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Client orders can be aggregated with each other or with the firm's orders provided that certain criteria are met.\nA firm is not required to perform aggregation."},{"question":"Which of the following communications would not have to follow the COBS promotions rules?","options":["A telephone communication requested by an existing client to promote readily realisable securities","An advert in a national newspaper communicating the logo of the firm, a contact point and the regulated activities provided","A video presentation sent out to potential clients promoting investments in UK government gilts","A direct offer communication to promote investment in derivatives"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"All would need to follow the communications rules except the one simply showing the firm and its services. These general types of promotions are often referred to as image advertising."},{"question":"When a firm trades with an eligible counterparty, which one of the following rules does not apply?","options":["Selling and advising","Personal account notices","Client assets","Client classification"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"COBS have a limited application to eligible counterparties and the advising and selling regulations do not apply."},{"question":"A firm is approached by an authorised collective investment scheme (CIS) in relation to designated investment business. Which of the following best represents the conditions under which the CIS will get classified as a professional client?","options":["Turnover of €40 million and own funds of €2 million","Turnover of €40 million and a balance sheet of €15 million","It is always classified as a professional client","It can never be classified as a professional client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Authorised collective investment schemes are per se professional clients."},{"question":"According to client asset rules (CASS), a firm may deposit client money in which of the following?","options":["A central bank","A credit granting institution unauthorised by the BCD","A non-EEA registered credit granting institution","A money market instrument"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under the Client Asset Rules, client money can be deposited in a central bank, a BCD credit institution (one that is authorised through the Banking Consolidation Directive), a qualifying money market fund or a bank authorised in a third country."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following needs to be included in a firm's order execution policy?","options":["Standard settlement instructions","Costs and charges","Prices","Execution venues"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The firm must include information about the different execution venues that it uses.\nThe firm must include details of the execution venues that consistently achieve the best possible results for their clients."},{"question":"Eligible counterparty status is not available to:","options":["An insurance company","A supranational organisation","A credit institution","An expert individual client"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The others are per se eligible counterparties. Individuals cannot become eligible counterparties, no matter what their knowledge and experience."},{"question":"When providing which of the following activities must a retail client be assessed for appropriate levels of knowledge by the firm?","options":["An execution only share transaction","Executing a deal on an equity warrant","Recommending a foreign exchange spot transaction","Arranging a transaction on a corporate bond"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Appropriateness needs to be assessed and risks need to be highlighted for non-advised services in complex products. Non-advised services include execution only and arranging. Complex products include derivatives and warrants."},{"question":"To whom do the client money rules apply?","options":["All clients","All customers","Retail clients only","Professional clients only"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Client Money Rules are part of the Client Asset Rules and they apply to all clients."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of an eligible counterparty?","options":["They are only categorised as such for certain business","They can be individuals who have opted up from an elective professional client","They can be offered the same protection under COBS as a retail client","Many of the COBS rules are disapplied for eligible counterparties"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Only large undertakings can opt up from professional clients. Eligible counterparties are only considered as such for eligible counterparty business. It is possible for an eligible counterparty to request the same protection under COBS as a retail client."},{"question":"A firm designs a new investment product for first-time retail investors. Which action best demonstrates the Products and Services Outcome in practice?","options":["Targeting the product at clearly identified customer groups with matching needs and objectives","Offering the product to all client categories to maximise product awareness and understanding","Reviewing the product when the regulator requests evidence of compliance or customer harm","Promoting the product through generic materials ensuring target market is omitted for confidentiality"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Products and Services Outcome requires products and services to be designed to meet the needs and objectives of the target group. Product providers must identify a target market and carry out regular reviews, while distributors must market consistently with that target market."},{"question":"An analyst at an authorised firm makes a trade for their personal account in the units of a UCITS collective investment scheme. According to the FCA rules, how soon should they contact their compliance team to inform them of any increase in the number of units they hold?","options":["Immediately irrespective of the analyst's relationship with the CIS","Within one business day if the analyst has no relationship with the CIS","Immediately if the analyst has no relationship with the CIS","There is no need if the analyst has no management relationship with the CIS"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"So long as the person is not involved with the management of the UCITS CIS undertaking then the personal account dealing rules do not apply."},{"question":"If a person associated with the firm wishes to make a cold call, they must:","options":["Call between 9am and 5pm","Intend to discuss geared packaged products","Give a contact point for the client during the call","Not contact an existing client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The calls must be made at an appropriate time of day. Geared products are not considered appropriate of cold calls and existing clients can be called if they envisage a call. The client must be given a contact point, the identity of the individual and the firm and be allowed to terminate the conversation at their request."},{"question":"A firm discovers that an elective professional client's portfolio has reduced in size to less than €150,000 and the number of trades has been reduced to an average of four per quarter over the last 12 months. What actions should the firm take?","options":["None. The individual is a professional client and can be dealt with as such","Reclassify the individual as a retail client and notify the client of the action","Discuss the rationale for the change in behaviour and hold off reclassification for one quarter","Suggest that client increases their trading frequency or asks to be reclassified"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA states that the firm must take appropriate action if the client no longer meets the tests set down for opting up. The firm must, however, notify the client of this change in categorisation."},{"question":"If a financial analyst (a relevant person) is providing investment research for dissemination to its clients in particular financial investments, when would the analyst be permitted to deal on that information?","options":["They would never be able to deal on that information","They would always be allowed to deal on that information","They would be permitted to deal on the information if they submitted all confirmations of transactions to the firm's compliance","They would be permitted to deal on the information if granted approval by the firm's compliance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Although the analyst would be permitted to deal in this circumstance, it would be an exceptional circumstance for the firm's compliance to grant approval."},{"question":"The requirements for record keeping in respect of a firm conducting MiFID business is:","options":["Six years","Five years","Three years","Indefinitely"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"MiFID explicitly states that firms must keep records for a period of five years.\nIf the record sets out rights and obligations of the investment firm and the client, it should be kept for the duration of the relationship with the client. Competent authorities may require firms to keep these records for up to five years after that relationship ceases."},{"question":"When is the FCA's Conduct of Business Rule relating to suitability required? When the firm is performing:","options":["Discretionary MiFID services on behalf of retail and professional clients","Discretionary MiFID services on behalf of retail clients only","Execution only MiFID services on behalf of retail clients only","Execution only MiFID services on behalf of any customer"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Principle 9 requires firms to take reasonable care etc. for discretionary and advisory clients. In practice, this is achieved through compliance with the suitability rule. Suitability is required to be assessed for discretionary and advisory business, but not for execution-only business. The rule covers 'all customers' for MiFID business, and so therefore A is the best answer."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, how long must a firm retain records of trade confirmations sent to a client?","options":["Three years from date of trade","Three years from date of dispatch","Five years from date of dispatch","Five years from date of trade"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"In relation to MiFID business, trade confirmation records have to be kept for five years from date of dispatch."},{"question":"When carrying out client orders the firm must ensure:","options":["That professional clients must be informed of any material difficulties in executing the order","The order of execution is based on an ad-hoc policy","The order must be recorded within one business day","Comparable orders must be carried out sequentially except in specific conditions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Retail clients must be informed of problems. The execution policy must not be generated 'on the fly'. Orders must be promptly and accurately recorded - there is no specific timeframe given."},{"question":"An existing client approaches their firm and requests that they give him a lower level of protection. Which of the following would be appropriate action for the firm to take? The firm must:","options":["Offer this level of protection based on specific client instructions","Only offer this level of protection if the client in question is already deemed a professional client","Not offer this level of protection as if it were appropriate to do so it would have categorised the client as such already","First assess the client and then, if necessary, warn them of the additional risks"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A lower level of protection would involve a client asking for 'elective' status - or to opt up to the next category of client. This requires assessment by the firm involved before a decision is made."},{"question":"When a person is communicating a non-written financial promotion on behalf of an authorised firm which of the following must they always do?","options":["Identify himself and the regulator of the firm","Ensure the conversation is recorded and the tape kept securely","Ensure the call is made during office hours","Identify himself and the firm he represents"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The requirements for a non-written financial promotion are:\n1. Only do so at appropriate time of day - there is no requirement that this is made during office hours 2. Identify himself and the firm he represents at the outset and make clear the purpose of the call 3. Clarify that the client can continue with or terminate the communication at any time that he requests it 4. Give a contact point to any client with whom he arranges an appointment."},{"question":"A retail client would normally expect to get a periodic statement of their non-leveraged security based account at least:","options":["Annually","Every six months","Every three months","Whenever a transaction is executed"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"MiFID II has amended this rule. A retail client will generally get a periodic statement every three months. If the portfolio is leveraged then the periodic statement is at least monthly."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes Chinese walls? An arrangement that requires:","options":["Paperwork held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business to be withheld from, or not to be used by, persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business","Information held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business not to socialise with other persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business","Information held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business to be withheld from, or not to be used by, persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business","Information held by a person in the course of carrying on one part of its business not to talk to persons with or for whom it acts in the course of carrying on another part of its business"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This is the definition given in the FCA's glossary of terms."},{"question":"All of the following may be considered \"proper fees\" under the FCA inducements rules EXCEPT:","options":["Costs for safekeeping and administration","Legal fees","Charges for the settlement of transactions","The cost of general office software"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Proper fees necessary for the provision of the business (e.g. custody costs, settlement fees and legal fees) are permissible under the MiFID II inducement rules."},{"question":"A firm is assessing whether a benefit may be accepted as a minor non-monetary benefit under the inducements rules. Which benefit is specifically listed as acceptable?","options":["Participation in seminars on the features of a specific financial instrument","Corporate access services arranged for the firm by an execution broker","Administration services for the operation of a research payment account","Analysis of historical price data sourced through an information provider"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Participation in conferences, seminars and other training events on the benefits and features of a specific financial instrument or investment service as an acceptable minor non-monetary benefit. The other options are listed in the source as goods or services that the FCA does not consider to be research for RPA purposes, and they are not identified here as acceptable minor non-monetary benefits."},{"question":"According to COBS, which of the following is not considered an execution venue?","options":["Systematic internalisers","Retail broker","Multilateral trading facility","Regulated market"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For the purposes of the provisions relating to best execution in COBS and in COLL, execution venue means a regulated market, an MTF or OTF, a systematic internaliser, or a market maker or other liquidity provider or an entity that performs a similar function in a third country to the functions performed by any of the foregoing."},{"question":"In which of the following circumstances may a firm aggregate a client's order with an own account order?","options":["It is likely that the aggregation will not work to the disadvantage of each of the clients concerned","Whenever aggregation is required","The firm has disclosed orally or in writing to clients, either specifically or in the client agreement, that the effect will work to their advantage","Aggregation is never allowed"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The firm should believe that aggregation will not work to the disadvantage of the clients. However, they should also notify the clients that a disadvantage is possible. Firms should also have an aggregations policy."},{"question":"In relation to MiFID business, what minimum value must be exceeded by the portfolio of an elective professional client?","options":["250,000 euros","500,000 euros","1m euros","5m euros"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The portfolio has to be worth at least 500,000 euros."},{"question":"A firm provides portfolio management to a professional client and wishes to consume third-party research. Which payment method is permitted under the rules?","options":["Paying for the research directly from the firm's own resources","Receiving the research free where the provider also supplies execution services","Charging the cost through general commission with no separate identification","Recovering the cost through de minimus hospitality agreed with the research provider"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"In-scope firms may receive research only if they pay for it either directly from their own resources or from a research payment account. The other options do not meet the permitted payment methods set out in the research payment rules."},{"question":"If a client is unwilling to provide sufficient information for a firm to assess suitability in respect of a personal recommendation, what should the firm do next?","options":["Act for a professional client on a non-advised execution only basis as long as the client provides instruction for the trades","Send a suspicious transaction and order report on the client to the FCA for market abuse without delay","Make an appropriate recommendation or take a decision to trade for him provided the client has accepted a risk warning","Make an appropriate personal recommendation to the customer on a take it or leave it basis"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"There is no requirement to report the client to the FCA.\nIf the firm does NOT obtain sufficient information, it must not make a personal recommendation to the client or take a decision to trade for him.\nIt would not be possible for a firm to make a personal recommendation as it does not have sufficient information.\nThe firm can still act for a professional client on an execution only basis.\n\nThis question would work in exactly the same way if it was for a retail client instead"},{"question":"Which one of the following is not defined as the client of a firm?","options":["A client of an appointed representative of the firm","A person acting through an agent","A person to whom a financial promotion is communicated by the firm","A potential client"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The agent is considered to be the client of the firm unless agreed otherwise."},{"question":"Which of these is true in relation to comparisons provided in a promotion for a MiFID investment?","options":["A distinction must be made between absolute and proportional differences","The source of the data must be stated","The comparison must explain that the results depend on the individual circumstances of each client","The rules are most stringent for qualifying credit, home purchase plans or home reversion plans"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If comparisons are made, they must be meaningful and presented in a fair and balanced way. For MiFID business, the data sources for the comparisons must be cited, as must any key facts and assumptions used. If tax treatment is mentioned, firms must explain that this depends on the individual circumstances of\neach client and that it may be subject to change. Information included in financial promotions must be consistent with that given in the course of carrying on business. The rules do not apply for qualifying credit, home purchase plans or home reversion plans."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of the order execution policy?","options":["A firm must be able to demonstrate on request that it has followed the policy","A firm must notify clients about any material changes to their order execution arrangements","A firm must monitor the effectiveness of the policy on a transaction by transaction basis","The clients of the firm must consent to the order execution policy before the firm can deal for them"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It is true that the firm must monitor the effectiveness of the policy, but not on a trade by trade basis. The COBS states that it must be done 'regularly' and that the policy as a whole must be reviewed annually."},{"question":"A retail client buys an advised authorised unit trust and later decides to cancel. Within what period must the client exercise the cancellation right?","options":["Within 7 calendar days of receiving the cancellation notice","Within 7 business days of the transaction being executed","Within 14 calendar days of the transaction being executed","Within 14 calendar days of receiving the cancellation notice"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"A client who receives a cancellation notice for an advised purchase of an authorised unit trust or OEIC must exercise the right to cancel within 14 days. The other time periods are not stated for this example."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is LEAST LIKELY to be a professional client?","options":["Bank of England","IMF","Pension fund","Local authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The Bank of England, IMF and pension providers are all examples of 'per se' professional clients. Under MiFID II local authorities and municipalities are to be treated as retail clients."},{"question":"In relation to financial promotions, the guidance for a firm wishing to communicate or approve a financial promotion aims to amplify which Principles for Businesses?","options":["'Customers' interests' and 'Communication with clients'","'Communication with clients' and 'Customers: relationship of trust'","'Market conduct' and 'Customers' interests'","'Customers' interests' and 'Customers: relationship of trust'"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Principle 6 - Customers' interests and Principle 7 - Communications with Clients."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Home state basis rules","back":"Investment research and personal account dealing rules apply to UK firms wherever in the world the business is conducted, regardless of establishment location."},{"front":"Post-Brexit COBS and EEA branches","back":"COBS no longer applies to a UK MiFID firm's business carried on from an establishment in an EEA state; third-country MiFID firms with a UK branch are subject to COBS."},{"front":"AR appointment notice period","back":"Principals must notify the FCA of a new appointed representative appointment 30 days before it takes effect."},{"front":"Retail client (definition)","back":"Any client who is not a professional client or an eligible counterparty."},{"front":"Per se professional client - large undertaking (MiFID)","back":"Meets any 2 of: balance sheet total EUR20 million; net turnover EUR40 million; own funds EUR2 million."},{"front":"Large undertaking - non-MiFID (share capital test)","back":"Called-up share capital or net assets of at least GBP5 million at any time in the past 2 years."},{"front":"Large undertaking - non-MiFID (alternative test)","back":"Meets 2 of: balance sheet EUR12.5 million; net turnover EUR25 million; average 250 employees in the year."},{"front":"Large trust - professional client threshold","back":"Trustee of a trust with assets of at least GBP10 million at any time in the past 2 years."},{"front":"Large pension scheme - professional client threshold","back":"At least 50 members and assets under management of at least GBP10 million."},{"front":"Elective professional client - qualitative test","back":"Firm assesses the client's expertise, experience and knowledge and believes they can make their own investment decisions and understand the risks."},{"front":"Elective professional client - quantitative test (MiFID)","back":"Any 2 of 3: 10 significant transactions/quarter average over 4 quarters; portfolio >EUR500,000; 1+ year relevant professional experience."},{"front":"Local authority elective professional - portfolio threshold","back":"Financial instrument portfolio must exceed GBP10 million, plus one other condition (trading frequency, experience, or LGPS administering authority)."},{"front":"Agent as client - default rule","back":"If a firm knows A acts as agent for B, it treats A as the client, unless it agreed in writing to treat B as the client, or A's role is mainly to reduce the firm's duties to B."},{"front":"Client agreement record retention","back":"Longer of 5 years or the duration of the relationship; indefinitely for pension transfers, opt-outs, or FSAVCs."},{"front":"Fair, clear and not misleading","back":"The core financial promotion standard, amplifying Principles 6 and 7 (or Principle 12/Consumer Duty for in-scope firms)."},{"front":"Financial promotion approval 'gateway'","back":"Since 2023, authorised firms need specific FCA permission to approve financial promotions for unauthorised third parties; without it, they may only approve their own."},{"front":"NMMI","back":"Non-Mass Market Investment - banned from being promoted to retail investors."},{"front":"RMMI","back":"Restricted Mass Market Investment - may be promoted to retail investors but subject to restrictions (eg no non-readily realisable securities/P2P agreements)."},{"front":"High-risk investment cooling-off period","back":"Minimum 24 hours for first-time investors with a firm before investing in a high-risk product."},{"front":"Prescribed risk summary reading time","back":"Should take no more than 2 minutes to read (firms may diverge for valid reasons)."},{"front":"Past performance minimum period","back":"At least the preceding 5 years (or whole life of investment/index/service if shorter), shown in complete 12-month periods."},{"front":"Suitability - required information","back":"Knowledge and experience; financial situation (ability to bear losses); investment objectives and risk profile."},{"front":"Suitability assumption - per se professional clients","back":"Firm may assume both knowledge/experience AND ability to bear related investment risk (for UK MiFID business)."},{"front":"Suitability assumption - elective professional clients","back":"Firm may assume knowledge/experience, but NOT the ability to financially bear investment risk."},{"front":"Suitability report - pension timing","back":"Within 14 days of concluding the contract, where cancellation rules apply."},{"front":"Suitability report - life policy timing","back":"Before the contract is concluded, unless oral/urgent cover, then durable medium immediately after conclusion."},{"front":"Insistent client","back":"A client who rejects (or wants to alter) a firm's personal recommendation, and wants the firm to facilitate the different transaction anyway."},{"front":"Appropriateness - when not required","back":"Execution-only/RTO service, non-complex instrument, at client's initiative, client told the protection is lost, and firm complies with conflicts obligations."},{"front":"Non-complex financial instrument examples","back":"Shares on a UK/EEA regulated market; money market instruments/bonds without embedded derivatives; UCITS fund holdings; other COBS 10A.4.1-compliant instruments."},{"front":"KIID","back":"Key Investor Information Document - required for UCITS schemes; covers objectives, past performance/scenarios, costs, and risk/reward profile."},{"front":"KID (PRIIPs)","back":"Key Information Document - standardised, maximum 3 A4 pages, required for packaged retail and insurance-based investment products."},{"front":"PRIIPs UCITS exemption expiry","back":"31 December 2026 - the extended exemption period for UCITS KIID/KFD from the PRIIPs KID requirement."},{"front":"Cancellation period - life and pension products","back":"30 calendar days."},{"front":"Cancellation period - cash deposit ISAs and other non-life/pension products","back":"14 calendar days."},{"front":"Cancellation record-keeping - pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs","back":"Retained indefinitely."},{"front":"Cancellation record-keeping - life policy/pension contract","back":"At least 5 years."},{"front":"Cancellation record-keeping - other cases","back":"At least 3 years."},{"front":"Disclosure of conflicts - status","back":"A last resort, used only when organisational arrangements cannot manage the conflict with reasonable confidence; must be in a durable medium."},{"front":"Information barriers","back":"'Chinese walls' - arrangements withholding information in one part of a firm's business from another part, to prevent conflicts of interest."},{"front":"Investment research (definition)","back":"Information recommending/suggesting an investment strategy, intended for public distribution, labelled as research and presented as objective/independent - not a personal recommendation."},{"front":"Dealing ahead prevention","back":"Financial analysts with knowledge of unpublished research must not personally trade (or trade for others) until clients have had a reasonable opportunity to act on it."},{"front":"Minor non-monetary benefit conditions","back":"Disclosed before service; capable of enhancing quality; cannot impair the client's-best-interests duty; reasonable and proportionate."},{"front":"SME research exemption threshold","back":"Research on companies with market capitalisation below GBP200 million is exempt from the inducement rules."},{"front":"SME research exemption review date","back":"1 March 2025 (3 years after implementation)."},{"front":"RPA - excluded items","back":"Post-trade analytics, unanalysed price feeds/historical data, seminar fees, corporate access services, order/execution management services, RPA administration."},{"front":"Best execution standard - MiFID firms","back":"All sufficient steps."},{"front":"Execution factors","back":"Price, costs, speed, likelihood of execution and settlement, size, nature, or any other relevant consideration."},{"front":"Four-fold test","back":"Did the firm initiate the transaction? Does the client only ask for a quote? Can the client not identify price transparency? Has the client confirmed reliance on the firm?"},{"front":"Order execution policy review frequency","back":"At least annually, or when material changes occur."},{"front":"Client limit order - publication duty","back":"Must be made public immediately if it cannot be immediately executed, unless it is over normal market size."},{"front":"Reportable transaction deadline (UK MiFIR)","back":"As soon as possible, no later than close of the next business day (trade date + 1)."},{"front":"Post-trade transparency deadline - equities","back":"1 minute (unless waivers/deferrals apply)."},{"front":"UK EMIR - non-UK entities","back":"No UK EMIR reporting obligation, including UK branches of third-country firms."},{"front":"Short Selling Regulation notification deadline","back":"3.30pm on the trading day after the position was reached; calculated as at midnight on the day the position was reached."},{"front":"LSE trade reporting period","back":"7.15am to 5.15pm each business day."},{"front":"Off-order-book trade reporting deadline","back":"Within 3 minutes of execution (as close to real time as possible)."},{"front":"CREST settlement reporting deadline","back":"8.00pm on the day of trade (domestic transactions)."},{"front":"Confirmation/periodic statement record-keeping - UK MiFID business","back":"At least 5 years from dispatch."},{"front":"Confirmation/periodic statement record-keeping - other business","back":"At least 3 years from dispatch."},{"front":"Periodic statement frequency - standard","back":"At least quarterly."},{"front":"Periodic statement frequency - leveraged portfolio","back":"Monthly."},{"front":"Portfolio depreciation notification threshold","back":"10% or more fall since the last valuation, and multiples of 10% thereafter."},{"front":"Client money trust basis","back":"Statutory trust created under CASS 7.17.2R."},{"front":"Client bank account acknowledgement rule (current)","back":"Firms must obtain a signed acknowledgement letter for ALL client bank accounts before placing client money in them - the old 20-business-day grace period has been removed."},{"front":"DvP client money exemption limit","back":"Exempt unless settlement fails to occur by close of business on the 3rd business day following the due payment date."}]},{"id":"reg-ch3","number":3,"title":"Enhancing Market Integrity","examWeight":"19 of 75 questions (~25%)","summaryHtml":"\n<h3>Market Abuse (Section 1)</h3>\n<p>Market abuse is a statutory offence under FSMA; the UK regime came into force in 2001. The <strong>UK Market Abuse Regulation (MAR)</strong> (originally EU MAR, effective 2016, \"onshored\" after Brexit) sets the civil regime; the FCA's Handbook only gives <strong>guidance</strong>, not binding rules. The Financial Services Act 2012 (ss89-91) creates three related criminal offences: misleading statements (s89), false/misleading impressions (s90), and misleading statements about benchmarks (s91), with three defences (reasonable belief; conformity with price stabilising rules; conformity with control of information/information barrier rules). Penalties (Financial Services Act 2021): summary conviction - fine and/or up to 12 months; on indictment - up to 10 years (offences after 1 Nov 2021) or up to 7 years (offences up to 31 Oct 2021). Firms must maintain systems to detect and report suspicious orders/transactions via a <strong>STOR</strong> (Suspicious Transaction and Order Report) without delay; records must be kept 5 years and reviewed at least annually.</p>\n<h3>Insider Dealing (Section 2)</h3>\n<p>Under UK MAR, insider dealing covers using inside information to deal, cancel/amend orders, or recommend/induce another to do so. The criminal offence is set out in <strong>Part V of the Criminal Justice Act 1993 (CJA)</strong>: Section 52 creates three offences (dealing, encouraging, disclosing); Section 56 defines inside information (precise, not public, price-sensitive, relating to specified markets/instruments). The CJA was updated by the Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023 (in force 15 June 2023). General defences include not expecting a profit/avoided loss, reasonable belief information was widely disclosed, or would have acted the same regardless. Special defences exist for market makers acting in good faith, market information (e.g., a client's block trade order), and price stabilisation. Issuers must maintain insider lists and disclose inside information via a Regulatory Information Service (RIS).</p>\n<h3>Market Manipulation (Section 3)</h3>\n<p>Four forms: manipulating transactions, manipulating devices, dissemination (false/misleading information), and benchmark manipulation. PDMRs (persons discharging managerial responsibilities) must notify transactions within 3 business days and are barred from dealing during a <strong>closed period</strong> of 30 calendar days before results announcements. Accepted Market Practices (AMPs) are set by the FCA - currently none exist.</p>\n<h3>Money Laundering and Financial Crime (Section 4)</h3>\n<p><strong>POCA 2002</strong> creates five offences: concealing (s327), arrangements (s328), acquisition/use/possession (s329) - all up to 14 years; failure to disclose (s330) - up to 5 years; tipping off (s333A) - up to 2 years. The three stages of laundering are <strong>placement, layering, integration</strong>. Firms apply risk-based CDD (identify, verify, understand purpose), Simplified DD for low-risk relationships, and Enhanced DD for high-risk scenarios including PEPs (Politically Exposed Persons - EDD ceases 1 year after leaving prominent public function). Every firm must appoint an MLRO (UK-based, approved under SM&CR) and a nominated officer (may be the same person) to receive internal disclosures and report externally to the NCA.</p>\n<h3>Record-Keeping (Section 5)</h3>\n<p>CDD/identity records: 5 years from end of relationship. Transaction records: 5 years from completion, capped at a maximum of 10 years' retention.</p>\n<h3>Bribery Act 2010 (Section 6)</h3>\n<p>Four offences: bribing (s1), being bribed (s2), bribing a foreign public official (s6), and the corporate strict-liability offence of failing to prevent bribery (s7). Maximum penalty: 10 years' imprisonment (unlimited fine for the s7 corporate offence). Defence: \"adequate procedures\" per six MoJ principles (proportionality, top-level commitment, risk assessment, due diligence, communication, monitoring/review).</p>\n<h3>Fraud and Economic Crime (Section 7)</h3>\n<p>The Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act (ECCTA) created a new \"failure to prevent fraud\" corporate offence (effective 1 September 2025) for large organisations (meeting 2 of: turnover >£36m; balance sheet >£18m; >250 employees). Defence: reasonable fraud prevention procedures (six similar principles).</p>\n<h3>Disclosure and Transparency Rules (Section 8)</h3>\n<p>UK notifiable shareholding threshold is <strong>3%</strong> (retained from pre-Brexit EU rules, rather than the EU's 5%/10%/15% etc. scale), then every whole percentage point above. Notify the company within 2 business days; the company informs the market (e.g., via RNS) by the end of the next business day - not the FCA. Section 793 of the Companies Act 2006 lets a public company demand disclosure of beneficial ownership from suspected nominee shareholders.</p>\n","sections":[{"heading":"3.1 Market Abuse","html":"\n<p>Market abuse is a <strong>statutory offence</strong> covering financial market manipulation and insider dealing, damaging investor confidence and market integrity. The UK's market abuse regime is an offence under the <strong>Financial Services and Markets Act (FSMA)</strong> and came into force in <strong>2001</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Implementation of the Market Abuse Regulation (MAR)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The <strong>Market Abuse Regulation (MAR)</strong> came into effect in <strong>2016</strong>, updating the EU Market Abuse Directive (MAD).</li>\n<li>It extended scope to instruments traded off regulated markets (bilateral/OTC), covered spot commodity and related derivative markets, and tailored rules for SME issuers.</li>\n<li>Following implementation, the FCA Handbook provides <strong>guidance only</strong> (not binding rules) on matters governed by MAR.</li>\n<li>Post-Brexit, the EU MAR was \"onshored\"/\"retained\"/\"assimilated\" into UK law largely unaltered and is now called <strong>UK MAR</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>Market Abuse (Amendment) (EU Exit) Regulations 2019</strong> made three key amendments:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>No obligation to share information with EU authorities without a guarantee of reciprocity.</li>\n<li>The FCA took over the regulatory responsibilities previously held by ESMA and the European Commission (EC).</li>\n<li>Supplementary legislation is now subject to HM Treasury instead of the EC.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Civil and Criminal Regime</h4>\n<p>Insider dealing and improper disclosure are covered by the CJA 1993 (criminal) and by ss89-92 of the <strong>Financial Services Act 2012</strong> (misleading statements/practices), which replaced the former FSMA s397. The Financial Services Act 2012 created three offences:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Making misleading statements (<strong>Section 89</strong>)</li>\n<li>Creating false or misleading impressions (<strong>Section 90</strong>)</li>\n<li>Making misleading statements in relation to benchmarks (<strong>Section 91</strong>)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Three potential defences to a charge under ss89-91:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Reasonable belief that the act/conduct would not create a false or misleading impression.</li>\n<li>Conformity with the FCA's <strong>price stabilisation rules</strong> (allowing banks to support a new issue price for a limited period, with mandated disclosures).</li>\n<li>Conformity with the FCA's <strong>control of information rules</strong> (statements made on the basis of limited information behind an information barrier).</li>\n</ol>\n<h4>Penalties (Financial Services Act 2021)</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Summary conviction: fine not exceeding the statutory amount and/or jail up to <strong>12 months</strong>.</li>\n<li>Conviction on indictment: jail up to <strong>10 years</strong> or a fine, or both, for offences committed <strong>after 1 November 2021</strong>.</li>\n<li>Offences committed up to <strong>31 October 2021</strong>: jail up to <strong>7 years</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The FCA's Market Conduct Sourcebook (\"MAR\") sets standards: <strong>MAR 1</strong> covers market abuse requirements; <strong>MAR 2</strong> covers price stabilising rules. Civil sanctions range from financial penalties to public censure. In deciding between a penalty and censure, the FCA considers: whether the breach was deliberate/reckless; duration/frequency; benefit gained/loss avoided; evidence of systemic control failures; impact on market orderliness; risk of loss to consumers; extent of financial crime facilitated; aggregate effect of smaller incidents; speed of self-reporting; cooperation; likelihood of recurrence; compliance with other regulators (e.g., Takeover Panel); accuracy of information provided; and previous disciplinary record. It may also weigh market sophistication and the impact of a penalty/censure on markets or consumers.</p>\n<p>Criminal prosecution decisions follow the <strong>Code for Crown Prosecutors</strong> and Enforcement Guide, considering seriousness, victim loss, market distortion, profits/losses avoided, repeat risk, prior record, cooperation, dishonesty/abuse of trust, and personal circumstances. The FCA operates a \"<strong>no two bites of the cherry</strong>\" policy - it will not impose market abuse sanctions where a person is being criminally prosecuted for the same conduct, or bring disciplinary proceedings after a prosecution for substantially the same offence.</p>\n<h4>Prevention and Detection of Market Abuse (1.3)</h4>\n<p>UK MAR applies to executed trades, <strong>unexecuted orders</strong>, and <strong>requests for quotes (RFQs)</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Market operators/investment firms operating trading venues must establish effective arrangements, systems and procedures to prevent and detect insider dealing and market manipulation (including attempts), and report suspicious orders/transactions to the competent authority of the trading venue without delay.</li>\n<li>Firms arranging/executing transactions off-venue must maintain proportionate arrangements to detect and report suspicious orders/transactions.</li>\n<li>Where a firm has reasonable suspicion, it must report to the FCA <strong>without delay</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Required arrangements/systems/procedures must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Be appropriate and proportionate to the scale, size, nature and complexity of the business.</li>\n<li>Be assessed at least via an <strong>annual audit and internal review</strong>, updated as necessary.</li>\n<li>Be documented in writing (including changes), retained for <strong>five years</strong>.</li>\n<li>Allow analysis of every transaction/order executed, placed, modified, cancelled or rejected, on and off venue.</li>\n<li>Produce alerts for further analysis and cover the full range of trading activities.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Suspicious Transaction and Order Reporting (STOR)</h4>\n<p>Where there is reasonable suspicion an order/transaction (on or off a trading venue) may constitute market manipulation or insider dealing (including attempts), the person must notify the FCA without delay. A STOR must include:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identification of the submitter and their capacity (own account vs on behalf of third parties).</li>\n<li>Description of the order/transaction: type of order/trading (e.g., block trades), venue, price and volume.</li>\n<li>Reasons for suspicion.</li>\n<li>Means of identifying persons involved (including the person placing/executing and the person on whose behalf).</li>\n<li>Any other relevant information/supporting documents.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Firms may alternatively submit a <strong>market observation</strong> for suspicious activity not meeting the full STOR criteria (e.g., where the firm is not itself involved and lacks complete information).</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.2 Insider Dealing","html":"\n<h4>Inside Information and Insider Dealing (UK MAR)</h4>\n<p>Under UK MAR, <strong>inside information</strong> comprises:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Precise, non-public information relating to a financial instrument which, if made public, would be likely to significantly affect its price (or related derivatives).</li>\n<li>For commodity derivatives: precise, non-public information relating to the derivative or related spot commodity contract, expected/required to be disclosed under legal/market rules.</li>\n<li>For persons executing client orders: information conveyed by a client about their own pending orders.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Information is <strong>precise</strong> if it indicates circumstances/an event (existing, or reasonably expected to occur) specific enough to draw a conclusion about the likely price effect.</p>\n<p>Insider dealing under UK MAR occurs where a person:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Possesses inside information and uses it to acquire/dispose of instruments (for self or a third party).</li>\n<li>Uses inside information to cancel/amend an order placed before possessing the information.</li>\n<li>Recommends or induces another to deal on the basis of inside information.</li>\n<li>Recommends or induces another to cancel/amend an order on the basis of inside information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A person is an <strong>insider</strong> if they know the information is inside information, knowingly acquired from an inside source:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>They are an inside source themselves (director, employee, shareholder of an issuer - not necessarily the company whose securities are traded).</li>\n<li>They have access via their employment, office or profession (e.g., auditor, legal or corporate finance adviser).</li>\n<li>They have access, directly or indirectly, from a person who was an inside source or had access via employment (e.g., a director's spouse who overhears confidential takeover information at home).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Public Disclosure and Insider Lists</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Issuers must inform the public <strong>as soon as possible</strong> of inside information directly concerning them, via a <strong>Regulatory Information Service (RIS)</strong>, enabling fast, complete, correct and timely access.</li>\n<li>Issuers (and those acting for them) must maintain <strong>insider lists</strong> of everyone with access to inside information (employees, advisers, accountants, credit rating agencies), promptly updated for changes. <strong>Separate lists</strong> must be kept for each transaction, plus one for permanent insiders.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Unlawful Disclosure of Inside Information</h4>\n<p>Sharing inside information other than in the \"normal exercise of an employment, a profession, or duties\" is unlawful disclosure - a criminal offence, punishable by unlimited fines and/or injunctions. Permitted disclosure exceptions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Normal duties.</li>\n<li>Market soundings (e.g., to raise capital).</li>\n<li>Confidentiality-protected disclosure to facilitate a transaction, seek advice, or fulfil a legal obligation.</li>\n<li>Professional advice (lawyer, auditor).</li>\n<li>Takeover bids (to facilitate/seek commitment under the Takeover Code).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Criminal Justice Act 1993 (CJA) - Part V</h4>\n<p>The UK's criminal law on insider dealing is set out in <strong>Part V of the CJA 1993</strong>, updated by the <strong>Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023</strong> (in force <strong>15 June 2023</strong>), which updated the list of Regulated Markets.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 52 CJA</strong> - three ways of committing the offence:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dealing in the security.</li>\n<li>Encouraging another to do so.</li>\n<li>Disclosing inside information to another.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Section 56 CJA</strong> - inside information is information which: (a) relates to securities traded/admitted to trade (or subject to a request to admit) on a UK, EEA or Gibraltar Regulated Market, MTF or OTF; (b) relates to securities on Nasdaq, SIX Swiss Exchange or NYSE; (c) relates to other securities whose price depends on (a)/(b); (d) is specific or precise; (e) has not been made public; and (f) is price-sensitive.</p>\n<p>Since the 2023 update, \"security\" under the CJA aligns with the UK MiFID definition of \"financial instrument\", covering (among others): transferable securities; money-market instruments; units in CISs; derivatives on securities/currencies/rates/indices; cash-settled commodity derivatives; physically-settled commodity derivatives traded on a UK regulated market/MTF/OTF; credit risk transfer derivatives; financial contracts for differences; derivatives on climatic variables/freight rates/inflation; and emission allowances.</p>\n<p>Information is \"made public\" (non-exhaustive list) when it is: published per regulated market rules (e.g., via the LSE's RNS); contained in records open to public scrutiny (e.g., a shareholder register); or readily available to those likely to deal (even if it must be sought out with diligence/expertise, or paid for by subscription).</p>\n<h4>Market Manipulation and Market Soundings (intro)</h4>\n<p>UK MAR prohibits engaging or attempting to engage in market manipulation (detailed in 3.3). A <strong>market sounding</strong> is where an adviser/investment bank discusses a potential new issuance with investors to gauge appetite (coupon, size), on behalf of an issuer. Disclosing firms must: obtain consent to receive inside information; inform the recipient they are prohibited from dealing/encouraging dealing on it; inform the recipient of confidentiality obligations; keep records of information given and of recipients who declined consent; and advise recipients when information ceases to be inside information. Recipients must independently assess whether information received is genuinely \"inside information\".</p>\n<h4>General Defences to Insider Dealing</h4>\n<p>For dealing, or encouraging another to deal:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did not expect the dealing to result in profit (or avoided loss) because of the information.</li>\n<li>Reasonably believed the information had been so widely disclosed that no party would be prejudiced by not knowing it.</li>\n<li>Would have acted the same way regardless of possessing the information.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For disclosing only:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Did not expect any person to deal, or</li>\n<li>Did not expect the dealing to result in profit/avoided loss.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Special Defences</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Market makers</strong>: not guilty if acting in good faith in the ordinary course of market-making business; a further defence applies if acting to facilitate an acquisition/disposal under negotiation, using market information arising directly from those negotiations.</li>\n<li><strong>Market information</strong>: not guilty if the information held was market information (e.g., a client's pending block order) and it was reasonable to act as they did, having regard to the content of the information, the circumstances/capacity in which it was first obtained, and the capacity in which the defendant now acts. Example: a broker who knows a client wishes to buy 10,000 shares in XYZ plc and deals on that instruction has a market information defence (size of order relative to market impact is relevant).</li>\n<li><strong>Price stabilisation</strong>: stabilising managers/agents may support the price of new issues for a limited period post-issue under FCA Market Conduct Sourcebook Chapter 2 rules - a defence to insider dealing/manipulation allegations.</li>\n<li><strong>Legitimate behaviour</strong>: mere possession of inside information is not itself evidence of use. Legitimate activities include: effective information barriers; acting as a market maker; executing third-party orders; orders concluded before possessing the information; orders to satisfy a pre-existing legal obligation; conduct in a merger/takeover where information is made public at approval; and stake-building based on one's own decision to acquire/dispose.</li>\n</ul>\n"},{"heading":"3.3 Market Manipulation","html":"\n<h4>The Four Forms of Market Manipulation</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Form</th><th>Description</th></tr>\n<tr><td>1. Manipulating transactions</td><td>Transactions/orders not for legitimate reasons/not conforming to accepted practices, giving a false/misleading impression of supply, demand or price, or securing an abnormal/artificial price.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>2. Manipulating devices</td><td>Transactions/orders employing fictitious devices or any other form of deception or contrivance.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>3. Dissemination</td><td>Spreading information (by any means) giving a false/misleading impression, known or reasonably expected to be known as false/misleading by the disseminator - the form most harmful to investors.</td></tr>\n<tr><td>4. Benchmark manipulation</td><td>Transmitting false/misleading inputs relating to a benchmark, or any other behaviour manipulating a benchmark's calculation.</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Other behaviour \"considered as market manipulation\" under UK MAR includes: securing a dominant position over supply/demand (alone or in collaboration); trading at market open/close to mislead investors relying on displayed prices; placing/cancelling/modifying orders (including via algorithmic/high-frequency trading) to obscure genuine orders or create false signals; and \"scalping\" - voicing an opinion on an instrument (via media) having previously taken a position, then profiting from the market impact without disclosing the conflict of interest.</p>\n<h4>Legitimate Behaviour and Accepted Market Practices (AMPs)</h4>\n<p>Legitimate behaviour = conduct carried out for valid commercial reasons, in line with accepted market practices, without intent to mislead/distort (e.g., genuine hedging or investment strategy). The <strong>FCA</strong> is responsible for assessing/establishing AMPs in the UK - key points:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Transactions must be for legitimate reasons and conform to an established AMP.</li>\n<li>The FCA has the authority to establish AMPs, but <strong>currently no AMPs have been established</strong> in the UK.</li>\n<li>The FCA must consult stakeholders (issuers, service providers, market participants) before establishing an AMP.</li>\n<li>Identities of practitioners and the party responsible for transparency must be disclosed.</li>\n<li>Criteria considered include market structure, instrument types, and extent of retail participation.</li>\n<li>The prohibitions on insider dealing/market manipulation do not apply to buy-back programmes or stabilisation activities meeting specific UK MAR conditions.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>PDMR Transactions (Managers' Transactions)</h4>\n<p><strong>Persons Discharging Managerial Responsibilities (PDMRs)</strong> are members of an issuer's administrative/management/supervisory bodies (e.g., directors) or senior executives with regular access to inside information and power to make managerial decisions affecting the issuer's future. The definition extends to <strong>Persons Closely Associated</strong> (spouses, dependent children, entities controlled by the PDMR).</p>\n<ul>\n<li>PDMRs must notify the issuer (or emission allowance participant) and the FCA of every personal-account transaction in shares, debt instruments or related derivatives (or emission allowances/derivatives).</li>\n<li>Notification must be made promptly, <strong>no later than three business days</strong> after the transaction, containing: name; reason for notification; issuer/participant name; instrument description/identifier; buy/sell (and whether from option exercise); date/place; price and size.</li>\n<li><strong>Closed period</strong>: PDMRs are restricted from dealing in company shares/related instruments for <strong>30 calendar days</strong> before announcement of the interim or annual financial report. Exceptions may apply for severe financial difficulty requiring immediate fund realisation, or transactions under employee share schemes with no change in beneficial ownership.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Investment Recommendations</h4>\n<p>Persons/firms producing or distributing investment recommendations or suggested strategies must present information <strong>objectively</strong> and disclose interests/conflicts of interest. An investment recommendation is distinct from MiFID \"investment advice\"/personal recommendation - it is a proposal/strategy on financial instruments provided to more than one recipient (fund managers, wealth managers), typically short-term trading ideas.</p>\n<p>Required disclosures at the point of the recommendation include: clear distinction of facts from interpretations/opinions; clear indication of material information sources; clear labelling of projections/forecasts/price targets and their assumptions; date/time of production; a summary of valuation basis/methodology and assumptions (and any changes); clear explanation of recommendation meaning (buy/sell/hold) and time horizon, plus risk warnings/sensitivity analysis; planned update frequency; relevant date/time of prices referenced; and (ongoing obligations) disclosure of changes from prior recommendations on the same instrument within the preceding <strong>12 months</strong>, and a list of all recommendations disseminated in the preceding 12 months (date, identity of natural person(s), price target/market price at dissemination, direction, validity period).</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.4 Money Laundering and Financial Crime","html":"\n<h4>Introduction</h4>\n<p><strong>Money laundering (ML)</strong> turns proceeds of crime into apparently legitimate funds. AML provisions are a frontline defence against drug dealing, organised crime, human/wildlife trafficking, and terrorist financing, monitored via the <strong>National Crime Agency (NCA)</strong>. Key legislative sources:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Proceeds of Crime Act (POCA) 2002</strong> - defines criminal property (property is criminal only if the alleged offender knows/suspects it is); requires firms to report ML suspicions.</li>\n<li><strong>Serious Organised Crime and Police Act (SOCPA) 2005</strong> - amended POCA; addressed the \"Spanish bullfighter\" issue (conduct legal abroad but illegal in the UK would otherwise count as criminal property) by allowing a defence where the offender reasonably believed the conduct was not criminal where it occurred - subject to the Secretary of State's power to designate certain overseas conduct (e.g., serious tax evasion, drug cultivation) as still reportable.</li>\n<li><strong>Criminal Finances Act 2017</strong> - amends POCA; creates two corporate offences of failing to prevent the criminal facilitation of tax evasion (UK tax and foreign tax).</li>\n<li><strong>Money Laundering, Terrorist Financing and Transfer of Funds (Information on the Payer) Regulations 2017 (MLR 2017)</strong> - implemented the Fourth EU Money Laundering Directive (MLD4); sets out firms' systems/controls, training and KYC requirements. The Fifth Money Laundering Directive was transposed in 2020. Post-Brexit, the UK chose <strong>not</strong> to transpose the EU's Sixth Anti-Money Laundering Directive, considering its domestic law already compliant.</li>\n<li><strong>SYSC Sourcebook</strong> - high-level FCA rules/guidance on AML systems and controls (principles-based, not prescriptive), referring firms to <strong>JMLSG</strong> guidance for procedure.</li>\n<li><strong>JMLSG Guidance</strong> - produced by UK trade associations; not mandatory but HM Treasury-approved, so courts treat adherence as evidence of compliance.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>POCA 2002 - the Five Offences</h4>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Offence</th><th>Description</th><th>Max Penalty</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Concealing (s327)</td><td>Concealing or disguising criminal property</td><td rowspan=\"3\">Fine and/or up to 14 years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Arrangements (s328)</td><td>Being concerned in an arrangement facilitating acquisition/retention/use/control of criminal property for another (can include giving transaction advice)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Acquisition, use and possession (s329)</td><td>Acquiring, using or possessing criminal property, knowing/suspecting its origin</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Failure to disclose (s330)</td><td>Employee in the regulated sector fails to disclose knowledge/suspicion of ML/TF to a nominated officer as soon as practicable</td><td>Fine and/or up to 5 years</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Tipping off (s333A)</td><td>Regulated-sector person discloses a SAR/investigation likely to prejudice it</td><td>Fine and/or up to 2 years</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Section 330 (failure to disclose) requires three conditions: (a) the person knows/suspects (or has reasonable grounds to know/suspect) another is committing an offence; (b) the knowledge/suspicion arose during business in the regulated sector; (c) no disclosure was made to a nominated officer (e.g., the firm's MLRO) as soon as practicable. The <strong>CPS updated its prosecuting guidance on 2 June 2021</strong>, allowing standalone s330 prosecutions regardless of whether an ML offence has been substantiated.</p>\n<p>A defence to the first three offences (concealing, arrangements, acquisition/use/possession) exists if the person made the required disclosure to the nominated officer/MLRO (or, if they are the MLRO, to the NCA).</p>\n<p>The \"reasonable grounds to know or suspect\" test in s330 is the <strong>objective test</strong> - would a reasonable person have suspected, irrespective of the individual's protestations.</p>\n<p><strong>Section 342 POCA (Part 7)</strong> - prejudicing an investigation (applies to anyone, not just the regulated sector): making a disclosure likely to prejudice an investigation, or falsifying/concealing/destroying relevant documents. Defences: no knowledge/suspicion of prejudice; disclosure made in performance of a POCA duty; legal adviser acting professionally (unless furthering a criminal purpose); no knowledge documents were relevant; no intent to conceal facts. Penalty: fine and/or up to 5 years.</p>\n<h4>The Three Stages of Money Laundering</h4>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Placement</strong> - introducing criminal proceeds into the financial system (e.g., bank/building society account, bureau de change).</li>\n<li><strong>Layering</strong> - moving proceeds through complex transactions to obscure the link to source and beneficiary (rapid FX/securities trades, CISs, insurance-based products, cross-border transfers).</li>\n<li><strong>Integration</strong> - dirty money re-enters the legitimate economy appearing as clean funds.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>AML provisions target identification and suspicion-reporting mainly at placement/layering, and record-keeping to prevent reaching integration.</p>\n<h4>Systems and Controls (SYSC 6.3)</h4>\n<p>Firms must identify, assess, monitor and manage ML risk, considering customer/product/activity profiles, distribution channels, transaction complexity/volume, systems, and operating environment. Systems must include: staff training; annual MLRO report to senior management/governing body; documented risk policies; ML risk consideration in day-to-day operations and new product/customer decisions; and proportionate ID procedures that do not unreasonably exclude customers. A director/senior manager (often the MLRO) must hold overall AML responsibility.</p>\n<p>The <strong>MLRO</strong> receives/assesses internal suspicion reports and decides whether to report to the NCA; must be an FCA-approved Senior Management Function under SM&CR, based in the UK, with appropriate authority, independence, resources and access.</p>\n<h4>JMLSG Guidance</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Part I</strong> - general guidance for all financial sector firms; international context and UK legislative framework.</li>\n<li><strong>Part II</strong> - sectoral guidance (retail banking, wealth management, stockbroking, wholesale markets).</li>\n<li><strong>Part III</strong> - specialist guidance (electronic/wire transfers; UK financial sanctions regime).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Guidance requires firms to: take a risk-based, proportionate approach (documented policy, kept current, available to the FCA); simplify identity verification for many customer types; allow reliance on other firms' verification; introduce definitions (beneficial owners, PEPs); set CDD measures on a risk-based approach; set out reliance rules; specify when SDD/EDD apply; require scrutiny of complex/unusually large/unusual transactions; and maintain systems to respond to regulator enquiries.</p>\n<h4>Customer Due Diligence (CDD)</h4>\n<p>Three aspects at the outset of a business relationship:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Identifying the customer/beneficial owner (name, address, date of birth; beneficial owners for non-personal customers).</li>\n<li>Verifying that identity (e.g., passport/driving licence for individuals; Companies House checks for corporates).</li>\n<li>Obtaining information on the intended nature of the business relationship.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Tier</th><th>When applied</th><th>Examples/notes</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Simplified Due Diligence (SDD)</td><td>Lower ML risk counterparties</td><td>Other regulated firms; listed companies; beneficial owners of pooled accounts held by notaries/legal professionals; UK public authorities; community institutions; low-risk products (life assurance, e-money, pension funds, CTFs)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Standard CDD</td><td>Default</td><td>As above three-part process</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD)</td><td>Higher-risk situations (risk-sensitive basis)</td><td>High risk identified by firm's own assessment/supervisor info; high-risk third country; client not physically present; correspondent banking; PEP relationships; false/stolen ID provided; complex/unusually large or unusual-pattern transactions</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>If SDD does not apply, satisfactory ID evidence must be obtained/verified as soon as reasonably practicable; where verification is delayed (e.g., non-face-to-face business), the relationship should be restricted (e.g., transaction/transfer limits) until complete. If identity cannot be verified, the firm should not proceed and should consider reporting to the NCA.</p>\n<p>EDD requires: appropriate risk-management systems to identify PEPs/family/close associates; senior management approval to establish/continue the relationship; measures to establish source of wealth/funds; and enhanced ongoing monitoring.</p>\n<p><strong>PEP definition</strong>: \"an individual who is or has, at any time in the preceding year, been entrusted with prominent public functions, other than as a middle-ranking or more junior official.\" The obligation to apply EDD ceases <strong>one year</strong> after leaving office (longer if the firm considers it appropriate). Family members include spouse/partner, children and their spouses/partners, and parents. EDD is no longer required for family members/close associates once the PEP has left the prominent function, regardless of the one-year period.</p>\n<h4>MLRO and Nominated Officer</h4>\n<p>All firms (except sole traders, general insurance firms and mortgage intermediaries) must appoint an <strong>MLRO</strong> under FCA rules. <strong>MLR 2017</strong> requires firms to appoint a <strong>nominated officer</strong> to receive internal ML disclosures and make external NCA reports (also receives disclosures under POCA and the Terrorism Act 2000). In practice, the same person usually performs both roles and is called the MLRO.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Aspect</th><th>MLRO</th><th>Nominated Officer</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Primary role</td><td>Oversees the firm's entire AML compliance</td><td>Designated internal contact for suspicious activity reports</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Legal duty</td><td>N/A specifically</td><td>Must report suspicions to the NCA - failure is a criminal offence</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Internal function</td><td>N/A specifically</td><td>Recipient/initial reviewer of internal SARs</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Reporting function</td><td>Makes the final report to the NCA after considering internal suspicions</td><td>-</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Broader responsibilities</td><td>Staff training, CDD oversight, policy implementation</td><td>-</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>The MLRO's responsibilities span: policy development/maintenance; monitoring transactions and receiving/assessing internal reports; making external SARs to the NCA and maintaining a register of reports; conducting firm-wide risk assessments; developing/delivering AML training and fostering a compliance culture; liaising with regulators/law enforcement; and maintaining accurate AML records.</p>\n<h4>Disclosure of Suspicious Activities</h4>\n<p><strong>Section 24 MLR 2017</strong> requires firms to ensure relevant employees are made aware of ML/TF law and data protection requirements, and receive regular training on recognising/handling suspicious transactions; training records must be kept. A \"relevant employee\" is one whose role can contribute to identifying/mitigating/detecting ML/TF risk. Disclosure is a two-stage process: an employee discloses suspicion to the MLRO, who reviews and decides whether to escalate to the NCA. Reporting to the MLRO discharges the employee's legal responsibility; the MLRO's report to the NCA discharges theirs.</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.5 Record-Keeping Requirements","html":"\n<p>Firms must identify customers/beneficial owners, verify identities, and understand the purpose/nature of the relationship, obtaining sufficient information for a complete risk picture and ongoing monitoring - both at onboarding and throughout the relationship (to validate whether risk/beneficial ownership information remains current).</p>\n<p>Firms must keep copies of documents/information obtained for CDD/CTF purposes, and sufficient transaction records, for <strong>five years</strong> after:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the business relationship ends, or</li>\n<li>an occasional transaction.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Transactional records occurring within a business relationship need not be kept beyond a maximum of <strong>ten years</strong>. Where a firm is relied upon by others for due diligence checks, it must retain those records for the same period; a firm relying on another party's checks must be able to demonstrate why it can rely on them, and that records are retained for five years.</p>\n<p>The FCA Handbook (SYSC Sourcebook, Chapters 6 and 9) requires firms to take reasonable care to make and retain adequate records, including:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Evidence of identity (a copy, or a record of where a copy can be obtained).</li>\n<li>A record of every transaction carried out with/for the client in regulated activity, including internal/external reporting actions taken.</li>\n</ul>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Record type</th><th>Retention period</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Evidence of identity</td><td>5 years from the end of the relationship between firm and client</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Transactions</td><td>5 years from the date the transaction was completed</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Any other case</td><td>5 years from obtaining the information or creating the record</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Records must also include the dates AML training was given, the nature of the training, and the names of staff trained.</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.6 The Bribery Act","html":"\n<h4>Overview - Four Offences</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Offering, promising or giving a bribe to another person (<strong>Section 1</strong>).</li>\n<li>Requesting, agreeing to receive or accepting a bribe (<strong>Section 2</strong>).</li>\n<li>Bribing a foreign public official (<strong>Section 6</strong>).</li>\n<li>Corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery (<strong>Section 7</strong>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Penalties</h4>\n<p>Maximum <strong>10 years' imprisonment</strong> for individual offences; the corporate s7 offence carries an <strong>unlimited fine</strong>.</p>\n<h4>Key Sections</h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Section 1</strong> - bribery: offering/giving/promising a financial or other advantage in exchange for improper performance of a relevant function or activity.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 2</strong> - being bribed: requesting/accepting/agreeing to accept such an advantage in exchange for improper performance.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 3</strong> - defines \"relevant function or activity\" as any function of a public nature; any activity connected with a business, trade or profession; any activity in the course of employment; or any activity by/on behalf of a body of persons (corporate or unincorporated). Applies to both private and public sectors, and to activity performed outside the UK.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 4</strong> - \"improper\" performance = breach of the expectation of good faith/impartiality, or performance not as expected of a person in a position of trust.</li>\n<li><strong>Section 5</strong> - the standard is what a reasonable person in the UK would expect; for conduct occurring outside the UK, local practices/customs are disregarded <strong>unless</strong> they form part of the jurisdiction's written law (constitution, statute, or judicial opinion set down in writing).</li>\n<li><strong>Section 6</strong> - bribery of foreign public officials: promising/offering/giving a financial or other advantage (directly or via a third party) not legitimately due. A <strong>foreign public official</strong> (s6(4)) is an individual holding legislative, administrative or judicial posts, or performing a public function for a foreign country/its agencies, or an official/agent of a public international organisation. Applies only to the briber, not the official; covers bribery via intermediaries (\"through a third party\").</li>\n<li><strong>Section 7</strong> - failure of commercial organisations to prevent bribery on their behalf: a broad, strict-liability offence (no need to prove intention) applying to all commercial organisations with UK business, with vicarious liability for bribery by an employee, agent, subsidiary or other third party.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Hospitality and Facilitation Payments</h4>\n<p>Corporate hospitality is not automatically a bribe if it has a legitimate business aim and is reasonable, proportionate and appropriate. <strong>Facilitation payments</strong> are an offence <strong>unless</strong> permitted by the written law of the foreign official's country, or made under threat of violence.</p>\n<h4>Defence - Adequate Procedures</h4>\n<p>A firm has a defence to the s7 offence if it can show it had \"adequate procedures\" in place. The MoJ's six guiding principles:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Proportionality</strong> - action proportionate to the risks faced and size of business.</li>\n<li><strong>Top-level commitment</strong> - senior leadership visibly opposes bribery.</li>\n<li><strong>Risk assessment</strong> - understanding the firm's bribery risks (markets, counterparties).</li>\n<li><strong>Due diligence</strong> - knowing who the firm deals with.</li>\n<li><strong>Communication</strong> - policies/procedures communicated (including training).</li>\n<li><strong>Monitoring and review</strong> - keeping procedures current as risks change.</li>\n</ol>\n"},{"heading":"3.7 Fraud and Economic Crime","html":"\n<p>The <strong>Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act (ECCTA)</strong> targets economic crime and corporate transparency. It introduced:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>A new regulatory objective in the Legal Services Act 2007 on tackling economic crime.</li>\n<li>Companies House reforms to prevent/shut down fraudulent companies.</li>\n<li>Reforms to prevent abuse of limited partnerships.</li>\n<li>Additional powers to seize/recover suspected criminal cryptoassets.</li>\n<li>A new \"failure to prevent fraud\" offence.</li>\n<li>Measures addressing Strategic Lawsuits Against Public Participation (SLAPPs).</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Failure to Prevent Fraud</h4>\n<p>This new corporate criminal offence came into effect on <strong>1 September 2025</strong>. An organisation may be criminally liable where an employee, agent, subsidiary, or other \"associated person\" commits fraud intending to benefit the organisation, and the organisation lacked reasonable fraud prevention procedures.</p>\n<p>Applies to: all large incorporated bodies, subsidiaries and partnerships; large not-for-profit organisations (e.g., incorporated charities); and incorporated public bodies. A <strong>\"large organisation\"</strong> must meet at least <strong>two</strong> of the following:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Turnover of more than <strong>£36 million</strong>.</li>\n<li>Balance sheet total of more than <strong>£18 million</strong>.</li>\n<li>More than <strong>250 employees</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The offence applies to \"base fraud\" offences under <strong>Schedule 13 ECCTA</strong>: fraud offences under Section 1 of the Fraud Act 2006 (fraud by false representation; fraud by failing to disclose information; fraud by abuse of position); participation in a fraudulent business; obtaining services dishonestly; cheating the public revenue (common law); false accounting; false statements by company directors; and fraudulent trading. Aiding, abetting, counselling or procuring any of these also qualifies. <strong>Money laundering is NOT a fraud offence</strong> for these purposes.</p>\n<p>The base fraud offence must be committed by an \"associated person\": an employee, agent, subsidiary, or a person providing services for/on behalf of the organisation (while providing those services). The benefit may be financial or non-financial, and the organisation need not actually receive any benefit - it is enough that the organisation was <strong>intended</strong> to benefit.</p>\n<p>It is a defence if the organisation had <strong>reasonable procedures</strong> in place to prevent fraud. The Home Office's six fraud prevention principles: top-level commitment; risk assessment; proportionate risk-based prevention procedures; due diligence; communication (including training); monitoring and review.</p>\n<p>The offence creates <strong>strict liability</strong> - even if senior management were unaware of the fraud, the firm can be held liable, making the \"reasonable procedures\" defence critical in practice.</p>\n"},{"heading":"3.8 The Disclosure and Transparency Rules (DTR)","html":"\n<p>The <strong>Disclosure and Transparency Rules (DTR)</strong> are part of the FCA Handbook. Disclosure Rules are implemented by the FCA as listing authority; Transparency Rules give effect to the (former EU) Transparency Directive; both apply to issuers of securities admitted to trading on certain markets.</p>\n<h4>Disclosure Rules</h4>\n<p>Purpose: promote prompt/fair disclosure of relevant information; set out when an issuer may delay public disclosure of inside information; and set requirements to keep such information confidential to protect investors and prevent insider dealing.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Denying access</strong> - issuers must have effective arrangements to deny access to inside information to those who do not need it for their function.</li>\n<li><strong>Breach of confidentiality</strong> - issuers must be able to make prompt public disclosure via an RIS if confidentiality of legitimately-delayed inside information is compromised, including a <strong>holding announcement</strong> giving as much detail as possible, explaining why a full announcement cannot yet be made, and promising further details.</li>\n</ul>\n<h4>Transparency Rules - Notifiable Shareholdings</h4>\n<p>Purpose: ensure adequate transparency/access to information in UK financial markets. The EU Transparency Directive set notification thresholds of <strong>5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 50% and 75%</strong>. The <strong>UK retained its own threshold of 3%</strong> (and each percentage point above), pre-dating the Directive.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>An investor has a notifiable interest once holding <strong>3% or more</strong> of a public company's shares (including via voting rights attached to shares/financial instruments, not just direct share ownership).</li>\n<li>Above 3%, notification is required whenever the holding crosses a <strong>whole percentage point</strong> (e.g., 3.7% to 4.1% is notifiable; 3.7% to 3.9% is not; 5.4% to 4.9% is notifiable; 5.4% to 5.1% is not).</li>\n<li>A fall back below 3% must also be notified.</li>\n<li>Indirect holdings (e.g., via a proxy with discretion to vote) can also trigger notification.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Voting rights attached to shares are <strong>disregarded</strong> in six cases:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Shares acquired solely for clearing/settlement, within the period up to the third trading day after execution.</li>\n<li>Shares held by a custodian or bare nominee.</li>\n<li>Shares held (if less than 10%) by a declared market maker.</li>\n<li>Shares held (if not more than 5%) in the trading book of an investment firm/credit institution.</li>\n<li>Shares held as collateral.</li>\n<li>Shares acquired for stabilisation purposes, provided voting rights are not exercised.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Deemed knowledge of acquisition/disposal (triggering the notification clock) arises no later than <strong>two trading days</strong> after the transaction, or when it becomes unconditional (if conditional on an external event).</p>\n<p>Notifiable interests include shares held by <strong>connected parties</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>The investor's spouse.</li>\n<li>The investor's children (under 18).</li>\n<li>Companies controlled by the investor (control assumed where the investor holds at least <strong>one third</strong> of voting rights).</li>\n<li>Concert parties (agreements between two or more persons to act together, e.g., voting together) - if the combined holding reaches 3%+, it is notifiable as if a single holding.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Process</strong>: notify the company in writing within <strong>two business days</strong> of the 3% threshold (or subsequent percentage point) being reached; if listed, the company must then report to a primary information provider (e.g., LSE's RNS) by the end of the following business day. Notification is <strong>not</strong> made to the FCA. The company must maintain a register of notifications, available at its registered office.</p>\n<p><strong>Exemptions</strong>: fund managers/operators of regulated CISs (e.g., AUTs, OEICs) are non-beneficial holders exempt from the 3% rule; they are instead subject to disclosure at 5% and 10% (not the percentages between), then at every percentage above 10%. Market makers are exempt below 10%. Custodians are fully exempt from disclosure, provided they cannot control voting rights.</p>\n<h4>Companies Act 2006 - Section 793 Letter</h4>\n<p>Under <strong>Section 793 Companies Act 2006</strong>, a UK public company may send a written notice to any person it knows/suspects to be a shareholder (or to have been one within the last <strong>three years</strong>), requiring confirmation of their interest and, for past interests, the identity of any buyer if known. Usually sent to <strong>nominee companies</strong> (legal owner of shares on behalf of a beneficial owner) rather than individuals, to identify the true beneficial owner. A written response is required within a reasonable time; persistent non-response allows the company to apply to court to have the shares <strong>frozen</strong> (loss of voting rights, dividend entitlement, and ability to sell). The company must keep a record of Section 793 letters sent and responses received.</p>\n<h4>International Comparison - US</h4>\n<p>The US Corporate Transparency Act (CTA) 2021 requires companies to report beneficial ownership information to a registry maintained by the <strong>Financial Crimes Enforcement Network (FinCEN)</strong> (US Treasury). Required information: full legal name; date of birth; current residential/business address; and a non-expired US passport/state ID/driving licence number, or valid foreign passport number. Changes must be reported within <strong>one year</strong>, including: ownership exceeding or dropping below <strong>25%</strong>; a substantial change in company ownership; or a change in the beneficial owner's contact details.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"In what year did the UK's market abuse regime, established as an offence under FSMA, come into force?","options":["1993","2001","2016","2019"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The UK's market abuse regime under FSMA came into force in 2001. The CJA (criminal insider dealing) dates from 1993, the MAR came into effect in 2016, and the EU Exit amendments were made in 2019."},{"question":"Following Brexit, which of the following is NOT one of the three key amendments made by the Market Abuse (Amendment) (EU Exit) Regulations 2019?","options":["There is no obligation to share information with EU authorities without a guarantee of reciprocity","The FCA took over the regulatory responsibilities previously held by ESMA and the European Commission","Supplementary legislation is now subject to HM Treasury rather than the EC","The FCA Handbook rules on market abuse became legally binding for the first time"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA Handbook has always provided guidance (not binding rules) on UK MAR matters - this did not change as a result of the 2019 EU Exit amendments. The other three options are the genuine amendments made."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2012, which section covers the offence of making misleading statements in relation to benchmarks?","options":["Section 89","Section 90","Section 91","Section 397"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Section 89 covers making misleading statements, Section 90 covers creating false or misleading impressions, and Section 91 specifically covers misleading statements relating to benchmarks. These sections replaced the former FSMA Section 397."},{"question":"Under the Financial Services Act 2021, what is the maximum custodial sentence on conviction on indictment for a misleading statements offence committed after 1 November 2021?","options":["2 years","5 years","7 years","10 years"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"For offences committed after 1 November 2021, the maximum sentence on indictment is 10 years. Offences committed up to 31 October 2021 carried a maximum of 7 years. Summary conviction carries up to 12 months."},{"question":"For how long must firms retain written documentation of their market abuse detection arrangements, systems and procedures under UK MAR, and how frequently must these be reviewed at a minimum?","options":["3 years; reviewed every 2 years","5 years; reviewed at least annually","7 years; reviewed every 3 years","10 years; reviewed biennially"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"UK MAR requires documented arrangements to be maintained for five years and assessed at least through an annually conducted audit and internal review."},{"question":"A compliance officer identifies a suspicious order that has not yet been executed but appears intended to distort the market. Under UK MAR, what is the correct course of action?","options":["No action is required because no execution has taken place","A STOR should be submitted, as the requirement extends to attempted market abuse","The order should simply be cancelled with no further reporting","The matter should only be escalated internally, not to the FCA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The STOR requirement extends to attempts to commit market manipulation or insider dealing, even where no execution has taken place but there was intent to distort the market. The firm must notify the FCA without delay."},{"question":"Under the Criminal Justice Act 1993, Section 52 sets out three ways of committing the offence of insider dealing. Which of the following is NOT one of them?","options":["Dealing in the security","Encouraging another to deal","Disclosing inside information to another","Failing to report a colleague's suspected insider dealing"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Section 52 CJA creates three offences: dealing, encouraging another to deal, and disclosing inside information. Failure to report a colleague's suspected wrongdoing is a money laundering-related concept (POCA s330), not an insider dealing offence under the CJA."},{"question":"A director's spouse overhears confidential takeover information at home and buys shares in the target company. On what basis would they be considered an 'insider' under UK MAR?","options":["They cannot be an insider because they are not an employee of either company","They obtained the information indirectly from a person who was an inside source","They are automatically exempt as a family member","Only the director, not the spouse, can commit an offence"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A person obtains information from an inside source if they access it directly or indirectly from someone who was themselves an inside source or had access via their employment - as with a spouse who overhears confidential information from a director."},{"question":"The Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023, which updated the list of Regulated Markets for the purposes of Part V of the CJA 1993, came into effect on which date?","options":["1 January 2023","15 June 2023","1 September 2023","31 December 2023"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023 came into effect on 15 June 2023."},{"question":"A broker learns that a client wishes to buy a large block of shares before executing the client's buy order. Which special defence to insider dealing is most likely to apply?","options":["Price stabilisation","Market information","Legitimate behaviour via information barriers","Market maker good faith"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is the classic market information defence - the broker's knowledge of the client's pending order is 'market information', and acting on it to execute the client's instruction is a specific statutory defence, provided it was reasonable in the circumstances."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the general defences available to a defendant charged with insider dealing (or encouraging another to deal)?","options":["The defendant did not expect the dealing to result in a profit or avoided loss","The defendant reasonably believed the information had been widely disclosed","The defendant would have acted the same way regardless of the information","The defendant was acting on behalf of a regulated investment firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Acting on behalf of a regulated firm is not, by itself, a recognised general defence. The three genuine general defences are: no expectation of profit/avoided loss; reasonable belief of wide disclosure; and that the defendant would have acted the same way regardless."},{"question":"Which of the following is one of the four main forms of market manipulation under UK MAR?","options":["Insider list failure","Benchmark manipulation","Failure to disclose","Tipping off"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The four forms of market manipulation are: manipulating transactions, manipulating devices, dissemination, and benchmark manipulation. Failure to disclose and tipping off are POCA offences; insider list failure relates to MAR disclosure obligations, not manipulation itself."},{"question":"A PDMR (person discharging managerial responsibilities) executes a personal transaction in their employer's shares. Within how many business days must this be notified to the issuer and the FCA?","options":["1 business day","3 business days","5 business days","10 business days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"PDMR notifications must be made promptly and no later than three business days after the transaction."},{"question":"What is the length of the 'closed period' during which PDMRs are restricted from dealing in their company's shares before the announcement of an interim or annual financial report?","options":["7 calendar days","14 calendar days","30 calendar days","60 calendar days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The closed period is 30 calendar days before the announcement of the company's interim or annual financial report."},{"question":"Regarding Accepted Market Practices (AMPs) in the UK, which statement is correct?","options":["The FCA has established several AMPs covering common trading strategies","The FCA has the authority to establish AMPs, but currently none have been established in the UK","AMPs are established by the Bank of England, not the FCA","AMPs automatically apply to all algorithmic trading strategies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA has the authority under UK MAR to establish Accepted Market Practices after consultation, but as at the time of writing no AMPs have actually been established in the UK market."},{"question":"Under UK MAR investment recommendation disclosure rules, a firm changing its recommendation on a financial instrument must disclose the change and the date of the previous recommendation if it was disseminated within what preceding period?","options":["3 months","6 months","12 months","24 months"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where a recommendation differs from a previous recommendation on the same instrument disseminated during the preceding 12-month period, the change and date of the earlier recommendation must be indicated - an ongoing disclosure obligation."},{"question":"Under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002, which offence carries a maximum penalty of a fine and/or up to 14 years' imprisonment?","options":["Tipping off (s333A)","Failure to disclose (s330)","Acquisition, use and possession (s329)","Prejudicing an investigation (s342)"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Concealing (s327), arrangements (s328), and acquisition/use/possession (s329) are punishable by a fine and/or up to 14 years. Failure to disclose (s330) and prejudicing an investigation (s342) carry up to 5 years; tipping off (s333A) carries up to 2 years."},{"question":"An employee in the regulated sector suspects a client of money laundering but fails to report it to the firm's nominated officer. Which POCA offence, and what is the applicable test for liability?","options":["Section 333A tipping off; a subjective test only","Section 330 failure to disclose; an objective test (reasonable grounds to know or suspect)","Section 342 prejudicing an investigation; a strict liability test","Section 328 arrangements; a subjective test only"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This is a Section 330 failure to disclose offence. Liability can arise not only where the person actually knew or suspected, but also where there were reasonable grounds to know or suspect - the objective test, based on what a reasonable person would have suspected."},{"question":"Which piece of legislation addressed the 'Spanish bullfighter' issue, whereby conduct legal in its country of origin but illegal in the UK would otherwise count as criminal property under POCA?","options":["The Criminal Finances Act 2017","The Serious Organised Crime and Police Act (SOCPA) 2005","The Money Laundering Regulations 2017","The Bribery Act 2010"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"SOCPA 2005 amended POCA to provide a defence where the alleged offender reasonably believed the conduct was not criminal in the country where it took place, subject to the Secretary of State's power to still designate certain overseas conduct (e.g., serious tax evasion) as reportable."},{"question":"What are the three stages of money laundering, in the correct order?","options":["Layering, placement, integration","Placement, layering, integration","Integration, layering, placement","Placement, integration, layering"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The three stages are placement (introducing proceeds into the financial system), layering (moving funds through complex transactions to obscure origin), and integration (funds appear as legitimate, clean money)."},{"question":"A firm is establishing a business relationship with a new customer who is a beneficial owner of a pooled client account held by a firm of solicitors. Which due diligence tier is most likely to apply?","options":["Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD)","Simplified Due Diligence (SDD)","No due diligence is required at all","Only source-of-wealth checks are required"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Beneficial owners of pooled accounts held by notaries or legal professionals are one of the categories where Simplified Due Diligence may be applied, reflecting a lower assessed ML risk."},{"question":"Which of the following would trigger a requirement for Enhanced Due Diligence (EDD) under MLR 2017?","options":["The customer is a UK public authority","The customer is a listed company","The transaction involves a correspondent banking relationship","The product is a child trust fund"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Correspondent banking relationships are one of the specific circumstances requiring EDD under MLR 2017. UK public authorities, listed companies and CTFs are examples where Simplified Due Diligence may instead apply."},{"question":"Under the PEP definition used in AML regulation, an individual who has left a prominent public function ceases to require automatic EDD treatment after what minimum period?","options":["6 months","1 year","2 years","5 years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A firm's obligation to apply EDD to a former PEP ceases one year after they left office, or longer if the firm considers this appropriate given ongoing ML/TF risk."},{"question":"Which statement correctly distinguishes the roles of the MLRO and the Nominated Officer?","options":["The Nominated Officer oversees the firm's entire AML compliance framework, while the MLRO only receives internal reports","The MLRO and Nominated Officer are legally required to be different individuals within the firm","The Nominated Officer is the designated recipient of internal suspicion reports with a legal duty to report to the NCA; in practice this role is usually combined with the broader MLRO function","Only firms with more than 250 employees are required to appoint a Nominated Officer"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The nominated officer (required under MLR 2017) is the internal recipient of suspicion reports with the legal duty to escalate to the NCA. The MLRO has the broader FCA-driven compliance oversight role. In practice, the same individual usually performs both roles."},{"question":"Which of the following is the corporate, strict-liability offence created by the Bribery Act 2010, carrying an unlimited fine rather than imprisonment?","options":["Section 1 - bribing another person","Section 2 - being bribed","Section 6 - bribing a foreign public official","Section 7 - failure of a commercial organisation to prevent bribery"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Section 7 creates the corporate offence of failing to prevent bribery by an associated person, carrying an unlimited fine. Sections 1, 2 and 6 are individual offences carrying up to 10 years' imprisonment."},{"question":"Under the Bribery Act 2010, when is a facilitation payment to a foreign official NOT an offence?","options":["Whenever the payment is below £100","When it is permitted by the written law of the official's country, or made under threat of violence","Whenever the payment is made through a third party","Facilitation payments are never permitted under any circumstances"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Facilitation payments are generally an offence, except where the written law of the foreign official's country permits the official to accept the advantage, or where the payment is made under threat of violence."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the Ministry of Justice's six principles for 'adequate procedures' as a defence to the Bribery Act Section 7 offence?","options":["Proportionality","Top-level commitment","Risk assessment","Mandatory annual external audit by a Big Four firm"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The six MoJ principles are proportionality, top-level commitment, risk assessment, due diligence, communication, and monitoring and review. There is no requirement for a mandatory external audit by a specific category of auditor."},{"question":"Under the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act (ECCTA), an organisation is classified as 'large' (and therefore within scope of the failure to prevent fraud offence) if it meets at least two of which three criteria?","options":["Turnover >£10m; balance sheet >£5m; >50 employees","Turnover >£36m; balance sheet total >£18m; >250 employees","Turnover >£50m; balance sheet >£25m; >500 employees","Turnover >£1bn; balance sheet >£500m; >1,000 employees"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"A 'large organisation' under ECCTA must meet at least two of: turnover of more than £36 million, balance sheet total of more than £18 million, or more than 250 employees."},{"question":"When did the new 'failure to prevent fraud' corporate offence under ECCTA come into effect?","options":["1 September 2023","1 January 2024","1 September 2025","1 January 2026"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The failure to prevent fraud offence came into effect on 1 September 2025."},{"question":"Which of the following is explicitly stated NOT to be a 'base fraud offence' for the purposes of the failure to prevent fraud offence under ECCTA?","options":["Fraud by false representation","False accounting","Money laundering","Fraudulent trading"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The manual explicitly notes that money laundering is not an offence of fraud for these purposes. The base fraud offences under Schedule 13 ECCTA include fraud by false representation, false accounting, fraudulent trading and others, but not money laundering."},{"question":"An investor's shareholding in a UK-listed company rises from 3.7% to 3.9%. Under the UK DTR, is this notifiable?","options":["Yes, because it exceeds 3%","No, because it has not crossed a whole percentage point","Yes, because all changes above 1% must be reported","No, notification is only required for decreases in holdings"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Once above the 3% notifiable threshold, further notification is only required when the holding crosses a whole percentage point. A move from 3.7% to 3.9% does not cross a whole percentage point (e.g., from 3% to 4%), so it is not notifiable; a move to 4.1% would be."},{"question":"What is the UK's notifiable shareholding threshold under the DTR, and how does it differ from the EU Transparency Directive's approach?","options":["5%, the same as the EU threshold","3%, retained by the UK rather than adopting the EU's 5%/10%/15% scale","10%, higher than the EU's 5% threshold","1%, lower than any EU threshold"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The EU Transparency Directive set thresholds of 5%, 10%, 15%, 20%, 25%, 30%, 50% and 75%. The UK retained its own pre-existing threshold of 3% (and every percentage point above) rather than adopting the EU scale."},{"question":"Once a notifiable threshold is crossed under the UK DTR, within how many business days must the investor notify the company, and by when must the company inform the market?","options":["1 business day to notify the company; same day to the market","2 business days to notify the company; by the end of the following business day to the market","5 business days to notify the company; within 5 business days to the market","There is no fixed timeframe; disclosure must simply be 'prompt'"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The investor must notify the company in writing within two business days of the threshold being reached; the company must then report to a primary information provider (e.g., the LSE's RNS) by the end of the following business day. Notification is not made directly to the FCA."},{"question":"For the purposes of connected parties under the DTR, at what level of voting rights is an investor assumed to control a company?","options":["10%","20%","One quarter (25%)","One third"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Control of a company is assumed for connected-party purposes if the investor holds at least one third of its voting rights."},{"question":"Under Section 793 of the Companies Act 2006, what can happen if a shareholder persistently ignores a company's written notice requesting disclosure of their shareholding interest?","options":["The company can automatically cancel the shares","The company can apply to court to have the shares frozen, removing voting and dividend rights and the ability to sell","The FCA will impose a fine directly on the shareholder","Nothing further can be done as compliance is voluntary"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If Section 793 notices are persistently ignored, the company can apply to court to have the shares held frozen - the shareholder loses voting rights, dividend entitlement, and the ability to sell the shares."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is TRUE regarding market abuse?","options":["Intention does not need to be proved","The offence only relates to dealings in securities traded on a UK market","The maximum penalty is ten years in jail plus an unlimited fine","It applies only to UK approved persons"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Market abuse is 'effects' based legislation and therefore intention does not need to be proved. It applies in relation to all qualifying investments traded on a prescribed market, i.e. RIE, ROIE and all EU regulated exchanges. Generally the FCA will normally only prosecute for offences in relation to UK markets. It is a civil offence and therefore does not carry a jail sentence. It applies to all persons, i.e. individuals and institutions, whether they are located in the UK or overseas."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true regarding the insider dealing legislation under the Criminal Justice Act?","options":["Offences can be committed by individuals but not by companies","Encouraging a third party to deal on the basis of inside information is an offence","UK equities, gilts and related derivatives are all covered by the legislation","Disclosure of price sensitive information to a third party outside the company affected is not an offence"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Only individuals can be guilty of the offences. Any securities traded on exchange are covered. If the price sensitive information is disclosed to a third party from outside the company affected, it is still an offence and that third party becomes a secondary insider."},{"question":"All of the following are covered by the UK insider dealing legislation except:","options":["Options on shares","ADRs","Debentures","Forward foreign exchange contracts"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Insider dealing applies to MiFID instruments."},{"question":"The MLRO must report to senior management at least:","options":["Once every 6 months","Once every 12 months","Once every 18 months","Once every 24 months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The MLRO must report to senior management at least once every calendar year."},{"question":"According to the Money Laundering Regulations, which of the following is FALSE?","options":["Staff must be trained to recognise suspicious transactions","A firm must keep records for a minimum of three years","The firm must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer","The rules can apply to insurance companies"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The firm must keep records for five years."},{"question":"Which of the following is TRUE of the Money Laundering Regulations 2017?","options":["All firms subject to the regulations must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer","A firm cannot be prosecuted under the regulations if a clear audit trail has been documented","Records of customer identification must be kept for three years after the person ceases to be a customer","Disciplinary sanctions under the regulations are determined by the FCA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Technically, the MLR2017 requires the appointment of a 'nominated officer' - however, although the obligations of the MLRO under the FCA requirements are different from those of the nominated officer under POCA, the Terrorism Act or the MLR 2017, in practice the same person tends to carry on both roles, and is usually known as the MLRO. Although failing to keep proper records is an offence, the mere presence of a 'clear audit trail' does not stop the firm being prosecuted under any circumstances. The legislation and sanctions were written by HMT. Records must be kept for five years."},{"question":"Under the Money Laundering Regulations all of the following are considered high-risk factors EXCEPT:","options":["Correspondent banking transactions","Transactions related to tobacco products","Transaction related to items of archaeological significance","Transactions within a pooled client account"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Although transactions within a pooled account are no longer automatically afforded simplified due diligence (SDD), they are not, in themselves, a high risk factor.\n\nCorrespondent banking transactions relates to large value transactions between international entities/banks (NOSTRO account transfers)."},{"question":"Under which of the following is insider dealing a criminal offence?","options":["Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","Companies Act 2006","The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Criminal Justice Act 1993"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Note: FSMA 2000 gives the FCA the power to prosecute."},{"question":"Failure to comply with the FCA's rule on fair, clear and not misleading communications would also constitute a breach of which of the following?","options":["Criminal Justice Act","Order execution policy","Trust law","Misleading statements and impressions"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Failure to comply with the FCA's rule on fair, clear and not misleading communications would also constitute a breach of FSA 2012 s89-91 Misleading Statements and Impressions."},{"question":"Which activity is the subject of the defence in relation to distorting market prices of 'acting in accordance with the rules of'?","options":["Market making","Price stabilisation","Underwriting","Stock lending"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Behaviour which conforms to the share buy back and stabilisation regulations will not amount to market abuse. This is known as a 'safe harbour'."},{"question":"Which of the following gives the FCA the power to prosecute insider dealing offences?","options":["Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","Criminal Justice Act 1993","The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Companies Act 2006"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The offence is contained within the CJA 1993, however the ability of the FCA to prosecute comes from ss 401-402 FSMA 2000."},{"question":"Which transaction may be permitted during a closed period for a person discharging managerial responsibilities?","options":["A discretionary purchase to increase exposure before expected strong results","A sale to a family member to improve personal liquidity before publication","A transaction under an employee share scheme where beneficial ownership does not change","A purchase funded by a bonus that has not yet been disclosed to the market"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The source states that the closed-period restriction may be lifted for transactions under employee share schemes or similar transactions where there is no change in beneficial ownership. The other options describe discretionary dealing that is not identified as an exception."},{"question":"Which of the following would NOT be covered by the insider dealing legislation?","options":["FTSE index option","A foreign exchange forward","Debenture","FTSE futures contract"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Insider dealing legislation (CJA 1993) does not cover foreign exchange forwards."},{"question":"Which one of the following is NOT a money laundering offence, as defined in the Proceeds of Crime Act?","options":["Tipping off","Failure to report","Assistance","Dealing on price sensitive information"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The answer to 'dealing on price sensitive information' is an offence under the INSIDER DEALING legislation."},{"question":"Which of the following is not covered by the insider dealing legislation?","options":["Building society deposits","Gilts","Options on UK listed companies","Gilt futures"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Insider dealing covers MiFID instruments."},{"question":"What is the aim of the money laundering regulations?","options":["To set out the different money laundering offences","To set out the different penalties for money laundering","To prevent those with criminal records from opening bank accounts","To prevent the proceeds of crime from entering the financial system"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Money laundering rules are part of the general fight against crime."},{"question":"Which are TRUE of money laundering?","options":["The JMLSG Guidance Notes have received Treasury approval","It is a criminal offence not to report a suspicion","It is a criminal offence not to have procedures in place","All are true"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The courts are required to take account of guidance approved by the Treasury, such as the guidance notes. All are correct."},{"question":"Which reform is identified as one of the aims of the Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act?","options":["Introducing a new retail conduct regime for insurance intermediaries","Creating a new authorisation gateway for appointed representatives","Reforming Companies House to prevent fraudulent companies being created","Establishing a new compensation scheme for victims of market abuse"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Act is aimed at tackling economic crime and improving transparency over corporate entities, including reforms to Companies House to prevent the creation of, and shut down, fraudulent companies. The other options are not listed reforms under the Act."},{"question":"Market abuse is different from the criminal offence of insider dealing in all of the following ways except:","options":["Market abuse is based on the civil burden of proof, i.e. the balance of probabilities","It should be easier for the FCA to demonstrate abusive behaviour","It should be easier for the FCA to impose a custodial sentence","It can apply to commodities as well as commodity derivatives"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The burden of proof is less difficult to establish than beyond a reasonable doubt and hence it should be easier to demonstrate abusive behaviour. However, only fines can be imposed. Market abuse is applicable across the board."},{"question":"If the FCA is leading an investigation into insider dealing, who can use the special defence of 'acting in good faith'?","options":["Director","Person who has market information","Market-maker","Person involved in a hostile takeover"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Being a market maker is a special defence against the charge of insider dealing."},{"question":"A firm ends a business relationship with a client after completing customer due diligence and several monitored transactions. For how long must the firm keep copies of documents and information obtained for anti-money laundering and counter-terrorist financing purposes?","options":["For three years after the business relationship ends","For five years after the business relationship ends","For seven years after the business relationship ends","For ten years after the business relationship ends"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms must keep copies of documents and information obtained to meet customer due diligence and counter-terrorist financing requirements, and sufficient transaction records, for five years after the business relationship ends or after an occasional transaction. Ten years is the outer limit beyond which transactional records in a business relationship do not need to be kept."},{"question":"A chair of the board has access to price sensitive information about his company's shares. In which of the following circumstances could a director in the company trade?","options":["The director would follow the PDMR rules","The director would need permission from the FCA","The director would not be permitted to trade","Only if the director can prove they do not have any material price sensitive information"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"What the chair knows is irrelevant to the director's dealings. The director would go through the normal procedures."},{"question":"Which of the following is a provision of the Money Laundering Regulations?","options":["Employees should report all suspicions to the National Crime Agency","There is no need to identify new business","The regulations are considered guidance and are not legally binding","Training must be given to employees in relation to money laundering prevention"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Employees report to the MLRO. There is a need to identify new business or customers. The regulations are a legal obligation."},{"question":"If a financial analyst is providing investment research for dissemination to its clients in particular financial investments, when would they be permitted to deal on that information?","options":["The financial analyst, as a relevant person, would never be able to deal on that information","The financial analyst, as a non-relevant person, would always be allowed to deal on that information","The financial analyst, as a relevant person, would be permitted to deal on the information if they submitted all confirmations of transactions to the firm's compliance","The financial analyst, as a relevant person, would be permitted to deal on the information if granted approval by the firm's compliance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The personal account dealing rules state that permission to trade must be granted by Compliance. Although the analyst would be permitted to deal in this circumstance, it would be an exceptional circumstance for the firm's compliance to grant approval."},{"question":"Which disclosure of inside information is permitted under UK Market Abuse Regulation?","options":["Disclosure to a journalist to test likely market reaction before an announcement","Disclosure to a professional adviser to obtain advice on the transaction","Disclosure to a family member to discuss the commercial implications privately","Disclosure to a client to improve order timing before the information is published"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Permitted disclosure includes sharing inside information with a professional adviser, such as a lawyer or auditor, and other legitimate disclosures made in the normal exercise of duties. The other disclosures are not identified as permitted in the provided material."},{"question":"Before conducting designated investment business for a new customer, the money laundering regulations require an authorised firm to evidence identity. Which of the following is not true?","options":["Unless satisfactory evidence of identity is obtained in a timely manner, the business must not proceed","To identify someone, an official document bearing a photograph should always be obtained, whether the new business is opened face-to-face or through the post.","The register of electors, recent utility bills and bank statements are valid methods of verifying the address of a prospective customer","Records of identity must be kept for five years"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Although photographic identification is favourable, it is not always necessary. The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group states:\n\"a government-issued document (without a photograph) which incorporates the customer's full name, supported by a second document … which incorporates: - the customer's full name and - either his residential address - or his date of birth.\""},{"question":"In relation to the insider dealing legislation, which of the following individuals would be considered to be an insider?","options":["An employee who bought shares in the company after he heard that the chairman had purchased shares","A shareholder who purchased shares following receipt of published financial statements","The human resources director who purchases shares after being appointed to the position","The audit partner who sells shares after reading the audit report prior to publication of the financial statements"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The audit partner would be deemed to be an insider, as he has access to information which is not publicly available."},{"question":"Sue becomes suspicious of a 'high number of sales' by her client to an unknown party. Sue should report her fears to:","options":["The client","The FCA","The NCA","The MLRO"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The MLRO is the Money Laundering Reporting Officer. Every authorised firm must have one. They act as a conduit for information relating to money laundering from inside the firm to the relevant authorities."},{"question":"In the context of market abuse which of the following circumstances would not be considered an offence?","options":["Short selling","Price positioning","Abusive squeezes","Artificial transactions"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Short selling is a common acceptable trading strategy. The rest would be considered market abuse."},{"question":"Where can the law relating to insider dealing as a criminal offence be found?","options":["The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","The Criminal Justice Act 1993","The Companies Act 2006"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 outlines criminal money laundering offences, the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 creates regulatory powers for the FCA and PRA, and the Companies Act 2006 allows companies to audit their shareholder register (amongst other things)."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT true of insider dealing as defined under the Criminal Justice Act?","options":["It only covers UK regulated markets, UK MTFs and UK OTFs","Gilts and other government bonds are included in the law on insider dealing","The Financial Conduct Authority is responsible for prosecuting offences","A defence exists if the individual would have acted in the same way had they not had the information"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The legislation applies to MiFID instruments traded on UK/EEA/Gibraltar regulated markets, MTFs and OTFs, together with the NASDAQ, SIX and NYSE."},{"question":"With regards to money laundering, the FCA will check whether a firm is in breach of the rules on systems and controls with reference to the firm's adherence to:","options":["The conduct of business rules","NCA reporting rules","JMLSG guidance","The Proceeds of Crime Act"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group provides guidance of how to implement strategies to mitigate the risks of receiving and processing money for the purpose of laundering. The FCA set out their own requirements and standards for authorised firms in their sourcebook covering senior management arrangements, systems and controls (SYSC). When considering a breach of these requirements the FCA will make reference to whether the firm has followed the JMLSG guidance."},{"question":"To whom must the Money Laundering Reporting Officer report suspicious activities?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Action Task Force","The board of directors","National Crime Agency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Any suspicions must immediately be reported to the NCA. Any weakness in internal controls must be submitted to the firm's directors on an annual basis."},{"question":"Which of the following is an inter-governmental body which sets international standards that aim to prevent money laundering and terrorist financing activities and the harm they cause to society?","options":["Federation of Regulators Against Criminal Activities","Financial Action Task Force","European Securities Markets Authority","International Anti-Money Laundering Commission"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Financial Action Task Force (FATF) was established in 1989 to initially examine and develop measures to combat money laundering. The FATF is the global money laundering and terrorist financing watchdog. It is an inter-governmental body which sets international standards that aim to prevent these illegal activities and the harm they cause to society."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be considered a suspicious transaction under the Money Laundering Regulations?","options":["Customer services are asked to take payment from a different account than the account history shows is usual","Payment is from a debit card account with a different card holder name","The customer initiates several small transactions totalling less than €7,500; it is an unusual size for the customer's transaction history","The customer is unable to prove his identity through the usual means, but is willing to offer other forms of identification"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Any unusual behaviour exhibited by a customer that does not conform to their profile, characteristics or history may be deemed a suspicious transaction."},{"question":"Which of the following pieces of legislation cover money laundering?","options":["Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Regulated Activities Order 2001","Bank of England Act 1998","Financial Services Act 2012"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 is the legislation that defines the money laundering offences which are all punishable by a fine and/or a term of imprisonment."},{"question":"The Criminal Justice Act regarding insider dealing contains a special defence providing the person was acting in good faith. This defence applies to:","options":["Private investors","Market makers","Managers of unit trusts","ICVCs"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is a defence for market makers, providing they are acting in good faith in the ordinary course of business."},{"question":"If an employee notices a suspicious transaction, which of the following is the correct first course of action?","options":["Their line manager reports to the NCA","The MLRO reports to the NCA","The employee reports to the MLRO","The employee discusses the matter with the client"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"You must report to the MLRO who then reports to the NCA."},{"question":"What do insider dealing and market abuse have in common?","options":["They are defined by the Criminal Justice Act","They can both involve the use of non public information","Intent must be demonstrated","They are criminal offences"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Market abuse is dealt with by the Market Abuse Regulation. Market abuse does not require the person to have intended to abuse the market. Insider dealing is a criminal offence, however market abuse is a civil offence."},{"question":"Under which piece of legislation is the FCA empowered to issue fines for money laundering offences?","options":["FSMA 2000","POCA 2002","CJA 93","Companies Act 1985"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 grants the FCA the power to prosecute for money laundering offences under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002 and for insider dealing offences under the Criminal Justice Act 1993."},{"question":"Who would receive notification of a director trading shares in their own company?","options":["The Takeover Panel","Their company","The Bank of England","The Competition and Markets Authority"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directors inform their company within three business days when they trade in their own company's shares."},{"question":"At which stages of the money laundering process are investment firms generally most vulnerable to being involved in money laundering activities?","options":["Placement and layering","Layering and integration","Placement and integration","None of these stages"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"As a general rule, investment firms are most vulnerable to being involved in money laundering activities during the layering and integration stages of money laundering. Note: banks and building societies are most vulnerable at the placement stage."},{"question":"The FCA will consult which of the following sources of information in relation to money laundering offences?","options":["The Criminal Justice Act","The Joint Money Laundering Steering Group","The FCA Handbook","The Financial Services and Markets Tribunal"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Guidance, produced by the JMLSG, is provided to firms on how they should interpret and implement the anti-money laundering provisions. They are not mandatory but do highlight industry best practice. They are also approved by the Treasury, which means that if a firm can show that it adhered to them, the courts will take this into account as evidence of compliance with the legislation."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the specified types of market abuse?","options":["Insider dealing","Manipulating transactions","Processing proceeds of crime","Dissemination"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Processing proceeds of crime is the offence of money laundering."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be considered market abuse?","options":["An employee of a firm trades on price-sensitive information that has been disclosed through an RIS","A firm sells a large holding of bonds, a transaction that significantly reduces the market price, and then repurchases their holding at the lower price","A firm unintentionally submits incorrect trade details, which leads to a significant move in the market","A firm controls a large percentage of a company's shares and takes out a large up-bet on those shares with a spread-betting firm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Information disclosed through an RIS (regulatory information service), such as the LSE's Regulatory News Service, is considered to be public information. For B, the firm has engaged in a wash trade and D is an example of an abusive squeeze, both of which is a breach of the Market Abuse Directive. Market abuse does not need to prove intent, so C may also be a breach under the Market Abuse Regulation."},{"question":"In relation to money laundering, which of the following is FALSE?","options":["Money laundering offences are defined by the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002","Identification procedures are generally required for anyone wishing to engage in new business","The offences are not confined to the financial services industry","Firms have to report money laundering suspicions to the Financial Action Task Force (FATF)"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"FATF is an inter-governmental policy making body. In the UK, suspicions are reported to NCA."},{"question":"Inside information could be which of the following?","options":["Information which, if made public, could significantly affect the share price","Specific information on a takeover which has been printed in a newspaper","General information that a company is doing well","Public information announced by a shareholder"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Information which is not public, but would affect the share price if it was, would be classified as inside information."},{"question":"The FCA is pursuing an investigation into insider dealing. Which of the following would provide the accused with a special defence under the Criminal Justice Act against an accusation?","options":["Did not expect the recipient to deal","Market maker acting in ordinary course of business","Believed the information was published","Would have dealt anyway"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The other three are defined as general defences against insider dealing."},{"question":"Insider trading legislation does not cover which ONE of the following?","options":["Bond derivatives","Contract for difference on equity","Debentures","Forward foreign exchange contracts"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Insider trading only covers equity, debt, related derivatives and contracts for difference."},{"question":"Which of the following is true regarding money laundering?","options":["Placement and integration are the two stages in the process","All suspicions should be reported by employees to the MLRO","All suspicions must be reported by the MLRO to NCA","The role of MLRO is a certification function"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The three stages include layering. The MLRO has discretion to decide which suspicions are reported by the firm to NCA. The MLRO is a senior management function."},{"question":"Which two of the following are FALSE of the Money Laundering Regulations?\nIFailure to report a suspicion is a criminal offence\nIIFirms must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer\nIIINot reporting when there are 'reasonable grounds' to suspect money laundering exists is not an offence\nIVThe regulations only apply to drugs laundering operations","options":["I and II","I and III","III and IV","II and IV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The requirement (under the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002) to report when 'reasonable grounds' exist places an objective test of suspicion on the financial services industry."},{"question":"Which organisation would a Money Laundering Reporting Officer normally report suspicions of money laundering to?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","National Crime Agency","Joint Money Laundering Steering Group","Financial Action Task Force"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The MLRO must report any suspicions to the National Crime Agency."},{"question":"In relation to the insider dealing legislation which ONE of the following is a SPECIAL defence against insider dealing?","options":["The information was publicly available","The information was being passed on in the proper course of business","The person would have dealt anyway","The individual acted in accordance with the stabilisation rules"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The special defences are:\n1. Stabilisation rules\n2. Recipients of market information\n3. Market makers"},{"question":"If a chair of the board in a listed company wanted to deal in the shares of her own company, she would inform:","options":["The company","HM Treasury","The PRA","The ESMA"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"All directors must inform their company when they trade in their own company's shares. There is also a need to inform the FCA. This is a requirement of the MAR PDMR Regime."},{"question":"The MLRO is an officer of the firm with what characteristics?","options":["The authority to carry out the responsibilities","Complete independence from the company","Some information to allow a reasonable attempt at carrying out the relevant duties","The authority and sufficient independence within the firm to carry out the duty"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"'The authority to carry out the responsibilities' is partially true. Complete independence is not necessary for the MLRO and some information is insufficient.\n'The authority and sufficient independence within the firm to carry out the duty' is the best answer."},{"question":"Which of the following is least likely to supply information as an insider?","options":["A secretary","A technician","A director","A small shareholder"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"All employees may be guilty of insider dealing. Major shareholders may also have access to inside, price-sensitive information, but small shareholders are unlikely to enjoy such access."},{"question":"Which of the following instruments is NOT within the scope of the Criminal Justice Act 1993 (Insider Dealing)?","options":["Building Society share account","Warrant","Equity future","Gilt"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"A building society share account would not be covered as it is not a MiFID instrument."},{"question":"Which statement correctly describes accepted market practices under UK Market Abuse Regulation in the UK?","options":["They are established by trading venues for each instrument without regulatory approval","They are established by issuers for liquid securities with regular institutional trading","They are established by investment firms where clients have given prior written consent","They are established by the Financial Conduct Authority, although none currently exist"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The source states that the FCA is responsible for assessing and establishing accepted market practices in the UK market, but currently no accepted market practices have been established. The other options assign that role to bodies not identified in the source."},{"question":"Why does a firm have a written money laundering policy?","options":["So that management can identify specific customer investment requirements","To ensure that there is a framework with which to deal with money laundering","Only where enhanced due diligence is required due to the sensitive nature of the firm's client","To set out specific details in relation to the legislation provided in the Criminal Justice Act"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms must have an anti-money laundering policy statement in place; this provides a framework to the firm and its staff, and must identify named individuals and functions responsible for implementing particular aspects of the policy. The policy must also set out how senior management undertakes its assessment of the money laundering and terrorist financing risks the firm faces, and how these risks are to be managed."},{"question":"Primarily what kind of offence is insider dealing?","options":["Civil","Common law","Criminal","Tort"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Insider dealing is primarily a criminal offence as defined under the Criminal Justice Act. Where the regulator is unable to prove criminality, it has the ability to fall back on the civil offence of Market Abuse."},{"question":"The purpose of the Joint Money Laundering Steering Group (JMLSG) is:","options":["To provide firms with guidance in respect of money laundering regulations","To provide mandatory strategies on how to apply the money laundering regulations","To specify to the courts the penalties for money laundering offences","To instruct the FCA how to apply European money laundering legislation in the UK"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The purpose of the committee is to provide guidance in respect of the money laundering regulation. It is not mandatory, but can be used as a safe harbour against prosecution."},{"question":"Which TWO of the following would constitute insider dealing?\nIDealing on unpublished, price-sensitive information\nIIA director dealing in company shares immediately after the annual report is published\nIIIEncouraging another person to deal on unpublished, price-sensitive information\nIVBuying shares on rumours of a takeover","options":["I and IV","II and III","II and IV","I and III"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Dealing on, or encouraging another person to deal on, unpublished, price-sensitive information can constitute insider dealing. Note: if the annual results have been published, the information would not be classed as 'unpublished' any more. Also, 'rumours' are unlikely to constitute specific or precise inside information."},{"question":"To which of the following must a Money Laundering Reporting Officer report money laundering suspicions?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Action Task Force","National Crime Agency","Joint Money Laundering Steering Group"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"NCA stands for the National Crime Agency - a body that liaises with the police and customs in the investigation of money laundering and other criminal circumstances."},{"question":"Which of the following would not be committing an offence under the Criminal Justice Act 1993 if they purchased shares in ABC plc?","options":["An auditor to ABC who resigned three months ago who has confidential information on the company's results","A secretary to the office services manager in ABC who has seen an advance copy of an announcement to repurchase shares","A non-executive director of ABC who resigned seven months ago who expects profits to show an unexpected rise when published","A friend of the director of ABC who has obtained price-sensitive information from the director"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The non-executive director is not an insider since he does not have inside information from an inside source."},{"question":"Which of the following are TRUE of money laundering?\nIThere are three stages: placement, layering and integration\nIIAssisting a money launderer, failure to report and tipping off are all offences\nIIIThe firm must appoint an appropriate person to handle suspected cases\nIVRecords must be maintained for five years under the Money Laundering Regulations","options":["I, II and III","I, II, III and IV","I and IV","I, II and IV"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Note: other procedures include internal controls and training of staff."},{"question":"Which of the following would be committing an offence under the Criminal Justice Act 1993 if they purchased shares in ABC plc?","options":["A market maker who obtained price-sensitive information in the course of his business and the information is of a kind it would be reasonable to expect him to obtain","A director of ABC plc with price-sensitive information who undertook the transaction without intending to make a profit or to avoid a loss","A friend of a director of ABC plc who obtained information but was unaware that it was price sensitive","None of the above"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An insider must knowingly have inside information (i.e. price sensitive and not public) from a known inside source. If there is no intent, then there is no offence. Being a market maker is an acceptable defence."},{"question":"Which of the following is untrue of the FCA rules and the legislation on money laundering?","options":["As part of the FCA's operational objective to protect and enhance market integrity, it must make sure that firms take adequate steps to prevent and detect money laundering","A firm does not have to identify a client if the transaction is one off and for €30,000","A firm must provide training for its staff to recognise suspicious transactions","A firm is required to retain documentation for a period of five years from the date that the client ceases to be a client of the firm"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"B is false. One off transactions above €7500 require identification."},{"question":"Which ONE of the following is a defence against S89-91 of Financial Services Act 2012 - making misleading statements or impressions?","options":["Market maker acting in good faith","Adherence to Stabilisation Rules","Adherence to conflicts of interest guidance","Adherence to Takeover Code"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"There is a defence available if the statement is made in conformity with the price stabilisation rules of the FCA.\nActing in accordance with the Takeover Code would provide a defence against market abuse.\nThere is a market maker defence for insider dealing, but not for misleading statements and impressions."},{"question":"Which of the following are TRUE regarding insider dealing?\nIOffences are committed by individuals, not corporations\nIIEncouraging a third party to deal is an offence\nIIIUK equities and gilts and their derivatives are covered\nIVDisclosure of inside information, other than in the normal course of business, is an offence","options":["I, II, III and IV","I, II and IV","II and IV","I and III"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Insider dealing is a crime committed by INDIVIDUALS. Offences include dealing, encouraging and passing on information."},{"question":"Which of the following is FALSE with regard to the money laundering regulations?","options":["Failure to report suspicions is an offence under the regulations","A Money Laundering Reporting Officer is only required for large, complex organisations","Inadequate education of staff is an offence even if no money laundering transactions take place","Records of identification must be kept for five years after the termination of the relationship"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"An MLRO is a required control function in all firms."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the role of the Joint Money Laundering Steering Group in the financial sector?","options":["A governmental body that produces statutory regulation for financial institutions on fulfilling their duties in relation to money laundering","The FCA division responsible for the monitoring of provisions for money laundering in financial institutions","An independent body that provides guidance for financial institutions on fulfilling their duties in relation to money laundering","A branch of the National Crime Agency responsible for investigating money laundering activities in financial institutions"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The guidance notes produced by the JMLSG are not statutory. However, they have been given approval by HM Treasury, so will be taken into account by any court prosecuting under POCA."},{"question":"With regard to money laundering, all suspicions should be reported by employees to the MLRO, who in turn reports to?","options":["FCA","NCA","FATF","JMLSG"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Each firm, no matter how small, must appoint a Money Laundering Reporting Officer (MLRO) to whom suspicions should be reported. The MLRO will then, if appropriate, speak to the relevant authority, which is the National Crime Agency (NCA).\nFATF stands for Financial Action Task Force. JMLSG stands for Joint Money Laundering Steering Group."},{"question":"Which of the following points is true in relations to the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002?","options":["All businesses must identify clients","It is secondary legislation","Money Laundering covers property related to all crimes","JMLSG takes precedence over POCA 02"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"POCA 2002 is widely drafted. It specifies that money laundering relates to criminal property – that is, any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct."},{"question":"Graham has been accused of giving a misleading impression to the markets in the course of his professional activities. Which of the following would give him a defence under FSA 2012?","options":["Takeover rules","Price stabilisation rules","Stock lending rules","Underwriting rules"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"This question is asking about the offence of misleading statements and impressions under FSA 2012. Price stabilisation (the artificial support of the share price) is a specific defence for these offences."},{"question":"A fund manager lets the market know that they are very keen to buy substantial quantities of shares in Benno plc, when actually they hold a smaller quantity of shares in Benno that they plan to sell. Which of the following criminal offences, under the Financial Services Act 2012, is the fund manager most likely to be guilty of?","options":["Manipulating devices","Misleading statements","Disclosing inside information","Tipping off"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The fund manager is giving a misleading statement. ss89 - 91 of the Financial Services Act 2012 covers misleading statements (s89) and impressions (s90) as well as misleading impressions in respect of benchmarks (s91).\n'Manipulating devices' is a type of market abuse (a civil offence), which is covered by the Market Abuse Regulation (MAR).\nInsider dealing is covered under s52 of the Criminal Justice Act 1993.\n'Tipping off' is a money laundering offence, covered under s333 of the Proceeds of Crime Act 2002."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT an offence under the insider dealing legislation?","options":["Passing on price-sensitive information","A director dealing in their own company's shares two months before announcement of results","Dealing on price-sensitive information","Encouraging someone to deal on price-sensitive information"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directors are permitted to deal in their company's shares subject to:\n1. Proper disclosure.\n2. Not acting on inside information."},{"question":"For the purposes of money laundering if a client is acting on behalf of a third party, whose identity needs to be established?","options":["The client","The third party","Both the client and the third party","Neither the client nor the third party"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It will be the responsibility of the client/agent to establish the identity of their own client."},{"question":"In respect of Money Laundering Regulations which of the following is NOT true?","options":["Once an employee has reported suspicions to their Money Laundering Reporting Officer they have a further duty of disclosure to the authorities","It is a criminal offence not to report suspicions of money laundering","It is a criminal offence not to have adequate monitoring and reporting systems to prevent money laundering","Money Laundering Regulations relate to all serious crimes such as drugs dealing, counterfeiting and extortion"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The money laundering officer, not the employee, has an obligation to report suspicions to the National Crime Agency."},{"question":"At which stage of the money laundering process are the proceeds of crime separated from their source?","options":["Layering","Integration","Identification","Placement"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Placement is the physical disposal of cash, layering separates proceeds from their source and integration provides apparent legitimacy to criminally derived wealth."},{"question":"Which of the following is a general defence to insider dealing?","options":["Stabilisation","Market makers acting in the normal course of business","Would have dealt anyway","Recipients of market information"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The other three are special or technical defences to insider dealing. These provide situations where individuals may legally possess price-sensitive non-public information. 'Would have dealt anyway' is a general defence (available to anyone)."},{"question":"Market abuse involving manipulating devices would cover:","options":["Life insurance","Private company shares","Unsecured personal loans","Shares in an investment trust"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Investment trust shares are secondary market traded and are therefore covered."},{"question":"For the Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Controls with respect to money laundering, which one of the following is true?","options":["Appropriate training should be provided to employees who might be exposed to money laundering activities","The board can delegate their responsibilities for money laundering requirements to the MLRO","The board is required to monitor transactions for money laundering","A firm is required to provide the necessary systems and authority to the FCA appointed MLRO"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Under SYSC, with respect to money laundering, there is a requirement that appropriate training be provided to staff.\nThe board appoints the MLRO, not the FCA."},{"question":"Market making is a specific defence against insider trading if the market maker:","options":["Has the permission of the FCA to trade","Acts in good faith","Does not expect to make a profit","Thought the information was publicly available"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Market makers acting in good faith is a specific defence.\nNot expecting to make a profit or believing the information was public are general defences.\n\nNOTE: the words special and specific are interchangeable."},{"question":"Which of the following is an insider according to the Criminal Justice Act 1993?","options":["A person who pays for inside information","An employee whose work involves inside information","A person who obtains inside information by observation","A person who obtains inside information by dealing in securities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Answer D relates to a market maker in which there is a special defence. Answer C is not a breach of CJA 1993. Answer A is illegal but this does not make the buyer of the information an insider."},{"question":"A director of a company has recently informed a friend about a pending takeover of the company, telling them that since this information is not public, they can make a quick profit by buying the company's shares. The friend declines to trade on this information. Who has committed an offence under insider dealing legislation in the Criminal Justice Act?","options":["The director","The friend","Both","No one"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The friend did not trade on the information. Assuming that she/he did not pass on this information further, she did not commit a crime. The director committed a crime by disclosing insider information and encouraging someone to deal on that information."},{"question":"Under the Joint Money Laundering Steering Group Guidance Notes, which of the following would identify why enhanced due diligence is required for a politically exposed person?","options":["Due to their position, they are more vulnerable to corruption","They have the ability to withhold information","They live overseas","They work for an authorised firm"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The main factor used in identifying a politically exposed person (PEP) is their vulnerability to corruption. According to JMLSG Guidance this includes persons who are: heads of state, heads of government and ministers; members of parliaments; members of supreme courts, or of other high-level judicial bodies whose decisions are not generally subject to further appeal; members of courts of auditors or of the boards of central banks; ambassadors, and high-ranking officers in the armed forces; and members of the administrative, management or supervisory boards of State-owned enterprises.\nThese categories also include direct family members of the above, such as spouse, children and parents. They do not, however, include middle-ranking or more junior officials."},{"question":"Under the Money Laundering Regulations a firm must ensure that they have appropriate internal controls. One of these is the appointment of a money laundering reporting officer. Which of the following is not true of the MLRO?","options":["They fulfil a senior manager function within the firm","They are the internal and external point of contact for matters arising in relation to money laundering","They are responsible for taking reasonable steps to establish and maintain adequate arrangements for awareness and training","They are a member of the National Crime Agency"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The MLRO is an employee of the firm, and although they pass on suspicions to NCA they are not members of this agency."},{"question":"Which of the following are penalties available to the FCA for cases of market abuse?\nIRestitution\nIIUnlimited financial penalty\nIIIWithdrawal of approval\nIVIssue a public statement","options":["II only","I and II","II, III and IV","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"As market abuse is a civil offence, prison sentences are not applicable, but all the regulatory actions mentioned are available to the FCA."},{"question":"Which of the following prosecutes insider dealers?","options":["London Stock Exchange","Department for Business and Trade","Treasury","Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FCA can bring prosecutions for insider dealing. The London Stock Exchange has an obligation to monitor transactions to identify potential cases of insider dealing."},{"question":"Which of the following are in the scope of the market abuse regime:\nIShares traded on AIM\nIICopper futures traded on the LME\nIIIShares on the Official List of the LSE\nIVGas oil futures traded on ICE Futures","options":["III","II, III and IV","I and III","I, II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Investments traded on recognised investment exchanges are covered under market abuse. LME, LSE and ICE are all recognised investment exchanges in the UK."},{"question":"Which of the following penalties are NOT available to the FCA for cases of market abuse?","options":["Restitution order","Unlimited financial penalty","A prison sentence","Prohibition orders"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As market abuse is a civil offence, prison sentences are not applicable."},{"question":"Money laundering covers:","options":["All money","Only tangible assets derived from crime","All benefits of criminal activity","Only money from criminal activities"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Proceeds of Crime Act (POCA) 2002 is widely drafted. It specifies that money laundering relates to criminal property – that is, any benefit (money or otherwise) that has arisen from criminal conduct. Property is only criminal property if the alleged offender knows or suspects it is criminal property."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"When did the UK market abuse regime under FSMA come into force?","back":"2001."},{"front":"When did the EU/UK Market Abuse Regulation (MAR) come into effect?","back":"2016 (onshored into UK law as 'UK MAR' after Brexit)."},{"front":"FSA 2012 Sections 89-91","back":"Three offences: s89 misleading statements, s90 false/misleading impressions, s91 misleading statements re benchmarks - replacing former FSMA s397."},{"front":"Three defences to FSA 2012 ss89-91 charges","back":"Reasonable belief conduct was not misleading; conformity with FCA price stabilisation rules; conformity with FCA control of information rules."},{"front":"Max penalty on indictment for FSA 2012 offences after 1 Nov 2021","back":"Up to 10 years' imprisonment and/or a fine (up to 7 years for offences up to 31 Oct 2021)."},{"front":"Max penalty on summary conviction, FSA 2012 offences","back":"Fine not exceeding the statutory amount and/or up to 12 months' imprisonment."},{"front":"STOR","back":"Suspicious Transaction and Order Report - must be filed with the FCA without delay where there is reasonable suspicion of market manipulation or insider dealing (including attempts)."},{"front":"Four ways insider dealing occurs under UK MAR","back":"Using inside information to deal; cancelling/amending an order using it; recommending another deal; recommending another cancel/amend an order."},{"front":"Three ways to be an 'insider' with information from an inside source","back":"Being an inside source yourself; having access via employment/office/profession; obtaining it (directly or indirectly) from someone who was an inside source or had employment access."},{"front":"Insider list requirement","back":"Issuers must maintain lists of all persons with access to inside information, promptly updated; separate lists required per transaction, plus one for permanent insiders."},{"front":"Legal basis for criminal insider dealing offence in the UK","back":"Part V of the Criminal Justice Act 1993 (CJA)."},{"front":"CJA 1993 Section 52 - three offences","back":"Dealing in the security; encouraging another to deal; disclosing inside information."},{"front":"CJA 1993 Section 56 - inside information elements","back":"Relates to specified securities/markets; is specific or precise; has not been made public; is price-sensitive."},{"front":"Insider Dealing (Securities and Regulated Markets) Order 2023 - effective date","back":"15 June 2023 - updated the list of Regulated Markets under Part V CJA 1993."},{"front":"General defence to insider dealing - reasonable belief","back":"Defendant reasonably believed the information had been so widely disclosed that no one dealing would be prejudiced by not knowing it."},{"front":"Special defence - market maker","back":"Not guilty if acting in good faith in the ordinary course of market-making business."},{"front":"Special defence - market information (example)","back":"A broker executing a client's block order (e.g., 10,000 shares) has a defence, as the pending order is 'market information', provided it was reasonable to act as they did."},{"front":"Four forms of market manipulation","back":"Manipulating transactions; manipulating devices; dissemination; benchmark manipulation."},{"front":"PDMR","back":"Person Discharging Managerial Responsibilities - directors/senior executives with regular access to inside information and managerial decision-making power."},{"front":"PDMR notification deadline","back":"No later than 3 business days after the transaction, to the issuer and the FCA."},{"front":"Closed period length for PDMRs","back":"30 calendar days before announcement of the interim or annual financial report."},{"front":"Investment recommendation - ongoing disclosure lookback period","back":"12 months - changes from prior recommendations on the same instrument within the preceding 12 months must be disclosed."},{"front":"POCA 2002 - five offences","back":"Concealing (s327); arrangements (s328); acquisition/use/possession (s329); failure to disclose (s330); tipping off (s333A)."},{"front":"POCA ss327-329 max penalty","back":"Fine and/or up to 14 years' imprisonment."},{"front":"POCA s330 (failure to disclose) max penalty","back":"Fine and/or up to 5 years' imprisonment."},{"front":"POCA s333A (tipping off) max penalty","back":"Fine and/or up to 2 years' imprisonment."},{"front":"'Spanish bullfighter' issue - resolved by which Act?","back":"SOCPA 2005 - provided a defence where conduct legal abroad was reasonably believed not to be criminal there, subject to Secretary of State override for certain offences."},{"front":"Three stages of money laundering","back":"Placement, layering, integration."},{"front":"CDD - three aspects","back":"Identify the customer/beneficial owner; verify their identity; obtain information on the intended nature of the business relationship."},{"front":"PEP definition","back":"An individual who is, or has within the preceding year been, entrusted with prominent public functions, other than as a middle-ranking or junior official."},{"front":"EDD obligation for former PEPs ceases after","back":"1 year after leaving office (longer if the firm considers it appropriate given risk)."},{"front":"MLRO","back":"Money Laundering Reporting Officer - FCA-approved SMF role, must be UK-based, oversees firm's AML compliance and decides whether to escalate suspicions to the NCA."},{"front":"Record retention - evidence of identity","back":"5 years from the end of the business relationship."},{"front":"Record retention - transaction records cap","back":"Maximum of 10 years, even within an ongoing relationship."},{"front":"Bribery Act 2010 - four offences","back":"s1 bribing; s2 being bribed; s6 bribing a foreign public official; s7 corporate failure to prevent bribery."},{"front":"Bribery Act max penalties","back":"10 years' imprisonment (individual offences); unlimited fine (s7 corporate offence)."},{"front":"MoJ six principles for 'adequate procedures' defence (Bribery Act s7)","back":"Proportionality; top-level commitment; risk assessment; due diligence; communication; monitoring and review."},{"front":"ECCTA","back":"Economic Crime and Corporate Transparency Act - reforms Companies House, limited partnerships, cryptoasset seizure powers, and creates the failure to prevent fraud offence."},{"front":"Failure to prevent fraud offence - effective date","back":"1 September 2025."},{"front":"'Large organisation' thresholds under ECCTA (need 2 of 3)","back":"Turnover >£36m; balance sheet total >£18m; >250 employees."},{"front":"UK DTR notifiable shareholding threshold","back":"3% (and every whole percentage point above), retained from before the EU Transparency Directive's 5%/10%/15% scale."},{"front":"DTR notification timeline","back":"Investor notifies company within 2 business days; company informs the market (e.g., via RNS) by end of the next business day - not reported to the FCA."},{"front":"DTR - connected party company control threshold","back":"Control assumed at one third or more of voting rights."},{"front":"Companies Act 2006 Section 793","back":"Allows a public company to demand written disclosure of beneficial share ownership, typically from nominee companies; non-response can lead to a court order freezing the shares."},{"front":"US CTA - reporting trigger thresholds","back":"Ownership exceeding or dropping below 25%; substantial ownership change; change of beneficial owner's contact details - reported within 1 year."}]},{"id":"reg-ch4","number":4,"title":"Complaints and Redress","examWeight":"9 of 75 questions (~12%)","summaryHtml":"\n<h4>Chapter 4: Complaints and Redress — Night-Before Summary</h4>\n<p><strong>1. Consumer Rights (CRA 2015).</strong> The Consumer Rights Act 2015 consolidates consumer protection law in three parts: (i) rights/remedies for goods, digital content and services; (ii) unfair contract terms (the \"grey list\" is indicative, non-exhaustive); (iii) enforcement/miscellaneous. The FCA is a \"qualifying body\" under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999 and can seek undertakings or injunctions against unfair terms.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cancellation periods:</strong> life & pensions = 30 calendar days; cash ISAs, other non-life/pensions (advised or distance) = 14 calendar days.</li>\n<li>Firms must disclose cancellation rights in a durable medium before/at point of contract.</li>\n<li>Record retention: indefinite (pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs); 5 years (life/pension products); 3 years (other).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>2. Customer Complaints (DISP).</strong> Eligible complainants: consumers, micro-enterprises (&lt;10 staff, turnover/balance sheet ≤ €2m), charities (income &lt; £6.5m), trusts (net assets &lt; £5m), certain SMEs, guarantors. Firms must acknowledge, investigate, and send a final response or holding response <strong>within 8 weeks</strong> (15 business days, extendable to 35, for PSD/EMD complaints). Complainants have <strong>6 months</strong> from final response to refer to the FOS. Records: 5 years (MiFID/UCITS business), 3 years (other). Firms receiving 500+ complaints per 6-month period file enhanced returns.</p>\n<p><strong>3. FOS.</strong> Free to complainants; funded by firm levies and case fees. Compulsory jurisdiction (CJ) is automatic for authorised firms; Voluntary jurisdiction (VJ) is opt-in. <strong>Award limits (act/omission on/after 1 Apr 2025, complaint referred on/after 1 Apr 2025): £445,000</strong> (was £430,000 for acts from 1 Apr 2024); for acts before 1 April 2019, the limit is £200,000 (was £195,000). Limits are CPI-adjusted each 1 April. Super-complaints: FCA must respond within <strong>90 days</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>4. FSCS.</strong> Pays compensation when an authorised firm defaults. Key limits: <strong>deposits £110,000</strong> per person per firm (£220,000 joint; up from £85,000 from 1 Dec 2025); temporary high balances £1.4m for 6 months; <strong>investments £85,000</strong>; insurance 100% (compulsory/long-term/PI/death-incapacity) or 90% (other); pension provider failure = 100% uncapped; SIPP operator failure/bad pension advice = £85,000.</p>\n<p><strong>5. Data Protection.</strong> DPA 2018 replaced DPA 1998, implementing UK GDPR. 6 DPA principles: lawful/fair; specified purpose; adequate/relevant/not excessive; accurate; not kept longer than necessary; secure. Breach notification to ICO within <strong>72 hours</strong> of awareness where feasible; individuals informed \"without undue delay\" if high risk. ICO fines: higher maximum £17.5m or 4% global turnover; standard maximum £8.7m or 2%.</p>\n<p><strong>6. Whistleblowing.</strong> Based on PIDA 1998. Firms must appoint a <strong>whistleblowing champion</strong> (usually a NED, or senior manager/director for insurers), report to the board at least annually, and tell UK employees about FCA/PRA whistleblowing services. Certain firms (deposit-takers, Solvency II insurers) must appoint a champion under SM&CR.</p>\n","sections":[{"heading":"4.1 Consumer Rights","html":"\n<h4>4.1.1 Consumer Rights Act 2015 (CRA)</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Consumer Rights Act 2015 (CRA)</strong> is a UK law that consolidates consumer protection law for contracts involving goods, digital content and services. It gives consumers statutory rights and remedies, and reforms/consolidates the law on unfair terms in consumer contracts (Part 2). It aims to enhance consumer confidence and simplify enforcement.</p>\n<p>The Act has three parts:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Consumers' rights and remedies for supply of goods, digital content and services.</li>\n<li>Unfair terms in consumer contracts and notices.</li>\n<li>Miscellaneous/general provisions, including investigatory powers for consumer law enforcers and secondary ticketing.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Under the CRA, the <strong>FCA has statutory power to challenge unfair terms</strong> in financial services consumer contracts. The FCA is a <strong>qualifying body</strong> under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999, meaning it can:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>seek an undertaking from a firm to amend/remove an unfair term from future contracts, and</li>\n<li>apply to the court for an injunction to prevent a firm using/enforcing the term against existing customers.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The unfair terms provisions protect consumers against unfair terms in contracts and notices, <strong>including individually negotiated terms</strong>. A term is unfair if, <strong>contrary to the requirement of good faith</strong>, it causes a <strong>significant imbalance</strong> in the parties' rights/obligations to the detriment of the consumer.</p>\n<p>The CRA contains an indicative, <strong>non-exhaustive \"grey list\"</strong> of terms that may be regarded as unfair. A term not on the grey list is not necessarily fair. Fairness is assessed by:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the nature of the subject matter of the contract, and</li>\n<li>all circumstances at the time the term was agreed and all other terms of the contract (or related contracts), including implied terms.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The CRA requires terms to be <strong>transparent</strong>. A term failing the transparency requirement is not automatically unfair, but this must be assessed against all relevant factors, with the consequence that a term found unfair is not binding on the consumer.</p>\n\n<h4>4.1.2 Cancellation and Withdrawal Rights; Reflection Periods</h4>\n<p>Cancellation rights must be provided to retail customers transacting investment business. The rules apply to:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>firms providing retail financial products based on designated investments or deposits, and</li>\n<li>firms entering into distance contracts with consumers relating to deposits or designated investments.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Purpose: to give clients a chance to reconsider and cancel within a defined period. If exercised, the contract is terminated.</p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Product</th><th>Cancellation period</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Life and pension products</td><td><strong>30 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Cash deposit ISAs</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life and pensions products (advised, non-distance)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n<tr><td>Non-life and pensions products (distance contracts)</td><td><strong>14 calendar days</strong></td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Example: an advised purchase of an authorised unit trust (AUT) or OEIC has a <strong>14-day</strong> cancellation right.</p>\n<p>The cancellation period starts from the day the contract concludes, or the day the client receives contractual information, if later.</p>\n<p>Before (or, if not possible, immediately after) the consumer is bound, firms must tell consumers, in a durable medium, of:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>the existence of the cancellation/withdrawal right</li>\n<li>its duration</li>\n<li>the conditions for exercising it (including any amount payable)</li>\n<li>what happens if not exercised, and</li>\n<li>practical details of how to exercise it (including the address for notification).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This disclosure is not required if the consumer already receives similar information under COBS distance marketing/product information rules. Notification of cancellation is deemed effective if dispatched before the deadline expires; no reason needs to be given.</p>\n<p><strong>Record-keeping retention:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Indefinitely</strong> — pension transfers, pension opt-outs, FSAVCs.</li>\n<li><strong>At least 5 years</strong> — life policy, pension contract, personal pension scheme, or stakeholder pension scheme.</li>\n<li><strong>At least 3 years</strong> — any other case.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>On cancellation of an investment (e.g. a unit trust/UCITS fund), the client may not get back their full original investment — they receive the sale proceeds of their holding, which reflects market price movement.</p>\n<p>Source: FCA Website (Conduct of Business Sourcebook).</p>\n"},{"heading":"4.2 Customer Complaints","html":"\n<h4>4.2.1 Eligible Complainants</h4>\n<p>Access to the FOS requires (a) the complaint falls within FOS jurisdiction, and (b) the complainant is an <strong>eligible complainant</strong>:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Consumers</strong> — any person using or contemplating using services of an authorised firm/appointed representative.</li>\n<li><strong>Micro-enterprises</strong> — fewer than <strong>10 employees</strong> and turnover/balance sheet ≤ <strong>€2 million</strong> at time complaint raised.</li>\n<li><strong>Charities</strong> with annual income &lt; <strong>£6.5 million</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Trustees of a trust</strong> with net asset value &lt; <strong>£5 million</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Certain SMEs</strong> — annual turnover &lt; £6.5 million and balance sheet &lt; £5 million, or fewer than 50 employees.</li>\n<li>A person acting for purposes outside their business/profession, or a borrower under a buy-to-let credit agreement (may include ECP/professional clients in some cases).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The complaint must arise from a business relationship with the firm (customer, beneficial interest in a pension scheme, policyholder). Personal guarantors of business loans also have FOS rights. Other authorised firms are generally NOT eligible complainants, unless the complaint concerns an activity for which they hold no permission themselves. ECPs/professional clients (other than pension scheme trustees) are generally not eligible, unless the complaint concerns activity outside their ECP/professional classification.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2 Complaints Procedures (DISP)</h4>\n<p>Firms must have <strong>appropriate written procedures</strong> for handling expressions of dissatisfaction from eligible complainants (firms may extend these to other complainants voluntarily). Procedures apply whether the complaint is oral/written and justified/unjustified, as long as it relates to provision (or failure to provide) a financial service. Procedures must cover: receiving, acknowledging, responding to, and investigating complaints, and notifying complainants of FOS rights.</p>\n<p>The Complaint Handling Rules are in <strong>DISP 1 (\"Treating Complainants Fairly\")</strong> of the FCA Handbook.</p>\n<p><strong>Consumer Awareness Rules (DISP 1.2):</strong> firms must publish a summary of their internal complaints process and FOS referral rights; refer complainants in writing to these summary details at/immediately after point of sale; provide the summary in writing on request and when acknowledging a complaint.</p>\n<p><strong>Complaint Handling Rules (DISP 1.3):</strong> require effective, transparent procedures for reasonable/prompt handling, free of charge, allowing complaints via any reasonable means. Firms must identify/remedy recurring or systemic issues via:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>identifying root causes of complaints (including by product/service)</li>\n<li>analysing complaints resolved within one business day of receipt</li>\n<li>considering whether root causes affect other processes/products</li>\n<li>correcting root causes and prioritising action where reasonable</li>\n<li>regular reports to senior management on recurring/systemic problems</li>\n<li>keeping records of the analysis and senior management's response.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Complaints Resolution Rules (DISP 1.4):</strong> firms must investigate competently, diligently and impartially, obtaining further information as needed, and assess fairly, consistently and promptly: the subject matter; whether to uphold; appropriate remedial action/redress; and whether another firm may be jointly/solely responsible. Relevant factors: all evidence/circumstances; similarities with other complaints; FCA/regulator/FOS guidance; analysis of FOS decisions. The firm must explain its decision fairly, clearly, and not misleadingly, and comply promptly with any accepted redress offer.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.1 Stages and Timings</h4>\n<p>On receipt of a complaint, a firm must:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>send a <strong>prompt written acknowledgement</strong></li>\n<li>keep the complainant regularly informed of progress, and</li>\n<li>within <strong>8 weeks</strong>, send a written response — either a <strong>final response</strong> or a <strong>holding response</strong> (different timescales apply to PSD/EMD complaints — see 4.2.2.4).</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.2 Record Keeping and Reporting</h4>\n<p>Records must capture: the complainant's name; the substance of the complaint; and the resolution measures taken.</p>\n<p><strong>Retention:</strong> minimum <strong>5 years</strong> for MiFID business/UCITS collective portfolio management complaints; <strong>3 years</strong> for all other complaints — from date of receipt.</p>\n<p>Exception (annual report only, instead of standard reporting) where a firm's permissions relate to: credit-related activities/operating an electronic lending system with revenue &lt; <strong>£5 million</strong>; or only regulated claims management activities or regulated funeral plan activities.</p>\n<p>Firms must report to the FCA: total complaints received; total closed; total upheld/outstanding at start of period; total redress paid.</p>\n<p>Firms receiving <strong>500 or more complaints</strong> in a 6-month reporting period must additionally report: cause of complaints; number closed/upheld within different time periods; redress paid (upheld vs not upheld); and contextual information.</p>\n<p>A complaint is \"closed\" when the firm sends a final response, or the complainant confirms in writing acceptance of an earlier response. Under the two-stage approach, a complaint is closed if the complainant does not respond to the written response within <strong>8 weeks</strong>. The FCA publishes quarterly statistical complaints data.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.3 Final Responses to Complainants</h4>\n<p>A final response must do one of: accept the complaint and offer redress/remedial action; offer redress/remedial action without accepting; or reject the complaint with reasons. It must also inform the complainant that if still dissatisfied, they may refer to the FOS within <strong>6 months</strong> of the final response, and include the FOS leaflet.</p>\n<p>If unable to send a final response within 8 weeks, the firm must send a written response explaining why and indicating when the final response will follow, inform the complainant they may now refer to the FOS, and include the FOS leaflet.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.4 PSD/EMD Complaints</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Payment Services Regulations 2017</strong> (implementing the PSD, via HM Treasury; FCA is the nominated regulator) introduced different timescales for PSD/EMD complaints, implemented into <strong>DISP 1.6.2A</strong>.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Final response due by end of <strong>15 business days</strong> after receipt.</li>\n<li>In exceptional circumstances: send a holding response by end of the 15th business day (explaining the delay and a deadline), then send the final response by end of the <strong>35th business day</strong> after receipt.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>EMD complaint</strong> definition (FCA Handbook): any oral/written expression of dissatisfaction from/on behalf of an e-money holder about provision/failure to provide a financial service or redress determination, alleging financial loss/material distress/inconvenience, concerning rights/obligations under Part 5 of the Electronic Money Regulations.</p>\n<p><strong>PSD complaint</strong> definition: similarly, concerning rights/obligations under Parts 6 and 7 of the Payment Service Regulations.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.2.5 Improving Complaints Handling</h4>\n<ul>\n<li>\"Next business day\" rule: firms may handle complaints informally (no acknowledgement letter needed) if resolved within <strong>3 business days</strong> of receipt.</li>\n<li>All complaints must be reported to the regulator, including those resolved within 3 business days.</li>\n<li>A \"new complaints\" return sent <strong>twice yearly</strong>, detailing complaints received.</li>\n<li>A <strong>summary resolution communication</strong> raising FOS awareness, sent following resolution by the third business day.</li>\n<li>Consumer call costs capped at the maximum basic rate.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4.2.3 The Financial Ombudsman Service (FOS)</h4>\n<p>Under FSMA, the FCA has power to make complaint-handling rules; the independent dispute resolution body is the <strong>FOS</strong> (operated by Financial Ombudsman Service Ltd). It provides a free, impartial, efficient alternative to the courts, aiming for fair and reasonable outcomes. The FOS does not replace the courts — complainants may still take civil action, unless they have accepted the FOS's final decision. It is free to complainants; firms pay an annual <strong>levy</strong> plus <strong>case fees</strong> per accepted/investigated case.</p>\n<p><strong>Jurisdictions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Compulsory Jurisdiction (CJ):</strong> complaints from eligible complainants against FCA-authorised firms regarding regulated (and ancillary) activities unresolved within the firm's own timescales. Authorised firms are <strong>automatically</strong> subject to CJ.</li>\n<li><strong>Voluntary Jurisdiction (VJ):</strong> covers complaints outside CJ; firms opt in via contractual arrangement, becoming \"VJ participants\".</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>FOS Awards — compensation limits (fair compensation, excludes interest on award/costs and interest on costs):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Date complaint referred to FOS</th><th>Limit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Act/omission before 1 April 2019 (any referral date)</td><td>£195,000 (referrals before rise) / £200,000 (on/after 1 Apr 2025)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Act/omission on or after 1 April 2019, referral on/after 1 April 2024</td><td>£430,000</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Act/omission on or after 1 April 2019, referral on/after 1 April 2025</td><td>£445,000</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Key principle: the applicable limit depends on <strong>both</strong> the date of the act/omission complained about <strong>and</strong> the date the complaint is referred to the FOS. Limits are adjusted automatically each <strong>1 April</strong> in line with <strong>CPI</strong> for the preceding January. Once the FOS adjudicates, it asks the complainant to accept or reject; if accepted, the decision is <strong>final and binding on both parties</strong>.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.4 Super-Complaints and Mass-Detriment References</h4>\n<p><strong>Super-complaints:</strong> certain designated consumer bodies (designated by HM Treasury via primary legislation) may raise competition/consumer issues with the FCA where a UK market feature(s) appears to be significantly harming consumer interests. The <strong>FCA must respond within 90 days</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Mass-detriment</strong> = large-scale damage, harm or loss to consumers. A <strong>mass-detriment reference</strong> can be made by the FOS or a regulated firm where either: (1) there appear to be regular regulatory failures causing/risking consumer loss remediable by legal proceedings; or (2) a firm has regularly acted in a way that, if complained about, would likely be upheld by the FOS with an award made.</p>\n\n<h4>4.2.5 Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)</h4>\n<p><strong>Purpose:</strong> pays compensation to eligible claimants when an authorised firm defaults (insurance cover exhausted, firm insolvent/unable to pay claims). Funded by a levy on all authorised firms.</p>\n<p><strong>Excluded from claiming (not eligible claimants):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>other authorised firms; overseas financial institutions</li>\n<li>CISs, their trustees and operators</li>\n<li>some pension/retirement funds (subject to exceptions below)</li>\n<li>supranational institutions, governments, central administrative authorities</li>\n<li>provincial/regional/local/municipal authorities</li>\n<li>directors/managers of the defaulting firm and relatives (unless small mutual association with unsalaried directors, or a credit union)</li>\n<li>group companies of the defaulting firm (unless also a trustee of certain pension schemes)</li>\n<li>persons holding ≥5% of the defaulting firm's capital (or group firm's capital)</li>\n<li>auditors of the defaulting firm</li>\n<li>anyone who contributed to the firm's default</li>\n<li>large companies, large mutual associations, large partnerships</li>\n<li>persons convicted of money laundering in connection with the claim</li>\n<li>claims under the Third Parties (Rights against Insurers) Act 1930.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Exceptions that ARE eligible</strong> (despite being authorised firms), if the claim arises from an activity for which they lack Part 4A permission: sole trader firms; credit unions; trustees/operators of stakeholder/personal pension schemes; small businesses (turnover &lt; <strong>£1 million</strong>). Trustees of small self-administered/occupational pension schemes (where employer is not large) are also eligible.</p>\n<p><strong>Protected claims relate to:</strong> deposits; contracts of insurance; protected investment business; claims against the manager/trustee of an AUT or the ACD/depositary of an ICVC (claim made by unit-holder). Claims against a UK-authorised unregulated CIS operator are eligible (operating an unregulated CIS is designated investment business).</p>\n<p><strong>Compensation limits (per eligible person, per authorised firm, per product category):</strong></p>\n<table>\n<tr><th>Product</th><th>Limit</th></tr>\n<tr><td>Deposits (banks/building societies/credit unions, failure after 1 Dec 2025)</td><td><strong>£110,000</strong> per person, per licence (£220,000 for joint accounts). Increased from £85,000 (1 Dec 2025).</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Temporary high balances (e.g. property sale, inheritance)</td><td><strong>£1.4 million</strong> for 6 months (increased from £1m, 1 Dec 2025)</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance — compulsory, long-term, professional indemnity, death/incapacity due to sickness/injury (failure after 8 Oct 2020)</td><td><strong>100%</strong> protected, no upper limit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance — all other kinds</td><td><strong>90%</strong> protected</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance brokers/financial advisers — compulsory general insurance (no time limit)</td><td><strong>100%</strong> protected</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Insurance brokers/financial advisers — other general insurance</td><td><strong>90%</strong> protected</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Investments (failure after 1 Apr 2019)</td><td><strong>£85,000</strong> per person, per firm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pensions — pension provider fails</td><td><strong>100%</strong> of claim, no upper limit</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pensions — SIPP operator fails</td><td><strong>£85,000</strong> per person, per firm</td></tr>\n<tr><td>Pensions — bad pension advice</td><td><strong>£85,000</strong> per person, per firm</td></tr>\n</table>\n<p>Compensation covers financial loss only — for investment claims, it aims to restore the individual to the position they would have been in had they not invested.</p>\n"},{"heading":"4.3 Data Protection","html":"\n<h4>4.3.1 The Six Principles of the Data Protection Act 2018</h4>\n<p>The original <strong>Data Protection Act 1998</strong> governed how personal data must be handled to protect individuals' rights (personal data = data relating to identifiable living individuals). Following the EU <strong>General Data Protection Regulation (GDPR)</strong> in 2018, the DPA 1998 was repealed and replaced by the <strong>Data Protection Act 2018 (DPA 2018)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The DPA 2018 has a wider remit than the GDPR:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>covers processing outside EU law scope (e.g. immigration), applying adjusted GDPR standards;</li>\n<li>transposes the EU Law Enforcement Directive 2016/680 into domestic law — <strong>Part 3</strong> covers processing for criminal \"law enforcement purposes\";</li>\n<li>covers national security (outside EU law scope), based on Council of Europe Data Protection Convention 108, applying to intelligence services;</li>\n<li>sets out the <strong>Information Commissioner's Office (ICO)</strong>'s duties, functions, powers, and enforcement provisions.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>The 6 DPA 2018 principles</strong> — personal data must be:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>processed lawfully and fairly</li>\n<li>processed for purposes that are specified, explicit and legitimate</li>\n<li>adequate, relevant and not excessive</li>\n<li>accurate and kept up to date</li>\n<li>kept for no longer than necessary</li>\n<li>processed in a secure manner.</li>\n</ol>\n\n<h4>4.3.1a Purpose and the ICO</h4>\n<p>ICO mission: \"to uphold information rights in the public interest, promoting openness by public bodies and data privacy for individuals.\" This covers safeguarding/empowering individuals, promoting transparency, ensuring compliance with data protection/e-privacy law. <strong>Data controllers</strong> (entities that collect/process personal data) must register with the ICO.</p>\n\n<h4>4.3.2 UK GDPR and Data Subject Rights</h4>\n<p>Post-Brexit, GDPR requirements were retained in domestic law as the <strong>UK GDPR</strong>; high-level requirements are unchanged.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scope:</strong> UK GDPR applies to both controllers AND processors (DPA applies to controllers only). It also covers overseas controllers/processors whose activities relate to offering goods/services to, or monitoring the behaviour of, UK data subjects. Data may be exported outside the UK subject to UK GDPR conditions.</li>\n<li><strong>Accountability:</strong> controllers must be able to demonstrate compliance.</li>\n<li><strong>DPOs:</strong> mandatory for certain organisations (e.g. public authorities, or bodies that regularly/systematically monitor data subjects on a large scale) to appoint a <strong>Data Protection Officer</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Data protection principles (UK GDPR):</strong> lawfulness; fairness and transparency; purpose limitation; data minimisation; accuracy; storage limitation; accountability.</li>\n<li><strong>Consent:</strong> must be freely given, specific, informed, and an unambiguous indication of wishes (statement or clear affirmative action); purpose-limited; distinguishable in a written declaration and intelligible/plain language; must be as easy to withdraw as to give. Criminal convictions/offences are NOT considered \"sensitive data\" under UK GDPR.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Key rights of individuals:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Right to be Informed</strong> — about collection/use, purposes, categories of data, recipients, retention periods.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Rectification</strong> — correct inaccurate/incomplete data.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Erasure</strong> (\"right to be forgotten\") — deletion of personal data in certain circumstances (builds on the CJEU-established principle).</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Restrict Processing</strong> — e.g. while accuracy is verified, or where objecting to processing.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Data Portability</strong> — obtain/reuse personal data in a commonly used, machine-readable format to transmit to another controller.</li>\n<li><strong>Right to Object</strong> — no general right to object, but can object to processing based on legitimate interests/public tasks.</li>\n<li><strong>Rights related to Automated Decision-Making</strong> — right to understand logic and contest decisions made solely by automated means.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Data Security:</strong> controllers/processors must implement appropriate technical/organisational measures proportionate to risk (e.g. pseudonymisation, encryption, confidentiality of systems, regular testing).</p>\n<p><strong>Fines under UK GDPR:</strong> up to <strong>4% of annual worldwide turnover or £17.5 million</strong> (whichever greater) for breaches of certain provisions; up to <strong>2% or £8.7 million</strong> (whichever greater) for other specified breaches.</p>\n\n<h4>4.3.3 Breach Notification and Reporting</h4>\n<p>The ICO can investigate, take enforcement action and impose penalties for data breaches. <strong>Part 3 of the DPA 2018</strong> requires organisations to report certain personal data breaches to the ICO <strong>within 72 hours</strong> of becoming aware, where feasible. If likely to result in high risk to individuals' rights/freedoms, firms must also inform affected individuals <strong>without undue delay</strong>.</p>\n<p><strong>Personal data breach</strong> = a breach of security leading to accidental/unlawful destruction, loss, alteration, unauthorised disclosure of, or access to, personal data (more than just losing data).</p>\n<p><strong>Notify the ICO only if</strong> the breach is likely to result in a risk to individuals' rights and freedoms (e.g. discrimination, reputational damage, financial loss, loss of confidentiality/other economic or social disadvantage).</p>\n<p><strong>Report to ICO must include:</strong> nature of the breach (categories/approx. numbers of individuals and records concerned); DPO/contact point details; likely consequences; measures taken/proposed to address and mitigate.</p>\n<p><strong>Informing individuals:</strong> required without undue delay if <strong>high risk</strong> (a higher threshold than for notifying the ICO). Not required if: appropriate technical/organisational measures were applied (e.g. encryption); subsequent measures mean high risk is no longer likely; or it would involve disproportionate effort (in which case a public communication or similarly effective alternative is required). Information may be restricted where necessary/proportionate to avoid obstructing legal inquiries, prejudicing criminal investigations/prosecutions, or to protect public/national security or others' rights and freedoms.</p>\n<p><strong>Individual notification must include:</strong> nature of breach; DPO/contact details; likely consequences; measures taken/proposed.</p>\n<p><strong>ICO factors favouring a financial penalty:</strong> nature of personal data; duration/extent of contravention; number of individuals affected; importance/value/extent of breach; public importance; whether deliberate/premeditated; whether the controller ignored relevant advice; inadequate procedures/policies/practices.</p>\n<p><strong>Factors against a penalty:</strong> controller already complied with another regulator's requirements; contravention caused by circumstances outside the controller's direct control.</p>\n<p><strong>Two tiers of ICO penalty:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Higher maximum:</strong> £17.5 million or 4% of total annual worldwide turnover (preceding financial year), whichever is higher — applies to breaches of data protection principles, individual rights, or international data transfers.</li>\n<li><strong>Standard maximum:</strong> £8.7 million or 2% of total annual worldwide turnover, whichever is higher — applies to infringements of other provisions (e.g. administrative requirements).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Breaches can also lead to <strong>criminal prosecution</strong>, potentially with unlimited fines.</p>\n"},{"heading":"4.4 Whistleblowing","html":"\n<h4>4.4.1 Legal and Regulatory Basis for Whistleblowing</h4>\n<p>The <strong>Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998</strong> protects individuals from retaliation when they inform regulatory authorities of workplace concerns (\"whistleblowing\"). In 2013, the <strong>Parliamentary Commission on Banking Standards (PCBS)</strong> recommended banks put in place internal mechanisms for raising concerns and appoint a senior person responsible for their effectiveness.</p>\n<p><strong>Key whistleblowing rules require firms to:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>appoint an individual of sufficient seniority as their <strong>whistleblowing champion</strong> (FCA expects most firms to appoint a <strong>non-executive director (NED)</strong>, or a senior manager if there is no NED; insurers should apportion the role to a director or senior manager)</li>\n<li>put in place internal whistleblowing arrangements able to handle all types of disclosure from all types of individuals</li>\n<li>include text in settlement agreements explaining workers' legal right to blow the whistle</li>\n<li>tell UK-based employees about the FCA and PRA whistleblowing services</li>\n<li>present a report on whistleblowing to the board at least <strong>annually</strong></li>\n<li>inform the FCA if the firm loses an employment tribunal brought by a whistleblower</li>\n<li>require appointed representatives/tied agents to tell their UK-based employees about the FCA whistleblowing service.</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h4>4.4.2 Key Regulatory Requirements (FCA/PRA)</h4>\n<p>FCA/PRA whistleblowing rules require firms to establish, implement and maintain appropriate, effective arrangements to:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>handle disclosures effectively, including where confidentiality/anonymity is requested, and allow disclosure via a range of communication methods</li>\n<li>ensure effective assessment and escalation of reportable concerns, including to the FCA/PRA where appropriate</li>\n<li>include reasonable measures to prevent victimisation of whistleblowers by anyone under the firm's control</li>\n<li>provide feedback to whistleblowers where feasible/appropriate</li>\n<li>maintain: appropriate records of reportable concerns and their treatment/outcome; and up-to-date written procedures accessible to UK-based employees</li>\n<li>prepare: an annual report to the governing body/board on the operation/effectiveness of whistleblowing systems and controls (maintaining confidentiality of whistleblowers); and prompt reports to the FCA/PRA about each employment tribunal case lost where the claim was based on detriment (s.47B Employment Rights Act 1996) or unfair dismissal (s.103A Employment Rights Act 1996) for whistleblowing</li>\n<li>provide appropriate training for: UK-based employees; managers of UK-based employees (wherever based); and employees operating the firm's internal arrangements.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Firms' written procedures may note other appropriate routes exist (e.g. grievance/complaints procedures), but internal whistleblowing arrangements (SYSC 18.3.1R(2)) can be used after alternatives are exhausted. Firms may act against those making false/malicious disclosures.</p>\n<p><strong>Training for all UK-based employees should include:</strong> a statement the firm takes reportable concerns seriously; how to communicate concerns; examples of triggering events; examples of firm actions post-disclosure (incl. confidentiality protection); sources of external support (e.g. whistleblowing charities).</p>\n<p><strong>Training for managers (wherever based) should ensure they know:</strong> how to recognise a disclosure of a reportable concern; how to protect whistleblowers/confidentiality; how to give feedback where appropriate; steps to ensure fair treatment of anyone accused of wrongdoing; sources of internal/external advice.</p>\n\n<h4>4.4.3 Appointed Representatives and Tied Agents</h4>\n<p>Firms are encouraged to invite ARs/tied agents to adopt internal whistleblowing procedures. Firms must ensure their ARs/tied agents inform their UK-based employees (as workers) that they can make protected disclosures to the FCA, or the PRA if PRA-regulated.</p>\n\n<h4>4.4.4 Link to Fitness and Propriety</h4>\n<p>Evidence that a firm acted to the detriment of a whistleblower is regarded as serious by the regulator. It could call into question the firm's continuing satisfaction of <strong>Threshold Condition 5 (Suitability)</strong>, or an approved person's/SMR or Certification Regime employee's fitness and propriety status.</p>\n\n<h4>4.4.5 Whistleblowers' Champion</h4>\n<p>Under the <strong>SM&CR</strong>, certain firms (banks with deposit-taking permission, and insurance companies subject to Solvency II) must appoint a <strong>whistleblowers' champion</strong>. This person is responsible for ensuring/overseeing the integrity, independence and effectiveness of the firm's whistleblowing policies and procedures, including protecting whistleblowers from victimisation. The champion must have sufficient authority and independence, including access to appropriate resources (e.g. independent legal advice, training) and information. They need not have a day-to-day operational role and can be based anywhere, provided they can perform the function effectively and meet Senior Managers Regime criteria.</p>\n"}],"mcqs":[{"question":"Under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 (CRA), the FCA is designated as a 'qualifying body' under which piece of legislation, giving it power to challenge unfair contract terms?","options":["The Unfair Contract Terms Act 1977","The Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999","The Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","The Consumer Credit Act 1974"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The CRA confers on the FCA the status of a 'qualifying body' under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999, allowing it to seek undertakings or injunctions against unfair terms. The other options are separate pieces of legislation not referenced for this specific power."},{"question":"A retail client receives a cancellation notice following an advised purchase of an authorised unit trust (AUT). Within how many days must they exercise their right to cancel?","options":["7 calendar days","14 calendar days","21 calendar days","30 calendar days"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Non-life and pensions products (including advised AUT/OEIC purchases) carry a 14 calendar day cancellation period. 30 calendar days applies to life and pension products, not unit trusts."},{"question":"For how long must a firm retain records relating to a pension transfer where the client exercised cancellation rights?","options":["3 years","5 years","10 years","Indefinitely"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Records relating to pension transfers, pension opt-outs, or FSAVCs must be kept indefinitely. Life policies/pension contracts require 5 years, and other cases require 3 years."},{"question":"Which of the following would NOT normally be classified as an eligible complainant able to refer a complaint to the FOS?","options":["A consumer using the services of an authorised firm","A micro-enterprise with 8 employees and turnover of €1.5 million","A charity with an annual income of £8 million","A trustee of a trust with net assets of £4 million"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Charities are eligible complainants only if annual income is less than £6.5 million; a charity with £8 million income exceeds this threshold. The micro-enterprise (fewer than 10 employees, turnover under €2 million) and the trust (net assets under £5 million) both meet the eligibility thresholds."},{"question":"A firm receives a written complaint from an eligible complainant. Under DISP, within how many weeks must the firm send a final response or a holding response?","options":["4 weeks","6 weeks","8 weeks","12 weeks"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"DISP 1.4/1.6 require firms to send a final response or holding response within 8 weeks of receiving a complaint. This is the standard timescale; PSD/EMD complaints have a shorter 15-business-day timescale."},{"question":"A firm receives a complaint that falls within the definition of a PSD complaint under DISP 1.6.2A. Assuming exceptional circumstances apply and a holding response is sent, by when must the firm send its final response at the latest?","options":["By the end of the 15th business day","By the end of the 20th business day","By the end of the 35th business day","By the end of the 8th week"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Where exceptional circumstances prevent a final response within 15 business days, the firm sends a holding response by day 15 and must then send the final response by the end of the 35th business day following receipt of the complaint."},{"question":"Following a firm's final response to a complaint, within what period must the complainant refer the matter to the FOS in order to preserve their right to do so?","options":["3 months","6 months","9 months","12 months"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The final response must inform the complainant that they have 6 months from the date of the final response to refer the complaint to the FOS."},{"question":"A firm must retain records of a complaint relating to MiFID business for a minimum of how long from the date the complaint was received?","options":["1 year","3 years","5 years","7 years"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Complaints relating to MiFID business (or UCITS collective portfolio management) must be retained for at least 5 years; all other complaints require 3 years."},{"question":"Under DISP, what additional reporting obligation applies to a firm that receives 500 or more complaints during a six-month reporting period?","options":["It must appoint a whistleblowing champion","It must provide enhanced information including cause of complaints, timescales for closure/upholding, redress paid, and context","It must automatically refer all complaints to the FOS","It becomes subject to the Voluntary Jurisdiction of the FOS"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Firms receiving 500+ complaints in a six-month reporting period must provide enhanced data to the FCA covering cause, closure/upholding timescales, redress paid, and contextual information — beyond the basic totals all firms must report."},{"question":"Which of the following best describes the difference between the FOS's compulsory jurisdiction (CJ) and voluntary jurisdiction (VJ)?","options":["CJ applies only to complaints over £100,000; VJ applies to smaller complaints","Authorised firms are automatically subject to CJ for regulated activities; VJ covers complaints outside CJ and requires a firm to opt in contractually","CJ is funded by complainants; VJ is free","VJ decisions are binding while CJ decisions are not"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Authorised firms are automatically subject to the FOS's compulsory jurisdiction for regulated (and ancillary) activities. The voluntary jurisdiction covers complaints falling outside CJ, and firms must opt in by entering a contractual arrangement with the FOS, becoming 'VJ participants'."},{"question":"A complaint is referred to the FOS on 15 May 2025, concerning an act or omission that occurred on 10 June 2019. What is the applicable FOS maximum award limit?","options":["£195,000","£200,000","£430,000","£445,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The act/omission occurred on or after 1 April 2019 and the complaint was referred on or after 1 April 2025, so the applicable limit is £445,000. The lower limits (£195,000/£200,000) apply only where the act/omission occurred before 1 April 2019."},{"question":"Within how many days must the FCA respond to a super-complaint raised by a designated consumer body?","options":["30 days","60 days","90 days","120 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA must respond to a super-complaint within 90 days of it being raised. HM Treasury, through primary legislation, determines which consumer bodies may make super-complaints."},{"question":"Which of the following persons would generally be EXCLUDED from claiming compensation from the FSCS?","options":["A sole trader firm claiming in respect of an activity for which it has no Part 4A permission","A director of the defaulting firm claiming in a personal capacity","A small business with turnover under £1 million","A trustee of a small self-administered pension scheme where the employer is not a large company"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Directors and managers of the defaulting firm (and their relatives) are generally excluded from FSCS compensation, unless the firm is a small mutual association with unsalaried directors, or a credit union. Sole traders, small businesses under £1m turnover, and trustees of small self-administered schemes are eligible claimants."},{"question":"A bank fails on 15 December 2025. What is the maximum FSCS protection available to a single eligible depositor with that bank?","options":["£85,000","£110,000","£220,000","£1.4 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For failures after 1 December 2025, the FSCS deposit protection limit increased from £85,000 to £110,000 per eligible person per firm/licence (£220,000 for joint accounts)."},{"question":"A client deposits £1.2 million temporarily following the sale of their house. Under the FSCS temporary high balances rules (post 1 December 2025), how much of this is protected, and for how long?","options":["£85,000, for 6 months","£1 million, for 6 months","£1.4 million, for 6 months","The full £1.2 million, indefinitely"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Temporary high balances (e.g. from a property sale or inheritance) are protected up to £1.4 million for a period of 6 months, following the increase from £1 million effective 1 December 2025."},{"question":"A SIPP operator fails after 1 April 2019. What is the maximum FSCS compensation payable per eligible person, per firm?","options":["£50,000","£85,000","£110,000","100% of the claim, uncapped"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"If a SIPP operator fails, FSCS compensation is capped at £85,000 per eligible person per firm — the same limit as applies to bad pension advice and general investment claims. Only where the pension provider itself fails is compensation 100% uncapped."},{"question":"Under the FSCS rules for insurance firms failing after 8 October 2020, what proportion of a claim relating to compulsory insurance is protected?","options":["75%","90%","95%","100%"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Compulsory insurance, long-term insurance, professional indemnity insurance, and claims arising from death/incapacity due to sickness, injury or infirmity are 100% protected. All other kinds of insurance are 90% protected."},{"question":"Which of the following is NOT one of the six principles of the Data Protection Act 2018?","options":["Personal data must be processed lawfully and fairly","Personal data must be adequate, relevant and not excessive","Personal data must be transferred outside the UK wherever technically possible","Personal data must be kept for no longer than is necessary"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"There is no DPA 2018 principle requiring data to be transferred outside the UK — this is not a principle at all. The six genuine principles cover lawfulness/fairness, purpose limitation, data minimisation ('adequate, relevant and not excessive'), accuracy, storage limitation, and security."},{"question":"A firm becomes aware of a personal data breach. Within what timeframe must it report the breach to the ICO, where feasible?","options":["24 hours","48 hours","72 hours","7 days"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Part 3 of the DPA 2018 requires organisations to report notifiable personal data breaches to the ICO within 72 hours of becoming aware, where feasible."},{"question":"A firm suffers a data breach but the affected personal data was securely encrypted, meaning the breach is unlikely to result in high risk to individuals. What is the firm's obligation regarding notifying the affected individuals?","options":["It must still notify individuals within 72 hours","It has no duty to notify affected individuals directly, as one of the exemptions applies","It must notify the FOS instead of the individuals","It must notify individuals within 30 days regardless of risk"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The duty to inform individuals does not apply where appropriate technical/organisational measures (such as encryption) were applied to the affected data, meaning high risk is unlikely to materialise. The firm may still need to notify the ICO if there is a risk to rights and freedoms."},{"question":"Under UK GDPR, what is the 'higher maximum' fine the ICO may impose for breaches of the data protection principles?","options":["£8.7 million or 2% of annual worldwide turnover, whichever is higher","£17.5 million or 4% of annual worldwide turnover, whichever is higher","£20 million or 4% of annual worldwide turnover, whichever is lower","£17.5 million or 4% of UK turnover only, whichever is higher"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The higher maximum penalty under UK GDPR is £17.5 million or 4% of total annual worldwide turnover in the preceding financial year, whichever is higher, applying to breaches of core principles, individual rights, or international data transfer rules."},{"question":"Under UK GDPR, which of the following is a key difference in scope compared with the DPA?","options":["UK GDPR applies only to data controllers, while DPA applies to both controllers and processors","UK GDPR applies to both data controllers and processors, and can cover overseas entities offering goods/services to or monitoring UK data subjects","UK GDPR applies only within the UK, while DPA has extraterritorial effect","UK GDPR does not require consent to be freely given, unlike the DPA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"UK GDPR applies to both controllers and processors (unlike the DPA, which applies to controllers only) and has extraterritorial reach, covering overseas controllers/processors whose activities relate to offering goods/services to, or monitoring the behaviour of, UK data subjects."},{"question":"Who is generally expected by the FCA to be appointed as a firm's whistleblowing champion?","options":["The Chief Executive Officer","A non-executive director (or a senior manager if the firm has no NED); insurers should apportion the role to a director or senior manager","The Head of Compliance only","Any employee nominated by staff vote"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FCA expects most firms to appoint a non-executive director as their whistleblowing champion, or a senior manager where there is no NED. Insurers are expected to apportion the role to a director or senior manager."},{"question":"How frequently must a firm present a report on whistleblowing to its board?","options":["Monthly","Quarterly","At least annually","Only when a disclosure is received"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Firms must present a report on whistleblowing to the board at least annually, covering the operation and effectiveness of the firm's whistleblowing systems and controls while maintaining confidentiality of individual whistleblowers."},{"question":"What legislation originally introduced protection for individuals from retaliation for reporting workplace concerns to regulatory authorities (whistleblowing)?","options":["The Employment Rights Act 1996","The Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998","The Financial Services Act 2012","The Bribery Act 2010"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998 introduced legal protection for whistleblowers against retaliation. The Employment Rights Act 1996 is relevant to detriment/dismissal claims (s.47B and s.103A) but PIDA is the originating whistleblowing legislation."},{"question":"In what circumstances could a claim on the Financial Services Compensation Scheme be made?","options":["When an authorised firm becomes insolvent","Exclusively in response to claims by retail clients","Breach of principles of business","As an alternative to mediation by the Financial Ombudsman Scheme"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Financial Services Compensation Scheme was established to provide compensation where authorised persons and appointed representatives are unable to satisfy claims against them. Although the scheme mainly applies to retail clients, it can also apply to professional clients and eligible counterparties."},{"question":"Gina and Tobias open a joint deposit account in the UK. If they wish to ensure that they do not lose any money on the deposit, which of the following is the maximum deposit they could make and still ensure this?","options":["Up to £350,000","Up to £240,000","Up to £170,000","Up to £120,000"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"£120,000 is the amount is guaranteed by the FSCS per person. A joint account held by two people will receive protection on twice that."},{"question":"For how many years must a firm keep information on a complaint for MiFID business?","options":["Three years from the date it receives the complaint","Three years from the date it resolves the complaint","Five years from the date it receives the complaint","Five years from the date it resolves the complaint"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The firm must make and retain records for a minimum of five years from the date of its receipt of the complaint with regard to MiFID business."},{"question":"If the Financial Ombudsman Service makes an award it can be for:\nIActual financial loss\nIIPain and suffering\nIIIDamage to reputation\nIVDistress or inconvenience","options":["I, and II","I and III","I, II, III and IV","II, III and IV"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FOS can make awards for a range of reasons including financial loss, pain and suffering, damage to reputation and distress or inconvenience."},{"question":"Which of the following is true in relation to the Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS)?","options":["Deposits are not covered by the scheme","The maximum payout for investment business is unlimited","Claims must be made within a set time limit","All eligible counterparties are covered"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A claim must be brought to the FSCS within a set time (normally within six years of the date on which the event giving rise to the claim occurred). The maximum payout for a claim against an investment firm (i.e. in relation to protected investment business) is £85,000. Separate FSCS arrangements exist to protect deposit accounts at UK authorised banks and policy holders of UK authorised insurers."},{"question":"How is the Financial Services Compensation Scheme funded?","options":["By investment businesses authorised under the Financial Services and Markets Act 2000","By contributions from the European Central Bank","By the Treasury","By a small levy on all stock market transactions"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Compensation Scheme is funded by authorised investment business in the UK."},{"question":"All of the following are true of the Financial Services Compensation Scheme in relation to investments, except:","options":["It applies to business conducted through the appointed representative of an FCA firm","For the scheme as a whole, the total payout for compensation in any one year is unlimited","The scheme is funded partly by government funds and partly by levies charged on FCA members","Each claim has a maximum payout of £85,000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Financial Services Compensation Scheme is funded by levies on the authorised community only. The maximum claim for investments is now £85,000."},{"question":"If an insurer becomes insolvent, who would a small partnership apply to for compensation in light of a general insurance contract?","options":["FOS","FSCS","FCA","Bank of England"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Small partnerships can claim off the FSCS."},{"question":"Which of the following statements about a firm's internal complaints procedure is FALSE?","options":["The procedure must detail the method of response to complaints","It must cover how complaints will be investigated","Complaints must be dealt with by someone involved in the subject matter of the complaint","The complaints procedure must be in writing"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"It is important that complaints are dealt with by someone impartial and not involved in the same area."},{"question":"With whom must a processor of personal information register?","options":["Information Commissioner's Office","Financial Conduct Authority","Competition and Markets Authority","Department for Business Innovation and Skills"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The Data Protection Act requires registration with the information commissioner."},{"question":"A firm argues that a disputed term cannot be challenged because it does not appear on the grey list. What is the best response?","options":["The term is fair because only grey-list terms can be challenged under the Act","The term is presumed fair unless the consumer proves actual financial loss","The term may still be unfair because the grey list is indicative and non-exhaustive","The term is enforceable because the grey list applies only to non-financial services"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The grey list is indicative and non-exhaustive, so a term not appearing on it may still be unfair. Fairness is determined by considering the subject matter, the circumstances when agreed and the other terms of the contract."},{"question":"If an individual is awarded money in respect of restitution, to whom is it paid first?","options":["Financial Conduct Authority","Financial Services Compensation Scheme","Her Majesty's Treasury","Financial Ombudsman Service"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The money will be paid to the FCA first and then passed on to the individual concerned.\nIf the money had been in respect of compensation following a liquidation it would be paid out by the FSCS."},{"question":"How often must an authorised firm report details concerning complaints received, closed and outstanding to the FCA?","options":["Every eight weeks","Every 25 business days","Every six months","Annually"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"This report must be filed even if the firm has received no complaints against it."},{"question":"Which of the following is not an obligation on a regulated firm when ensuring complaints receive a proper response?","options":["Establish an internal procedure to handle complaints","Send a copy of all complaints to the FCA","Remedy any legitimate grievances","Inform the client of the Financial Ombudsman Service"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The regulator does not need to receive copies of all complaints. However, a summary report on how complaints were handled does need to be sent."},{"question":"A manager based overseas supervises staff in the United Kingdom. Under the whistleblowing training requirements, what must the firm ensure?","options":["Training is required only for managers physically based in the United Kingdom","Training is required only if the manager is an approved person under the Senior Managers and Certification Regime","Training is optional where local employment law already covers protected disclosures","Training is provided to managers of United Kingdom-based employees wherever the manager is based"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The rules require appropriate training for UK-based employees and for managers of UK-based employees wherever the manager is based. This reflects the focus on protecting disclosures made by UK-based staff rather than the manager's location."},{"question":"If a firm receives and deals with a complaint with respect to non-MiFID business, how long is it necessary to keep the records of the complaint?","options":["Five years from the date of the resolution of the complaint","Three years from the receipt of the complaint","Five years from the date of the complaint","Three years from the date of the resolution of the complaint"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"For non-MiFID business, the records are kept for at least three years."},{"question":"A firm settles an employment dispute with a worker and includes wording preventing the worker from contacting regulators. Which statement is most accurate?","options":["The clause is acceptable if the worker receives independent legal advice","The clause is acceptable if the concern does not involve consumer detriment","The firm must include wording explaining that workers have a legal right to blow the whistle","The firm must notify the Financial Conduct Authority only if the worker later makes a disclosure"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The whistleblowing rules require firms to put text in settlement agreements explaining that workers have a legal right to blow the whistle. A clause attempting to prevent contact with regulators would be inconsistent with that requirement."},{"question":"Under Data Protection Principle 1, information must be processed:","options":["Accurately and timely","Accurately and lawfully","Fairly and timely","Lawfully and fairly"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"There are a total of six enforceable principles of good practice contained in GDPR."},{"question":"Which of the following is an eligible complainant under the FCA regulations?","options":["A company that was considered to be a professional client at the time of problem","A private individual who has never worked in the financial sector","A FTSE 100 company with group turnover of more than £102.5 million","A CIS with balance sheet totals of more than €20 million and net turnover of €42 million"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The others are not considered eligible complainants. An eligible complainant is a small retail customer."},{"question":"If a firm receives a complaint with respect to MiFID business and deals with it within two weeks, how long is it necessary to keep the record of the complaint?","options":["Five years from the date of the resolution of the complaint","Three years from the date of the receipt of the complaint","Five years from the date of the receipt of the complaint","Three years from the date of the resolution of the complaint"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"With MiFID business, the records must be kept for at least five years."},{"question":"What is the maximum claim on the Financial Services Compensation Scheme in the event of an insolvency of a firm where the investor has lost money through protected investment business?","options":["£85,000","£50,000","£37,500","£120,000"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"An investor can claim up to £85,000 from the FSCS for investments."},{"question":"An authorised firm has made a mass detriment reference to the FCA, because it feels that if the customer complaint was referred to the FOS, that the FOS is likely to find in favour of the complainant.\nWhat action, if any, is the FCA required to make?","options":["The FCA must respond within 90 calendar days to the firm","The FCA must respond within 90 business days to the firm","The FCA has no obligation to respond at all, and can refuse to look at the matter","The FCA will refer the case to the FOS, giving its input on the matter"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA must respond within 90 calendar days to the party making a mass detriment reference."},{"question":"Which of the following is the requirement concerning the holding of personal data under Principle 3 of the Data Protection Act 2018?","options":["Extensive, relevant and adequate","Comprehensive, not excessive and relevant","Adequate, relevant and not excessive","Extensive, adequate and comprehensive"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The correct words from Principle 3 of the Data Protection Act are 'adequate, relevant and not excessive'."},{"question":"Which of the following does not have any rights under the Financial Services Compensation Scheme?","options":["A retail client with an ISA","Trustee of a scheme with assets of less than £1 million","A small business with regard to a general insurance contract","An authorised firm with respect to general insurance"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Authorised companies are not able to claim for general insurance contracts under FSCS."},{"question":"Which of the following is true in relation to the FCA's power to seek redress for consumers?","options":["Does not apply to breaches by market counterparties","Does not apply to transactions below £5,000","Is subject to a 28 day limit for the FCA to assess a claim","Includes breaches by unauthorised persons"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"In areas such as market abuse and money laundering the FCA have the power to seek redress from both authorised and unauthorised persons if their behaviour has an impact on consumers."},{"question":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 gives the FCA the power to do which of the following?","options":["To enforce criminal legislation, such as the Criminal Justice Act","To challenge unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts","To enforce civil legislation, such as the Market Abuse Regulation","To challenge unauthorised firms conducting regulated activities"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Consumer Rights Act 2015 allows the FCA to challenge unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts. \n\nThe Financial Services and Markets Act 2000 gives the FCA the power to do the other options."},{"question":"Which statement about transparency under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 is correct?","options":["A term that is transparent is automatically fair in all circumstances","A term that lacks transparency is necessarily unfair and void in every case","Transparency is relevant, but fairness must still be assessed in light of all relevant factors","Transparency applies only to notices and not to terms in consumer contracts"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The source states that the CRA requires terms to be transparent, but a term that fails the transparency requirement is not necessarily unfair by that fact alone. Fairness must be determined in the light of all relevant factors."},{"question":"Which of the following is UNTRUE in respect of the procedures of an authorised firm for dealing with client complaints?","options":["Firms must have adequate internal controls put in place","Firms must refer all complaints to the regulator","The firm must remedy legitimate grievances","The firm has a duty to inform clients of other avenues of complaint"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Although it is a requirement for relevant firms to 'report' data and statistics regarding numbers of complaints to the FCA, it is not a requirement to 'refer' complaints to the FCA. Internal complaints procedures must be in writing and cover the receipt of complaints, methods of response and how they will be investigated. Complaints should be handled by a member of staff with sufficient experience and authority, and who was not involved in the matter.\nIf a complaint cannot be resolved, the firm must inform the client about their rights to complain to the Financial Ombudsman and provide the client with the explanatory leaflet about the Financial Ombudsman Service."},{"question":"Which power does the Financial Conduct Authority have under the Consumer Rights Act 2015 in relation to unfair terms in financial services consumer contracts?","options":["It can impose criminal sanctions on directors who approve unfair terms","It can require immediate compensation to all affected consumers without court process","It can seek an undertaking to amend or remove an unfair term from future contracts","It can cancel any consumer contract automatically once a term is found to be unfair"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The FCA may seek an undertaking from a firm that it will amend or remove an unfair contract term from future consumer contracts. It may also apply to the court for an injunction, but the source does not state the broader powers described in the other options."},{"question":"If a complaint has not been resolved by a firm who should the complainant turn to next?","options":["FCA","Financial Ombudsman","FSCS","The Treasury"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The Ombudsman is approached only if the firm has not resolved the complaint."},{"question":"What is the maximum payout that will be made for deposits under the FSCS?","options":["£48,000","£50,000","£85,000","£120,000"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The FSCS currently pay out up to £120,000 for deposits."},{"question":"All of the following represent data protection principles under DPA 2018, except:","options":["Must not be unlawfully processed","Adequate, relevant and not excessive in relation to the purpose for which it is processed","Processed in a manner which is clear, fair and not misleading","Accurate and kept up to date"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Clear, fair and not misleading is part of the communications rules."},{"question":"Which person would generally be eligible to complain to the Financial Ombudsman Service, assuming the complaint arises from a relevant business relationship with the firm?","options":["A large partnership with annual turnover of £20 million","A charity with annual income of £5 million","An overseas financial institution using treasury services","An authorised firm complaining about an activity for which it has permission"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Eligible complainants include charities with an annual income of less than £6.5 million at the time the complaint is raised. Large partnerships are not listed as eligible complainants, and authorised firms are generally not eligible where the complaint concerns an activity for which they already have permission."},{"question":"All of the following must always be complied with in relation to customer complaints, except:","options":["The presence of written procedures in relation to investigating complaints","Always paying compensation","The creation of written procedures in relation to responding to complaints","Disclosure of the right to refer the complaint to the Financial Ombudsman Scheme"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Options A, C and D are compulsory in order to fulfil the FCA's requirements regarding customer complaints."},{"question":"Per the complaints procedures, how often are firms required to file a summary report of complaints received?","options":["Monthly","Quarterly","Semi-annually","Annually"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A firm is required to file a report with the FCA every six months, where a firm receives 500 or more complaints during a reporting period."},{"question":"Which of the following is not true regarding the Financial Services Compensation Scheme?","options":["Retail clients may claim in respect of all losses (subject to a maximum claim)","In certain circumstances professional clients may make a claim on the scheme","Eligible counterparties may never make a claim on the scheme","The scheme is funded by levies on authorised firms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Retail clients are 'eligible claimants', and so may claim on the scheme in respect of all losses (subject to a maximum claim). Any client of an insurance firm (including some professional clients and some eligible counterparties, but not other insurance firms) may also claim in respect of insurance only."},{"question":"A consumer and a firm negotiate a term individually rather than using standard wording. How does the Consumer Rights Act 2015 treat that term?","options":["It is excluded from review because only standard terms can be unfair","It is protected from challenge if the consumer signed the contract voluntarily","It is outside scope unless the term appears on the grey list","It is still capable of being unfair despite being individually negotiated"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Unfair terms provisions protect consumers against unfair terms in contracts and notices, including terms that have been individually negotiated. Individual negotiation does not remove the possibility that a term may be unfair."},{"question":"Who must a data controller be registered with under the Data Protection Act?","options":["Treasury","Information commissioner","MLRO","PRA"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Note: MLRO = Money Laundering Reporting Officer."},{"question":"If an eligible claimant wins a claim against a deposit placed in a UK based credit institution, what is the maximum payout that can be made?","options":["£85,000","€ 85,000","£120,000","€ 120,000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The question is testing your knowledge of the Financial Services Compensation Scheme: Who can pay out on losses suffered by eligible claimants with regard to deposits, investments and insurance - subject to limits. The maximum payout for deposits is now £120,000."},{"question":"The FCA requires that firms maintain records of complaints for a period of three years from the date of the receipt of the complaint for non-MiFID business. These records need not include details of:","options":["Any correspondence between the firm and the complainant, including details of any redress offered by the firm","The number of phone calls taken place in an attempt to resolve the complaint","The substance of the complaint","The name of the complainant"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"These records are required for the purposes of monitoring by the FCA."},{"question":"To whom, in the first instance, should a consumer make a complaint?","options":["The Financial Ombudsman Service","The FCA","A magistrate","The firm itself"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"Complaints should, in the first instance, be referred to the firm. If the dispute is not resolved, it may then be referred to the Financial Ombudsman Service."},{"question":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to:","options":["Exclude any contract terms in its documentation aimed at consumers","Define unfair terms in all consumer contracts in the United Kingdom","Force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms","Provide compensation for those subject to unfair contract terms"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"As a designated body under the Consumer Rights Act 2015, the FCA has the power to force financial services firm to amend or remove unfair contract terms."},{"question":"A firm uses a term in existing consumer contracts that the Financial Conduct Authority considers unfair. What further action may the Financial Conduct Authority take?","options":["Apply to the court for an injunction to prevent the term being used or enforced","Require the Financial Ombudsman Service to reopen all complaints linked to the term","Direct the Competition and Markets Authority to rewrite the firm's consumer contracts","Suspend the firm's permissions until all affected customers consent to the term again"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FCA can apply to the court for an injunction to prevent a firm from using or enforcing the term against existing customers. The other actions are not identified in the provided material."},{"question":"In which of the following situations would the Financial Services Compensation Scheme (FSCS) be called upon the make a compensation payment?","options":["Where the firm is unable to pay out to claimants","Where an official who is representing a customer’s complaint finds in favour of the client","Where the payment needs to be made to resolve a client’s pension dispute","Where the courts award a restitution order and there are insufficient funds to pay"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The FSCS will pay out compensation when an eligible claimant sees their financial institution become insolvent or the firm itself is in default. Since none of these words is available, the definition of default is the inability of the firm to pay due the bankruptcy, therefore the best answer."},{"question":"John works in a Spanish branch of company XYZ. Barry is one of the directors in the UK office who has found out that John has been involved in a criminal activity. Should Barry blow the whistle?","options":["Yes; only if the offence has been committed in Europe","Yes; regardless of where the offence has been committed","No; Barry's responsibility does not include Europe","No; it is not a problem connected to the UK office"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Whistleblowing refers to the Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998. PIDA allows an employee to make a protected disclosure if they believe a breach of law has been committed or an attempt to conceal this breach has occurred. PIDA allows for protected disclosure regardless of where the act has been committed."},{"question":"If a retail client wishes to ensure that they do not lose any money in a deposit, which of the following is the most suitable deposit or investment?","options":["Any amount in a UK account","Up to £85,000 in a UK account","Up to £120,000 in a UK bank account","Up to £150,000 in a UK bank account"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"£120,000 is the amount guaranteed by the FSCS."},{"question":"Which contracts are specifically identified as being covered by the Consumer Rights Act 2015?","options":["Contracts for goods, digital content and services","Contracts for securities, derivatives and wholesale deposits","Contracts for pensions, insurance and investment research","Contracts for mortgages, taxation and insolvency services"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The source states that the Act gives consumers statutory rights relating to consumer contracts for goods, digital content and services. The other options list categories not stated as the three core areas covered in the cited description."},{"question":"In relation to a breach of data protection, to whom does a data controller report?","options":["The Information Commissioner","The Chief Compliance Officer","The Comptroller of Data","The Secretary of State for Trade and Industry"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"The data controller is the firm responsible for controlling the personal data. Where a breach of data protection is detected or suspected it should report to the ICO."},{"question":"Harold has lost £70,000 through a failed investment scheme. How much will be paid if his claim to the compensation scheme is successful?","options":["50000","63000","70000","85000"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"Investors can claim for up to £85,000 of losses with regard to investments and will receive a payout of 100% of that claim."},{"question":"Which statement about the Financial Ombudsman Service is false?","options":["It is operationally independent of the FCA","It arbitrates in situations whereby the authorised firm's complaints procedures have already been used","It is for authorised corporate clients as well as individuals","It can consider complaints and make awards"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"The Financial Ombudsman Scheme is for 'eligible complainants' - retail clients that are also individuals, and certain small businesses, small charities and small trusts. Authorised firms are never eligible complainants."},{"question":"To whom is the Financial Service Compensation Scheme (FSCS) PRIMARILY accountable?","options":["The Treasury","FCA and PRA","Lord Chancellor","The First Lord of the Treasury"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The FSCS is accountable to the FCA and PRA primarily and to the Treasury indirectly through the regulators."},{"question":"A firm includes an individually negotiated term in a consumer investment contract allowing it to vary a key charge unilaterally after the contract is agreed. The term is transparent and does not appear on the grey list. Based on the Consumer Rights Act 2015, which conclusion is most accurate?","options":["The term is fair because transparency prevents any further fairness assessment","The term is fair because individually negotiated terms fall outside the unfair terms provisions","The term may still be unfair if, in the circumstances when agreed, it creates a significant imbalance contrary to good faith","The term is binding because only grey-list terms can be challenged by the Financial Conduct Authority"],"correctIndex":2,"explanation":"A term can still be unfair even if it is transparent, individually negotiated and not listed on the grey list. Fairness is assessed by considering whether, contrary to good faith, the term creates a significant imbalance to the consumer's detriment, taking account of the contract's subject matter, the circumstances when agreed and the other relevant terms."},{"question":"Which of the following statements is false with regards to whistleblowing?","options":["FCA firms must adopt and communicate to workers appropriate internal procedures for handling workers' concerns","The FCA would regard as a serious matter any evidence that a firm had acted to the detriment of a worker because he or she had made a protected disclosure","Evidence that the firm is acting to the detriment of an employee would call into question the fitness and propriety of the firm","The rules allow the firm and an employee to breach rules on client confidentiality at all times"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"The whistleblowing rules (SYSC18) do not allow employees to breach rules on client confidentiality 'at all times'. Generally client confidentiality rules should be followed, though there may be some limited circumstances where client confidentiality rules can be broken if required to make a relevant disclosure.\n\nThe rules on whistleblowing are found in FCA Handbook Block 1: High Level Standards in the Senior Management Arrangements, Systems and Control (SYSC) sourcebook. Note that the FCA can withdraw the firm's authorisation if it breaches these rules."},{"question":"If an eligible claimant puts in a claim with respect to a protected investment with the FSCS for £37,000 what would be the maximum payout?","options":["£85,000","£37,000","£33,500","£0"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"Investors can claim for up to £85,000 of losses with regard to investments and will receive a payout of 100% of that claim."},{"question":"A firm subject to the whistleblowing rules wants to allocate oversight of its internal arrangements to a mid-level compliance manager with limited authority. Based on the rules, what is the main regulatory issue?","options":["The role may be inappropriate because the individual may lack sufficient seniority","The role is unsuitable because only the Money Laundering Reporting Officer may hold it","The role is prohibited because whistleblowing oversight must be outsourced externally","The role is invalid because the Financial Conduct Authority appoints the individual directly"],"correctIndex":0,"explanation":"Firms are required to appoint an individual of sufficient seniority as whistleblowing champion. The champion must be able to ensure and oversee the integrity, independence and effectiveness of the firm's whistleblowing arrangements, which would be difficult if the person lacks adequate authority or resources."},{"question":"When must a firm normally send a final response to a complainant?","options":["Four weeks","Five weeks","Six weeks","Eight weeks"],"correctIndex":3,"explanation":"An acknowledgement letter must be sent promptly, and a final letter within eight weeks."},{"question":"Which arrangement best meets the regulatory requirement for a firm's internal whistleblowing procedures?","options":["A process limited to named employees reporting concerns through line management only","A process able to handle all types of disclosure from all types of individuals","A process restricted to concerns supported by documentary evidence at the outset","A process available only to employees based in the United Kingdom"],"correctIndex":1,"explanation":"The rules require firms to put in place internal whistleblowing arrangements able to handle all types of disclosure from all types of individuals. Limiting disclosures by channel, person or evidential threshold would not meet that requirement."}],"flashcards":[{"front":"Consumer Rights Act 2015 (CRA) — purpose","back":"Consolidates UK consumer protection law for goods, digital content and services; gives statutory rights/remedies and reforms unfair terms law (Part 2)."},{"front":"CRA 'grey list'","back":"An indicative, non-exhaustive list of contract terms that may be regarded as unfair; absence from the list does not guarantee fairness."},{"front":"Test for an unfair term under CRA","back":"Contrary to the requirement of good faith, it causes a significant imbalance in the parties' rights/obligations to the detriment of the consumer — applies even to individually negotiated terms."},{"front":"FCA's power under CRA re: unfair terms","back":"As a 'qualifying body' under the Unfair Terms in Consumer Contracts Regulations 1999, the FCA can seek undertakings from firms or apply to court for injunctions."},{"front":"Cancellation periods by product","back":"Life & pension products: 30 calendar days. Cash ISAs and other non-life/pensions products (advised or distance): 14 calendar days."},{"front":"When does the cancellation period start?","back":"From the day the contract concludes, or the day the client receives contractual information, if later."},{"front":"Cancellation record retention periods","back":"Indefinitely for pension transfers/opt-outs/FSAVCs; at least 5 years for life policy/pension/personal pension/stakeholder pension; at least 3 years for other cases."},{"front":"Eligible complainant — charity & trust thresholds","back":"Charity: annual income under £6.5 million. Trust: net asset value under £5 million."},{"front":"Eligible complainant — micro-enterprise & SME thresholds","back":"Micro-enterprise: fewer than 10 employees, turnover/balance sheet ≤ €2 million. SME: turnover under £6.5 million and balance sheet under £5 million, or fewer than 50 employees."},{"front":"DISP 1 title","back":"'Treating Complainants Fairly' — the FCA's Dispute Resolution: Complaints Sourcebook chapter setting out complaint handling rules."},{"front":"DISP 1.2 / 1.3 / 1.4","back":"1.2 Consumer Awareness Rules (publicise complaints process/FOS rights); 1.3 Complaint Handling Rules (root cause analysis, systemic issues); 1.4 Complaints Resolution Rules (competent, diligent, impartial investigation)."},{"front":"Complaint response deadlines","back":"Standard: final/holding response within 8 weeks. PSD/EMD complaints (DISP 1.6.2A): final response within 15 business days, extendable to 35 business days in exceptional circumstances."},{"front":"Time limit to refer a complaint to the FOS after final response","back":"6 months."},{"front":"Complaint record retention periods","back":"At least 5 years for MiFID business/UCITS complaints; at least 3 years for all other complaints, from date of receipt."},{"front":"Enhanced FCA reporting threshold","back":"Firms receiving 500 or more complaints in a 6-month reporting period must provide enhanced complaint data (cause, timescales, redress, context)."},{"front":"'Next business day' informal complaint handling rule","back":"Firms may handle complaints informally, without an acknowledgement letter, if resolved within 3 business days of receipt."},{"front":"FOS jurisdictions — CJ vs VJ","back":"Compulsory Jurisdiction (CJ): automatic for FCA-authorised firms re: regulated activities. Voluntary Jurisdiction (VJ): covers complaints outside CJ; firms opt in contractually as 'VJ participants'."},{"front":"FOS funding","back":"Free to complainants; funded by an annual levy on firms plus case fees per case accepted/investigated."},{"front":"FOS award limits","back":"Act/omission on/after 1 Apr 2019, referred on/after 1 Apr 2025: £445,000 (£430,000 if referred on/after 1 Apr 2024). Act/omission before 1 Apr 2019: £200,000 (£195,000 pre-2025)."},{"front":"FOS award limits — annual adjustment","back":"Adjusted automatically on 1 April each year using CPI for the preceding January."},{"front":"FOS decision acceptance","back":"If the complainant accepts the FOS decision, it is final and binding on both parties."},{"front":"Super-complaint — FCA response deadline","back":"90 days. HM Treasury (via primary legislation) designates which consumer bodies may raise them."},{"front":"Mass-detriment reference","back":"Can be made by the FOS or a regulated firm where there are regular regulatory failures or a pattern of complaints likely to be upheld, causing large-scale consumer harm."},{"front":"FSCS purpose","back":"Pays compensation to eligible claimants when an authorised firm defaults and cannot meet valid claims; funded by a levy on authorised firms."},{"front":"FSCS deposit protection (failures after 1 Dec 2025)","back":"£110,000 per eligible person, per firm/licence (£220,000 for joint accounts) — up from £85,000."},{"front":"FSCS temporary high balances protection","back":"£1.4 million for 6 months (increased from £1 million on 1 Dec 2025) — e.g. proceeds of a property sale or inheritance."},{"front":"FSCS investment claims limit","back":"£85,000 per eligible person, per firm (failures after 1 April 2019)."},{"front":"FSCS pensions — provider vs SIPP operator failure","back":"Pension provider fails: 100% of claim, no upper limit. SIPP operator fails, or bad pension advice: £85,000 per eligible person per firm."},{"front":"FSCS insurance protection levels (failures after 8 Oct 2020)","back":"100% protected: compulsory, long-term, professional indemnity insurance, and death/incapacity claims. 90% protected: all other kinds of insurance."},{"front":"FSCS small business eligibility threshold","back":"Annual turnover of less than £1 million (eligible even though an authorised firm, if the claim is outside its Part 4A permission)."},{"front":"DPA 2018 — six principles","back":"Lawful & fair; specified/legitimate purpose; adequate/relevant/not excessive; accurate; not kept longer than necessary; secure."},{"front":"UK GDPR vs DPA — scope difference","back":"UK GDPR applies to both controllers and processors (DPA applies to controllers only) and has extraterritorial reach over overseas entities targeting UK data subjects."},{"front":"Data breach — ICO notification deadline","back":"Within 72 hours of the firm becoming aware, where feasible (DPA 2018, Part 3). Individuals must be told 'without undue delay' if high risk — a higher threshold than for the ICO."},{"front":"UK GDPR fines","back":"Higher maximum: £17.5m or 4% of annual worldwide turnover, whichever higher (principles/rights/transfers). Standard maximum: £8.7m or 2%, whichever higher (other provisions)."},{"front":"Right to Erasure vs Right to Data Portability","back":"Erasure ('right to be forgotten'): deletion of personal data in certain circumstances. Portability: obtain personal data in a machine-readable format to transmit to another controller."},{"front":"Whistleblowing champion — typical appointee","back":"A non-executive director (or senior manager if no NED); insurers apportion the role to a director or senior manager. Under SM&CR, deposit-taking banks and Solvency II insurers must appoint one."},{"front":"Whistleblowing board reporting & founding legislation","back":"Firms must report on whistleblowing to the board at least annually. Whistleblowing protection stems from the Public Interest Disclosure Act (PIDA) 1998."},{"front":"Employment Rights Act 1996 — whistleblowing sections","back":"s.47B (detriment for protected disclosure) and s.103A (unfair dismissal for whistleblowing) — firms must report to the FCA/PRA if they lose a tribunal case based on these."}]}],"chapterCount":4,"mcqCount":568,"flashcardCount":188};
